          "name": "Ya-Yi Huang",
          "search_text": "ya-yi huang not all reefs bleached: upwelling-driven thermal refugia on lyudao (green island) in taiwan during the fourth global event the 2023\u20132024 global mass coral bleaching event\u2013the fourth worldwide episode following 1998, 2010, and 2015\u20132017\u2013caused extensive coral loss across major reef regions. yet, despite widespread reports of ecosystem collapse, certain small islands with unique hydrodynamic regimes experienced markedly reduced bleaching impacts. these rare refuges provide crucial insights into how local physical processes can buffer coral communities under accelerating climate change. here, we assessed spatial variation in bleaching severity around lyudao (also known as green island), a volcanic island 40 km off the southeastern coast of taiwan. the island lies within the kuroshio current, where downstream island-wake upwelling produces two distinct thermal environments: the northern and eastern coasts are approximately 1.5 \u00b0c cooler than the southern and western coasts. we surveyed 13,823 coral colonies across nine sites at 5 m and 10 m depths, using a five-level scale to quantify bleaching severity. only 31.39% of colonies exhibited severe bleaching (levels 4\u20135), and 12.73% were fully bleached or dead\u2013substantially lower than many indo-pacific/ caribbean reports from the same event. juvenile corals showed slightly higher susceptibility (mean severity 2.72) than adults (2.65). dominant genera (montipora, porites, isopora, acropora, and pocillopora) consistently showed higher bleaching in the warmer south/west, whereas colonies in the cooler north/east exhibited less bleaching. site bleaching response index (sbri) values ranged from 3.29 to 4.41 in the south/west and from 2.25 to 2.74 in the north/east. linear regression demonstrated a strong positive relationship between accumulated heat stress and bleaching severity, confirming the role of kuroshio-driven upwelling in shaping spatial thermal resilience. our findings show that local hydrodynamic cooling can meaningfully mitigate bleaching impacts even during a global mass bleaching event. lyudao emerges as a rare \u201chope spot\u201d in taiwan, highlighting the importance of natural physical refugia for sustaining coral reefs in a rapidly warming world.",
