          "name": "Elizabeth Buccheri",
          "search_text": "elizabeth buccheri variability in reproductive capacity of hermaphroditic corals and the implications for local reef management hard corals predominantly utilise broadcast spawning to achieve sexual reproduction. it is becoming increasingly clear that the success of such spawning can be subject to allee effects in which fertilisation success declines rapidly with greater separation among colonies. yet different coral species naturally exist across a range of densities at local scales. if we assume that reproductive modes have evolved to maximise breeding success, it is likely that corals with naturally lower densities may exhibit different reproductive traits to compensate for a higher risk of allee effects. alternatively, reproductive traits might be highly conserved, and lower density may simply reflect a less successful reproductive strategy. here, we compiled fertilisation metrics for hermaphroditic spawning corals from the published literature to conduct a global review of the drivers of variability in reproduction across species. fertilisation success was examined as a function of coral egg size, localised density, and habitat preference (specialist vs generalist) for 28 hermaphroditic species across caribbean, western atlantic, and indo-pacific reefs. given the paucity of density data at a species resolution, particularly for the indo-pacific, expert-elicitation was conducted to gather localised, species-specific colony distribution metrics. we find that species commonly observed at low-densities are often more efficient fertilisers than high-density species, as indicated by their lower ec50 values, which show they can reach 50% fertilisation at lower sperm concentrations. this suggests that low density species have likely evolved mechanisms, like advanced gamete properties, to fertilise more efficiently. as coral populations continue to thin due to accelerated anthropogenic disturbance, it will be increasingly important to mitigate allee effects on reproductive output. understanding the sensitivity of different species to such effects will help target appropriate and effective restoration actions and help maintain reproductive populations on local scales.",
