          "name": "Alexandra Cormack",
          "search_text": "alexandra cormack evaluation of microfragmentation as a coral restoration technique: investigating the physiology of microfragments post-fragmentation and post-bleaching anthropogenically-induced ocean warming is driving global losses of coral cover; restoration organizations seek to improve reef health state by growing corals quickly and efficiently. many of these groups utilize microfragmentation techniques on massive, slow-growing corals to rapidly grow reef-building corals. however, microfragmentation does induce stress upon these corals, possibly leading to negative trade-offs. this study evaluated trade-offs of two different fragment sizes of orbicella faveolata \u2013 microfragments and large ramets \u2013 by investigating initial mortality post-fragmentation, comparing short and long-term growth rates, and observing how each fragment type responded to the 2023 caribbean bleaching event. within the first 25 days post-fragmentation, 34% of microfragments had died. mortality was not correlated to symbiont association, tissue biomass, or metabolic storage (total lipid weight, protein, carbohydrate, triglyceride, sterol, wax ester, and phospholipid energy content). after three months in nursery post-fragmentation, microfragment arrays (formed of the 7-8 healthiest microfragments from each genet) and large ramets (from the same genets) had similar symbiont community composition, tissue surface area growth, and instantaneous calcification rates. however, once temperatures began to rise, microfragment arrays were observed bleaching before large ramets, then losing tissue and dying due to diminished energy reserves. our findings suggest that microfragmentation on massive, slow growing corals can cost tissue immediately post-fragmentation, and lead to vulnerability to thermal stress even after months of recovery. this study found trade-offs associated with microfragmentation; this knowledge may aid restoration organizations in informed decision making.",
