          "name": "Elizabeth Wu",
          "search_text": "elizabeth wu investigating aquaporin-mediated transport in the context of coral bleaching coral bleaching is driven in part by oxidative stress, however the mechanisms that regulate free radical movement across cell membranes and that initiate bleaching-associated signaling cascades remain poorly understood. aquaporins (aqp) are a family of membrane-bound proteins that enable passive, gradient-driven transport of water and other small molecules such as hydrogen peroxide (h\u2082o\u2082) in several animal models. elucidating the transport mechanism of h\u2082o\u2082, a key reactive oxygen species produced in aerobic metabolism and known to increase in association with coral bleaching responses, would reveal a previously uncharacterized mechanism linking intracellular redox balance to bleaching susceptibility. preliminary proteomic analyses of pocillopora acuta identified four aquaporin-like proteins (paaqp3, 4, 9, and 12) sharing high sequence identity with human and pocillopora damicornis homologs. among these, paaqp3 was selected for further investigation due to its known localization in animal cell and phagosomal membranes, suggesting a potential role in both the coral host cell membrane and the phagosome-like symbiosome membrane. importantly, mammalian aqp3 is a transporter of h\u2082o\u2082 due to its electrochemical similarities to water. immunoblotting combined with immunofluorescence helped probe live p. acuta cells for the expression and localization of aqp3-like proteins (paaqp3, 20-25 kda). in parallel, molecular dynamic simulations of aquaporins indicate that the presence of these channels lowers the energetic barrier for h\u2082o\u2082 diffusion across lipid bilayers, supporting its role as an h\u2082o\u2082 transporter. together, these preliminary findings support the involvement of aqp3-like proteins in mediating intracellular h\u2082o\u2082 signaling in coral cells during stress. understanding how coral aquaporins contribute to free radical transport provides new molecular insight into coral bleaching mechanisms and may inform strategies for identifying or engineering more resilience in corals.",
