          "name": "Ioan Fuller",
          "search_text": "ioan fuller the role of antioxidants and prooxidants in redox equilibria in the coral symbiocyte and its disruption during thermal stress oxidative stress within the coral symbiocyte in response to thermal stress is considered a leading cause of coral bleaching. thermal stress disrupts redox homeostasis, inducing a state of oxidative dysregulation whereby the production of reactive nitrogen species (rns) and reactive oxygen species (ros) surpasses the antioxidant capacity of the coral symbiocyte. study of the prooxidant-antioxidant balance is therefore key to understanding the mechanism of coral bleaching. \r\nin this study, we measured intracellular rns and ros and the corresponding antioxidant response in live pocillopora acuta cell cultures under controlled thermal stress. the activities of three primary enzymatic antioxidants were measured: catalase (cat), superoxide dismutase (sod) and glutathione peroxidase (gpx). pairing analysis of antioxidant activity with rns/ros production provided a robust method to identify the state of oxidative stress in the coral symbiont and the physiological impact of this stress. with the use of applied thermal stress and the addition of exogenous prooxidants, it was possible to identify a critical inflection point in the antioxidant-prooxidant balance in live cell culture where enzymatic defenses become saturated resulting in runaway stress. this approach provides insight into redox equilibria in the coral symbiocyte, its disruption in response to thermal stress, and highlights potential opportunities for targeted intervention with exogenous antioxidants to mitigate bleaching.",
