          "name": "Kailey Pascoe",
          "search_text": "kailey pascoe tracking reef recovery in papah\u0101naumoku\u0101kea with underwater photogrammetry coral reef ecosystems are affected by climate-driven disturbances, yet relatively few studies document long-term changes following major physical damage. in 2018, hurricane walaka passed over lalo in papah\u0101naumoku\u0101kea as a category 3 hurricane, with the wave energy generated by the hurricane reaching depths of 27 m (90 ft) and causing severe physical disturbance. this study presents pre- and post-hurricane data from 2017 to 2025 at the disturbance site. repeated structure-from-motion (sfm) photogrammetry surveys over the same 50 m\u00b2 survey area were used to quantify changes in benthic cover, coral colony abundance, colony size distributions, and three-dimensional structural metrics. a previously acropora-dominated reef with approximately 70% live coral cover experienced severe damage, resulting in 99.7% rubble and no remaining live coral colonies in 2017. fine-scale habitat complexity metrics were also substantially altered. . by 2021, recruitment was recorded, including pocillopora meandrina (56 colonies, 2\u201312 cm) and porites lobata (100 juveniles, \u22644 cm), along with reef binders like macroalgae and sponges. across the site, 3d colony surface areas were approximately 2.6 times greater than 2d planar measurements, indicating contributions to local structural complexity. surveys conducted between 2022 and 2025 confirmed continuing recovery of the site, showing overall increases in live coral cover, growth of individual colonies, and continued presence of early-recruiting species. fish belt transect surveys also documented a shift from near-absence in 2019 to 35 species observed by 2021, representing multiple trophic groups. by integrating multi-year sfm benthic surveys and fish observations, this study contributes to a quantitative record of long-term changes at the site affected by the major hurricane event, forming the important baseline for the evaluation of ecological responses to changes in benthic structure. our findings support continuing efforts to strengthen monitoring and management activities within papah\u0101naumoku\u0101kea. understanding biodiversity through habitat complexity in hawaiian coral reefs coastal reef ecosystems are increasingly affected by climate-driven disturbances and local stressors that alter habitat structure and ecological function before visible declines in coral health occur. monitoring approaches capable of detecting these early structural changes at spatial resolutions relevant to ecological processes and management needs are important for assessing reef condition. integrating remote sensing with in-situ assessments provides an opportunity to develop scalable baselines that improve the detection of ecological change and support management decision-making. here we combine regional airborne remote sensing from the global airborne observatory (gao) with diver-based fish surveys and high-resolution structure-from-motion (sfm) photogrammetry to quantify spatially explicit and integrated metrics of habitat complexity across the study area of the hawaiian islands. a gao-derived 12-strata habitat classification guided stratified-random sampling across 110 reef sites spanning 225 km of coastline. at each site, sfm surveys produced digital elevation models, which were used to extract structural indicators including surface complexity, slope, curvature, and vector ruggedness (vrm). orthophotomosaics provided species-level coral identifications, colony size estimates, morphology, and benthic cover. complementary in-situ fish surveys documented species richness, biomass, and abundance. these multi-scale datasets allow us to examine how coral assemblage traits and fine-scale three-dimensional structure relate to fish assemblage characteristics across functional groups, and how shifts in benthic composition correspond to measurable changes in habitat structure. multivariate analyses identify the structural and biological features most strongly associated with variation in ecological condition and provide metrics that may respond to disturbance before changes in coral cover become detectable. integrating seascape-scale mapping with photogrammetric and in-situ ecological surveys establishes a framework for monitoring reef condition across nested spatial scales. these combined datasets can assist managers in identifying areas that may be vulnerable to environmental change, selecting locations for targeted protection or restoration, and evaluating the effectiveness of management actions across hawai\u2018i\u2019s reef ecosystems.",
