          "name": "Kate Fetkenhour",
          "search_text": "kate fetkenhour peroxynitrite as a key mediator of nitrosative-oxidative stress in live coral cells exposed to thermal stress the oxidative stress hypothesis is currently considered the leading explanation of coral bleaching, the most significant ecological threat to coral reefs today. reactive nitrogen species (rns), particularly nitric oxide (no), have been implicated in this stress response cascade. notably, no can react with superoxide to form peroxynitrite (onoo-), a highly reactive oxidant that bridges nitrosative and oxidative stress pathways. unlike many ros that are neutralized by antioxidant defenses, onoo- inflicts direct oxidative and nitrative damage to proteins, lipids and organelles, linking environmental stress to irreversible cellular injury.\r\nin this study, we measured intracellular onoo- in live pocillopora acuta cell cultures, providing real-time insight into the dynamics of the coral-symbiont relationship under thermal stress. peroxynitrite production was measured relative to total reactive oxygen species (ros) within individual coral cells through a dual-labeling fluorescence approach. probes specific to intracellular ros and rns were optimized for use in the live cell culture, and spectral unmixing via confocal microscopy was employed to disentangle dye-specific fluorescence from the endogenous fluorescence this allowed us to test the hypothesis that overall ros accumulation is not the primary driver of bleaching or symbiont loss, but that specific, highly cytotoxic rns-derived oxidants such as onoo- play a more direct mechanistic role in initiating cellular damage and disrupting symbiosis.",
