          "name": "Katie Cramer",
          "search_text": "katie cramer tracking long-term change in caribbean coral reef water quality using historical data caribbean coral reefs are imperiled due to multiple human stressors. although it is widely suspected that coral loss has been associated with declining reef water quality from land-based pollution, it has been difficult to quantify this link due to the lack of consistent and accurate water quality monitoring. to assess the role of land-based pollution in recent coral declines, we compiled and synthesized existing paleoecological, historical, and modern data on reef water quality across the caribbean region spanning 30 reef sites from 12 countries and encompassing the period from 1970-2021. we focused on two aspects of water quality that have been most consistently collected in the caribbean over the past 50 years and that have been shown to have direct impacts on corals: nutrients and water clarity. we analyzed trends in water quality within well-studied countries or reef tracts and across the wider caribbean region, allowing us to understand how the differing histories of land use and human population and varying oceanographic conditions across locations have played a role in the varying trajectories of reef water quality change and ecosystem health. on a regional scale, nutrient levels notably increased and water clarity notably decreased between the 1970s and 2000, nitrogen to phosphorous ratios were negatively correlated with coral cover, and by the 1970s nutrient concentrations had already exceeded established thresholds beyond which coral health is compromised. detailed investigations of case study sites revealed that coral cover declines were often related to water quality declines even in marine protected areas and at offshore reef sites previously thought to be less influenced by land-based pollution. this synthesis of various types of long-term historical data confirms the urgent need to monitor reef water quality and to mitigate land-based pollution to improve reef health and resilience in the face of increasing climate change impacts.",
