          "search_text": "aidan stockley environmental drivers of habitat and species composition on a fringing reef in the solomon islands fringing reefs are under pressure from land-use, unsustainable fishing, and climate change. this study maps spatial patterns in substrata and species on a fringing reef at vavanga, solomon islands to support planning on land and sea. drone and underwater videos were used to georeference 4,000 underwater images. points on images were assigned to taxa and substrata and percentage cover interpolated in relation to distance offshore and to freshwater inputs using classification and regression trees. different substrata and biota were correlated with distance to the reef crest and freshwater streams. after rain, drone videos show plumes of sediment-laden freshwater extending over the reef. logging has occurred previously and may begin again, but it is uncertain what downstream effects occur. coral cover along the channel increases seaward from the river mouth but even inshore there are large colonies of montipora and porites we modelled in 3d from video. the reef flat south-east of the river contrasts strongly with the north-western side. on the south-east side, coarse sand runs along the village foreshore; extends out through seagrass beds (thalassia hemprichii); coral rock, sargassum, padina and turbinaria ornata; then sparse montipora digitata, acropora spp. and pocillopora verrucosa towards the reef crest; and high hard coral cover (>40%), coralline algae and halimeda on the upper slope. north-west of the river, sharp ridges of weathered karst limestone and silt cover the inner reef flat and there is little sand or seagrass but sparse macroalgae and increasing coral cover offshore. mapping finer-scale patterns in reef benthos provides information on species associations, shifts in distribution and spatial heterogeneity at different scales. we discuss how these techniques can be used to inform local communities on the impacts of logging, coastal development and climate change and be applied elsewhere.",
