          "name": "Graham Hinchliffe",
          "search_text": "graham hinchliffe georeferencing synchronised drone and underwater video of coral reef benthos fringing reefs are ecologically and culturally important environments at the land- sea interface, yet their location exposes them to damaging human impacts from coastal development, sedimentation, unsustainable fishing and climate change. despite this, fine-scale features and underlying ecological processes are seldom mapped at resolutions fit for local management. this limits participatory planning and constrains detection of nearshore environmental change. to address this, we used spatial video from low-cost unoccupied aerial vehicles (uavs or drones) synchronised with video cameras mounted on a customised snorkeller propulsion vehicle to georeference underwater images of reef substrata and epibenthos. percentage cover of taxa and substrata were visually estimated from points overlaid on the images and spatially interpolated across and along 3 km of fringing reef at vavanga village, kolombangara island, solomon islands. data points were used to map species distributions in response to their environment; classify drone photo-mosaics; train machine learning algorithms to identify features; and establish transects to estimate temporal change, reducing the need for physical markers. we discuss challenges and solutions to mapping over and under water including the lack of stationary landmarks, effects of altitude, waves, sun-glint and methods for georeferencing underwater imagery. integrating synchronised aerial and underwater perspectives within a geospatial framework is a powerful and visually engaging approach to monitor reef condition, enabling both technical analysis and community interpretation.",
