          "name": "Samantha Gillis",
          "search_text": "samantha gillis a geospatial analysis of a coral reef flat in the solomon islands informed by ecological and cultural knowledge coral reefs support a quarter of known marine biodiversity and provide food security to a majority of island nations, while occupying less than 1% of the ocean floor. in the anthropocene we face major environmental challenges, requiring rapid and realistic action to salvage our biome. human prosperity is often considered when prioritising reef conservation, yet when the people who rely on and live amongst these ecosystems are insufficiently included in the reefs\u2019 future plans, then all foresight is restricted, and thus ineffective. my phd will aim to visualise the benefits of weaving scientific practice and melanesian culture to evaluate the temporal fluctuation of a customary fringing reef. to complement geospatial surveys, i will co-develop a pictograph with vavanga village to visualise their traditional ecological knowledge (tek), local ecological knowledge (lek), and culture. i will use low-cost drone photogrammetry to geospatially map the marine substrata and biota, creating an orthomosaic of the customary marine area. aerial transects and underwater visual census will be conducted to embed species distribution and abundance into the geospatial map for multivariate analysis. to record the community\u2019s tek and lek, semi-structured interviews will be held with key stakeholders. once expert knowledge is collected, community meetings will be held for all stakeholders to co-develop a story map. the result will depict knowledge of population dynamics through solomon island western province iconography. interactive physical and digital models of the reef will share specialist ecological knowledge with the wider community. geographical information systems (gis) will enable changes in the condition of the reef between surveys to be mapped to help avoid, remedy, or mitigate adverse changes. an interactive website relating the qualitative story map to the quantitative geospatial map will provide complimentary context to both data visualisations and allow vavanga to continue using the digital story map into the future.",
