      "search_text": "auckland university of technology aidan stockley graham hinchliffe samantha gillis aidan stockley environmental drivers of habitat and species composition on a fringing reef in the solomon islands fringing reefs are under pressure from land-use, unsustainable fishing, and climate change. this study maps spatial patterns in substrata and species on a fringing reef at vavanga, solomon islands to support planning on land and sea. drone and underwater videos were used to georeference 4,000 underwater images. points on images were assigned to taxa and substrata and percentage cover interpolated in relation to distance offshore and to freshwater inputs using classification and regression trees. different substrata and biota were correlated with distance to the reef crest and freshwater streams. after rain, drone videos show plumes of sediment-laden freshwater extending over the reef. logging has occurred previously and may begin again, but it is uncertain what downstream effects occur. coral cover along the channel increases seaward from the river mouth but even inshore there are large colonies of montipora and porites we modelled in 3d from video. the reef flat south-east of the river contrasts strongly with the north-western side. on the south-east side, coarse sand runs along the village foreshore; extends out through seagrass beds (thalassia hemprichii); coral rock, sargassum, padina and turbinaria ornata; then sparse montipora digitata, acropora spp. and pocillopora verrucosa towards the reef crest; and high hard coral cover (>40%), coralline algae and halimeda on the upper slope. north-west of the river, sharp ridges of weathered karst limestone and silt cover the inner reef flat and there is little sand or seagrass but sparse macroalgae and increasing coral cover offshore. mapping finer-scale patterns in reef benthos provides information on species associations, shifts in distribution and spatial heterogeneity at different scales. we discuss how these techniques can be used to inform local communities on the impacts of logging, coastal development and climate change and be applied elsewhere. graham hinchliffe georeferencing synchronised drone and underwater video of coral reef benthos fringing reefs are ecologically and culturally important environments at the land- sea interface, yet their location exposes them to damaging human impacts from coastal development, sedimentation, unsustainable fishing and climate change. despite this, fine-scale features and underlying ecological processes are seldom mapped at resolutions fit for local management. this limits participatory planning and constrains detection of nearshore environmental change. to address this, we used spatial video from low-cost unoccupied aerial vehicles (uavs or drones) synchronised with video cameras mounted on a customised snorkeller propulsion vehicle to georeference underwater images of reef substrata and epibenthos. percentage cover of taxa and substrata were visually estimated from points overlaid on the images and spatially interpolated across and along 3 km of fringing reef at vavanga village, kolombangara island, solomon islands. data points were used to map species distributions in response to their environment; classify drone photo-mosaics; train machine learning algorithms to identify features; and establish transects to estimate temporal change, reducing the need for physical markers. we discuss challenges and solutions to mapping over and under water including the lack of stationary landmarks, effects of altitude, waves, sun-glint and methods for georeferencing underwater imagery. integrating synchronised aerial and underwater perspectives within a geospatial framework is a powerful and visually engaging approach to monitor reef condition, enabling both technical analysis and community interpretation. samantha gillis a geospatial analysis of a coral reef flat in the solomon islands informed by ecological and cultural knowledge coral reefs support a quarter of known marine biodiversity and provide food security to a majority of island nations, while occupying less than 1% of the ocean floor. in the anthropocene we face major environmental challenges, requiring rapid and realistic action to salvage our biome. human prosperity is often considered when prioritising reef conservation, yet when the people who rely on and live amongst these ecosystems are insufficiently included in the reefs\u2019 future plans, then all foresight is restricted, and thus ineffective. my phd will aim to visualise the benefits of weaving scientific practice and melanesian culture to evaluate the temporal fluctuation of a customary fringing reef. to complement geospatial surveys, i will co-develop a pictograph with vavanga village to visualise their traditional ecological knowledge (tek), local ecological knowledge (lek), and culture. i will use low-cost drone photogrammetry to geospatially map the marine substrata and biota, creating an orthomosaic of the customary marine area. aerial transects and underwater visual census will be conducted to embed species distribution and abundance into the geospatial map for multivariate analysis. to record the community\u2019s tek and lek, semi-structured interviews will be held with key stakeholders. once expert knowledge is collected, community meetings will be held for all stakeholders to co-develop a story map. the result will depict knowledge of population dynamics through solomon island western province iconography. interactive physical and digital models of the reef will share specialist ecological knowledge with the wider community. geographical information systems (gis) will enable changes in the condition of the reef between surveys to be mapped to help avoid, remedy, or mitigate adverse changes. an interactive website relating the qualitative story map to the quantitative geospatial map will provide complimentary context to both data visualisations and allow vavanga to continue using the digital story map into the future.",
