          "name": "Andrew Negri",
          "search_text": "andrew negri oil toxicity towards the coral acropora millepora is time and light dependent the outcomes of marine oil spills depend on the local conditions at the spill site, the length of the exposure and the sensitivity of relevant biological receptors in the area, including tropical reef-building corals. while oil toxicity towards marine organisms has been studied for decades, tropical environmental risk assessments for oils often only use generic threshold values or limited experimental data; neither of which account for local environmental conditions (e.g. light spectrum and intensity) or exposure lengths. producing high quality toxicity data for oils, and other pollutants that pose hazards to humans, has been further hindered by the difficulties in performing experimental work. here we present the first experimental data on the time- and light-dependent sensitivity of the tropical coral acropora millepora to the water-soluble fraction of a commonly used heavy fuel oil (hfo).\r\ncoral fragments were exposed for 14 days to serial dilutions of low energy water accommodated fractions of hfo in a novel flow-through system, under visible light either in the presence or absence of ecologically relevant ultraviolet radiation (uv). both exposure duration and uv significantly increased oil toxicity, with partial mortality observed within 24 h under uv, compared with after 96 h in its absence. similarly, lateral growth was negatively affected at lower concentrations shorter exposure times in the presence of uv. the flow-through system improved data quality and interpretability by extending exposure times while maintaining stable concentrations and enabling continuous, low-handling monitoring of coral health. these findings highlight the critical importance of incorporating local environmental conditions and realistic exposure durations into environmental risk assessments and spill-response planning for tropical marine ecosystems. challenges and innovations in predicting hydrocarbon toxicity for coral reef species predicting the ecological consequences of petroleum hydrocarbon (hc) spills in tropical marine environments remains a major scientific challenge. each spill exhibits a dynamic and highly variable chemical profile, limiting the broader applicability of toxicity thresholds generated from traditional oil\u2010exposure ecotoxicity tests. an emerging alternative is the use of species-specific critical target lipid body burdens (ctlbbs), which quantify the inherent sensitivity of marine taxa to representative hcs. when paired with measured or modelled dissolved hc compositions, ctlbbs enable prediction of acute or chronic toxicity for any oil type and at any stage of weathering, offering a mechanistic, transferable framework for assessing potential impacts on coral reef species.\r\nthis presentation synthesises recent advances in generating and validating ctlbbs for tropical marine taxa and explores their application in predicting toxicity of complex petroleum mixtures. ctlbbs are derived from structure\u2013function relationships developed through targeted ecotoxicity testing with individual hcs, assuming additivity of effects within mixtures. these predicted toxicity values are contrasted with empirical outcomes from ecotoxicity tests conducted with partially weathered oils in a tropical marine case study. newly derived ctlbbs for tropical species such as corals enable direct comparison of their sensitivity against all taxa within the broader target lipid model (tlm) database and substantially improve the ability to model toxicity for any oil composition relevant to coral reef ecosystems.\r\nthe results highlight both the promise and current limitations of ctlbb- and tlm-based approaches for coral reef applications. while widely used internationally to support spill damage and risk assessments, further work is required to refine model parameters for tropical species and reef-specific environmental conditions. priority research areas include validating model performance under tropical light and temperature regimes, expanding ctlbb datasets for reef taxa, and integrating model outputs with ecological risk frameworks tailored to coral reef habitats and time variable exposures that characterize oil spills.",
