          "name": "Annie Guillaume",
          "search_text": "annie guillaume from pixels to spatial patterns: high-resolution coral distribution maps across the great barrier reef accurate spatial predictions of benthic species are essential for reef-scale biodiversity monitoring and conservation. however, distribution maps of coral species across reef networks are lacking. currently, many research and management projects rely on expert knowledge or coarse resolution distribution maps, which can limit the extrapolation of projects to novel reefs. species distribution models (sdms) covering reef networks are therefore required at spatial resolutions relevant to research and management to effectively upscale and support coral sampling, monitoring and deployment efforts. to this end, topographic variables as fine as 10m\u00b2 spatial resolutions derived from bathymetry digital elevation models (dems) are emerging as valuable predictors for use in coral ecological modelling across the gbr. we harness these topographic variables to present novel high-resolution abundance sdms for a range of dominant coral taxa across reefs of the gbr. we make use of a comprehensive high-resolution coral abundance dataset, extracted from photogrammetry images of 245 permanent plots (measuring 72m\u00b2 each) strategically spread across contrasting environmental ranges and habitats (e.g., exposed/sheltered, deep/shallow) at seven reefs in the central and southern gbr. these coral abundance data are modelled using fine-resolution topographic variables derived from bathymetric dems at multiple spatial resolutions (ranging from 10-60m resolutions). the resulting maps provide a reef-wide perspective on local-scale processes, where the abundance-based sdms offer deeper insights into habitat preferences and population dynamics than when using presence-only data. this work highlights the potential of combining advanced imaging and fine-resolution topographic variables to generate scalable, repeatable assessments of coral distribution patterns, supporting both ecological understanding and management needs.",
