          "name": "Deborah Burn",
          "search_text": "deborah burn four years of fate tracking: growth and survival of 24,000 coral colonies throughout australia\u2019s great barrier reef using large-area 3d photogrammetry corals exhibit remarkable diversity in their vital rates (growth, survival, and shrinkage) dependent on taxonomic identity, colony size, and environmental conditions. understanding how this demographic diversity changes temporally and shapes reef trajectories under escalating thermal stress remains a critical challenge.  \r\n\r\nhere, we quantify the annual vital rates of >24,000 individually tracked coral colonies over 4 years across australia\u2019s great barrier reef (gbr). large-area 3d photogrammetry was used to generate spatially co-registered, scaled 2d orthomosaics for 352 permanently marked plots each year from 2021-2024. coral colonies within orthomosaics were digitised to quantify changes in planar area and survival through time. we tested for variation in vital rates across nine coral taxa, a range of colony sizes, and across environmental gradients associated with structural complexity, depth, exposure, shelf position and latitude. moreover, our survey period encompassed two major bleaching events: regional bleaching in the central gbr in 2022 and widespread bleaching across the full latitudinal range in 2024. \r\n\r\nthis talk showcases the spatial, temporal and taxonomic diversity in coral vital rates at unprecedented scales, including responses of individuals to repeated heat stress. broad analyses reveal marked variation in colony fates among taxa, colony sizes, environments and years, partly driven by bleaching events within our survey period. focusing on colonies in the central gbr that experienced heatwaves in 2022 and 2024, we show how individual bleaching histories shape subsequent fates, including changing bleaching susceptibility, altered growth rates, and mortality likelihood. these findings reveal the demographic pathways through which repeated heat stress reshapes assemblages, identifying the environments and coral groups most likely to contribute to recovery versus those at greatest risk of decline. this integration of digital imagery with demographic modelling allows individual vital rates to be quantified across multiple disturbances, offering critical insights for predicting assemblage-level responses and future reef trajectories.",
