          "name": "Jordan Ivey",
          "search_text": "jordan ivey rrap indigenous futures: outcomes from a three-year program enabling indigenous-led coral larval restoration and marine monitoring on the great barrier reef rrap indigenous futures was established to prepare great barrier reef traditional owner groups for the emerging reef restoration and adaptation industry through genuine capacity building grounded in indigenous pedagogies and repeated hands-on learning in coral larval restoration. the program aimed to demonstrate that traditional owner rangers can deliver deployment-ready larval restoration interventions on their sea country and establish their place in future rrap deployments.\r\nacross three years, approximately 30 indigenous rangers from eight indigenous ranger groups completed practical training in larval pool mobilisation, coral spawn collection, larval culturing, device-based settlement, larval cloud releases, device deployment, site selection for larval restoration, marine monitoring and cultural mapping. the program was co-designed with indigenous governance groups and the indigenous project lead to ensure it was built from indigenous foundations, and was strengthened by additional indigenous participation including support staff, interns, trainees, researchers, media specialists and respected elders. all engagement followed cultural protocols and the principles of free, prior and informed consent, guided by the australian institute of marine science\u2019s indigenous partnerships policy and plan.\r\nthis presentation reports final-year outcomes, where three-years of capability development culminated in ranger teams delivering coral slick collection, larval rearing, settlement and deployment with targeted support. their performance was assessed against published larval restoration benchmarks to evaluate operational readiness. marine monitoring training focused on foundational methods relevant to coral larval restoration, combining diver-less approaches such as ruvs, reefscan and reefcloud annotation workflows with diver-operated benthic surveys to support consistent assessment of early-life coral survival and site condition.\r\nthe program also progressed the development of structured training materials that can support broader delivery across first-nations sea country. rrap indigenous futures demonstrates a scalable model for preparing first nations ranger groups to lead and deliver reef restoration and adaptation activities on the great barrier reef and in other indigenous-managed marine environments.",
