          "name": "Kerryn Crossman",
          "search_text": "kerryn crossman a narrow combination of coral community characteristics facilitate reassembly on the great barrier reef. reassembly of biological communities to their pre-disturbance configuration is the epitome of resilience. as such, reassembly remains a common aspirational target among ecologists and managers. however, reassembly is just one of many possible community reorganisation pathways during recovery, casting doubt on the rationality of this aspiration. here, we characterise the probability of post-disturbance reassembly to a reference community composition across 72 reefs of the great barrier reef over the past 30 years. reassembly was uncommon, occurring for 43% of recovery trajectories. a low probability of reassembly was facilitated by a wide variety of scenarios, while a high probability of reassembly was only facilitated when a narrow combination of conditions are met: 1) the first or second recovery cycle since the reference period 2) less than 21 years since the end of the reference period, 3) post-disturbance coral cover > 50% and 4) post-disturbance community at least 84% similarity in composition compared to the reference community. notably, the probability of reassembly dramatically declines 10 years after the reference period. community reassembly may be an unreasonable aspiration in the management of contemporary, complex ecosystems, unless there is additional evidence that the new assembly represents long lasting declines in ecosystem functioning.",
