          "search_text": "magena marzonie predicting multi-species coral genomic vulnerability to a warming ocean climate change drives uneven adaptive trajectories across coral taxa and populations. yet identifying vulnerable species and populations where adaptive capacity will be insufficient remains a major barrier to effective protection. we applied a seascape genomics framework across six species from four major reef-building genera (acropora, isopora, montipora, and pocillopora). samples spanned two contrasting biogeographic systems: lord howe marine park, the world\u2019s most southerly coral reefs, and the coral sea marine park, reef atolls representing more thermally dynamic conditions. \r\n\r\nneutral population structure consistently revealed distinct genetic clusters between lord howe and the coral sea marine park across all taxa. against this demographic backdrop, we identified distinct species-specific genomic architectures of climate adaptation reflecting divergent life histories, dispersal capacity, and heat tolerance thresholds. genotype-environment association (gea) analyses detected putative adaptive loci linked to thermal regimes, oceanographic currents, and depth. brooding corals showed twice the number of environmentally associated loci as spawning corals. however, adaptive loci in spawners were driven by several environmental predictors, whereas brooders were associated with one dominant predictor. \r\n\r\ngenomic offsets were projected to estimate allele frequency changes required to maintain adaptation under 2050 and 2100 climate scenarios (ssp2-4.5, ssp5-8.5). lord howe populations consistently exhibited higher genomic offsets than the coral sea, indicating a larger mismatch between existing adaptive variation and future thermal regimes at lord howe. this heightened vulnerability aligns with lord howe\u2019s cooler, more stable thermal environment, which may constrain selection for heat tolerant populations and limit the potential for rapid evolutionary responses. in contrast, most coral sea atolls displayed comparatively lower offsets, suggesting stronger evolutionary buffering against future warming scenarios. \r\n\r\ntogether, these results indicate that no single intervention strategy will be optimal across all coral taxa or populations and demonstrate how integrating multi-species geas with genomic offset forecasting can guide prioritisation for conservation, management, and restoration initiatives.",
