          "name": "Manuel Gonzalez-Rivero",
          "search_text": "manuel gonzalez-rivero status of coral reefs of the world coral reefs occur in over 100 countries and territories, support global biodiversity and underpin the livelihoods of hundreds of millions of people, generating over usd $9 trillion in ecosystem services annually. as one of the most vulnerable ecosystems on the planet to anthropogenic pressures, evaluating and reporting on the global status and trends of coral reefs is critical to guide conservation actions and raise awareness of global and local drivers of coral loss. here, we present results from the latest and most comprehensive global compilation and analysis of monitoring data through the global coral reef monitoring network (gcrmn). the gcrmn is an operational network of the international coral reef initiative (icri) that aims to provide the best available scientific information on the status and trends of coral reef ecosystems for their conservation and management. the network comprises scientists, managers, and organisations across 10 regional nodes that monitor coral reefs at their local scales. the \u201cstatus of coral reefs of the world: 2025\u201d report describes decadal trends in key coral reef indicators at global, regional, and sub-regional scales, based on a quantitative analysis of a global dataset comprising 87,701 surveys conducted across 124 countries and territories, at 37,799 sites between 1973 and 2025. the scale of this analysis provides an in-depth global picture of the condition and common challenges facing the world's coral reefs. the results will be presented both as a global synthesis and as regional analyses to examine region-specific and common patterns of change over the last four decades. the data collation and report production also provided valuable lessons and key insights into pathways to address known challenges in collecting, analysing, integrating, and applying monitoring data to inform conservation actions in the 21st century. photogrammetry for coral reef monitoring and management: effective and efficient benthic monitoring at scales ranging from millimetres to hectares mapping, monitoring, and modelling ecosystems across relevant spatio-temporal scales is essential for effective reef management and conservation. a significant challenge is the lack of baseline knowledge regarding the spatial distribution, size distribution, and vital rates of dominant coral taxa, and their relationships to the environment.\r\n\r\nthe ecological intelligence for reef restoration and adaptation (ecorrap) program addresses this knowledge gap by creating empirical data from reference sites that span from the southern end of the great barrier reef up to the torres strait, encompassing cross-shelf , depth, and wave exposure gradients. this program established the largest single dataset of benthic community composition, structure, habitat complexity and coral demography.\r\n\r\nover five years, we collected 3d longitudinal data across more than 17 hectares of reef, digitised ~25,000 coral colonies, and developed robust standard operating procedures for data acquisition, processing, extraction, and integration. we applied novel benthic monitoring technologies to maximise efficiency and effectiveness at scales spanning millimetres to hectares, generating crucial population and landscape ecological insights. this talk will present a selection of studies utilising ecorrap's data, showcasing its breadth and potential for monitoring coral reef dynamics and informing management. \r\n\r\ncase studies will include:\r\n(1) method development for efficient data collection, high-performance data throughput, ai-assisted extraction and analysis.\r\n(2) ecological research examining the impact of multiple massive bleaching events, coral demography, and population models to inform coral reef restoration planning. \r\n\r\nthe program generated critical information for evidence-based decision-making, which has the potential to significantly enhance the success of coral reef restoration efforts, ensuring the continued ecological and societal contributions of these vital ecosystems.",
