          "name": "Marji Puotinen",
          "search_text": "marji puotinen exposure risk to damaging cyclone waves drops in the near future across almost all of the great barrier reef extreme waves generated by tropical cyclones intermittently cause catastrophic damage to coral communities on reefs, diminishing their ability to perform key ecosystem services like shoreline protection and habitat provision for fish.  people often assume that such storms will become more damaging to reefs as the climate continues to warm. however, for the great barrier reef, numerical wave models run for thousands of future cyclone tracks generated by a global climate model for both the current climate and a near future (2050) time frame at a worst case 8.5 degree scenario show that this is not the case.  for both relatively common (return time 10 years) and rare (return time 100 years) cyclone events, the modelled exposure of the vast majority of reef communities to cyclone generated waves capable of damaging most vulnerable corals either does not change or declines when compared to the current climate.  we tested this for a range of modelled wave parameters including significant wave height and bottom stress, and separately for each of 8 incoming wave directions, and for the magnitude of wave energy as well as its duration over a presumed damage threshold. while exposure to long lasting damaging waves and extreme wave heights was highly spatially variable within and between reefs, the lack of an increase in exposure was consistent across the vast majority of the great barrier reef. these results illustrate the importance of spatially explicit modelling of reef exposure to cyclones instead of applying \u2018common sense\u2019 approximations. importantly, however, this modelling informs the risk of exposure to but not actual damage from cyclone waves.  actual damage depends on local scale characteristics of corals that affect their structural vulnerability (size, growth form, history of other disturbance, strength of attachment to the substratum) which are poorly known for most reefs. building global communities for climate action for coral reefs through collaborative art art provides a joyful, low-stress way to invite people (kids and associated adults) to experience first-hand what positive action towards a better future taken in community and collaboration with other people feels like. for example, in 2021, i asked kids to draw what they love most about trees as a way to help cool the earth \u2013 to help penguins, coral reefs and people (https://kidscareaboutclimate.org).  2,629 kids from 213 schools and 33 nations drew pictures of trees, and in 2022 the tree planting company \u201815 trees\u2019 (https://15trees.com.au) planted a native australian tree for each drawing.  a global judging panel selected winners, who either won zoom calls with artists, authors and scientists or had their drawings featured in australian geographic magazine. the grand prize-winning drawing circumnavigated antarctica with sailor lisa blair as a small flag on her yacht climate action now as she became the fastest person to ever do so unassisted and non-stop. i mosaic\u2019d all the drawings to form a bigger picture of a penguin planting a tree on earth and printed it in sections for form a giant 7 m high by 4.25 m wide banner. nearly 9000 kids in 15 nations have since seen and touched the giant banner, and we\u2019ve filmed many of them via drone \u2013 spelling messages for world leaders like help, act now, save earth, and too hot. videos of this have been shown at four united nations climate meetings, and one was part of the global stocktake of climate action around the world. in 2025, the giant banner was part of a marathon week-long tree hug in perth, australia that coincided with the first week of #cop30. a further 930 people from 46 nations submitted tree hug photos in solidarity with the nearly 13,600 people involved in the initiative (#kidscareaboutclimatechange) since 2018.",
