          "name": "Martina Burgo",
          "search_text": "martina burgo dynamics of tropical macroalgal communities: succession, competition, and facilitation macroalgal communities exhibit vertical stratification, yet canopy\u2013understorey interactions in tropical systems remain poorly understood, with previous research largely focused on herbivory or competition with corals. we investigated the spatial patterns and temporal dynamics of macroalgal assemblages across nine inshore reef sites in the central great barrier reef every 7 weeks for 14 months. specifically, we surveyed three turbid island systems (orpheus, havannah, magnetic island) characterized by seasonal sediment inputs and high resuspension. community structure varied markedly across space, with orpheus island showing greater heterogeneity than havannah and magnetic island. the primary driver of temporal change was the seasonal succession of the canopy. canopy structure (i.e., height and density) peaked between november and march (austral summer), reaching up to 1.5 m in height and 40 thalli m^-2 in density. the canopy\u2019s structure drove the overall community composition through a seasonal trade-off between competition and facilitation. during the summer peak, high canopy height was strongly associated with lower community richness and a lower abundance of understorey macroalgae (competitive exclusion). in contrast, canopy density was positively associated with higher understorey abundance in winter, indicating a temporal shift toward facilitation, where the residual canopy provides structural refuge. winter increases in understorey abundance and blooms of ephemeral macroalgae resulted in increased community richness. at the taxon-specific level, multivariate latent variable models suggested distinct co-occurrence patterns, with strong negative associations within canopy-forming macroalgal genera (e.g., sargassum and sirophysalis), and more diffuse, sometimes positive, interactions among understorey macroalgal taxa. taxon-specific trajectories were highly spatially variable, indicating that local conditions mediate community turnover. together, these findings highlight the dynamic nature of tropical macroalgal assemblages and underscore the importance of interactions between taxa and spatial context in shaping community structure and composition on inshore reefs.",
