          "name": "Nicole Ryan",
          "search_text": "nicole ryan contrasting bleaching responses across western australia's remote oceanic reefs under extreme heat stress prolonged, anomalously warm sea surface temperatures (sst) surrounding the austral summer 2024- 2025 triggered widespread and severe coral bleaching across most of western australia\u2019s (wa) coral reefs, including the remote and ecologically significant oceanic reefs of ashmore reef, scott reef, and the rowley shoals. heat stress exceeded 12 degree heating weeks (dhw), a threshold typically associated with widespread bleaching and mortality across most coral groups.\r\nthese reefs have distinct heat stress histories, some having experienced multiple bleaching events over the last three decades, while the rowley shoals has, until the recent event, largely escaped the impacts of marine heatwaves. this raises questions about resilience, recovery, and adaptation under accelerating climate change. we summarise long-term community and demographic trends, integrated with complementary ecological and physical information to provide context to bleaching observations following peak heat stress in march and april 2025 and compare to previous bleaching events.  \r\nthis was the worst bleaching event on record for the rowley shoals, causing >75% mortality. in contrast, more moderate impacts were recorded for ashmore and scott reefs, suggesting their previous exposure to severe bleaching may have afforded them some resilience. these trends provide valuable insights into community trajectories critical to informing management strategies aimed at improving resilience of reefs under future climate scenarios.",
