          "name": "Samuel Matthews",
          "search_text": "samuel matthews operationalising resilience-based management across scales: integrated decision-support and fine-scale habitat modelling for cots control on the gbr resilience-based management (rbm) has been adopted to address climate-driven change on coral reefs, yet few examples exist of effective rbm at scale. the crown-of-thorns starfish (cots) control program on the great barrier reef provides a practical example of a meta-adaptive approach to rbm: a deliberate strategy of starting simple, then incrementally adding scale and complexity while maintaining on-ground resilience outcomes. here we outline the core principles of the cots control program's meta-adaptive approach and its evolution.  using two case studies we illustrate how modelling and decision support has enabled the scaling of site-based cots control intervention  to deliver regional-scale resilience benefits. first, we outline the program\u2019s decision-support architecture using established business intelligence software repurposed into in-house conservation intelligence dashboards that integrate monitoring, control and opertional data. these tools provide near real-time analysis on outbreak severity, coral condition and program performance, and are explicitly structured around key decision points (e.g. site status, annual reef prioritisation), targeting and supporting each element of the adaptive management cycle. second, we present emerging fine-scale species distribution models as a complementary decision layer to guide within-reef targeting by identifying high-risk habitats and to maximise coral protection per unit effort. together, these case studies show advanced and mature decision support platforms enable effective rbm and provide clear pathways for emerging science to be incorporated into cots control program decision making across spatial scales. these examples underscore how meta-adaptive principles of learning by doing alongside continuous modelling, assessment, evaluation and adjustment around clear decision points can turn a nominally local intervention into a scalable and self-reinforcing rbm program. the cots control program offers a transferable template for other reef systems seeking to link decision-support, emerging science and on-ground action to support reef resilience across scales.",
