          "name": "Sven Uthicke",
          "search_text": "sven uthicke edna monitoring using species specific digital pcr delivers early detection of crown-of-thorns seastar outbreaks on the great barrier reef, australia despite increasing pressure from climate change, coral loss through predation by crown-of-thorns seastars (cots) remains a significant contributor to the coral reef crises in the indo-pacific region. on the great barrier reef (gbr), cots outbreaks are managed through intervention by culling, supported through cross-institutional research to maximise efficiency of management. while early detection of emerging outbreaks is essential for timely intervention, current monitoring methods (e.g., manta tows) only identify established cots outbreaks. over the last 10 years, we developed a species-specific edna method using digital droplet pcr and demonstrated that this method provides detection and a quantitative estimate for cots at much lower densities compared to visual methods. here, we present data from 4 years of large-scale monitoring on 20-50 reefs annually and 7 years of data from a sentinel site in the northern gbr (lizard island). comparison to catch per unit effort (cpue) data from cots control vessels confirms that our method provides a quantitative estimate and detects cots at very low densities with sufficient precision to support operational decision-making. the seven-year time series in the northern gbr detected the initiation of the 5th outbreak around 2022/23. similarly, during large-scale surveys from 2021 to 2025, we also measured increasing cots edna signal on many reefs in the northern section of the gbr.  given the relatively high costs of research vessels we currently focus on transferring the method for field collection to ships of opportunity. for instance, this method has been operationalised for use on queensland park and wildlife service vessels and cots control vessels. we propose to use edna as part of an ongoing monitoring for early outbreak detection and early management intervention on the gbr, but also suggest that this method is easily transferable to other countries.",
