          "name": "Almog Haim",
          "search_text": "almog haim ecology of the rariphotic zone in submarine canyons of the gulf of eilat the deep sea is earth\u2019s largest ecosystem, yet it remains among the least explored. strong depth-related gradients in light, temperature, oxygen, and salinity structure distinct ecological zones, each supporting specialized biological communities. the rariphotic zone (130\u2013300 m) represents a critical transition between mesophotic coral ecosystems and the deep sea, hosting unique assemblages of corals, sponges, and fishes. although the gulf of eilat is one of the most extensively studied regions of the red sea, research has focused primarily on shallow and mesophotic habitats, leaving the rariphotic zone\u2013and particularly submarine canyons\u2013largely uncharacterized.\r\nearly submersible surveys documented deep coral frameworks and suggested high levels of endemism at these depths, while more recent studies have revealed pronounced changes in community composition with depth. however, the combined effects of canyon morphology, depth gradients, and environmental conditions on rariphotic communities remain poorly understood.\r\nhere, i present an ongoing study addressing these gaps through a structured survey of the rariphotic zone in submarine canyons of the gulf of eilat. using an integrated approach that includes rov-based visual surveys, three-dimensional photogrammetry, baited remote underwater video systems (bruvs), and sediment environmental dna (edna) analyses, i characterize benthic and fish communities across multiple depths (100, 200, and 300 m) inside and outside submarine canyons. long-term mooring stations deployed at corresponding depths provide continuous physical data to support biological observations.\r\nby integrating visual, molecular, and environmental datasets, this study establishes the first comprehensive ecological baseline for rariphotic canyon habitats in the gulf of eilat and provides new insights into the structure and biodiversity of deep coral-associated ecosystems.",
