          "name": "Gal Eyal",
          "search_text": "gal eyal beyond refugia: predictability, unpredictability, and coral persistence from high-latitude shallows to mesophotic depths coral framework ecosystems at high latitudes and mesophotic depths are potential climate refugia for shallow coral reefs, yet their long-term stability and resilience under intensifying global change remain unclear. here we contrast the ecological histories of two such potential refugia: the high-latitude shallow reefs of moreton bay (queensland, australia) monitored seasonally over 3 years (2021-2024), where live coral cover declined by ~30%, and the mesophotic coral ecosystem of eilat (red sea, israel) monitored annually over decades, where upper-mesophotic coral cover declined by ~50% since 2010. both systems exhibit ecological shifts from historically complex assemblages dominated by coral taxa to present-day states increasingly dominated by turf algae and macroalgae, while massive, stress-tolerant coral species persist in low abundance. these transitions are linked to the interaction of acute extreme events (e.g., marine heatwaves, cold spells, floods, turbidity pulses, internal wave-induced temperature anomalies) with rising background environmental variability. although both systems are considered refugia, neither has remained static. rather, the relative predictability of environmental fluctuations appears to underpin continued coral persistence. these findings challenge the notion that climatic refugia are defined solely by environmental stability, suggesting instead that seasonal or periodic variabilities in long-term environmental conditions can buffer coral communities against climate stress. in contrast, acute extreme events drive assemblage volatility and accelerate phase shifts from coral to algal dominance. this synthesis underscores that environmental predictability, rather than stability, better explains ecological persistence in marginal and extreme reef environments. it further implies that conservation prioritisation under climate change should focus on reef habitats characterised by predictable environmental regimes and manageable extremes, as these are better positioned than unpredictable systems to sustain coral assemblages in an era of increasing climatic uncertainty.",
