          "name": "Noa Simon-blecher",
          "search_text": "noa simon-blecher when night becomes light: in situ impacts of alan on coral reef microbiomes and coral stress responses rapid coastal development and population growth have dramatically increased artificial light at night (alan), raising concerns about its ecological impacts on marine environments. in this study, conducted in situ at 6 m depth on natural coral reefs in the gulf of aqaba (eilat), we examined how continuous nighttime illumination from white and yellow leds affects (i) benthic community settlement and associated microbiomes on terracotta tiles over one year, and (ii) the physiological stress response of acropora valida colonies over five months.\r\nsettlement analyses revealed distinct invertebrate assemblages under each light treatment, with significant differences in species prevalence between illuminated tiles and controls, as well as clear contrasts between the yellow and white-light conditions. algal taxa and fish eggs also varied across treatments, indicating broad effects on early-stage community assembly. microbiome profiling showed light-dependent shifts in bacterial diversity and composition, with the white-light treatment exhibiting the most pronounced departure from the control state. notably, the ratio of pathogenic to potentially beneficial bacterial groups differed across treatments, pointing to disrupted microbial community balance under alan. physiological measurements of a. valida mirrored these patterns: corals exposed to white light exhibited elevated chlorophyll concentrations and modifications in oxidative stress pathways, consistent with increased cellular stress. in contrast, corals under yellow light displayed comparatively mild physiological alterations. together, this in situ, depth-resolved experiment demonstrates that nighttime led illumination particularly broad spectrum white light can significantly alter benthic recruitment, microbial community structure, and coral stress physiology, underscoring the need to consider spectral characteristics when mitigating alan in coastal zones.",
