          "search_text": "giovanni giallongo restoring functions, not just coral coverage: functional gains from large engineered colonies coral reef restoration efforts are expanding rapidly in response to accelerating ecosystem degradation, yet the recovery of ecosystem functions often lags behind the return of coral cover. one contributing factor is the widespread use of small, nursery-grown outplants that fail to replicate the ecological roles of large, mature colonies lost during disturbance events. mature corals provide essential functions including habitat creation, nutrient cycling, larval retention, and modulation of fine-scale hydrodynamics and light. to address this gap, we developed large engineered colonies (lec), engineered biological entities composed of 5 or 10 isogenic stylophora pistillata colonies (each at transplantable size) affixed to a 3d-printed base and designed to approximate or exceed the functional size of natural adult colonies. over one year of post-transplant monitoring, we compared the performance of the two lec sizes to conventional single-colony outplants. lec entities rapidly supported diverse associated communities: resident invertebrates and fishes established within the first month and stabilized by month four, with size 10 lec hosting richer community than size 5 (median shannon diversity index 1.5 \u00b1 0.43 and 1.2 \u00b1 0.4, respectively). all colonies within each lec fused within three months, further increasing structural complexity. in contrast, single-colonies failed to sustain a stable community of coral inhabitants trapezia cymodocee and neopomacentrus miryae (median shannon diversity index 0 \u00b1 0.4) over the same period, indicating markedly slower community development. additionally, initial biophysical measurements show that lec modify near-colony flow, enhance larval retention potential, and influence local nutrient concentrations more effectively than single-colony outplants. our results demonstrate that lec restore key ecological functions at substantially more rapidly than standard restoration practices. as such, they represent a promising, scalable tool for enhancing the functional recovery of degraded reefs. future work will optimize materials, evaluate long-term structural performance, and test scalability across diverse reef environments.",
