          "name": "Sara Swaminathan",
          "search_text": "sara swaminathan trophic niche partitioning among herbivorous fishes across gradients of reef health herbivorous coral reef fishes are a diverse guild that evolved specialized traits and behaviors, creating \u201ctrophic niches\u201d that enable coexistence in resource-limited environments. on many present\u2010day reefs, however, coral loss, algal overgrowth, and reductions in herbivore biomass have altered the ecological backdrop against which these species interact. as such, it remains unclear whether the feeding behaviors of reef herbivores change as coral reefs degrade. if evolved, competition\u2011driven behaviors remain rigid, herbivores may continue to specialize on distinct prey, thereby maintaining extensive, efficient grazing that supports reef recovery. conversely, an abundance of basal resources could erode competition, leading to generalized feeding on a few highly preferred items and a loss of grazing efficacy. to test these scenarios, we surveyed feeding behavior and diet composition of the ten most common caribbean herbivorous fishes, across both healthy and degraded reefs in panama, belize, and the dominican republic. using dna metabarcoding of three distinct gene markers (16s, 23s, and coi), we (a) defined discrete dietary signatures for each species, independent of location or reef health, (b) documented intraspecific dietary shifts across locations and gradients of reef degradation, and (c) quantified interspecific trophic niche partitioning and overlap. finally, we integrated dietary and behavioral data to identify mechanisms underlying trophic niche partitioning. our results reveal that evolved feeding specializations persist even in resource\u2011rich settings, yet new patterns of herbivore functioning emerge under altered reef states. these findings highlight both the roles of evolutionary history and ecological flexibility in shaping modern reef herbivory. further, they offer insights into how herbivores may respond to future coral reef degradation, as well as the potential for herbivores to reverse state shifts and facilitate reef recovery.",
