          "name": "Margot Webster",
          "search_text": "margot webster macroinvertebrate gleaning in south west madagascar: socio-economic importance and sustainability concerns small-scale fisheries (ssfs) are critical to the livelihoods and food security of millions of people globally, particularly in low-income coastal regions where alternative livelihoods are limited. in south-west madagascar, vezo communities rely almost entirely on marine resources, and locally managed marine areas (lmmas) have emerged as key tools for context-appropriate, community-led fisheries management. they implement a suite of management measures, including no-take zones across coral reefs, seagrass beds and mangroves, temporary octopus closures, and bans on destructive fishing practices.\r\nmarine macroinvertebrate gleaning, primarily undertaken by women and children at spring low tide, represents a vital but largely overlooked component of vezo livelihoods. despite its importance for food security, income generation, and the ecological functioning of coastal ecosystems, this fishery remains data-poor and mostly absent from monitoring and management. this study provides one of the first comprehensive characterizations of the multi-species gleaning fishery in the velondriake lmma. using ecological surveys of gleaned habitats, landing surveys, and socio-economic interviews with gleaners, we assessed ecosystem condition, invertebrate populations, the structure and importance of the fishery, and its role within the broader socio-ecological system.\r\nresults highlight the high diversity of invertebrate species harvested and the fishery\u2019s essential contribution to household food security and, for many gleaners, their only source of income. however, sustainability concerns are evident: heavily targeted species show low abundances and small size structures, gleaned habitats exhibit degraded ecological conditions, and some species may be locally disappearing. given the fishery\u2019s socio-economic importance and the ecological role of macroinvertebrates in supporting higher-value fisheries, integrating gleaning into existing management frameworks is essential. these findings provide baseline data to inform adaptive, community-led decision making and to support more holistic resource management; and we discuss potential strategies tailored to an lmma contect. fine scale variability in coral bleaching and mortality susceptibility in pemba, zanzibar during the 2024 mass coral bleaching event pemba island, in the zanzibar archipelago, hosts extensive coral reefs which support communities heavily reliant on small-scale fisheries. these reefs were previously considered potential thermal refugia due to cold upwellings in the pemba channel. yet in 2024, the western indian ocean experienced severe heat stress, driven by el ni\u00f1o and indian ocean dipole events which resulted in widespread coral bleaching.  \r\nwe collected in situ colony-level data on bleaching status (non-bleached, pale, bleached, dead), colony size and genus across depth gradients, alongside a continuous temperature series. benthic composition and reef rugosity were assessed in early 2024 prior to bleaching and will be resurveyed in early 2026.  \r\nsignificant bleaching and mortality were observed in 2024, with 53.6% of colonies bleached to some degree in april and 38.4% showing at least partial mortality by may, falling within a similar range as reefs across the region. interestingly, deeper reefs (15 m) were significantly more affected than shallower reefs (5 m), as were larger colonies (>1 m). genera differed strongly in their response, with fungia and pachyseris highly susceptible to bleaching but suffering comparatively low mortality, leading to high fine-scale variability in outcomes across sites. in 2025, bleaching was minimal (<4%), although 34.5% of colonies showed signs of paling. temperature records show a +1\u00b0c anomaly during the 2024 bleaching season (northeast monsoon), with warming extending to at least 25 m depth, compared to the same season in 2025. \r\nthese results demonstrate substantial spatial, size and taxonomic variation in bleaching and mortality and indicate that the pemba channel may not act as a thermal refugia under el ni\u00f1o conditions. fine-scale monitoring, benthic surveying and tracking temperature regimes together provide key insights into the processes shaping reef resistance in the anthropocene. repeated monitoring will clarify the legacy effects of coral bleaching on these precious ecosystems.",
