          "name": "Les Kaufman",
          "search_text": "les kaufman national environmental policies and the meaning of coral reef restoration west atlantic coral reefs have been devastated by mass bleaching and disease, piled on centuries of overfishing and overdevelopment.  the paris accords are important, but the recovery of atmospheric chemistry will take centuries, and reef recovery much longer if key species and processes disappear.  hard coral enhancement is being utilized to overcome recruitment limitation by dwindling coral populations.  this is difficult and expensive to bring to a meaningful scale but may be required to achieve the legislated goals of national environmental policies.   in belize, fragments of hope had achieved the near-complete recovery of ecosystem values of coral communities in laughing bird caye national park.  after delivering much value, the foh success was subsequently nearly erased by coral disease and mass bleaching events.  other restoration efforts experienced similar setbacks. annual photomosaic monitoring has allowed us to assess the alignment of observed outcomes at various stages of recovery with goals set forth in belize\u2019s blue bond program and relevant national environmental laws.  the atlantic coral reef estate as a whole is undergoing sweeping change as hard coral-dominated assemblages have shifted toward soft coral and sponge communities with scattered stony corals.  this dance of science, policy, and intervention could be made more meaningful if we (1) are more specific about conservation and recovery goals, (2) operate under a rational policy focused on assisted enabling of natural regenerative processes, and (3) we develop accountability and efficiencies in the experimental process of inventing coral reef stewardship strategies that are adaptive in today\u2019s strange world.",
