          "name": "Morgan Bennett-Smith",
          "search_text": "morgan bennett-smith ecological and mechanistic drivers of the collapse of the iconic anemone-anemonefish mutualism how reef populations respond to extreme climatic events is a fundamental question for twenty-first-century marine ecology and conservation. documenting local collapses of iconic populations can offer insights into broader patterns of decline and help to communicate the issue to the public. here, we monitored the anemonefish (amphiprion bicinctus) and their host anemones (radianthus magnifica) across three central red sea reefs from 2022\u20132024, encompassing an unprecedented marine heatwave that peaked at ~22 \u00b0c-weeks of degree heating weeks. across all reefs we observed a sequence of 100% anemone bleaching, followed by 94.3\u2013100% anemonefish mortality, and ultimately 66.4\u201394.1% anemone mortality. to better understand why anemonefish mortality follows anemone bleaching, we conducted complementary laboratory experiments at boston university. we monitored anemonefish (amphiprion percula) behavior and anemone (entacmaea quadricolor) nematocyst discharge in response to environmentally relevant heat stress. we show that fish alter their behavior and that anemone nematocyst discharge changes in ways that would likely increase fish vulnerability to predation following prolonged heat stress and bleaching. this integrative approach links catastrophic population collapses and breakdown of mutualistic interactions in the wild to their proximate mechanisms in the laboratory. together, our studies reveal how and why marine heatwaves disrupt one of the ocean\u2019s most iconic mutualisms. finally, by pairing these findings with widely circulated visual documentation of bleached anemones and anemonefish (featured by national geographic, mongabay, and others), we also aim to leverage the familiarity of and affection for this mutualism to strengthen public engagement with climate-driven reef decline.",
