          "name": "Mu-Han Chen",
          "search_text": "mu-han chen coral phenotypic plasticity and performance across diel thermal variability regimes the persistence of coral populations depends on their resistance to escalating heat stress and extreme thermal events. high diel thermal variability (dtv) has been linked to reduced bleaching, with corals from more thermally variable reefs often exhibiting greater thermal tolerance and gene expression plasticity than those from more stable environments. however, dtv is also associated with shifts in other physicochemical parameters on a reef, complicating its effects and requiring further resolution to determine its specific role. in this study, we investigated how dtv and broader environmental contexts shape coral phenotypic plasticity and thermal performance. given the potential energetic costs of these responses, we also evaluated links between environments and trade-offs among calcification, energetic reserves, and reproductive investment, key life-history traits underlying coral survival and long-term reef dynamics. we identified four sites (two low-dtv, two high-dtv) within the bocas del toro reef complex, panam\u00e1, where populations of the reef-building coral siderastrea siderea experience distinct dtv despite similar mean temperatures. we first characterized coral and symbiont phenomes (composite physiological metrics), trophic ecology, genome-wide gene expression, full thermal performance curves, fecundity, and historical growth records of corals from these thermally contrasting reef sites. next, we conducted a year-long reciprocal transplant experiment between low- and high-dtv environments to examine coral phenotypic plasticity in phenomes, growth, gene expression, and thermal tolerance. we simultaneously deployed loggers at each site to capture a continuous year-long record of seawater parameters (e.g., temperature, salinity, dissolved oxygen, ph) and collected nutrient profiles at three time points to determine which variables covary with dtv or may confound its effects. our study elucidates how environmental heterogeneity shapes the extent to which long-lived reef-building corals can employ phenotypic plasticity to persist under rapid climate change. these insights will strengthen predictions of coral resilience to future warming and directly inform effective restoration strategies.",
