          "name": "Sarah W. Davies",
          "search_text": "sarah w. davies interactions between symbiosis and innate immunity in cnidarians many cnidarians, including reef-building corals, establish symbiotic relationships with photosynthetic algae in the family symbiodiniaceae. these partnerships are sensitive to temperature anomalies, which cause symbiont loss and increased mortality. several studies have implicated host innate immunity as playing a central role in the maintenance of cnidarian-algal symbiosis and it has been shown that establishing symbiosis with different genera of algal symbionts modulates thermal tolerance. over the last several years our group has investigated interactions between symbiotic state, algal symbiont type, innate immunity, and thermal tolerance across different life history stages in a model anemone exaiptasia pallida (aiptasia), two facultatively symbiotic corals (oculina arbuscula, astrangia poculata), and a tropical reef-building coral (pocillopora damicornis). using experimental and genomic approaches, our results demonstrate that host innate immunity is modulated by symbiotic state and that establishing symbiosis with specific algal strains modulates host innate immunity in distinct ways, which lead to differences in thermal tolerance and responses to microbial challenge in the lab. overall, this presentation will integrate results from several recent studies integrating imaging, molecular, genomic, and single-cell rnaseq technologies that further support the role of host immunity in the maintenance of cnidarian-symbiodiniaceae partnerships and shed light on how different symbiont genera can modulate this immunity and ultimately influence host resistance to pathogens and bleaching.",
