          "name": "Shelby Gantt",
          "search_text": "shelby gantt exploring how cryptic diversity interacts with environment to determine coral reproductive ecology and thermal tolerance corals exhibit diverse sexual systems that can strongly influence their population ecology and resilience. in gonochoric corals, where individual colonies produce only eggs or sperm, differences in reproductive investment are expected to drive sex-specific vulnerability to environmental stress. for example, greater energetic costs of oogenesis may reduce the resources available for stress tolerance, making egg-producing colonies more susceptible to disturbances, such as repeated or extended heatwaves. in addition, physiology, symbiosis, and resilience can also be influenced by cryptic genetic diversity within coral species, yet the role of cryptic diversity in shaping coral reproductive ecology remains largely unexplored. this study investigates how environment, physiology, energetics, and symbiosis interact to shape the reproductive traits and thermal tolerance of two cryptic lineages of siderastrea siderea across a thermal variability gradient in bocas del toro, panam\u00e1. three complementary investigations were conducted: (1) to assess how habitat characteristics and lineage identity influence sex ratios and reproductive investment, colony lineage, sex, and fecundity were quantified at six reef sites over two reproductive cycles (2024\u20132025). (2) to identify physiological and symbiotic mechanisms underlying observed sex ratios, host energetic reserves, trophic status (stable isotopes), gene expression, and microbial (16s) and symbiodiniaceae (its2) community composition were characterized for each colony. (3) then to test how thermal tolerance varies among sexes, lineages, and environments, an acute heat stress experiment (cbass) was conducted in 2025. by integrating spatial, physiological, and symbiotic perspectives, this work moves beyond lineage identification to uncover how thermal environment, reproductive mode, and symbiotic partnerships interact to influence coral energetics, sex expression, and reproductive potential. these findings reveal not only mechanisms by which hidden genetic diversity mediates coral responses to environmental variability, but also provide new insights into how sex- and lineage-specific traits may determine responses to warming oceans.",
