          "name": "Mathieu Adg\u00e9",
          "search_text": "mathieu adg\u00e9 floating photovoltaic: a co-design opportunity between coral reef ecosystem protection, low carbon electricity production, and private-sector investment ? coral reef ecosystems are increasingly threatened by global warming, which is driving more frequent and intense bleaching events. however, coral restoration efforts aim to preserve reef surface and biodiversity. shading corals to reduce bleaching has been proposed, but like many interventions, its implementation remains limited in scale due to high costs. \r\n\r\nin french polynesia, limited land availability but extensive lagoon areas create an opportunity to test marine renewable energy technologies. this context led to a unique collaboration between total energies and criobe to develop a floating photovoltaic (fpv) project in the lagoon of ra\u02bbi\u0101tea. the project aims to evaluate whether fpv could contribute both to low-carbon electricity production and support coral reef ecosystem resilience. \r\n\r\nthe project is deliberately multidisciplinary. ecological monitoring assesses how prolonged shading affects coral energy budgets in nurseries and the long-term growth of coral pinnacles under different light regimes. in addition, a life-cycle assessment evaluates the impacts of fpv in a lagoon on resource use and pollutant emissions, while a remote-sensing-based assessment examines whether fpv is the most suitable technology across the different polynesian islands and evaluates its socio-technical potential.\r\n\r\nby combining ecological monitoring, engineering development, and spatial feasibility studies within a research-industry collaboration, this project aims to generate evidence on whether fpv can simultaneously support coral reef resilience and accelerate the transition toward low-carbon, locally produced energy. ultimately, this project aims to inform french polynesian and south pacific decision-makers and energy companies about the conditions under which fpv could become a viable contribution to regional energy independence while potentially supporting coral reef ecosystem resilience.",
