          "name": "William Zozaya",
          "search_text": "william zozaya rapid identification of thermotolerant corals by combining acute stress assays with photophysiological and biochemical markers coral restoration urgently requires fast, ecologically relevant, in-situ approaches to proactively identify thermotolerant colonies. the 2024 mass-bleaching event provided a rare opportunity to classify acropora nasuta and acropora hyacinthus in the field across multiple mo\u2019orea sites as resistant or sensitive, providing verified phenotypes for assay development. we compared three complementary heat-stress designs using clonal fragments: (i) a cbass acute assay with four increasing temperature treatments; (ii) an et25 acute assay targeting the temperature that induces a 25% decline in photosynthetic efficiency (fv/fm) relative to baseline; and (iii) a two-week medium-term exposure at +2\u00b0c above the local bleaching threshold. for each assay we quantified non-destructive photophysiology (fv/fm and standardized image-based bleaching score) and biochemical oxidative status (superoxide dismutase, catalase, total protein, total antioxidant capacity, and lipid peroxidation, lpo). we then tested the ability of individual marker and multi-marker combinations to discriminate fragments into resistant versus sensitive categories, using cross-validated models. non-destructive photophysiology alone showed highly variable predictive power across assays: the two-week exposure reached 73% correct classification, whereas cbass dropped to 53%, et25 performed intermediately.\r\ntaken together, the results show that fv/fm and bleaching scores alone offer limited power to correctly recover the resistance vs sensitive status documented in the field in 2024. incorporating antioxidant enzymes levels, protein content, total antioxidant capacity, and lpo often improved accuracy across assays and species, highlighting their value for detecting physiological resilience not captured by photophysiology alone.\r\noverall, assay performance is strongly context-dependent and influenced by the suite of markers measured. integrating additional physiological markers substantially enhances the predictive accuracy of heat-stress assays for identifying thermotolerant donors in restoration programs. this framework offers pragmatic, evidence-based guidance for selecting  thermotolerant donors and fragments for climate-smart restoration during future heatwaves.",
