          "name": "Maxine Mouly",
          "search_text": "maxine mouly environmental history and preconditioning to co-varying temperature and ph variability shape coral calcification and stress tolerance mounting evidence suggests corals native to environments with strong abiotic variability possess enhanced tolerance to those stressors, which can provide insights into how corals might cope with climate change. yet, we lack a mechanistic understanding of how such tolerance is acquired. moreover, it remains unknown whether experimentally preconditioning corals to environmental variability can replicate stress tolerance found in corals from variable habitats, and how stressor intensity and exposure duration combine to modulate tolerance. while research has focused on the potential for thermal preconditioning to enhance heat tolerance, ocean warming and acidification act together and can reduce calcification, casting uncertainty over the future reef-building capacity of scleractinian corals. we collected porites furcata from two sites with distinct environmental regimes in cura\u00e7ao (southern caribbean)\u2013a multi-stressor, variable inland bay and more stable fringing reef. we exposed corals ex situ to four variability regimes of co-varying temperature and ph by altering stressor intensity and duration in an orthogonal design. this preconditioning period lasted 12 weeks. it was followed by a two-week warming and acidification stress test simulating future climate conditions to investigate how short-term preconditioning and long-term environmental history shape stress tolerance. we quantified calcification using buoyant weight and alkalinity anomaly measurements and assessed changes in skeletal structure using 3d photogrammetry. preconditioning produced similar effects on corals from both origins: corals exposed to long-duration variability calcified best when the intensity remained low, while corals from high-intensity treatments calcified more when variability was brief rather than prolonged. although calcification rates were similar among bay and reef corals after preconditioning, bay corals from all treatments maintained higher calcification after exposure to severe stress, suggesting enhanced stress tolerance. our findings demonstrate that, in this system, long-term environmental history drives tolerance to combined warming and acidification\u2013a tolerance that may be difficult to acquire through short-term acclimation. rethinking depth-generalism in caribbean corals: cryptic diversity and microhabitat partitioning early studies of reef-building corals highlighted their broad environmental distributions, often remarking on their high degree of phenotypic plasticity across habitats. subsequent quantitative assessments revealed greater depth specialization than initially recognized, a pattern reinforced by genomic studies exposing depth-partitioned cryptic lineages. however, as only a small fraction of recognized depth-generalist species have been examined genomically, it remains unclear what proportion of reef-building coral species are truly depth opportunistic. focusing on the southern caribbean\u2013where a disproportionate number of such assessments have been conducted\u2013we addressed this question using an ecological and reefscape genomic analysis. by combining quantitative surveys of species depth distributions (5\u201360 m) with evidence of depth-associated genetic partitioning, we identified montastraea cavernosa and stephanocoenia intersepta as the remaining depth-generalist species whose genetic structure has yet to be evaluated in this region. genome-wide sequencing of both species (n = 321) across the depth gradient in cura\u00e7ao revealed three and five sympatric cryptic lineages, respectively, each with distinct yet partially overlapping depth distributions. to explore whether cryptic lineages are ecologically partitioned at depths where they overlap, we leveraged photogrammetric mapping to characterize colony-level microhabitat and morphology. we uncovered microhabitat niche partitioning among syntopic lineages, evinced by differences in colony angle, local rugosity, and degree of occlusion. although colonies from different lineages were visually indistinguishable, we found differences in colony size and polyp density at depths where they co-occurred. overall, our results demonstrate that the last apparent depth-generalist species on southern caribbean reefs comprise specialist cryptic lineages that are partitioned across both depth and microhabitats. these findings therefore challenge the existence of depth-generalism among corals in this region and, more broadly, the longstanding assumption that wide depth distributions reflect high phenotypic plasticity. appreciating this genetic diversity and its ecological significance will greatly improve our ability to predict\u2013and ultimately bolster\u2013coral responses to climate change.",
