          "name": "Susanne Baehr",
          "search_text": "susanne baehr patterns of cryptic biodiversity in pacific mesophotic coral ecosystems revealed by long-term arms deployments understanding biodiversity patterns and their drivers has become a central theme in mesophotic coral ecosystem research. studies on fishes and benthic assemblages consistently show strong depth-driven species turnover, highlighting how compositionally distinct mesophotic communities are from shallow reefs. unfortunately, research has focused largely on conspicuous taxa, leaving the cryptic fauna that makes up most reef biodiversity largely unknown at mesophotic depths. on shallow reefs, autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms) are a widely used tool for sampling cryptic biodiversity, but their application in the mesophotic zone has been very limited. to help fill this gap, our team deployed arms at 10, 50, and 100 m across four pacific locations. here we report the first retrieval of 13 arms, conducted in october 2025 on guam after eight years of deployment, yielding more than 2000 organisms, including several new records and new species. sessile communities quantified at low taxonomic resolution revealed strong depth structuring. permanova showed depth as the dominant factor shaping assemblages, and phylum richness increased significantly from 10 to 100 m. the top plates displayed marked shifts with increasing depth: the shallow unit was heavily scrapped by fishes, with remaining biomass consisting of turf and macroalgae; at 50 m, crustose coralline algae (cca) and macroalgae were most common; and at 100 m, cca remained prevalent, but sponge cover increased. additional depth-linked patterns included a decreasing biomass gradient, abundant mollusks and brachiopods at 50 m, and higher cnidarian diversity at 100 m, including depth-specialist scleractinians. these preliminary results show that even coarse taxonomic data capture strong depth structuring in cryptic communities, mirroring patterns known for fishes and corals. continued taxonomic and molecular work will produce a comprehensive baseline of mesophotic biodiversity at a broad geographic scale, support evaluation of shallow\u2013deep connectivity, and characterize the functional organization of deep communities. vivid colouration in mesophotic reef fishes: adaptive responses to spectrally restricted light environments rapid light attenuation with depth acts as selective pressure on visual systems and colouration in marine fishes. as longer wavelengths are absorbed first, the spectrum narrows with depth and becomes blue-dominated. this directly affects colour appearance and function. below 15-20 m, red fluorescence allows some species to reintroduce long wavelengths and restore colour contrast for communication, prey or predator detection, and camouflage. in deep, aphotic environments, many fishes rely on darkening and minimal reflectance to stay inconspicuous. however, the mesophotic zone (50\u2013150 m depth) is an intermediate environment where sunlight is reduced to a weak (1\u20135%), narrow blue band. here, we present observations on mesophotic fish colour patterns from an in situ photographic archive and aquarium trials under simulated mesophotic light conditions. many mesophotic fishes express surprisingly warm yellows to reds, yet in their native light environment these hues, reds in particular, appear grey and may merely act as depth-dependent camouflage against low-contrast pale backgrounds. some species, however, possess reflective patches that remain conspicuous under low light and seem to enhance close-range signalling or detection. a striking feature of the mesophotic genus odontanthias is a head pattern of intense green fluorescence. because green wavelengths are absent from the ambient light, it reintroduces a colour contrast against both the blue-dominated background and the surrounding skin, which appears grey because of its red (but non-fluorescent) pigmentation. the functions of green fluorescence may mirror those proposed for red fluorescence in shallower fishes, including signalling, enhancing visual contrast, or aiding active photolocation of prey or predators, but experimental tests are needed for confirmation. therefore the mesophotic zone is the underexplored borderline for the evolution of colour and vision. it offers a promising basis for hypothesis-driven data collection on the mechanisms and functions of fish colouration at the limit of light.",
