          "search_text": "emma saso thermal stress tolerance of the massive coral pavona clavus from the gal\u00e1pagos islands predicting species resilience to environmental change requires understanding how phenotypic and transcriptional plasticity interact with local adaptation. in corals, this plasticity is mediated by the cellular stress response (csr), a tiered molecular response that mitigates damage from thermal extremes. under a 1.5\u00b0c warming scenario, corals are predicted to decline 70-90% by 2100, making the identification of bleaching resilience mechanisms critical. pavona clavus is one of the few reef-building corals persisting in the gal\u00e1pagos islands despite repeated exposure to el ni\u00f1o warming and cold-water upwelling events. to characterize the graded csr and bleaching sensitivity, colonies from three islands\u2013darwin (low variability, warmer regime), isabela (high variability, strong upwelling), and espa\u00f1ola (intermediate conditions)\u2013were subjected to stepwise heat stress (32.5, 34.0 and 35.5 \u00b0c) or cold stress (16, 14, 12 \u00b0c), using the coral bleaching automated stress system. we measured bleaching, photochemical efficiency, symbiont composition and transcriptomic responses via 3\u2019 mrna sequencing. darwin corals hosted symbiodiniaceae types d and c and exhibited greatest bleaching resistance, while isabela and espa\u00f1ola colonies hosted only type c symbionts and bleached more severely. extreme temperatures triggered the highest differential gene expression compared to controls (26\u00b0c). in darwin corals, upregulated genes were enriched for stress response pathways (e.g. heat shock proteins), while metabolic pathways were broadly downregulated; pro-apoptotic genes were strongly expressed at the highest heat exposure. our results demonstrate habitat-driven differences in csr intensity and symbiont associations that correspond to thermal tolerance in p. clavus. understanding the molecular pathways conferring resilience under both warming and cooling extremes will support management efforts aimed at sustaining coral symbioses in a changing climate.",
