      "search_text": "california state university monterey bay ava besecker cheryl logan ava besecker coral growth and symbiont dynamics during the 2023\u20132024 el ni\u00f1o inform climate-resilient restoration in the gal\u00e1pagos climate change has driven widespread coral bleaching and mortality, yet some corals persist through repeated marine heatwaves. in the gal\u00e1pagos, extreme conditions provide a natural laboratory for studying resilience, and some corals have persisted even after declines during the 1982\u201383 el ni\u00f1o event. this study investigates coral resilience in a coral restoration site during a natural warming event associated with the 2023-2024 el ni\u00f1o. we monitored coral survival, bleaching and growth using structure-from-motion photogrammetry, and evaluated how symbiont composition and genotype affected thermal tolerance throughout el nin\u0303o. clonal fragments of pocillopora spp. were monitored within the reef revival nursery on isabela island, gal\u00e1pagos and at two nearby depth-matched outplanting sites with differing environmental characteristics. coral growth varied within and between outplanting sites, with 96% of colonies growing faster at the more protected site (bah\u00eda), where corals showed significantly higher growth rates (2.96 cm/year) compared to the more exposed site (calera, 2.1 cm/year). differing from the rest of the eastern tropical pacific, no widespread bleaching was observed despite elevated sea surface temperatures in the region (april-may 2024 degree heating weeks = 9). genotype and symbiont composition contributed to interindividual differences in growth rates. coral haplotypes thought to be less heat tolerant (haplotype 3) grew faster than those thought to be more heat tolerant (haplotype 1). interestingly, haplotype 3 hosted a higher ratio of the heat-tolerant symbiont durusdinium even though this haplotype is thought to associate less with durusdinium than haplotype 1. this pattern suggests active ecological and evolutionary shifts within local coral populations that were once considered entirely clonal, with novel host\u2013symbiont combinations emerging in regions where they were historically absent. identifying host\u2013symbiont pairings associated with higher growth during warming conditions will strengthen the development of climate-resilient coral nurseries in the galapagos and guide similar restoration strategies worldwide. cheryl logan mechanisms of thermal tolerance in gal\u00e1pagos corals corals in the eastern tropical pacific (etp) inhabit harsh conditions outside the typical range of most tropical reefs, experiencing high temperature variability, high nutrient levels, and relatively acidified waters. in the gal\u00e1pagos, upwelling and recurrent enso events further expose corals to extreme cold and warm anomalies that can lead to bleaching. to examine how corals persist under such extremes, we conducted an archipelago-wide survey of gal\u00e1pagos coral tolerance to heat and cold stress using a portable bleaching stress system. thermal tolerance assays on pocillopora and pavona across seven islands revealed clear spatial patterns: corals from the coolest region (isabela) were the most heat-sensitive, while those from the warmer northern region (darwin and wolf) exhibited the broadest thermal tolerances. we then tracked the fate of the relatively vulnerable isabela corals at a new restoration site over a two-year period. we found higher coral genetic diversity, variation in growth rates, and more diverse coral-symbiont associations in pocilloporids than previously reported. when combined with the archipelago-wide results, these findings suggest that isabela pocilloporids have higher adaptive potential and suitability for restoration than expected. our findings highlight the gal\u00e1pagos as a natural laboratory for understanding how corals persist in extreme and fluctuating environments and reveal physiological traits that may shape coral resilience under future ocean conditions.",
