          "name": "David Jen",
          "search_text": "david jen do coral species and substrate type shape seaweed communities and their microbiomes? macroalgae are long recognised as significant competitors of corals, engaging in multiple physical, chemical, and close-proximity interactions that can harm coral health. the nature and intensity of these interactions vary across both seaweed and coral species. the great barrier reef outlook report 2024 identifies climate-induced heat stress as the main driver of coral decline, alongside impacts from land-based runoff, coastal development, direct human use, and low-salinity extended flood plumes. coral recovery has also been hindered in some inshore reef sites due to the persistence of macroalgal cover, which limits coral recruitment and regrowth. therefore, understanding the mechanisms that govern macroalgae-coral interactions are increasingly critical for predicting and mitigating further degradation of inshore reef ecosystems. \r\n\r\nthere is a critical knowledge gap in understanding how newly recruiting macroalgae interact with existing corals, what drives their settlement patterns and seasonal dominance, and how these processes can be managed to prevent macroalgae overgrowth, while supporting coral restoration.  \r\n\r\nto investigate early macroalgal settlement on coral reefs, we deployed a range of settlement substrates (glass, standard concrete, and low-carbon concrete \u201cblue\u201d tiles; 2.5 \u00d7 2.5 cm each) on a 1.2 \u00d7 1.2 m frame transplanted with healthy coral fragments (acropora, pocillopora, turbinaria, micromusa) in-situ. these tiles will be monitored throughout the year (2026) alongside assessments of the surrounding benthic community to provide a better lead in understanding macroalgae-coral interaction while 1) identifying when seaweed species settle onto substrates 2) whether certain seaweed have substrate preferences, or responses to abiotic factors 3) observing patterns of seaweed settlement in proximity to specific coral species and examine whether specific species of corals naturally attract/deter/prevent growth of algal settlement. these findings will enhance knowledge on the influence of algal growth on coral restoration, contributing to the reef 2050 long-term sustainability plan for a resilient and healthy great barrier reef ecosystem.",
