          "name": "Stephanie Reynaud",
          "search_text": "stephanie reynaud metabolic reorganization and physiological plasticity of porites under naturally acidified reefs anthropogenic co\u2082 emissions are intensifying ocean acidification, with major implications for coral calcification and overall reef functioning. yet, coral populations inhabiting naturally low-ph environments provide a valuable framework for assessing the mechanisms underlying potential acclimatization or adaptive responses. in the palau archipelago, we conducted an integrative physiological and biochemical assessment of porites spp. colonies from control and naturally acidified sites, quantifying parameters associated with trophic and symbiotic status, carbon acquisition pathways, energy reserves, redox homeostasis, and calcification dynamics.\r\ncolonies from acidified reefs displayed attenuated antioxidant defenses and reduced cellular stress markers, indicative of a physiological recalibration to sustained low-ph exposure. isotopic signatures of host tissues and symbiotic dinoflagellates, together with decreased carbonic anhydrase activity, support a shift in inorganic carbon utilization away from bicarbonate uptake toward direct co\u2082 diffusion. this shift likely relaxes the energetic demands associated with active bicarbonate transport and may facilitate the maintenance of photosynthesis, respiration, and biomass despite altered carbonate chemistry. the absence of metabolic suppression, combined with increased biomass, suggests reorganization of central metabolic pathways, potentially analogous to a warburg-type metabolic phenotype.\r\nhowever, the energetic budget conferred by modified carbon acquisition strategies appears insufficient to sustain robust calcification under acidified conditions. although linear extension rates are maintained or enhanced, skeletal density is reduced, consistent with the increased energy expenditure required to elevate ph within the extracellular calcifying fluid. this elevated energetic demand contributes to diminished lipid reserves, potentially compromising long-term stress resilience.\r\ncollectively, our findings demonstrate that porites spp. exhibit substantive physiological plasticity under chronic acidification, mediated by shifts in carbon metabolism and redox regulation. nevertheless, these adjustments entail significant trade-offs that may heighten vulnerability to future multifactorial stressors. elucidating the metabolic pathways underpinning these responses will be essential to predicting coral persistence in rapidly changing oceans.",
