      "search_text": "centre de recherches insulaires et observatoire de l'environnement de moorea oc\u00e9ane moeino oc\u00e9ane moeino impact of the 2024 bleaching event in french polynesia in 2024, according to the national oceanic and atmospheric administration (noaa), the world experienced the fourth global coral bleaching event ever recorded, and the second within the past decade. these increasingly frequent and intense thermal anomalies represent major threats to coral reef ecosystems, particularly in french polynesia where sea temperature has risen well above historical norms. this study aimed to assess the extent of coral bleaching during the 2024 event, and to determine how its severity varied across spatial factors (archipelagos and depth) as well as among different coral genera. \r\nto quantify bleaching levels, photoquadrats were collected in two polynesian archipelagos: the society islands and the tuamotu archipelago. degree heating weeks (dhw) were calculated for each site to estimate accumulated thermal stress. bleaching rates and coral health categories were then extracted through image analysis and compared between spatial factors and taxa. \r\nresults show that the tuamotu archipelago exhibited significantly higher bleaching and mortality rates, with 95% of colonies affected, compared to the society islands. this difference strongly correlates with higher dhw values recorded in the tuamotu region, indicating a stronger exposure to prolonged heat stress. among coral genera, porites exhibited the highest thermal resistance, with only half the proportion of bleached colonies reported compared to more sensitive taxa such as acropora and pocillopora. however, intraspecific variability was observed with some individuals within sensitive genera displayed higher thermotolerance, suggesting potential resilience or adaptive capacity. \r\noverall, these findings highlight the uneven impact of the 2024 bleaching event across french polynesia and underscore the importance of incorporating community composition into bleaching assessments to better predict reef trajectories. understanding spatial bleaching patterns and species-specific responses is crucial to support restoration efforts under more recurrent marine heatwaves. future work will investigate long-term thermal resistance in survivors and test whether resilience can be transmitted to offspring.",
