          "name": "N\u00e9stor Rey Villiers",
          "search_text": "n\u00e9stor rey villiers functional ecology of zooxanthellate octocorals on caribbean coral reefs the submitter has requested this abstract is considered for a poster only.\r\n\r\nshallow zooxanthellate octocorals in the caribbean sea may exhibit trophic plasticity, enabling them to adapt to environmental changes driven by anthropogenic activities. in this study, we evaluated the trophic strategies of two octocoral species and their effect on growth rates in reefs with contrasting environmental conditions in northwestern cuba. ten colonies of eunicea flexuosa and plexaura k\u00fckenthali were collected from two reefs under eutrophic and oligotrophic conditions, and the hosts were separated from their symbionts (sym) by centrifugation for stable isotopes analysis of nitrogen (\u03b415n) and carbon (\u03b413c). the overlap between host and symbiont standard ellipse area corrected for sample size in e. flexuosa was 34.7% in oligotrophic reefs and 27.1% in eutrophic reefs, indicating a mixotrophic strategy. in contrast, p. k\u00fckenthali showed a lower niche overlap between host and symbiont in both oligotrophic (8.5%) and eutrophic (6.6%) reefs, reflecting a more heterotrophic strategy. the \u03b413chost-sym values were significantly lower in p. k\u00fckenthali than in e. flexuosa under both environmental conditions, corroborating the heterotrophic prevalence of the former specie. in p. k\u00fckenthali, \u03b413chost-sym was negatively and significantly correlated with growth rate, suggesting that heterotrophy contributes to colony growth. in e. flexuosa, both \u03b415nsym and \u03b415nhost were positively and significantly correlated with growth rate, highlighting the importance of autotrophy. these results emphasize the plasticity and distinct trophic strategies of the two octocoral species, as well as their role in sustaining growth under eutrophic environmental conditions. octocorals' growth and survival under contrasting environmental conditions on caribbean reefs the submitter has requested this abstract is considered for a poster only.\r\n\r\noctocorals exhibit resilience to a range of local and global stressors in the caribbean sea, a trait that their rapid vertical growth may partly explain. this study aimed to quantify the growth rate, survival, and mortality of two octocoral species across a eutrophication gradient in cuban reefs. the gradient was characterized using microbiological variables, nutrient concentrations, and stable nitrogen isotopes. at each site, a permanent belt transect (250 \u00d7 2 m) was established to monitor colony height, width, and area over one year. the mean vertical growth rate of eunicea flexuosa was 2.4 cm yr\u207b\u00b9 (range: 0.1\u20137.7 cm yr\u207b\u00b9), while plexaura k\u00fckenthali exhibited 1.9 cm yr\u207b\u00b9 (range: 0.1\u20139.4 cm yr\u207b\u00b9). the growth rates of both species were significantly higher in impacted reefs by polluted river basins, and the eutrophication gradient, water visibility, and sediment accumulation on the bottom explained 36\u201378% of the observed variability. e. flexuosa had 40.1% mortality and 59.9% survival, whereas p. k\u00fckenthali showed 35.2% survival. spatial differences in mortality and survival were not explained by eutrophication gradient, water visibility, or sediment accumulation, but hydrodynamic stress accounted for 59% of mortality variability across species. these results highlight the resilience of both species, which grow faster in eutrophic reefs with reduced water visibility and higher sedimentation, contributing to the ecological success of octocorals in the caribbean sea.",
