          "name": "Jeong-Hyun Lee",
          "search_text": "jeong-hyun lee phanerozoic fluctuations in sponge and coral reef abundance and the rise of scleractinian corals corals and sponges are major reef-builders of the phanerozoic. three key groups are: corals (rugosans, tabulates, scleractinians), hypercalcified sponges (archaeocyaths, stromatoporoids, sphinctozoans, inozoans, chaetetids), and synsedimentarily lithified siliceous sponges (lithistids and some hexactinellids). they differ in temperature and oxygen requirements, as well as in their abilities to calcify. these influences can be discerned in the phanerozoic abundance and environmental distribution of reefs over 25-50 ma timescales. \r\n\r\nreefs formed by lithified siliceous sponges are common in the cambrian-ordovician and triassic- cretaceous, often with microbial carbonates. this time-distribution appears to be positively correlated with widespread environmental conditions, such as anoxia and elevated temperature, that favored their synsedimentary calcification. in contrast, they declined during intervals with cooler temperatures and increased oxygenation such as the cenozoic. \r\n\r\nhypercalcified sponge and coral reefs were abundant, and commonly co-occurred, during intervals of moderate temperature and oxygenation. both show marked episodes of decline at mass extinction events linked to increased temperature and anoxia. cenozoic hypercalcified sponges are only conspicuous in cryptic or relatively deep forereef habitats. they appear to be azooxanthellate and lack the size, and probably growth rate, that made stromatoporoids important reef-builders in the mid-paleozoic. with a few exceptions, siliceous sponges appear to have lacked the synsedimentary calcification necessary to form lithified reefs during the cenozoic. in contrast, increase in scleractinian corals may be related to cooling and oxygenation favoring the temperature-sensitive algal symbionts (e.g., zooxanthellae) that assist their shallow-water growth and calcification, and to the evolution of branched morphotypes that aid reproduction and recovery from disturbance. \r\n\r\nthus, global fluctuations in oxygenation and temperature have influenced sponge and coral reef formation throughout the phanerozoic, as well as during mass extinction events. cenozoic oxygen increase and global cooling likely favored scleractinians over hypercalcified sponges in shallow tropical reefs. with holocene global warming, these conditions may be changing.",
