          "name": "Piper Zola",
          "search_text": "piper zola estimating infection and bioerosion rates of the boring sponge cliona celata via direct contact and larval dispersal with applications to reef carbonate budgets the process of bioerosion by boring sponges plays a crucial role in shaping biogenic carbonate reef systems, including mollusc beds and coral reefs, through calcium carbonate recycling, sediment creation, and reef accretion control. despite their ecological importance, few studies have compared infection intensity and bioerosion capabilities between the different mechanisms of boring sponge infection (ie., direct contact versus larval recruitment) onto substrate among carbonate reefs. to assess differences in infection mechanisms by the boring sponge cliona celata, a field experiment was deployed in charleston harbor (charleston, sc, usa). clean oyster shells, which served as calcium carbonate substrate, were either directly exposed to sponge via physical attachment to infected substrate or to sponge larval recruitment via larvae present in the water column. results revealed greater boring hole size, percent surface area infected, and number of boring holes in substrate exposed to physical contact with sponge infection, suggesting a higher infection rate and intensity compared to infection via larval settlement alone. results also reveal that infection via physical contact generated a higher bioerosion rate compared to larval settlement. we estimate that c. celata in direct contact with new substrate has a bioerosion rate of 0.730 \u00b1 0.080 kg caco3 m-2 yr-1. boring sponge infection has important ramifications for the function and structure of carbonate reef habitats around the world. our findings suggest that contact-based spread can accelerate substrate loss and contribute to net reef degradation.",
