          "name": "April Johns",
          "search_text": "april johns microbial signatures of coral resilience in naturally acidified reefs coral reefs are declining worldwide in response to intensifying environmental stressors driven by climate change. ocean acidification, in particular, can stunt coral growth and weaken calcium carbonate skeletons, further increasing their susceptibility to damage or loss. yet a subset of corals naturally thrive in chronically low-ph environments, such as volcanic co\u2082 seep sites, suggesting some possess mechanisms that support resilience to persistent acidification. all coral holobionts comprise a diverse community of microorganisms that support critical processes such as nutrient cycling, stress tolerance, and pathogen defense. given the coral microbiome\u2019s role in maintaining coral health and its capacity to shift in response to environmental change, we hypothesize that it contributes to the resilience observed in low-ph-tolerant corals. investigating how microbial communities in these extreme corals differ in composition, function, and structure provides an opportunity to understand how the microbiome adapts to acidification and to identify pathways relevant to projected ocean conditions.\r\n\r\nin this study, corals were sampled from naturally elevated co\u2082 environments off shikinejima island, japan, and abitle island, papua new guinea. using shotgun metagenomic sequencing, we characterized the microbial taxa and functional traits associated with prolonged acidification. we also cultivated and sequenced microbial enrichments from these samples to pair culture conditions with metagenomic analyses while building a biobank of coral-associated microorganisms. preliminary results reveal variation in taxonomic composition and functional profiles across acidified environments, providing a basis to explore how local ocean geochemistry shapes the coral microbiome and whether these patterns generalize across geographically distinct reef systems.\r\n\r\nthis research will ultimately bridge ecological discovery with application by guiding the development of microbial-based approaches to enhance coral stress tolerance. together, these efforts lay the foundation for a deeper understanding of coral microbiome-driven responses to ocean acidification and for informing intervention strategies with potential to support coral resilience in a changing ocean.",
