      "search_text": "conservation society of pohnpei eugene joseph eugene joseph evolution of coral-reef fisheries management in pohnpei, federated states of micronesia in 2005, the conservation society of pohnpei (csp) began several long-term coral-reef fisheries monitoring programs to protect their biodiversity and marine resources. early results were used to educate communities about declining resources, and in particular unsustainable harvesting of groupers during spawning events. sharing simple science with communities created support for protecting groupers during this critical time, and in turn, community support led to legislation that protected groupers for 4 critical months each year. while not perfectly designed and enforced, policies were refined and led to significant improvements in grouper stocks over the next 10 years. csp next began the consultation process for a network of no-take marine protected areas around pohnpei. over 10 years, mpa designs have been refined, enforcement has been improved, and positive outcomes have been realized for both marine resources and fishers. pohnpei now boasts the most effective network within the micronesia coral-reef monitoring program, and the traditional leaders were awarded funding to host annual cross-site mpa sharing events. despite these management gains, recent fisheries-dependent surveys have revealed that harvesting continues to increase outside of mpa with some stocks declining. recent results from our fisher surveys are now being used to improve species-based harvesting rules for many dominant species. cumulative efforts will be contextualized and future directions will be shared.",
