          "name": "Derek Manzello",
          "search_text": "derek manzello heat-driven functional extinction of caribbean acropora corals from florida\u2019s coral reef the frequency and severity of marine heatwaves are increasing, resulting in widespread degradation of the function, structure, resilience, and adaptive capacity of ecosystems. coral reefs are one of the most heat-vulnerable marine ecosystems; heat-driven mass bleaching events have caused mortality and reef degradation for > 40 years, with impacts continually increasing in scale and severity. half the atlantic ocean\u2019s reef-building coral species face a risk of extinction due to ocean warming and other threats in the coming two decades, with elkhorn (acropora palmata) and staghorn coral (acropora cervicornis) among the most critically endangered. in 2023, a record-setting marine heatwave triggered the ninth mass coral bleaching event on florida\u2019s coral reef (fcr) since 1987.  degree heating weeks, a satellite-derived metric of heat exposure, were 2.2 to 4-fold higher in 2023 than all prior years in the satellite record.  in-water surveys (n = 525) tracked the fate of 52,356 wild and restored colonies across 391 distinct sites and found that 97.8\u2013100% of the a. palmata and a. cervicornis colonies died in the florida keys and dry tortugas.  mortality was lower offshore southeast florida (37.9%), reflecting cooler temperatures in this region.  since the late 1970s, multiple stressors had already reduced the ecological relevance of acropora in florida, but the 2023 heatwave marks their extirpation from nearly all of the florida keys reef track and functional extinction for the entirety of fcr. on caribbean reefs, a. palmata has dominated the reef crest zone, while a. cervicornis has dominated the mid-slope zone for at least the past 250,000 \u2013 500,000 years.  in florida, a. palmata was disproportionately responsible for reef construction over the holocene.  thus, the 2023 bleaching and mortality event in florida is not only ecologically but also geologically significant.",
