          "name": "Morgan Pomeroy",
          "search_text": "morgan pomeroy mass caribbean-wide acropora coral mortality overshadows emergent thermal tolerance during the fourth global coral bleaching event (gcbe4), coral reefs across the caribbean faced unprecedented marine heatwaves and coral bleaching in 2023 and 2024. impacts to critically endangered acropora corals, in peril for over half a century, are of particular concern following these repeated events. here, we investigate mortality of a. cervicornis and a. palmata from 2023\u201324 throughout the caribbean, spanning 1,107 sites. heat stress on most reefs was more than twice the threshold recognized for mortality risk. accounting for unequal sampling, region-wide estimated median mortality at this level of heat stress was 75.2% for a. cervicornis and 87.6% for a. palmata. survival varied significantly by geographic subregion; median estimated mortality for both taxa was greatest in the greater antilles and floridian subregions, exceeding 90%. median mortality was lowest in the western and southern caribbean subregions, at roughly 7% for both species. coral inhabiting areas with historical heat stress \u2265 8\u00b0c-weeks and depths > 6 m experienced significantly lower (two-to-five fold) mortality, indicating these acroporids have some adaptive capacity and may find refuge in deeper waters. while remnant populations may be better suited to survive future heat stress events, the ongoing loss of genetic diversity, reproductive capacity, and shrinking windows for recovery suggests acropora caribbean-wide remain at risk of a functional extinction in the coming decades.",
