      "search_text": "coral soul marina palacios marina palacios rebuilding temperate mesophotic coral ecosystems: a five-year case study of dendrophyllia ramea restoration, protection, and fisheries stewardship in the alboran sea mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces) are biodiversity hotspots and potential climate refugia, yet they are increasingly threatened as human and climate pressures extend into deeper waters. we present deepcore, a deep-coral restoration case study at punta de la mona (southern alboran sea, granada mpa, spain). since 2021, the project has addressed the collapse of temperate mesophotic habitats formed by dendrophyllia ramea at 30\u201360 m depth. lost fishing gear and debris have broken colonies and triggered severe pathologies, affecting ~75% of the local population and reducing reef structural and functional complexity. deepcore combines multidisciplinary research, active restoration, and social co-management with public authorities, fishing guilds, and scientific institutions to tackle both impacts and their root causes.\r\n\r\nresearch comprises high-resolution mapping of d. ramea distribution, oceanographic characterization, population assessments (disturbance, connectivity, genetics, epibionts/parasites), and physiological studies (biometry/photogrammetry, reproduction/histology, pathology). this evidence base guides restoration and supported a 2025 management plan regulating fishing and anchoring.\r\n\r\ndeepcore is among the first efforts to restore mces in situ using technical divers and closed-circuit rebreathers. work occurs under demanding conditions (\u224850 m depth, currents to 7 m/s, ~12\u00b0c, low visibility), yielding new knowledge on a poorly known deep species and the logistics of deep restoration.\r\n\r\nrestoration follows a depth-adapted pipeline: (1) removal of ghost nets and garbage for recycling; (2) rescue of fragmented colonies and transport to deep nurseries via scooter-assisted dives; (3) recovery nurseries at 40 m with repeated treatment of pathologies, sediment and parasite removal, and monitoring; and (4) transplantation with a precise, low-impact resin-anchoring technique to rebuild reef patches and three-dimensional complexity.\r\n\r\nto date, 4.5 t of debris have been removed, >5,700 m\u00b2 of habitat restored, and ~1,000 colonies rehabilitated or outplanted. survival exceeds 80% after five years, alongside increases in associated fauna (+45%) and fish abundance (+75%).",
