      "search_text": "department of conservation - new zealand lyndsey holland lyndsey holland new zealand\u2019s conservation services programme: advancing understanding and mitigation of commercial fishing impacts on protected corals the coral biota of aotearoa new zealand is remarkably rich and diverse, encompassing more than 300 legally protected species. many corals thrive offshore in deeper waters and numerous research surveys and biodiversity inventories have greatly expanded our knowledge of their distribution and diversity. these voyages have also built a national collection of catalogued specimens, which provides an invaluable resource for ongoing research. our understanding of deep-sea corals has also advanced through the work of government fisheries observers, who collect data and specimens from commercial vessels. as corals frequently occur as bycatch in new zealand\u2019s deep-sea trawl fisheries, their observations continually underpin critical insights into coral ecology and anthropogenic impacts. \r\n\r\nthe department of conservation, the government agency responsible for wildlife management, administers the conservation services programme (csp). csp supports research into bycatch and the broader environmental impacts of fishing through the administration of cost-recovered levies from quota owners of commercial fish stocks. since these impacts include direct effects on protected species, csp runs a dedicated scientific programme to better understand and mitigate fishing pressures on marine fauna. this includes corals in four taxonomic groups as described in legislation: all species in the octocorallia, scleractinia, antipatharia, and the stylasteridae.\r\n\r\nfunding through conservation service levies is legally entrenched, providing ongoing csp support for research relevant to managing the impacts of commercial fishing. research has spanned diverse themes\u2013connectivity, species distribution modelling, reproduction, genetics, and biodiversity. this talk highlights the breadth of coral research undertaken through csp, now in its 30th year, and explores the programme\u2019s distinctive model, its scientific contributions, ongoing challenges, and future directions for both research and coral conservation in new zealand. in aquarium observations on reproduction of goniocorella dumosa, a deep-sea coral from the aotearoa new zealand region little is known of the reproductive traits and dispersal potential of many deep-sea corals, and in-aquarium spawning has been observed for very few species globally. here, we present in-aquarium observations of the reproduction of goniocorella dumosa, a habitat-forming deep-sea scleractinian stony coral found in the new zealand region. in contrast to previous understanding that g. dumosa were broadcast spawners, colonies of g. dumosa held in an aquarium released large (approx. 1.1 mm x 0.8 mm) free-swimming planula larvae. we describe the observed larval behaviour, settlement preferences and post-larval growth and development of g. dumosa. the more limited dispersal potential of larvae from a brooding species compared to a broadcast spawning coral has significant implications for both population connectivity, and for the potential recovery of this species from disturbance by human activities. this in turn could influence management and protection strategies for g. dumosa and their habitat.",
      "connection_count": 2
