          "search_text": "darryl anthony valino linking 2d measurements to 3d complexity metrics to assess habitat capacity in turbid coral reef systems turbid reefs are often assumed to be structurally simple, ecologically degraded, or of limited conservation value. their apparent success in marginal settings is typically assessed through two-dimensional coral surveys, which may be prone to overestimating reef condition. high coral cover values in turbid reefs often reflect the dominance of low-profile taxa, such as encrusting and plating corals that grow horizontally across the substrate. to address this, we linked 2d colony diameter measurements to 3d habitat capacity metrics, termed absolute spatial refuge (asr, dm\u00b3). we combined underwater photogrammetry and ex-situ light scanning to construct and measure 262 three-dimensional models of dominant coral genera (n = 24) representing five major growth forms from turbid reefs in singapore. bayesian gaussian generalized linear models were used to describe and predict how colony diameter scales with asr across different growth forms. colony diameters of all corals measured in phototransect surveys from pulau hantu and pulau satumu were then used as inputs to predict in-situ asr values. standard reef health indices were also derived, including coral cover , abundance, density, and diversity. both reefs exhibited high coral cover but contrasting ecological parameters and structural capacities.  pulau hantu, characterized by low diversity and low colony density, showed a higher mean asr (3.05 \u00b1 1.79 m\u00b3 m\u207b\u00b2) driven mainly by patches of large goniopora colonies. pulau satumu, with higher diversity and smaller yet more numerous colonies, had a lower mean asr (0.45 \u00b1 0.06 m\u00b3 m\u207b\u00b2). comparisons between colony diameter and asr size-frequency distributions revealed distinct skewness and kurtosis patterns, emphasizing that increases in colony size do not necessarily yield proportional gains in habitat volume depending on morphology.  our findings demonstrate the importance of integrated asr-based metrics into monitoring programs, including the reanalysis of legacy datasets, to better evaluate both ecological and functional trajectories of coral reef systems. in the shadows: cliff shading creates horizontal zonation and bleaching refuge on coral reefs karstic island reefscapes are common across southeast asia. limestone cliffs rise hundreds of meters above fringing reefs and cast persistent shadows that alter underwater light environments. while light availability is a major driver of coral photo-physiology and community structure, the ecological implications of natural cliff-induced shading on corals remain poorly understood. here we investigate the effects of cliff shading on light and temperature regimes, to understand how benthic communities respond to these changes across a consistent depth range. lagen cove located in palawan, philippines, is a shallow reef bordered by two karst cliffs. three reef zones were delineated based on the duration and extent of daily shadow exposure and photosynthetic active radiation (par) loggers were deployed to quantify light availability within these areas. mean daily light integrals (dli) revealed that shaded zones received only 37\u201344% of the light reaching the central bay. overall, shaded sites experienced ~50% shorter light duration, with light peaks restricted to midday. temperature profiles closely mirrored light patterns, suggesting shaded zones are also cool-water microhabitats. benthic surveys showed highest coral cover in the central bay, decreasing toward the shaded zones, while the opposite pattern was observed for algae and soft corals. orthomosaics revealed clusters of bleached fungiidae colonies within shaded areas, possibly reflecting lateral migration to escape light and heat stress during a period of thermal bleaching. our findings demonstrate that cliff shading generates distinct horizontal zonation patterns on reefs, independent of depth. these naturally shaded reefs may also function as local refugia under thermal stress, underscoring the need to prioritise geomorphically shaded habitats in management and conservation frameworks.",
