          "name": "Alexandre Siqueira",
          "search_text": "alexandre siqueira defining present and future coral reef environmental envelopes coral reefs exist within a broad seascape of abiotic conditions. the combination of these conditions \u2013 the environmental envelope \u2013 defines the physiological limits within which reef-building organisms construct the geological structures that support extraordinary biodiversity. yet, despite early efforts nearly three decades ago, we still lack a clear, quantitative framework that explains the conditions under which reefs develop. here, we integrate three major recent advances \u2013 high-resolution coral reef maps, large-scale environmental data, and cutting-edge machine-learning tools \u2013 to: (1) quantify the multivariate abiotic conditions of reef occurrences; (2) delineate global reef limits and identify marginal environments; and (3) forecast future changes in reef occurrence under climate change. leveraging sixteen key abiotic variables, we show that coral reefs occupy a remarkably narrow proportion (~16%) of the available environmental space in shallow oceans. machine-learning models accurately reproduced this envelope, identifying minimum monthly sea surface temperature and light-penetration depth as the two strongest predictors of reef occurrence globally. interrogating model outputs revealed two major patterns. first, locations with suitable environments but without reefs are rare, suggesting that reefs already occupy nearly all conditions that permit growth. second, many reefs occur near their environmental limits. these areas provide the first strictly quantitative, multivariate definition of marginal reefs, moving beyond traditional single-variable thresholds. finally, we use our framework to forecast shifts in the probabilities of reef occurrence under future warming. by 2100, mean probabilities decline by 8.5% and 13.7% under moderate (ssp1-2.6; +1.8 \u00bac) and high emissions (ssp3-7.0; +3.6 \u00bac) scenarios, respectively, with marginal reef environments more than doubling in both cases. nonetheless, some temperate and currently reef-free regions show localised increases in suitability. taken together, our work establishes a robust global framework for understanding, mapping, and forecasting the environmental limits of coral reefs, providing an empirical basis for coral reef conservation prioritisation.",
