          "name": "Marcos Barbeitos",
          "search_text": "marcos barbeitos ensemble learning in ecological niche modeling of the siderastrea (cnidaria:scleractinia) atlantic complex: an information theory approach ecological niche modeling (enm) is an invaluable tool for the prediction of future coral reef ranges in times of global change. enm algorithms learn niche dimensions automatically from labeled training data and output continuous variables that express habitat suitability of unsurveyed areas, thus performing a regression task known as supervised machine learning (ml). predictions outside of the training area, or into the future, are called extrapolations or generalizations. extrapolations are especially prone to errors because predicted instances were not encountered during training. one way to improve generalization accuracy is to use an ensemble of models, drawing on the complementarity of their predictions to reduce the likelihood of model under- or overfitting to the training data. we introduce a novel approach to ensemble learning that combines model outcomes into a probabilistic framework and applied it to the siderastrea\u00a0atlantic complex as\u00a0a working example. we employed 9\u00a0enm algorithms\u00a0and used information theory to\u00a0build\u00a0uncertainty surfaces.\u00a0our method\u00a0uncovered surprising amounts of spatially-explicit variation in model performances,\u00a0both within\u00a0and among species, that cannot be captured by statistics traditionally employed in ml evaluation (receiver operating characteristic, true skill statistic, etc.). we also introduced an entropy weighting strategy into enm that integrates uncertainty into probabilistic ensemble predictions and, at the same\u00a0time, prevents ensemble contamination by the chaotic variance of overfitted models.\u00a0our approach uncovered cross-area species transferability between the caribbean and brazil and a highly suitable stretch for two species of the genus siderastrea along the western african coast, a result that was recently corroborated by citizen-science reports.\u00a0although employed in the context of spatial extrapolation, the method is readily extensible to temporal projections, whose reliability must be carefully assessed to ensure sensible management decisions. the approach was fully implemented in a free r package. cryptic speciation and demographic history of the genus favia in the south atlantic ocean ocean transgressions and regressions during the last pleistocene glaciation exposed and submerged extensive portions of the brazilian continental shelf. because the abrolhos reef complex is the only place that harbors all shallow-water coral species described for brazil, it was traditionally hypothesized as a refugium for coral populations, such as those of favia gravida. to test this scenario, we analyzed approximately 20,000 snps obtained through rad-seq from nine populations distributed along the brazilian coast. phylogenetic and population structure analyses (svdquartets, snapp, and snmf) consistently revealed three genetically distinct lineages (northern, northeastern, and eastern) whose geographic distributions mirror their genetic boundaries, indicating independent evolutionary histories rather than a recent split from a single ancestral population. demographic reconstructions with stairway plot 2 revealed pronounced population bottlenecks in the eastern and northeastern lineages. importantly, we tested multiple combinations of mutation rates and generation times, and all parameter sets converged on the same bottleneck signature, reinforcing that the demographic contraction is a robust signal in the data rather than an artifact of model assumptions. bayesian and spatial migration analyses (bayesass, eems) revealed minimal recent gene flow, with migration rates < 2% of individuals per generation among lineages. diyabc simulations further supported a scenario of multiple, isolated refugia (pp = 0.91). together, these findings challenge the prevailing single refugium paradigm and suggest that favia gravida persisted in at least three semi-independent refugia, each maintaining unique genetic diversity. conserving these regions is therefore essential for preserving the species\u2019 evolutionary potential.",
