          "name": "Ping Hung Chang",
          "search_text": "ping hung chang photoperiod regulation and multi-layer aquaculture for the cultivation and restoration of tripneustes gratilla the eco-friendly cultivation and aquaculture of sea urchins are extremely important for the restoration of coral reef areas; the relationship between sea urchins and the coral reef ecosystem is inseparable. this study utilized a method of controlling photoperiods, employing traditional abalone baskets in a multi-layered farming system within a tank, and feeding fresh ulva (sea lettuce) to tripneustes gratilla to monitor their growth and feeding rates. the results show that when culturing the sea urchins in a three-tiered stereoscopic basket system, individuals in the middle and lower layers grew faster than those in the top layers treatment did. this is likely related to the weaker light intensity in the lower sections of the culture tank. furthermore, the nocturnal feeding rate was approximately twice that of the diurnal rate. this research provides key techniques that integrate ecological conservation with rapid intermediate culture practices. this aquaculture technology helps to save time and costs while simultaneously enhancing the growth and feeding rates of the sea urchins, offering substantial benefits for sea urchin proliferation and the broader restoration of the coral reef ecosystem.",
