          "name": "Erin Tharp",
          "search_text": "erin tharp assessing spatial and temporal variation in mesophotic reef fish assemblages across flower garden banks using rov-based video transects this study quantifies spatial and temporal variation in mesophotic reef fish assemblages at the flower garden banks national marine sanctuary (fgbnms) using high-resolution mohawk rov video transects. new surveys conducted in 2025 were compared with archived  rov datasets from 2014-2015 at bright, geyer, and mcgrail banks, to evaluate decadal trends in species composition, abundance, and community structure. analyses of the historical dataset reveal pronounced spatial and depth-related patterns: each bank hosts distinct fish communities, with geyer bank supporting more deep-reef specialists and bright bank exhibiting the highest species richness. depth-structured assemblages were characterized by shallow mesophotic zones dominated by benthic feeders and reef-associated taxa, and deeper habitats support midwater predators and bottom-dwelling piscivores. despite clear shifts in species, trophic structure remained consistent across banks and depths. ongoing analyses will integrate benthic habitat annotations to assess relationships between fish assemblages, depth, and habitat complexity, providing a more holistic view of species-habitat interactions. by establishing an ecological baseline and assessing decadal shifts in fish community structure, this work will improve our understanding of mesophotic reef dynamics and inform adaptive, spatially explicit management strategies for the fgbnms. this aim serves as the foundation of my broader dissertation, which compares shallow and mesophotic coral physiology through analyses of total lipid and fatty acid composition in two boulder coral species, investigates depth-related variation in algal symbiont community structure, algal symbiont density, and chlorophyll concentration between these species and depths, and examines stakeholder perceptions of reef conservation. together, these data will provide a comprehensive evaluation of the ecological and socio-environmental factors shaping reefs in the fgbnms.",
