          "search_text": "lindsay spiers harnessing coral resilience through nursery-to-reef restoration processes: the use of microbiome and metabolomic analyses as tools to optimize restoration the loss of corals in the wild and within coral nurseries in florida during the summer of 2023 highlighted the importance of identifying resilient coral species and genotypes. new biomass is traditionally added to coral nurseries through acquiring either sexual recruits or wild collected corals of opportunity. however, keeping these corals in nurseries is labor intensive, and significant time and effort in coral care is often expended before practitioners select fit genotypes for incorporation into the restoration pipeline. this study aimed to identify coral traits indicative of resistance and/or resilience to environmental stressors through the evaluation of microbiomes and metabolomics during the collection, propagation, and grow-out of corals within an in-water nursery. colpophyllia natans and diploria labyrinthiformis corals of opportunity were collected from nearshore reefs in three regions of the florida keys. survival analysis identified differences according to genotype, collection location, and initial size. metabolomics showed significant differences initially by collection location with the upper keys site separating from the others. after 2 months in the coral nursery, metabolomes became more diverse as compared to initial collections, and the middle keys site now separated out. because of the location specific differences in survival and metabolomics, we are identifying specific metabolites that may be associated with survival. microbiome analysis revealed that communities were significantly influenced by genotypic differences and temporal variation. in addition, specific bacterial families were associated with survival in both c. natans and d. labyrinthiformis in the months after transport to the coral nursery. by identifying specific microbes and metabolites positively or negatively associated with coral survival, we can incorporate a new tool to select for potentially resilient corals for restoration.",
