          "name": "Rob Ruzicka",
          "search_text": "rob ruzicka thirty years of monitoring and how the 2023 marine heatwave and stony coral tissue loss disease changed coral reefs in the florida keys for three decades coral monitoring programs led by the florida fish & wildlife conservation commission have documented how numerous disturbances have altered the reefs in florida.  even after multiple thermal stress events, disease outbreaks, and harmful algal blooms, small populations of the most important framework building species still persisted.  however, during the last decade stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) and the 2023 marine heatwave have reshaped reef structure and function on florida\u2019s reefs.  scltd, considered the most lethal disease outbreak to ever occur in western tropical atlantic and caribbean, inflicted high rates of mortality on more than 20 coral species.  although the outbreak stage concluded in 2022, sctld ravaged florida reefs for eight years.  novel models we developed that integrated data from multiple long-term monitoring programs and focused on nine ecologically important, but vulnerable species to sctld, estimated that >75 million coral colonies were lost in florida.  the 2023 marine heatwave was also unprecedented, with seawater temperatures surpassing thresholds for coral bleaching and inducing heat-shock related mortality at levels never previously recorded.  as a result of these two destructive stressors,  the ecological status of several coral species in florida are now in peril.  two species, acropora palmata and dendrogyra cylindrus are now considered functionally extinct with very few colonies left in the wild, and several others have had their populations reduced by >90%.   this presentation will summarize these changes and the current state of florida\u2019s coral reef and showcase the novel models created from these long-term datasets  and visual products that illustrate changes over a 30-year time period.",
