      "search_text": "florida fish and wildlife conservation commission lindsay spiers rob ruzicka lindsay spiers harnessing coral resilience through nursery-to-reef restoration processes: the use of microbiome and metabolomic analyses as tools to optimize restoration the loss of corals in the wild and within coral nurseries in florida during the summer of 2023 highlighted the importance of identifying resilient coral species and genotypes. new biomass is traditionally added to coral nurseries through acquiring either sexual recruits or wild collected corals of opportunity. however, keeping these corals in nurseries is labor intensive, and significant time and effort in coral care is often expended before practitioners select fit genotypes for incorporation into the restoration pipeline. this study aimed to identify coral traits indicative of resistance and/or resilience to environmental stressors through the evaluation of microbiomes and metabolomics during the collection, propagation, and grow-out of corals within an in-water nursery. colpophyllia natans and diploria labyrinthiformis corals of opportunity were collected from nearshore reefs in three regions of the florida keys. survival analysis identified differences according to genotype, collection location, and initial size. metabolomics showed significant differences initially by collection location with the upper keys site separating from the others. after 2 months in the coral nursery, metabolomes became more diverse as compared to initial collections, and the middle keys site now separated out. because of the location specific differences in survival and metabolomics, we are identifying specific metabolites that may be associated with survival. microbiome analysis revealed that communities were significantly influenced by genotypic differences and temporal variation. in addition, specific bacterial families were associated with survival in both c. natans and d. labyrinthiformis in the months after transport to the coral nursery. by identifying specific microbes and metabolites positively or negatively associated with coral survival, we can incorporate a new tool to select for potentially resilient corals for restoration. rob ruzicka thirty years of monitoring and how the 2023 marine heatwave and stony coral tissue loss disease changed coral reefs in the florida keys for three decades coral monitoring programs led by the florida fish & wildlife conservation commission have documented how numerous disturbances have altered the reefs in florida.  even after multiple thermal stress events, disease outbreaks, and harmful algal blooms, small populations of the most important framework building species still persisted.  however, during the last decade stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) and the 2023 marine heatwave have reshaped reef structure and function on florida\u2019s reefs.  scltd, considered the most lethal disease outbreak to ever occur in western tropical atlantic and caribbean, inflicted high rates of mortality on more than 20 coral species.  although the outbreak stage concluded in 2022, sctld ravaged florida reefs for eight years.  novel models we developed that integrated data from multiple long-term monitoring programs and focused on nine ecologically important, but vulnerable species to sctld, estimated that >75 million coral colonies were lost in florida.  the 2023 marine heatwave was also unprecedented, with seawater temperatures surpassing thresholds for coral bleaching and inducing heat-shock related mortality at levels never previously recorded.  as a result of these two destructive stressors,  the ecological status of several coral species in florida are now in peril.  two species, acropora palmata and dendrogyra cylindrus are now considered functionally extinct with very few colonies left in the wild, and several others have had their populations reduced by >90%.   this presentation will summarize these changes and the current state of florida\u2019s coral reef and showcase the novel models created from these long-term datasets  and visual products that illustrate changes over a 30-year time period.",
