          "name": "Emma Garcia",
          "search_text": "emma garcia the growth, calcification, and photosynthetic yield of various species of crustose coralline algae crustose coralline algae (cca) are a diverse group of red algae that can calcify a calcium-carbonate skeleton, which contributes to the construction of reef frameworks. yet ccas are often underrepresented in benthic surveys and carbonate budget models due to their cryptic morphology and identification challenges. to gather a better understanding of the variations in cca calcification, we calculated calcification rates for eight species of cca.  to do so, we measured the initial length, width, and height (cm) of seven cca chips for eight morpho-species. the chips were photographed, and the planar surface area was measured using imagej after five weeks to calculate calcification rates. after the initial measurements, two species exhibited a rapid decline, characterized by pigment loss. these samples were later identified through molecular analysis as belonging to the genus neogoniolithon, which is typically found in areas of higher light intensity. to test the effects of light availability on growth and photosynthesis, half of the chips were placed in a high-light environment (141 par), and the other half in a low-light environment (44 par). photosynthetic efficiency, quantified as the maximum quantum yield (fv/fm), will be measured using pulse-amplitude modulation (pam) fluorometry. as this is ongoing research, we hypothesize that specimens 4 and 8, belonging to the genus neogoniolithon, will exhibit greater performance under high-light conditions, while specimen 10, belonging to the genus harveylithon, is expected to perform better under low-light conditions. overall growth and calcification for each tile will be remeasured at the end of the light trials. this research fills a knowledge gap regarding species-specific physiological responses to light availability and can help develop more accurate reef carbonate budget models.  further research is encouraged into how ccas can contribute to understanding reef frameworks, resilience, and potential changes in reef growth.",
