          "name": "Farah Oyhenart",
          "search_text": "farah oyhenart sediment trapping mechanisms by long sediment-laden algal turf on coral long sediment-laden algal turfs (lsats) are increasingly recognized as a significant barrier to coral recruitment across the florida reef tract. lsats are mats of upright turf algal assemblages that trap and hold sediment within their filaments. these sediment-rich mats can occupy large areas of degraded reefs and are associated with reduced juvenile coral abundance.   this suggests that sediment-turf interactions may suppress the recovery of corals on reefs. however, few studies have tested whether the dynamics between lsat length and sediment retention can be replicated in a laboratory setting. to evaluate the link between turf height and sediment retention, we conducted a controlled experiment examining how artificial turf length influences sediment retention under standardized flow conditions. we constructed five artificial turf mats ranging from 0 to 15 mm (0, 2, 5, 10, and 15 mm) in height based on previous field observations. using field-based sediment/depth relationships from duran et al. (2024), we estimated the corresponding sediment depth for each turf length. each trial in the experiment involved dispersing the sediment load onto the corresponding turf length, allowing a consistent, one-minute flow to pass over it, and weighing both the retained and the lost sediment. sediment retention increased with turf height, with the 10 and 15mm length retaining the most sediment. inversely, the 0-2 mm turf height retained the least sediment after exposure to the simulated current. our findings demonstrate that the functional relationship between turf height and sediment retention observed in florida reefs can be replicated under controlled conditions in a laboratory setting. because lsat has been shown to inhibit larval settlement and juvenile survival, our results provide ex situ support for turf-driven sediment trapping as a barrier to coral settlement. this establishes a foundation for future studies focused on sedimentation-suppressing coral settlement and on management strategies.",
