          "name": "Robert Tomasetti",
          "search_text": "robert tomasetti correcting carbonate bias in calcifying reef algal stoichiometry: global c:n:p trends over five decades calcifying macroalgae are vital to coral reefs, contributing to carbonate production, food webs, stabilization, and coral settlement. while calcification rates are well studied, stoichiometric datasets often overestimate organic carbon in calcifiers by reporting total carbon, conflating organic and inorganic fractions. we analyzed a reef-only global dataset of benthic seaweed stoichiometry (1975\u20132025), applying scaling factors derived from literature values and loss-on-ignition analyses (n = 152) to correct calcifying taxa for inorganic carbon. our dataset included 3,104 samples (985 new from south florida) across 55 regional reefs (263 sites) and 102 genera (18 calcifying). after correction, median molar c:n:p ratios were 1032:58:1 overall, 652:51:1 for calcifiers, and 1397:64:1 for non-calcifiers, indicating elevated carbon and nitrogen relative to phosphorus on reefs compared to global historical baselines (550:31:1) and modern observations (801:40:1) including non-reefs. non-calcifying taxa incorporated significantly more carbon and nitrogen relative to phosphorus than calcifiers. non-parametric tests confirmed significant stoichiometric differences between groups for c:n (p < 0.0001), c:p (p < 0.0001), and n:p (p < 0.001). temporal trends revealed a significant decline in c:n for calcifiers (p < 0.001) but no significant changes in c:p or n:p, while non-calcifiers showed steep increases in c:p and n:p over five decades (p < 0.001), consistent with rising nitrogen relative to phosphorus on reefs. these patterns suggest fleshy macroalgae could outperform calcifiers under nitrogen enrichment, potentially accelerating reef community phase shifts and reducing calcifier abundance, with implications for reef resilience. organic carbon ranged from 15-87.64% in calcifiers, highlighting a 22\u2013667% overestimation of organic carbon in calcifiers in published datasets, underscoring carbonate bias in reef carbon budgets. however, given a high proportion of florida samples in our dataset, we stress the need for expanded stoichiometric data from reefs across diverse oceanic basins to better capture global variability and improve reef nutrient and carbon models.",
