          "name": "Silvana Guzman",
          "search_text": "silvana guzman persistent long sediment-laden algal turfs (lsat) reshape benthic structure and limit coral recruitment on florida\u2019s coral reef coral reefs in the caribbean have undergone profound ecological change over the last several decades, with florida\u2019s coral reef experiencing one of the steepest regional declines in coral cover (>75%). as structural complexity and carbonate production diminish, benthic communities have reorganized around alternative states, including long sediment-laden algal turfs (lsat), dense, sediment-rich turf systems that may inhibit coral recovery but remain poorly quantified. here, we integrate seasonal surveys, habitat complexity metrics, and experimental manipulations to resolve the spatiotemporal dynamics of lsat and their ecological consequences across six sites spanning the florida reef tract. lsat occupied approximately 40% of benthic substrates and exhibited substantial seasonal variation, with both percent cover and sediment depth peaking in summer and winter. habitat attributes strongly modulated lsat development: flatter, low-rugosity surfaces accumulated deeper sediments and supported thicker lsat assemblages, while more complex microhabitats tended to resist lsat formation. juvenile coral presence declined sharply with increasing lsat sediment depth, and logistic regression models revealed the strongest negative relationship during summer, coinciding with peak sediment loads. sediment-removal experiments further demonstrated rapid re-establishment of lsat within days, indicating a highly resilient and self-reinforcing benthic state. collectively, our findings identify lsat as a persistent, sediment-driven turf assemblage that reduces settlement-quality habitat for corals and may constrain reef-scale recovery trajectories. quantifying lsat\u2019s drivers and seasonal behavior provides critical insight into how sediment dynamics, topographic complexity, and turf assemblages interact to shape future reef resilience and highlights emerging management opportunities focused on mitigating sediment accumulation and restoring functional substrate conditions for coral recruitment.",
