          "name": "W. Ryan James",
          "search_text": "w. ryan james a system level assessment of trophic function reveals importance of shallow seagrass beds for coral reef fish coral reefs exist within an interconnected mosaic of habitats, and reef fish rely on this habitat mosaic for foraging. identifying areas within the habitat mosaic of high resource quality can improve the design and inform the location of marine protected areas (mpa). however, due to the reliance of multiple energetic resources, it can be difficult to identify areas within the seascape with high resource quality, especially since these areas can differ between species. in this study, we constructed energetic resource landscapes, or e-scapes, for four mesoconsumer reef fish to identify areas of high resource quality across the florida keys. e-scapes combine stable isotope analysis and habitat maps to generate consumer specific maps of energetic resource distribution and trophic function. we found that for all species, both reefs and shallow water seagrass beds displayed high trophic function, but this was not the case for deeper seagrass beds near reefs. results from this study indicate that not all seagrass beds provide the same energetic resources for reef fish, and this should be incorporated into mpa design.",
