          "name": "Eduardo Islas-Dominguez",
          "search_text": "eduardo islas-dominguez \"suppressed reef accretion\" of submerged keep-up reefs in bermuda: an analogous model of past and future reef accretion? the ongoing threat to thriving coral reefs is currently attributed to several factors, including ocean warming, acidification, pollution, and disease outbreaks. consequently, conventional reef development models may not accurately predict how reefs will respond in the future. in the atlantic, non-traditional reef settings have been largely overlooked as potential analogues for past and future reef development. this is the case for bermuda, an isolated atoll-like platform, which is located at 32\u00b0n. rotary drill cores and vibracores have been extracted and analysed in detail. key drivers of holocene reef development indicate a \u201csuppressed accretion\u201d mode. the reefs of bermuda exhibit a steady vertical accretion in the holocene with an average rate of 2.18 \u00b1 0.66 m/kyr, significantly lower than the reported average rates of 3\u20134 m/kyr for western atlantic reefs. the \u201csuppressed accretion\u201d mode is a complex process that reflects the combined influence of multiple factors, including the composition of the coral fauna (dominated by massive, stress-tolerant taxa and lacking competitive acroporids), environmental energy, sediment flux, bioerosion, taphonomic framework loss, and sea-level rise. together, these circumstances result in the formation of reefs that have remained permanently submerged for several metres, establishing a submerged keep-up relationship between the living reefs and sea level throughout their entire development. in contrast to western atlantic reefs, which commonly show a declining trend in accretion rates throughout the mid to late holocene, the continuously submerged nature of bermuda reefs has prevented them from undergoing the same decline, enabling virtually uninterrupted accretion. the development model emerging from these mid to late holocene reefs provides a valuable analogue for understanding reef responses under comparable environmental conditions and circumstances, whether in the geological past or in an increasingly uncertain future.",
