      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Natalie Scott",
          "search_text": "natalie scott try, fail, learn: lessons learned and adaptations in year 1 of guam coral reef initiative\u2019s pilot coral nursery guam hosts a remarkably diverse coral community, with approximately 400 species of stony corals forming reefs that provide coastal protection and essential ecosystem and economic services. restoration has become a crucial component of local coral reef management as increasingly frequent bleaching events, extreme weather conditions, and other disturbances have pushed guam\u2019s reefs past the point of natural recovery. in june 2024, guam coral reef initiative\u2019s (gcri) pilot in situ coral nursery was installed in the piti bomb holes marine preserve to support ongoing restoration efforts by incorporating a wide variety of coral morphologies and species, with the goal of enhancing reef resilience by targeting functional and genetic diversity. the first year of this project focused on establishing best practices for nursery husbandry and propagation of six understudied coral species and evaluating species-specific preferences and nursery success by monitoring survivorship, health, and growth metrics. as a small program in a remote location, gcri faced many challenges due to limited capacity, complex reef ecology, lack of resources, limited data availability regarding various coral species, and several unexpected impacts. here, we highlight several of these challenges and lessons learned, supported by relevant data products and analyses, and gcri's resulting adaptations and management outcomes. topics will include creative low-cost solutions, variable successes of experimental propagation methods, nursery structure modifications, and addressing health impacts such as bleaching, sedimentation, and predation. during the planning process, gcri encountered challenges finding detailed information on project methods, logistics, species selection, and successes or failures of other indo-pacific projects, indicating a need for more regional knowledge exchange and collaboration. gcri aims to promote the open discussion of challenges unique to the pacific islands and the sharing of failures, lessons learned, and unique methodologies between practitioners, especially as restoration efforts continue scaling up amid increasing local stressors and climate impacts.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Try, Fail, Learn: Lessons learned and adaptations in year 1 of Guam Coral Reef Initiative\u2019s pilot coral nursery",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "f6251ce7-2c57-4008-ae54-a60d509ff5db"
            },
            {
              "title": "Winners and Losers: Trait Tradeoffs in Disease Response and Growth of Pacific Corals in Restoration",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "2c66793a-0915-4a66-a27c-6ec028532011"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Remedy Rule",
          "search_text": "remedy rule winners and losers: trait tradeoffs in disease response and growth of pacific corals in restoration guam hosts over 5000 marine species, including over 400 species of corals. culturally and economically important, guam\u2019s coral reefs support traditional chamoru fishing practices, attract hundreds of thousands of tourists annually, and provide coastline protection. despite their high value, these reefs have experienced extensive declines from local and global stressors to the point where active restoration is needed. to address this decline, in june of 2024, the guam department of agriculture\u2019s coral reef initiative (gcri) established a new coral nursery in piti bomb holes marine preserve (pbhmp) focusing on six understudied species. early assessments revealed notable differences in survivorship and growth across species and genotypes. acropora latistella emerged as one of the top performers, exhibiting rapid growth rates and nearly 100% survivorship. however, in july 2025, the pbhmp suffered an unprecedented poor water quality event, triggering a coral disease outbreak that killed hundreds of soft and stony corals within the preserve, including nursery corals. species and genotypic differences in susceptibility to disease were observed. notably, a. latistella was particularly susceptible with 38.6% of fragments succumbing to disease, significantly more than the other 5 restoration species. within a. latistella, genotype mortality ranged widely: 0 to 88.6%. given the considerable variability both within and between species, the importance of species and genotype diversity in reef restoration is highlighted. to explore the long-term effects of disease, survivorship and growth rates will be measured and compared with pre-disease values as well as with unaffected genotypes. understanding these differences is critical for improving nursery design, prioritizing resilient corals, and demonstrates that diverse assemblages are more likely to withstand disturbance events, thus maintaining long-term survival and growth of restored corals.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Winners and Losers: Trait Tradeoffs in Disease Response and Growth of Pacific Corals in Restoration",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "2c66793a-0915-4a66-a27c-6ec028532011"
            },
            {
              "title": "Try, Fail, Learn: Lessons learned and adaptations in year 1 of Guam Coral Reef Initiative\u2019s pilot coral nursery",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "f6251ce7-2c57-4008-ae54-a60d509ff5db"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 2,
      "talk_count": 2,
