          "search_text": "halwi the influence of environmental variables and habitat heterogeneity on echinoderm diversity in two regions of eastern indonesia echinoderms play a significant ecological role in coral reef dynamics, such as grazing algae and cycling nutrients. in addition to being valuable contributors to ecosystem function, their sensitivity to habitat conditions and environmental stressors also makes them bioindicators of reef health. despite increasing recognition of the importance of environmental factors in shaping benthic communities, the specific relationships between echinoderm diversity, benthic composition, and benthic habitat structure remain poorly understood. in this study, we assessed the relationships between echinoderm diversity, benthic composition, and habitat structure across 30 sites in the maluku and southeast sulawesi provinces of indonesia. we counted 2084 individual echinoderms from 42 species in replicate belt transects, assessed benthic composition from photo quadrats, and derived habitat heterogeneity metrics from satellite imagery. we used generalised linear mixed models (glmm) and non-parametric multi-dimensional scaling (nmds) to investigate differences in echinoderm diversity and community composition across benthic and habitat heterogeneity gradients. the glmm revealed that echinoderm diversity increased significantly with increases in algal diversity and mean habitat heterogeneity. in contrast, live coral cover, rubble, and provincial differences did not have any effect on echinoderm diversity and abundance. nmds revealed echinoderm community composition also changes with relative abundance of key benthic characteristics, with genera linckia and choriaster associated with greater sand and rubble cover and phanogenia and ophiomastix increasing with more coral cover. these findings highlight that both habitat structural complexity and diversity of food resources are primary drivers shaping echinoderm diversity.",
