          "name": "Abigail Turnlund",
          "search_text": "abigail turnlund unravelling the role of crustose coralline algae microbiomes on coral larval settlement in the great barrier reef crustose coralline algae (cca) are an important source of coral larval settlement cues; however, settlement success varies across cca species and it remains unclear whether these cues originate from the host and/or the cca microbiome. furthermore, cca microbiomes are species-specific, so potential microbial settlement cues could differ between different cca. to investigate the presence and potential commonality of cca-associated microbial cues, we characterized the microbiomes of 14 coralline algal species and a calcareous non-coralline alga that induced settlement for 14 great barrier reef coral species with varying levels of success. we used a suite of community analyses like linear regression, differential abundance, indicator species, and random forest analyses to identify amplicon sequence variants (asvs) associated with coral larval settlement. we found asvs that correlated with settlement for each coral species and were largely coral species-specific.  in addition to potential coral species-specific microbial inducers or inhibitors, we found asvs associated with high or low settlement that were shared between the corals dipsastrea favus, echinophyllia aspera, lobophyllia corymbosa, mycedium elephantotus, and platygrya sinensis, suggesting common settlement inducing or inhibiting cues.  specifically, candidatus nitrosopumilus sp. and filomicrobium sp. were found as potential shared inducers and asvs within the pirellulaceae and flavobacteriacae families were identified as potential inhibitors. shared asvs associated with high coral settlement were typically found across multiple cca species, while asvs associated with low settlement were generally confined to low settlement cca species (e.g., lithothamnion cf. proliferum and amphiroa cf. foliacea). these findings add insight into the identity of potential microbial taxa associated with settlement cues and what constitutes an attractive substrate to encourage coral recruitment. utilizing profiling techniques to characterize coral symbiont communities in four corals across the caribbean stress resistance and resilience of coral holobionts depends not only on the coral host\u2019s ability to mitigate and respond to stressors,      but also on the interaction with their endosymbiotic zooxanthellae.  while most coral species associate with specific algal species (symbiodiniaceae), these associations can vary and influence the thermal stress performance of the holobiont. as part of a global project investigating the short-term heat stress response of corals using standardized assays, we sought to characterize symbiont taxonomic profiles for four coral species across three locations in the caribbean. we utilized complementary methods to construct these profiles. first, we pcr-amplified and sequenced the its-2 marker gene to generate profiles with the symportal analytical framework. second, we generated transcriptomes using rna-seq and retrieved its-2 sequences from the cdna libraries. by comparing these concurrent symbiont profiling strategies, we assess the validity of transcriptome-generated profiles and the potential underestimation of symbiont diversity masked by amplification biases in the pcr-based approach. while the generation of such profiles allowed for the characterization of inter- and intra-specific genotypic variation in the symbiont assemblages, the high dimensionality of the resulting data poses difficulties for quantifying compositional change. we therefore applied diffusion maps, a manifold learning method, to reduce the dimensionality of the data and identified the main dimensions of the compositional variation. by exploring the application of complementary methods to characterize symbiont assemblages and novel methods to quantify compositional shifts, we hope to improve the characterization of coral-symbiont associations from existing data to inform further stress-tolerance experiments, field surveys, and reef restoration practitioners. comparing crustose coralline algae microbiome manipulation methods and subsequent coral larval settlement response microbial symbiosis is prevalent on coral reefs, and a multitude of reef organisms, like corals and sponges, rely on their microbiome for nutrition, defense, and to boost environmental resilience, among other functions. like corals, crustose coralline algae (cca) are reef builders that have unique and species-specific microbial communities. the cca holobiont is an important source of reef invertebrate larval settlement cues, including cues for coral larvae. cca-associated microbial communities alter their composition when temperature and ph change, however, our understanding of how that changes coral larval settlement cue production in the caribbean is in its infancy. this study aims to compare microbiome manipulations and microbial sampling strategies to inform future cca-microbial symbiosis experimentation design. specifically, we tested two different antibiotic combinations, antibiotic conditioning time (24 h vs. 48 h), and microbial sampling technique (swabs vs. whole cca chip) with the aim to reduce the cca-associated microbial community of a caribbean cca species. we will utilize 16s amplicon sequencing to document changes in the cca-associated microbial community. furthermore, we tested the effectiveness of antibiotic treatment on larval settlement of the coral, orbicella faveolata by performing a no-choice coral larval settlement assay with cca chips conditioned for 48 hours in both antibiotic combinations, positive, and negative controls. we found that coral larval settlement significantly differed between cca treated with antibiotic combination 1 for 48 hours (6.0 \u00b113.4%) and untreated cca controls (55.5 \u00b1 27.4%; p<0.05). metagenomic sequencing of the cca chips used in the experiment is ongoing to explore differences in microbial functional profiles among the two antibiotic treatments and the controls. this study\u2019s results will help inform future experimental methods that could probe how the cca host and associated microbial communities interact to affect host functioning, environmental resilience, and larval settlement cue pathways.",
