          "name": "Nils R\u00e4decker",
          "search_text": "nils r\u00e4decker breakdown of host nutrient control fuels symbiont guanine crystals formation during heat stress the collapse of the cnidarian\u2013algal symbiosis during bleaching is driven by a breakdown of mutualistic nutrient cycling that ultimately results in host starvation. yet the cellular processes underlying both the destabilization and the potential recovery remain poorly resolved. here, we combine physiological measurements, proteomics, raman microscopy, and a newly developed cryonanosims workflow to characterize the heat-stress response in the aiptasia model system at subcellular resolution. we show that heat stress induces a gradual loss of host control over nitrogen availability, leading to excess nitrogen supply to algal symbionts. cryonanosims imaging reveals that symbionts rapidly assimilate host-derived catabolic nutrients to support their own growth, coinciding with a reduction in chloroplast volume and a collapse of carbon translocation to the host. furthermore, we find that symbionts develop large nutrient-storage vacuoles\u2013occupying up to 50% of the cell volume\u2013that are enriched in essential elements and contain immense nitrogen reserves in the form of crystalline guanine. these guanine stores facilitate rapid symbiont proliferation following bleaching, thereby promoting recovery of the symbiosis. taken together, our results identify algal nutrient storage as a critical mechanism of post-bleaching resilience in cnidarian\u2013algal partnerships.",
