      "search_text": "hong kong polytechnic university james kh fang james kh fang bioaccumulation of microplastics in stony corals from hong kong stony corals have been proposed as potential long-term sinks for microplastics. in this study, we examined microplastics in coral skeletons, coral tissue, and surrounding sediments, collected from hong kong. our results indicate that the majority of microplastics within corals were embedded in the skeletons (~70%), compared with ~30% found in the tissue. coral skeletons contained up to ten times more microplastics per unit volume than adjacent sediments. smaller plastic particles predominated across all sample types, with polyethylene (pe), polyethylene terephthalate (pet), polypropylene (pp), and polystyrene (ps), identified as the most common polymers. using hong kong as a case study, these findings support the view that coral skeletons act as long-term biogenic sinks for microplastics, in line with observations from other regions.",
