          "name": "Jamie Craggs",
          "search_text": "jamie craggs decoding environmental factors responsible for phased shifted ex situ broadcast coral spawning, to inducing multiple out of season events per year. in 2012, the horniman museum and gardens began developing techniques to induce predictable ex situ broadcast coral spawning events through a programme known as project coral. a novel mesocosm system was designed to facilitate induced spawning and is now widely used around the world to support coral research and reef restoration initiatives. this mesocosm design presents unique opportunities to investigate the environmental conditions responsible for triggering coral reproductive events.\r\n\r\nbroadcast coral spawning correlates strongly with several environmental signals, including seasonal temperature, solar irradiance, and lunar and diel cycles. however, few robust experimental studies have examined the individual roles of these putative cues in initiating spawning. in situ, many of these parameters exhibit strong collinearity, making it difficult to determine which factor exerts the greatest influence on seasonal spawning. as a result, significant debate remains in the scientific literature. a reliable and highly controllable ex situ mesocosm system therefore provides a unique opportunity to investigate phase-shifted spawning at various temporal scales, enabling multiple reproductive events within a single year and increasing access to gamete material for experimentation.\r\n\r\nhere, we present a three-year phase-shifting broadcast coral spawning study (2019\u20132021) using the reef-building corals acropora millepora and galaxea fascicularis. this study explores the role of sunlight dynamics and temperature regimes in controlling out-of-season spawning events.\r\n\r\nin addition, we discuss examples of manipulating the reproductive seasons of corals from different geographical regions to explore the potential for inter-geographical hybridisation, and how this approach may help us better understand the boundaries of reproductive isolation between species. securing a future for the worlds most endangered coral: the chagos brain coral, ctenella chagius the chagos archipelago in the indian ocean is among the world\u2019s most remote coral reef systems, yet it has been severely affected by anthropogenic climate change. although many sites have shown signs of recovery after repeated bleaching events, certain species have experienced dramatic declines. most notable is the endemic chagos brain coral, ctenella chagius, once considered one of the archipelago\u2019s most abundant corals. by 2019, fewer than 50 individuals were recorded across multiple scientific expeditions.\r\n\r\nwith model predictions for a strong 2023 el ni\u00f1o event indicating a heightened risk to the species\u2019 survival, an emergency expedition was launched by zsl and the university of oxford, with support from the horniman museum and gardens and the mus\u00e9e oc\u00e9anographique de monaco. surveys carried out at two atoll, diego garcia and middle brother, resulted in the identification of 121 distinct c. chagius genotypes, representing the current known global population. each colony was photogrammetrically documented and geolocated.\r\n\r\ntwelve colonies were transported to spawning facilities at the horniman, london. these were used to study the species\u2019 reproductive biology for the first time and begin developing a species-level conservation recovery plan. because of the rarity of c. chagius and the need to avoid invasive sampling, six additional coral species (leptoria irregularis, leptoria phrygia, platygyra daedalea, platygyra crosslandi, acropora lorpies, and acropora desalwii) were collected as proxies to establish the region\u2019s broad reproductive windows.\r\n\r\nafter two consecutive years of successful ex situ spawning (march\u2013april 2024 and february\u2013may 2025), c. chagius was confirmed to be a brooding species. these efforts have provided critical insight into its reproductive traits and produced more than 100 f1 juveniles. asexual fragments of the parent colonies and equal numbers of juveniles have been distributed among partner institutions, creating a secure biobank and significantly improving the prospects for the species\u2019 long-term survival.",
