          "name": "Jane Cita Syafitri Sudrajat",
          "search_text": "jane cita syafitri sudrajat coral restoration drives rapid shifts in the functional structure of reef fish communities active coral restoration is being increasingly used across the tropics to combat damage to reef ecosystems. however, most restoration projects to date have primarily used live coral cover to monitor their success, with few projects or studies investigating how reef fish communities respond to coral restoration. reef fish, especially those closely associated with live coral, are vulnerable to disturbance and coral loss, making them essential indicators of restoration success. recently, functional trait-based approaches have offered a framework for assessing ecological communities' responses to habitat change. here, we evaluate reef fish community composition using a functional traits approach to determine the community-level impacts of coral restoration. working on one of the world's largest coral restoration projects, located on bontosua island, south sulawesi, indonesia, we compared the composition of reef fish communities across healthy, degraded, and restored habitats. our findings demonstrate that coral restoration has led to increases in coral cover, fish abundance, and the number of functional entities (fes) of fish. individual fes exhibited varying abundance patterns across habitat types and restoration stages: some fes showed consistent increases over time post-restoration, while others peaked at intermediate stages of restoration or declined over time. these varied responses highlight that restoration does not lead to positive responses from all fish functional groups, but creates \u2018winners\u2019 and \u2018losers\u2019, depending on species\u2019 traits. understanding these dynamics is essential for holistic evaluation of restoration success, and for designing strategies that help to restore diverse and functionally rich fish communities.",
