      "search_text": "institut de recherche pour le developpement aline tribollet catherine sabinot aline tribollet bioeroding ostreobium algae promote scleractinian coral larval recruitment carbonates colonized by benthic microboring algae are dominant substrates of settlement for coral larvae in today\u2019s degraded reefs. yet the impacts of microbioeroding filamentous ostreobineae (bryopsidales) on scleractinian larval settlement and recruit fitness are unknown. here, reef-collected stylophora pistillata coral planula larvae were exposed in vivo to natural ostreobium-containing microboring assemblages. these experiments were completed by in vitro exposure of aquarium released larvae of the laboratory model pocillopora acuta to monoalgal bioeroding biofilms formed by four ostreobium strains of the (reef-detected) rbcl p1 lineage, or their crude organic extracts. metamorphosed larvae, development profiles and post-settlement clonal polyp budding were measured across short (3-5 days) to long-term (20-77 days) periods. besides exposure time \u2018s influence on larval metamorphosis, we demonstrated a positive influence of ostreobium-dominated microboring communities, which was confirmed for monoalgal biofilms of three of the four tested ostreobium strains and their organic extracts, suggesting chemical communication between beneficial microbioeroding ostreobiaceae and coral larvae. additionally, exposure to ostreobium accelerated the clonal budding of coral recruits, with a strain-specific response. these findings reveal the critical importance of ostreobium sp. in a key life cycle transition of scleractinian corals, with perspectives in bioeroding microbiome engineering of substrates to promote larval settlement.\r\n\r\nkeywords: ostreobium sp., microboring algae, carbonate, coral, metamorphosis, settlement, polyp budding catherine sabinot from totems to ancestor species to engage shifts in views, practices, and structures recent studies in new caledonia, including questionnaire surveys of over 500 people and semi-structured interviews with almost 200 individuals, have demonstrated the significance that the population of the country attributes to marine species. a total of 132 marine animals were spontaneously described as emblematic, with many of these being recognised as totems for kanak clans, making them key species from an oceanian perspective!\r\nthe term 'totem' is familiar to everyone in oceania. this polysemous term originated in the americas several centuries ago and has since been adopted by many oceanic language speakers. however, its multiple meanings mean it is sometimes misunderstood, particularly in the field of ocean management, producing an essentialising and simplified view of the world. after describing the diversity of the country's emblematic animals and the reasons why inhabitants define them as such, this paper will analyse the term 'totem' and its recent developments in new caledonia, with a particular focus on the role of a group of customary oceanic referents in the emergence of the term 'ancestral species', offering a way to achieve transformative change in environmental management. we will analyse its gradual appropriation in the management policy of the coral sea natural park, thereby reinforcing socially and culturally informed conservation.we will examine how this semantic change reflects a progressive shift in views, structures and practices aimed at achieving a just and sustainable world.",
