      "search_text": "institute of zoology catherine head catherine head coral connectivity across remote reef systems in the indian ocean connectivity of coral populations is critical for coral recovery following disturbances such as bleaching events. consequently, understanding reef connectivity and integrating source/sink dynamics into area and network-based management is key to effective management, particularly for remote and isolated reef systems. remote reefs in the western and central indian ocean have been impacted by multiple bleaching events but have also exhibited high capacity for recovery, and therefore provide a model system to assess the mechanism by which connectivity between isolated reef systems influences resilience of coral populations. here, we investigate the genomic population structure of a species of the important reef-building coral acropora (acropora aff. tenuis) across six locations in the chagos, seychelles, and iles glorieuses islands in the central-western indian ocean. \r\n\r\nusing a combination of target enrichment of ultra-conserved elements (uces) from collected coral specimens, and particle tracking simulations from the region to understand the influence of ocean currents on the population structure, we show that gene flow between all six locations is asymmetrical: connectivity between chagos and the inner seychelles is particularly strong, but we also detected a potentially permeable dispersal barrier between the inner and outer seychelles.\r\n\r\nthese connectivity patterns are only partially explained by the ocean current system. we discuss potential causation for these findings, including sub-mesoscale oceanographic processes, stepping-stone effects, and the timing and duration of spawning events, as well as implications for the wider indian ocean. overall, our results indicate strong connectivity between reef systems, supporting the hypothesis that connectivity has enhanced recovery capacity from previous disturbances. however, the ability of these reefs to recover from future bleaching events will depend on the frequency and severity of future events.",
