          "name": "Johanna Gijsbers Alejandre",
          "search_text": "johanna gijsbers alejandre undescribed host diversity and symbiodiniaceae associations in the genus leptoseris at mesophotic depths mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces) have gained interest because of both their refuge potential and their ability to host unique and distinct biological communities. in terms of reef-building corals (order scleractinia), leptoseris is among the most dominant genera at lower mesophotic depths throughout the indo-pacific. interestingly, based on conventional taxonomy, many of the reported species at mesophotic depths appear to be depth-generalists; however, detailed molecular studies targeting mesophotic members of these genera remain limited. while traditional molecular markers have helped delineate species in both genera, assessments have been hindered by their limited resolution; these can now be conducted in greater detail using reduced-representation genome sequencing approaches. here, we provide a phylogenomic assessment of the dominant mesophotic species in the genus leptoseris (based on nextrad sequencing) and their associated endosymbionts (through its2 amplicon sequencing). all nominal species were successfully resolved; however, substantial undescribed diversity was uncovered across three of the four focal species, concerning sympatrically occurring and mesophotic-associated lineages. species delimitation analyses confirmed the presence of several undescribed species, which are now awaiting further taxonomic assessment. regarding the endosymbiont community, we observed almost exclusive association with the genus cladocopium, comprising 17 its2 profiles grouped into four clusters. although symbiont types were shared across depths, statistical analyses indicated a certain degree of host-symbiont specificity and significant variations influenced by geographical and ecological factors. overall, this study sheds light on the hidden diversity of this ecologically important genus. first genome-wide assessment of the etp endemic pavona gigantea: unraveling vertical and horizontal connectivity in the mexican pacific the mass bleaching event of 2023 caused devastating damage in the mexican pacific, particularly in southern regions like huatulco, oaxaca, where some reefs experienced extremely high mortality rates (50-93%). the endemic pavona gigantea was among the most severely affected species; therefore, understanding the environmental cues and genetic adaptations driving its resilience is critical for the management and conservation of the species.\r\nthis study provides the first reefscape genome-wide assessment of p. gigantea, testing the hypothesis that environmental heterogeneity defines horizontal and vertical connectivity across reefs in southern mexico. using high-resolution environmental profiling, reduced-representation sequencing, and biophysical dispersal modeling, we assessed the genetic connectivity of this species in the bays of huatulco and investigated the ecological drivers that may have influenced p. gigantea survival rates during the recent mass bleaching event. despite the limited bathymetric range and area of these reefs, preliminary analyses reveal unexpectedly high environmental complexity. distinct hydrographic zones, driven largely by oxygen and thermal gradients, create depth-dependent microrefugia that may act as barriers to gene flow and promote local adaptation. overall, these results reveal how environmental gradients shape genomic structure and resilience in p. gigantea, offering critical insight into how coral populations may persist in the rapidly changing eastern tropical pacific.",
