          "search_text": "najatul su ad abdullah environmental dna reveals fish biodiversity patterns across coral reefs and marine ecosystems in pahang coastal waters, malaysia coral reef ecosystems in malaysia are increasingly threatened by climate change impact and anthropogenic pressures, yet fish biodiversity patterns remain poorly characterised due to the limitations of conventional survey methods. environmental dna (edna) metabarcoding offers a sensitive and non-invasive alternative for detecting cryptic and low-abundance species. this study applied 12s rrna metabarcoding to examine spatial and depth-structured patterns of reef fish diversity across 5 offshore stations and 5 coral reef sites in pahang coastal waters. a total of 38 water samples were collected across different depth strata and processed using oxford nanopore (ont) promethion sequencing platform available on oceanxplorer research vessel. after dna extraction and quality filtering, 19 samples yielded robust amplifications, detecting over 900 fish taxa spanning over 100 families. fish assemblages showed clear spatial differentiation, with coral reef site mbes1bw exhibiting the highest species richness (406 species) and distinct community composition, while lowest species richness (126 species) were observed at open water site ox5. notably, several taxa of conservation or fisheries importance, including predominant yellowtail fusilier (caesio cunning), snappers (lutjanidae), and butterflyfishes (chaetodontidae), were detected at various sites and depths. these findings demonstrate the effectiveness of edna metabarcoding for resolving fine-scale biodiversity patterns and provide essential baseline information to support coral reef management and monitoring in malaysia.",
