          "name": "Julia Klinges",
          "search_text": "julia klinges nature and nurture: harnessing performance variation to build heat-tolerant restoration pipelines in west hawai\u02bbi the year 2015, the warmest on record in west hawai\u2018i, led to widespread, severe coral bleaching causing a ~50% loss of coral cover in the region. projected ocean warming will cause severe coral bleaching similar to that experienced in 2015 on an annual basis by 2040 in west hawai\u2018i. the \u02bb\u0101ko\u02bbako\u02bba reef restoration program, a partnership between arizona state university (asu) and the hawai\u02bbi division of aquatic resources (dar), is poised to improve reef-wide thermal stress outcomes by integrating resilient coral genotypes into coral reproduction efforts on the west coast of hawai\u02bbi. as pocillopora meandrina is prone to bleaching yet provides essential fish habitat and is readily bred, we identified resilient lineages through stress testing in the summer of 2025. we evaluated thermal tolerance of pocillopora meandrina from 5 environmentally-distinct and community-prioritized sites from throughout west hawai\u02bbi to assess local adaptation to past stress. exposure of these corals to an ecologically-relevant warming regime produced a quantitative ranking of 58 genotypes of p. meandrina as well as assessments of genetic relatedness for a species with limited population genetics data. we also present preliminary data on the efficacy of coral stress-hardening using hypervariable temperature regimes to enhance coral temperature tolerance over measured thresholds. symbiont and host traits were correlated with historical site environmental parameters (e.g., benthic rugosity and other habitat characteristics, subsurface groundwater discharge and other water quality characteristics) to determine whether genetic identity or site history is the strongest predictor of resilience. stress-tested corals will be cultured and propagated at the ridge-to-reef restoration center and integrated into reproduction efforts conducted by asu, potentially producing millions of thermally-tolerant coral larvae. dar will use this data to develop guidelines for the integration of resilience interventions into restoration efforts in west hawai\u02bbi.",
