          "name": "Charlotte Dale",
          "search_text": "charlotte dale traditional taxonomy has obscured the diversity and biogeography of acropora in the western indian ocean the western indian ocean (wio) possesses a biogeographically significant history, shaped by the closure of the tethys sea and semi-permeable dispersal barriers, such as the mozambique channel. for the purposes of this study, we define the wio as encompassing the coastal and island areas of somalia, kenya, tanzania, mozambique, south africa, the comoros, madagascar, the mascarene islands, the seychelles, and the chagos archipelago. the geological legacy has driven high regional endemism in many marine taxa, including fishes, echinoderms, and molluscs. in contrast, the wio\u2019s scleractinian coral fauna has long been considered anomalous, being dominated almost entirely by species with widespread indo-pacific distributions, which may imply differences in life history or ecology. however, recent phylogenomic advances suggest that morphology-based coral taxonomy has underestimated species richness and provincialism, particularly in speciose genera such as acropora. according to current taxonomy and inferred geographic ranges, the wio contains approximately 35 to 40 valid acropora species, the vast majority of which are described as broadly distributed indo-pacific taxa, with an estimated two percent regional endemism. to reassess this framework, we used an integrated approach combining genomic-scale phylogenetics with comprehensive morphological character analysis. we reconstructed the most extensive phylogeny of wio acropora to date, comprising more than 300 specimens sampled across the region and complemented with material from the wider indo-pacific and red sea. our results fundamentally revise the known biogeography of wio acropora and find that currently accepted taxonomy likely inflates estimates of species richness in the indian ocean while underestimating regional endemism, because taxa restricted to the wio are misidentified as broadly distributed indo-pacific species. phylogenomic results indicate that approximately half of the acropora lineages in the wio are endemic. taxonomic recognition of this unique diversity is critical for accurate extinction-risk assessments and essential for evidence-based coral conservation, management, and restoration across the region.",
