          "name": "Eshna Kulkarni",
          "search_text": "eshna kulkarni investigating the relationship between environmental dna detectability and marine turtle presence green sea turtles are crucial keystone species for coral reef ecosystems, and with the species\u2019 recent status change from vulnerable to least concern, furthering conservation and monitoring efforts for the species is crucial. environmental dna (edna) detection has great potential to further sea turtle monitoring and management programs if we could determine species abundance and population sizes without relying solely on traditional physical or visual surveys. the knowledge gained from such endeavors would greatly benefit the species along with their coral reef ecosystems. \r\n\r\nthis study aims to further previous sea turtle edna research and investigate whether a correlation exists between organism abundance and edna amount shed. we examined if areas with more green sea turtle sightings, taken as a proxy for turtle abundance, contain larger amounts of turtle edna. \r\n\r\nwe conducted repeated field sampling at six marine sites around north queensland between october and december 2025. these included three sites where encountering turtles is highly likely, and three sites where turtle encounters are less likely. expected results from this study can hold great significance for sea turtle monitoring, specifically for providing alternate methods for traditional turtle monitoring techniques, and are expected to eventually lead to new breakthroughs in investigating sea turtle abundance and population density amidst coral reef habitats.",
