          "name": "Haley Davis",
          "search_text": "haley davis severe coral bleaching reduces post-recovery thermal performance of surviving acropora cervicornis genotypes in ex situ nursery in 2023 little cayman\u2019s coral nursery suffered extensive coral bleaching induced by the fourth global mass coral bleaching event. over 90% of acropora cervicornis in the nursery died, eradicating seven out of the ten genotypes present. it is hypothesized that exposure to sublethal thermal stress could acclimate corals to higher temperatures, making them more tolerant to future heatwaves. while this hypothesis has been tested in laboratory-based experiments, it is unclear if exposure to heatwave events renders surviving corals in the wild more or less vulnerable to subsequent events. to test this, we compared the thermal performance of surviving a. cervicornis genotypes measured pre-bleaching (april 2022) to post-bleaching (june 2024). our findings showed that the response to extreme heat waves was not unilateral, but rather each genotype had variable acclimatory responses. for example, genotype y showed a strongly decreased maximum photosynthetic yield (rmax) of gross primary productivity (gpp) after exposure to the heatwave compared to before. contrarily, genotype ob showed a similar rmax of gpp before and after the bleaching event. both genotypes had similar thermal optima of gpp across this timescale. taken together, these results suggest that the surviving a. cervicornis do not seem to become more thermally tolerant, or have higher thermal performance after being exposed to extreme heat stress; rather, these corals have genotype-specific responses with some maintaining similar thermal performance, and others having decreased photosynthetic yield. these findings underscore that thermal priming via natural exposure to marine heatwaves does not reliably enhance thermal tolerance in surviving corals and may, in some genotypes, exacerbate vulnerability to future climate-driven warming.",
