          "name": "Isabelle Ng",
          "search_text": "isabelle ng the evolution of habitat use and pectoral fins in wrasses and parrotfishes (family labridae) high aspect ratio pectoral fins serve the function of enhanced swimming performance and are thought to permit successful navigation in high flow environments in some reef-associated fishes. while the relationship between habitat and swimming performance has been long established across multiple fish families, it has been minimally investigated within an evolutionary framework. therefore, the main aim of this study was to examine the evolutionary link between different wave energy environments and pectoral fin aspect ratio in one of the most diverse and abundant families on reefs globally: the labridae. by applying phylogenetically informed data imputation techniques, we were able to map the evolution of habitat use and fin aspect ratio across 90% of the species in the most recent labridae phylogeny. phylogenetic comparative analyses revealed that the earliest, most probable transition towards high wave energy habitats occurred during the early miocene. evolutionary shifts in fin aspect ratio primarily occurred during the late miocene, suggesting that habitat transitions preceded morphological adaptations. overall, wrasses inhabiting different wave energy environments appear to be exposed to different regimes of natural selection. indeed, clustering of similar habitats corresponding to similar fin aspect ratios was observed in certain parts of the phylogeny. however, a moderate evolutionary correlation detected between wave energy and fin aspect ratio suggests that there are other influential variables. using linear models, we further examined the effect of habitat with other ecological variables, such as water column position and trophic guilds, on fin aspect ratio. fitting the models both with and without the phylogeny showed that most of the variation in fin aspect ratio is explained by phylogenetic relatedness. overall, this study highlights the importance of accounting for phylogenetic relationships in ecological research, by revealing that the connection between habitat and swimming performance in wrasses is largely explained by shared ancestry.",
