          "name": "Katharine Prata",
          "search_text": "katharine prata global macrogenetics reveals consistent reproductive mode differences in coral dispersal informing conservation quantifying coral larvae dispersal distances and effective population sizes are essential for predicting population persistence, recovery and adaptive capacity. using a meta-analysis of genetic and genomic datasets across major reef regions, we estimated dispersal distances (\u03c3) and local effective population sizes (nn) for diverse coral taxa and evaluated how these parameters vary with reproductive traits.  dispersal distances ranged from metres to >100 km and nn <100 to >10k, with reproductive mode being the dominant predictor for realised dispersal. brooding species typically dispersed over metres and exhibited small nn (~100), whereas broadcast spawners dispersed tens of kilometres and typically had larger nn (>200). a weak but significant positive relationship between \u03c3 and nn explained only 25% of the variation, indicating that demographic processes and life-history traits often decouple dispersal from local effective population sizes. these results have direct implications for conservation and restoration. dispersal estimates provide guidance on marine protected area size and spacing, the likelihood of demographic and evolutionary connectivity, and the design of restoration programs. integrating \u03c3 and nn with biophysical and eco-evolutionary models will strengthen predictions of population resilience under climate change. overall, our framework provides spatially appropriate, biologically grounded estimates of dispersal and effective population size that can inform management strategies aimed at sustaining coral reefs in a rapidly changing ocean.",
