          "name": "Leighton Levering",
          "search_text": "leighton levering crown-of-thorns starfish feeding rates and impact on reef-building corals suppressed by a mass bleaching event the great barrier reef (gbr) and other indo-pacific regions are increasingly subject to major acute disturbances, such as population irruptions of crown-of-thorns starfish (cots) and heat-induced coral bleaching. given their increasing incidence, acute disturbances are likely to occur simultaneously or successively, though effects of major acute disturbance interactions remain understudied. here, changes in the feeding behavior of cots during an emerging population irruption are examined at lizard island (northern gbr) amid the gbr-wide mass coral bleaching event in 2024. scooter-assisted large area diver-based surveys were conducted to investigate changes in cots density and feeding, and coral cover changes were examined using point-intercept transects. between 2023-2025, coral cover declined by 49%, most of which is attributable to the mass bleaching event. cots feeding rates significantly declined over the course of the study, with both number of coral colonies and combined tissue surface area consumed per starfish declining by over 40%. despite reduced feeding, cots density increased by 96% from 2023-2025. relative consumption of coral genera by cots was consistent during the study period, with acropora spp. making up >80% of cots diet throughout the study. the 2024 bleaching event may have reduced feeding rates and the ecological impact of individual cots; however, the longer-term effects of cots are likely to exacerbate the coral loss caused by mass coral bleaching, especially with continued increases in cots densities.",
