          "name": "Lucy Southworth",
          "search_text": "lucy southworth ecological impacts of the 2022 hunga tonga - hunga ha\u2019apai volcanic eruption on tonga\u2019s coral reefs the 2022 eruption of the hunga tonga\u2013hunga ha\u2019apai (hthh) submarine volcano in tonga produced the most powerful volcanic eruption on earth in over a century, generating atmospheric waves, tsunamis, and an extensive volcanic plume that blanketed much of tonga with ash. while terrestrial impacts were immediately apparent, the ecological consequences for nearshore coral reefs remain largely unknown. this study leverages pre- and post-eruption ecological surveys across 107 sites spanning the northern (vava\u2019u), central (ha\u2019apai), and southern (tongatapu) island groups of tonga, including transect-based assessments of benthic assemblages, fish and invertebrate communities, and reef structural complexity to capture ecological impacts.\r\n\r\ninitial observations indicate extensive, spatially variable reef damage, with coral cover loss estimated at >50% in southern ha\u2019apai and tongatapu, formation of large rubble fields, extensive macroalgal blooms, and displacement of massive porites boulders. in contrast, northern reefs (vava\u2019u) appear largely unaffected. early signs of recovery include high coral recruitment on newly formed volcanic substrates and rapid colonization by fish species with limited dispersal, highlighting both the resilience of remote reef sites and the potential role of chronic human stressors in slowing recovery in inhabited areas.\r\n\r\nthis work demonstrates the value of integrated ecological monitoring for quantifying the magnitude and drivers of sudden, large-scale reef disturbance. by linking spatial patterns of reef damage and recovery to proximity to the eruption, environmental exposure, and human activity, these findings provide a rare natural experiment on the temporal dynamics of coral ecosystems and inform strategies for future reef monitoring, management, and resilience-building in island nations.",
