          "name": "Ma Victoria Stephane Asio",
          "search_text": "ma victoria stephane asio local knowledge matters: civil society\u2019s contribution to marine governance palawan, the largest of the 82 provinces in the philippines, comprises over 1,700 islands that host unique marine flora and fauna, making it one of the country\u2019s key marine biodiversity hotspots. given its island geography, residents\u2013particularly those living in coastal areas\u2013rely heavily on marine and coastal resources such as fish, mangroves, corals, and seagrass. however, these resources are increasingly under threat from overfishing, expanding tourism, climate-related risks, and coastal development. in response, higher governance bodies, particularly government agencies and non-government organisations, have tapped grassroots civil society organisations (csos), such as fisherfolk associations and cooperatives, to support local management and conservation of marine and coastal areas. often described as \u2018community watchdogs\u2019, these community-based organisations provide services that higher governance bodies may be unable to deliver, including monitoring marine protected areas (mpas), communicating local knowledge, and voicing perspectives of local and indigenous fishing communities. despite the critical support that csos can offer, in-depth understanding of their diverse roles in marine and coastal management remains limited. this study examines how grassroots csos in palawan contribute to marine management, focusing on the activities they implement, and how they become engaged in local governance processes. we draw on a qualitative analysis of focus group discussions (fgds) with grassroots csos in el nido, palawan. the fgds aimed to identify the roles and experiences of csos in marine management. our findings highlight several critical roles, including representing local community needs, advocating for marine management and conservation, and patrolling mpas. moreover, grassroots csos bring together local knowledge and relay it to higher levels of governance, ensuring community perspectives are included in broader decision-making. overall, the study identifies governance arrangements that can strengthen cso engagement in marine and coastal management in the philippines, while contributing to broader discussions on cso capacity and meaningful participation in local governance systems.",
