          "name": "Marine Pablo",
          "search_text": "marine pablo dynamics of endozoicomonas colonisation and aggregate formation across early coral life stages among the microbial partners associated with reef-building corals, bacteria of the family endozoicomonadaceae are globally prevalent and frequently occur within coral tissues as dense clusters known as coral-associated microbial aggregates (camas). although endozoicomonas associations can vary over the coral life cycle, current evidence from several coral species points to early larval stages as a key window for successful colonisation. newly settled recruits also appear permissive to colonisation, with recent experimental supplementation of acropora kenti with endozoicomonas acroporae inducing camas within host tissues. however, the precise timeframe and cellular mechanisms underlying endozoicomonas colonisation and subsequent cama formation during early development remain unclear. here, we inoculated acropora kenti larvae with native endozoicomonas acroporae across five key developmental stages from gastrula (36 h post-fertilisation) to early settled juveniles and monitored endozoicomonas colonisation and cama formation using fluorescent in situ hybridisation (fish). this detailed sampling approach enabled the selection of specific timepoints for transcriptomic analysis, comparing host responses to e. acroporae with those to the non-cama-forming halomonas smyrnensis. in addition, 16s rrna gene sequencing and fish were performed in parallel pre- and post-spawning to characterise the bacterial composition of parent colonies and assess the potential impact of spawning on camas within adult coral tissues. by resolving the temporal dynamics of endozoicomonas colonisation in early coral life stages and characterising host gene expression responses during colonisation and cama formation, this study provides new insights into early-life host\u2013microbe interactions in corals.",
