          "name": "Merle Schlawinsky",
          "search_text": "merle schlawinsky integrating edna and visual sightings to estimate the occupancy of the giant triton snail on the great barrier reef detecting rare reef species remains a major challenge for coral reef research and management. the giant triton snail (charonia tritonis), a large predatory gastropod and natural predator of crown-of-thorns starfish, occurs at extremely low densities across the great barrier reef (gbr), making traditional visual surveys challenging for assessing its distribution. here, occupancy of the giant triton snail across the gbr was estimated by integrating diver sighting records captured by the aims long-term monitoring and crown-of-thorns starfish control programs with environmental dna (edna) detections. a hierarchical bayesian occupancy framework was used to separate ecological occurrence from imperfect detection and to integrate both visual and edna-based observations. the spatial hierarchy of the sampling design (reef \u2192 site \u2192 transect/filter replicate) was explicitly accounted for, allowing inference across multiple scales. preliminary model outputs indicate that the edna method is a valuable complement to visual surveys, improving detection and revealing occurrences that may otherwise go undetected. this integration of molecular and traditional survey data provides the first quantitative assessment of giant triton occupancy across the gbr. the approach demonstrates how edna can extend the reach of existing monitoring programs, improve detection probability estimates, and inform conservation management of rare reef species critical to ecosystem balance.",
