          "name": "Momoe Yoshida",
          "search_text": "momoe yoshida toward a reliability metric for satellite-derived sea surface temperature for coral bleaching monitoring satellite sea surface temperature (sst) observations are essential for understanding ocean conditions, weather patterns and marine ecosystems. the noaa geo-polar blended sst (gpb) is a daily global dataset at 0.05\u00ba resolution, widely used in ocean forecasting, storm tracking, fisheries management and coral reef monitoring. this study focuses on the nighttime-only near real-time subset of gpb, which underpins noaa coral reef watch\u2019s bleaching monitoring tools. \r\n\r\nthe gpb integrates observations from multiple polar-orbiting and geostationary satellites. uncertainties arise from differences in measurement capabilities and processing algorithms across satellite data sources, as well as from the gap-filling technique used to produce a gap-free global field. these uncertainties can affect the reliability of sst values and, in turn, influence the reliability of coral bleaching alerts.\r\n\r\nhere, i present ongoing efforts to evaluate the reliability of the nighttime near real-time gpb dataset. specifically, i analyse key diagnostic variables available within the processing system, including but not limited to the quantity of measured temperature values from geostationary and polar-orbiting satellites (i.e., local data density); the spatial scale over which the gap-filling algorithm operates (which vary in space and time); and the recent temporal variability in sst. these variables form the foundation of a new reliability metric designed to highlight areas and periods of higher and lower confidence in the sst values. this work will support improved bleaching predictions to provide more informed decisions for coral reef conservation.",
