          "name": "Morgan Pratchett",
          "search_text": "morgan pratchett where have all the corals gone? increasing vulnerability and volatility in coral assemblages. coral reef ecosystems are increasingly subject to severe and large-scale disturbances, such as pan-tropical mass coral bleaching and mortality caused by the latest global coral bleaching event. increasingly severe disturbances are by definition less selective, such that the differential responses among corals with contrasting life histories relates more to their capacity for recovery rather than mortality rates. paradoxically, increasing incidence of major disturbances may lead to increasing predominance of coral species with opportunistic or regenerative (\u2018weedy\u2019) life history strategies, that are themselves highly vulnerable to major disturbances. ongoing disturbances then lead to highly pronounced boom-and-bust dynamics. this scenario is very different to the inexorable decline in live coral cover expected to occur with escalating disturbances, and the consequences for key ecosystem processes and future resilience are yet to be considered. this study presents evidence of shifts in coral assemblages, where there is increasing predominance of weedy coral species at several distinct locations throughout the indo-pacific and corresponding changes in vulnerability and volatility. critically, rapid increases and high levels of overall coral cover may belie fundamental changes in the underlying structure and vulnerability of coral assemblages, which will have consequences for ecosystem functions and services.",
