          "name": "Nicholas Ford-Learner",
          "search_text": "nicholas ford-learner supporting human rights as a transformative approach to coral reef conservation: the role of international conservation organisations international conservation organisations are well positioned to advance effective and equitable coral reef management by empowering coastal communities \u2013 many of whom rely on small-scale fisheries - to govern resources through secure rights and meaningful access to decision-making processes. a human rights-based approach to conservation, which focuses on protecting and promoting the basic human rights of small-scale fishers, can support just and effective stewardship of coral reefs. this research seeks to understand how international conservation organisations operating in coastal areas globally, support human rights principles through their policies and practices. while progress has been made, critical gaps remain in policy commitments by conservation organisations to certain rights, such as social development and decent work and recognizing local groups' rights and knowledge. case studies of conservation programs working in small-scale fisheries in colombia, indonesia, and kenya, also reveal that program effort needs to focus on improving the socioeconomic conditions of coastal communities, their rights to access reef resources, and equitable value chains of coral reef fisheries. given the multitude of challenge faced by small-scale fishing communities, it is imperative that conservation organisations utilise their position of influence to create a supportive environment for them, fostering sustainable development, economic viability, and improved social wellbeing.",
