          "name": "Rachel Neil",
          "search_text": "rachel neil co-culture with chromis viridis facilitates a \u2018wilder\u2019 pocillopora verrucosa holobiont in captivity wild coral colonies gain benefits from associations with schooling fish, including improved growth and increased resilience to environmental stress. to test if such benefits persist in captivity, we co-cultured asexual fragments of pocillopora verrucosa with various combinations of chromis viridis and live feed availability for 3 months. during this time multiple aspects of the coral holobiont were monitored, including growth, photosymbiont health, nutritional status and microbial community composition. access to live feeds (mixed artemia, rotifers and microalgae) resulted in the highest growth, protein concentration and symbiont densities within the coral fragments. total lipid content was higher in corals that had access to dissolved wastes from schools of c. viridis, though triacylglyceride concentrations were reduced in all captive corals compared to samples taken from wild p. verrucosa colonies. interestingly, while the microbial communities of wild p. verrucosa colonies were dominated by the microbial symbiont endozoicomonadaceae (96 \u00b1 2.5 % relative abundance), samples taken from corals after acclimation to captivity but prior to the experiment commencing showed an ~50% decline in the relative abundance of this taxa. after three months, corals subjected to treatments without fish physically present in the tanks displayed low endozoicomonadaceae populations (0.2 \u2013 1.9 %), whilst significantly higher relative abundances of endozoicomonadaceae (48-89%) were observed in p. verrucosa co-cultured with fish. overall, the co-culture of captive corals with fish displays a range of effects including improved coral health and microbiome stability, facilitating \u2018wilder\u2019 captive corals and highlighting co-culture\u2019s potential to enhance coral cultures for both ornamental and restoration purposes.",
