          "name": "Rana Elmesseri",
          "search_text": "rana elmesseri from coral immunity to combating antimicrobial resistance: a novel antimicrobial peptide from heliofungia actiniformis coral reefs are experiencing rapid decline, highlighting critical gaps in our understanding of coral immunity and survival mechanisms. corals are continuously exposed to potential pathogenic bacteria, making innate immune defences essential for their protection. among these defences, antimicrobial peptides (amps) are near-universal effectors across eukaryotes, yet their roles in coral immunity remain poorly investigated. while amps have been extensively studied in the model cnidarian hydra, limited attention has been given to coral-derived amps.\r\nrecently, we identified a novel hydramacin-like amp, previously characterized in hydra, in the coral heliofungia actiniformis. this study investigates its antimicrobial activity and its influence on coral-associated microbial aggregates (camas), providing insights into host\u2013microbiome interactions and immune regulation. furthermore, our findings explore the relationship between heliofungia and its bacterial symbiont endozoicomonas, elucidating its potential role in coral immunity. collectively, this research advances understanding of coral immune strategies while highlighting the dual ecological and biomedical relevance of this newly identified amp, which exhibits potential activity against both coral and human pathogens.",
