          "name": "Ryan Mcandrews",
          "search_text": "ryan mcandrews environment-dependent cooperation in a pair-bonding coral reef rabbitfish on coral reefs, rabbitfish are functionally important because they forage on algae in structurally complex areas that are inaccessible to most other herbivores. they often exhibit a cooperative feeding tactic, forming symbiotic long-term pair bonds where one partner feeds and the other adopts a vigilant posture. despite their functional importance, the environmental drivers of vigilance are unknown. this is especially relevant as coral reefs undergo rapid change, including declines in predator biomass, loss of structural complexity, and shifts toward turf-dominated benthos.\r\n\r\nwe are conducting a year-long investigation into how variation in local biotic reef conditions influences cooperative feeding behaviour in siganus doliatus pairs. at four sites around lizard island (northern great barrier reef, australia), we conducted repeated behavioural observations of six pairs per site across three seasonal trips. at each site, underwater visual census transects quantified predator and competitor abundance and biomass. within each pair\u2019s territory, we measured structural complexity (rugosity) and epilithic algal turf cover and height. we are testing how these environmental variables predicted vigilance, foraging, and territorial defence.\r\n\r\nwe predicted more frequent vigilance with higher predator or competitor abundance and greater structural complexity. instead, these expectations were not met in the data analysed thus far: relationships between environmental variables and cooperative behaviour were highly variable among sites and pairs. this heterogeneity suggests that rabbitfish cooperation is not uniformly driven by these environmental variables. instead, further analysis may reveal interactions among factors, partner dynamics, or unresponsiveness to environmental variability. unresponsiveness to environmental variability could be interesting, indicating stereotyped behaviours or overriding social factors.\r\n\r\nunderstanding when and where paired rabbitfish cooperate provides insight into the stability of their social symbiosis and its sensitivity to ongoing reef change. such context-dependent behaviour may have implications for pair-bond fidelity, the functional roles rabbitfishes play, and their resilience within rapidly transforming reef ecosystems. unrealised potential: sediment filters the function of coral reef herbivores and detritivores understanding how environmental stressors suppress key ecological functions is essential for developing sensitive indicators of reef condition. herbivorous fishes are widely recognised as critical contributors to coral reef resilience, yet their functional roles may be substantially constrained by sedimentation\u2013a pervasive stressor on inshore reefs. we integrate trait-based functional metrics with behavioural observations to assess how sedimentation filters both the composition and functional performance of herbivore assemblages.\r\n\r\nwe surveyed herbivore assemblages across six fringing reef sites in fiji that varied in sedimentation rate and composition. for each species present, we defined a potential functional role using key morphological and ecological traits linked to food acquisition. we then used video-based foraging observations to evaluate how much of the potential function was actively realised through foraging. these were compared using multivariate and fourth-corner analyses to link trait expression with sedimentation variables.\r\n\r\nrealised functional space (species foraging) occupied \u22641\u201345% of the potential functional space (species present) across sites, suggesting strong environmental filtering of functional performance. nearly half of the herbivore species present were never observed feeding, particularly those most sensitive to sediment ingestion. this presented a disconnect between potential and realised functional roles. foraging was disproportionately concentrated on vertical substrates, which accumulate significantly less sediment than horizontal surfaces, further suggesting that sedimentation restricts where functions are realised. trait\u2013sedimentation associations showed that fine sediments, silicates and detrital loads significantly filtered both species presence (potential function) and foraging behaviour (realised function). these results show that sedimentation can decouple species presence from functional performance, and that assessments relying on community composition alone will overestimate the ecological functions being delivered.",
