          "name": "Seth Keep",
          "search_text": "seth keep implications of intracolonial genetic variability on fitness of juvenile coral chimeras the unique bipartite life cycle of corals provides an early ontogenetic window for the natural occurrence of chimerism. although common and described in nature, few studies have focused on chimerism aside from observation, and even fewer have explored the degree of associated fitness tradeoffs. this study generated coral chimeras derived from spat across varying levels of relatedness and subjected them to thermal stress, to investigate fusion likelihood and fitness implications among juveniles. using controlled gametic crosses stained with inert dye (e.g., nile blue and neutral red), we measured the extent of fusion among larvae across degrees of relatedness (e.g., full siblings, half siblings, and unrelated) and parental identity (shared maternity versus shared paternity), to understand when fusion is likely to occur. following settlement, juvenile chimeras and singletons were exposed to heat stress, with temperature ramped from 28\u2103 to 34\u2103 over four weeks, with the aim to induce mortality. we monitored growth and mortality of spat every two days to understand if chimeras retain a mosaic of traits (e.g., differential tolerance to temperature, and therefore partial mortality), and hence, if intracolonial genetic variation within chimeras allows intracolonial phenotypic variation. the findings of this study improve our understanding of chimerism and the implications of intracolonial genetic variability on chimeric fitness. furthermore, the study informs the unit of selection for juvenile corals, providing a preliminary understanding of how targeted settlement may increase the diversity of traits within chimeric individuals to influence adaptive potential and survival.",
