          "name": "Sophie Renton",
          "search_text": "sophie renton the undiscovered genomics of vertical transmission (parent to offspring) in symbiodiniaceae using pacbio hifi sequencing photosynthetic microalgae (family symbiodiniaceae) are essential symbiotic partners of coral. corals depend critically on microalgal symbionts for nutrients and acquire them via two distinct transmission modes: internally from parent to offspring via the eggs (vertical transmission) or among unrelated hosts through the environment (horizontal transmission). vertically transmitted (vt) symbionts and their coral hosts possess a unique level of fidelity, often exhibiting strong species-specific association. vertical transmission is a well-studied phenomena in non-coral taxa and is understood to influence the genomic evolution of both the host and symbiont through co-evolution dynamics. the genomic effect of vertical transmission and the role of co-evolution in symbiodiniaceae is poorly understood. this is because vt symbionts have proven recalcitrant to traditional algal cultivation methods, making their genomes largely inaccessible. to overcome this challenge, we developed a novel, culture-free method that successfully isolates vt symbionts, making their dna accessible for high-quality genomic investigation. we validated this method using vertically transmitting coral species montipora aequituberculuta, montipora turtlensis, and porites cylindrica from the palm islands, great barrier reef. using a metagenomic approach, supported by deep hifi sequencing, we present the first highly contiguous genome assembled from a targeted sample of vt symbiodiniaceae from p.cylindrica. complemented by transcriptomics and gene models this genome provides a high quality and critical reference for comparative and phylogenetic studies of symbiodiniaceae. as an example for the use of this genome, we will present preliminary work that assesses the genetic and strain diversity of vt symbiodiniaceae within the eggs of three distinct p.cylindrica colonies.",
