          "name": "Stephanie Di Perna",
          "search_text": "stephanie di perna implications of pco2 variability and concentrations for rates of calcification and dissolution of coral skeletons, carbonate sediments and living corals coastal environments have highly dynamic carbonate chemistry, often with much greater ranges in pco2 than in the open ocean. however, our understanding of the impact of this variability on coral reef benthic organisms is still in its infancy. this study investigated how average pco2 levels and pco2 variability jointly influence calcification and dissolution of different types of reef carbonates (including coral skeletal carbonate, carbonate sediment and living coral) under pre-industrial conditions with low variability (mean 270 \u00b5atm \u00b1 50 \u00b5atm), contemporary conditions with low and medium variability (mean 410 \u00b5atm \u00b1 50 and 150 \u00b5atm) and ocean acidification conditions with low, medium and high variability (mean 900 \u00b5atm \u00b1 50, 150 and 300 \u00b5atm). rubble, sediments and nubbins of two coral species (montipora aequituberculata and acropora millepora) were maintained under treatment conditions for 10 weeks. net calcification (ncalc) was assessed via buoyant weight differences for all carbonate types. for corals, respirometry and total alkalinity anomaly measurements were used to compare day and night organic and inorganic metabolic rates before completion of the experiment. under contemporary pco2 conditions, medium pco2 variability elevated ncalc for the two coral species and the sediments compared to low variability. in ocean acidification conditions, high variability reduced ncalc for m. aequituberculata, sediment and rubble. pre-industrial conditions minimized the weight loss of sediments, but had little impact on weight changes in corals and rubble. a. millepora day calcification rates and m. aequituberculata night calcification rates increased at medium compared to low variability in contemporary conditions but decreased with variability in ocean acidification conditions. the results demonstrate that natural variability in pco2 is a major driver of the metabolism of corals, and of the accumulation of carbonate structures on coral reefs. consequently, it is critical to include local variability in seawater chemistry into monitoring efforts and predictive tools.",
