          "name": "Sydney Bell",
          "search_text": "sydney bell is everything everywhere? environmental filtering shapes free-living symbiodiniaceae diversity across australia approximately 85% of coral species acquire symbiodiniaceae from the environment, yet the biogeography and ecological drivers of these free-living partners remain unknown, particularly outside the indo-pacific. here, we characterized the environmental pool  of symbiodiniaceae communities across four major australian marine protected areas (mpas) - the great barrier reef (gbr), lord howe island (lhi), ningaloo and the houtman abrolhos islands (hai) - using its2 metabarcoding. to account for differences in sampling depth, spatial structure and extent across mpas, we evaluated alpha (\u03b1), beta (\u03b2), and gamma (\u03b3) diversity using three normalization approaches (standardized sample size, spatially constrained pairwise comparisons, and coverage-based rarefaction). all methods produced consistent results, namely that diversity varied across mpas. using coverage-standardized richness (q = 0) as a representative metric, \u03b1 diversity was highest for the gbr (3,655 \u00b1 127 amplicon sequence variations - asvs), intermediate for ningaloo (3,468 \u00b1 131) and lhi (2,845 \u00b1 565), and lowest for the hai (2,669 \u00b1 602).  lhi had the lowest turnover (\u03b2 = 0.93) and the gbr the highest (1.16). diversity (\u03b2) differed significantly between regions, best explained by a combination of spatial and environmental gradients, including distance from shore, productivity, bathymetry, photosynthetic active radiation (par), phosphate, and salinity (dbrda/permanova, all p < 0.01). regional richness (\u03b3) was 23.9-42.7% less in hai, lhi, and ningaloo relative to the gbr. these results demonstrate strong regional structuring of free-living symbiodiniaceae, with environmental filtering by key physical variables likely shaping symbiont community composition at both local and regional scales. this study establishes the importance of local protection for these critical coral reef symbionts and provides an important baseline for predicting how corals\u2019 symbionts will respond to ongoing environmental pressure.",
