          "name": "Taleatha Pell",
          "search_text": "taleatha pell interspecific competition alters density-dependent responses during coral larval settlement variation in density\u2010dependent processes within and between populations underpins the strength and direction of biotic interactions that regulate recruitment and survival in sessile marine organisms. however, past studies on density-dependent coral settlement have explored taxa in isolation or assessed larvae from wild coral spawn slicks likely containing a mixture of broadcast spawning taxa. the influence of inter-specific coral competitors on density-dependent settlement patterns has not previously been explored. by manipulating larval supply densities that combined equal ratios of a broadcast spawning acropora species with a smaller taxon (montipora or playgyra), this study found that inter-specific competition changes either the magnitude or direction of density-dependent settlement. inter-specific settlement trials highlighted the dominance of competitive, larger acropora species over the smaller m. aequituberculata and p. daedalea with increasing larval supply densities. occupation of preferred settlement substrate, flexibility in settlement preference and, in the case of a. spathulata and p. daedalea, density-dependent mortality from contact in mixed species clumps of settlers and larvae contributed to changing density-dependent patterns in inter-specific settlement trials. in single taxa trials, only p. daedalea displayed density-dependent settlement, compared to density-independent responses in acropora species and m. aequituberculata. substrate specificity and space competition with increasing density likely explain the negative density-dependent settlement response in p. daedalea. these findings highlight that interspecific interactions can reshape density\u2010dependent settlement dynamics, underscoring the need to consider species composition and larval densities in both natural recruitment processes and restoration practices.",
