          "name": "Tiny Remmers",
          "search_text": "tiny remmers automating estimates of coral colony-level bleaching severity on large-extent orthomosaic: integrating ai approaches from rapidbenthos with bleaching detection coral bleaching events are becoming more frequent, more severe, and are occurring on a global scale. enhancing the efficiency of monitoring tools to document these events is essential to better understand how coral reefs will be impacted by the accelerated pace of stress. however, quantifying bleaching impacts from field imagery or in-water observation is currently a highly time-consuming process, as expert annotators must manually assign a bleaching score to each colony, a process that is difficult to scale up to the ecosystem level of disturbance. \r\nto address this challenge, we developed a tool that automatically estimates the severity of bleaching from large-extent (tens to hundreds of square meters, containing hundreds to thousands of colonies) orthomosaic. in this study, we integrated a five-class bleaching detection model into the rapidbenthos orthomosaic instance segmentation pipeline to automatically quantify colony-level bleaching severity.  the workflow begins by segmenting the orthomosaic using a coral detection yolo (you only look once) model. next, a point annotation model trained in reefcloud is applied to the underlying images during the annotation phase. finally, a convolutional neural network (cnn) model assigns a bleaching score to each colony based on segmented underlying images.\r\nwith this tool, researchers can gain deeper insights into the demographic impacts of bleaching, as colony taxa, size, relative location, and bleaching score are all extracted automatically. automating bleaching detection from images enables faster data generation and reporting\u2013an essential capability in a time when disturbances are increasing in frequency.",
