          "name": "Tom Bridge",
          "search_text": "tom bridge implications of taxonomic upheaval for the biogeography of indo-pacific acropora phylogenomic data are revealing that acropora species previously considered as single morphologically variable species widely distributed across the indo-pacific actually comprise complexes of morphologically similar but genetically distinct lineages with much smaller geographic ranges. somewhat paradoxically, the substantial increase in provincialism in acropora also indicates that while the total diversity of acropora is far higher than the 120\u2013160 species proposed in the influential taxonomic revisions of wallace (1999) and veron (2000), species richness many sites is often lower than previously thought, particularly at isolated or peripheral locations. for example, phylogenomic analyses of the acropora fauna at the remote christmas island in the indian ocean indicate that species richness is ~50% lower than previous estimates using traditional morphological taxonomy, and that the species turnover is high even between relatively proximal regions such as ningaloo reef. these patterns challenge the prevailing assumptions that have underpinned coral biogeography for the last half a century that local diversity can be inferred from regional species pools, and that small-ranged endemic species are rare. instead, our results suggest local-scale factors such as isolation and habitat persistence over geological timescales exert a strong influence on species richness, taxonomic composition and endemism at any particular location. these findings highlight the need to reassess conservation priorities, as many newly recognised species have restricted ranges, increasing their vulnerability to localised disturbances and indicating that connectivity from adjacent regions will have little influence on recovery of coral populations on ecologically relevant timescales.",
