          "search_text": "ahmed alsaggaf alginate beads as a coral probiotic encapsulator and delivery tool the use of coral probiotics, or beneficial microorganisms for corals (bmcs), has emerged as a promising strategy to minimize and mitigate the negative effects of rising sea temperatures on coral reefs. extracted from healthy corals, bmcs have been proven to facilitate higher survivorships and retention of healthy vitals in ex situ and recently in situ applications. here, we present the use of alginate-based biotechnological tools to deliver bmcs to corals. alginate has been well-studied in harsh conditions ranging from soil to stomach acids for its ability to preserve and protect encapsulated organisms for long-term controlled release and storage. our experiments aimed to assess the effectiveness of encapsulation, release kinetics, retention of viable cells, and behavior underwater. this was done by testing single-strain versus consortia beads and quantifying released cells across two temperature regimes (26 and 32 \u00b0c), covering the red sea seasonal temperature range. we then tested the in situ application of bmcs to corals using beads as part of a restoration experiment. alginate beads were found to retain viable bmcs and allow them to grow and be released over the maximum time tested,  three weeks, at an average rate of ~10\u2076 cells hr-1 when inoculated in artificial seawater. temperature had little to no significant effect on the release dynamics. ultimately, acropora spp. and pocillopora spp. fragments inoculated with bmc beads in situ demonstrated improved health over longer-term weekly applications, indicated by fv/fm measurements compared to control fragments. with these results and further refinements, we aim to establish a robust, scalable, and biocompatible tool to facilitate bmc-based therapies for coral reef restoration and conservation efforts globally.",
