          "name": "Albert Rubio Hidalgo",
          "search_text": "albert rubio hidalgo changing fish communities across gradients in the northern red sea predatory fish communities play a critical role in the structure and stability of coral reef food webs, yet their responses to environmental and anthropogenic change in the red sea remain poorly understood. as reefs in the northern red sea face increasing pressures from large-scale coastal developments, quantifying structural and functional changes in their fish communities is critical for developing effective monitoring and management strategies that maintain reef functioning. to study these predatory assemblages, 398 bruvs were deployed over four years (2020, 2023, 2024, and 2025) along the northern saudi arabian red sea coast across a range of reef types and depths (1-50 m). spatial patterns in functional, alpha, beta, and zeta diversity were quantified across gradients of fishing pressure and environmental conditions. several functional traits were significantly influenced by both depth and fishing pressure. generalized linear models revealed a significant decrease in beta diversity and an increase in zeta diversity at lower fishing pressures, indicating stronger heterogeneous turnover in fish communities where fishing pressure is higher. to assess the impact of fishing on individual species, we applied joint species distribution models to evaluate whether weak stock-strong stock dynamics contribute to observed species turnover. when fisheries target commercially valuable and more resilient species (strong stock), these populations can support otherwise unsustainable exploitation of less resilient or lower-value species (weak stock). examining these dynamics helps identify which predator species and functional groups are most vulnerable and can guide appropriate fisheries-management interventions. overall, our results demonstrate the key role of anthropogenic and environmental gradients in structuring predatory coral reef fish assemblages in the red sea, and how multiple diversity metrics can capture subtle but ecologically important community shifts. this understanding is vital for predicting future ecological change and informing conservation strategies aimed at maintaining the functional roles of predatory fishes on coral reefs.",
