          "name": "Corinne D'anna",
          "search_text": "corinne d'anna from larvae to recruits: engineered substrates improve early life success of red sea corals a cornerstone of successful reef restoration is the recruitment and survival of coral larvae, which underpin the replenishment of coral populations. however, these early life stages experience high mortality rates, creating a significant bottleneck in population recovery. optimizing settlement substrates is one proposed mechanism for improving restoration outcomes. here, we investigate how ion supplementation, substrate porosity, and surface topography influence the settlement success of coral larvae from two red sea coral species currently used in restoration efforts. coral larvae from acropora spp. were exposed to various substrate types in controlled settlement assays: commercially available restoration substrates (coralassist plugs, secore stars) and custom-engineered climatecrete tiles that incorporated variations in inorganic enrichment (caco\u2083 content, mg\u00b2+, sr\u00b2+, fe\u00b3+), porosity, and surface topography (flat vs. grooved). multiple trials were conducted during the 2024 and 2025 spawning seasons. during settlement assays, we quantified larval behavior, settlement rate after 48h, and early post-settlement survival after 7 weeks. in the first set of assays, acropora spp. settlement rates were highest on climatecrete tiles with low carbonaceous sand content (22% settled) and high carbonaceous sand content tiles (18% settled), relative to coralassist plugs and secore stars. in the second set of assays, the engineered climatecrete substrates incorporating grooves, fe\u00b3+ ion additives, or increased porosity outperformed all other materials, achieving the highest settlement rates (38-41% settled) and the highest 7-week survival rate (33% survival). these findings demonstrate that caco\u2083-rich substrates, specific inorganic ion enrichments, and increased structural complexity can significantly enhance both larval settlement and early survival, with promising implications for substrate design in large-scale reef restoration applications.",
