          "name": "Fleur Bruggemann",
          "search_text": "fleur bruggemann host-specificity shapes the seasonal dynamics and resilience of coralline algal microbiomes in the red sea coral reefs are experiencing accelerating environmental pressures that undermine coral survival and reef functioning. in the red sea, recent temperature-driven coral bleaching events have drastically reduced coral cover on shallow reef flats. yet, crustose coralline algae (cca) remain abundant and are increasingly recognized as key ecosystem engineers that facilitate reef resilience and recovery. because important cca functions are microbially mediated, understanding the natural variability of cca-associated bacteria, and their sensitivity to environmental stressors, is essential but remains poorly resolved. we investigated spatial and temporal variation in microbiomes associated with two abundant but undescribed cca species in the central red sea, a region characterized by strong environmental seasonality and extreme summer temperatures. the two focal species differed in their fouling-resistance mechanisms: species 1 regularly sloughs epithelial layers, whereas species 2 does not. ten individuals of each species were tagged and repeatedly sampled across four seasons at one nearshore (abu shusha) and one offshore (cement wreck) shallow reef flat. sequencing of the 16s rrna v3\u2013v4 region showed that bacterial communities were strongly host-specific, clearly distinct from seawater, and significantly structured by both site and season. season emerged as the dominant driver of microbiome composition in both hosts, with pronounced shifts during summer. summer assemblages were enriched in opportunistic and potentially pathogenic taxa, including vibrio spp. recovery trajectories diverged between host species: while species 1 microbiomes returned to pre-summer compositions after peak temperatures, species 2 microbiomes remained dispersed, suggesting slower or incomplete recovery to summer stress. overall, this work reveals that cca hosts exhibit distinct microbiome responses to summer stress, emphasizing the need to further investigate how host-microbiome interactions shape cca health and their broader ecological roles on coral reefs.",
