          "name": "Jayce Carlson",
          "search_text": "jayce carlson development and carbonate accretion of early coral reef communities on coral reefs of papua new guinea calcifying organisms underpin coral reef frameworks by producing calcium carbonate (caco\u2083). crustose coralline algae (cca) play an additional ecological role by producing chemical cues that facilitate coral larval recruitment, making them essential to reef recovery. however, changes in carbonate chemistry due to ocean acidification (oa) jeopardizes calcification and impairs cca performance. natural co\u2082 seeps create a unique low-ph environment that can act as a natural analogue and in situ laboratory for exploring impacts of future ocean conditions. we deployed calcification accretion units (caus)\u2013stacked pvc settlement tiles \u2013for one year at five sites in papua new guinea: an active volcanic co\u2082 seep, a control reef, and three open-ocean sites. the seep site experiences continuous volcanic gas release and elevated temperatures, representing a naturally occurring environment where warming and oa may co-occur. after recovery, tiles were photographed for benthic community analyses, decalcified to quantify carbonate accretion, and examined under a stereomicroscope for coral recruits. early successional communities varied by site and by side. across all sites, cca dominated the upward-facing tile, covering ~66% of the surface, and a total of 117 coral recruits were identified. higher cca cover is positively correlated with carbonate accretion and coral recruitment, indicating that cca are essential to early reef-building processes. environmental monitoring showed that the spatial footprint of simulated oa conditions (i.e., low ph) at the seep was limited, while temperatures were consistently warmer than at control sites. although the seep did not function as a strong analogue for near-future oa, it may provide a valuable in situ laboratory for examining the impacts of ocean warming. collectively, our findings underscore the foundational role of cca in early reef development and demonstrate that high cca cover enhances coral recruitment, reinforcing its pivotal role in sustaining reef resilience under changing ocean conditions.",
