          "search_text": "jessica bouwmeester first frozen coral biorepository for the red sea cryopreservation is emerging as a critical tool for safeguarding coral genetic diversity and supporting assisted reproduction as reefs continue to decline globally. although the red sea hosts some of the world\u2019s most thermally tolerant coral lineages, the region has lacked a coordinated approach to long-term germplasm banking. here, we report the establishment and initial content of the first frozen coral biorepository for the red sea, created to preserve priority genetic resources and support future breeding and restoration efforts. in april 2022 in the central red sea (thuwal, saudi arabia), sperm was collected from acropora eurystoma (n = 7), a. hemprichii (n = 13), and platygyra daedalea (n = 3) and cryopreserved using controlled-rate freezing (20 \u00b0c/min), yielding 53 archived vials. fresh sperm collected at a target concentration of 10^7 cells/ml exhibited species-specific differences in motile concentration, with a. eurystoma showing the highest motile concentration (1.8 x 10^7 cells/ml), followed by p. daedalea (1.4 x 10^7 cells/ml) and a. hemprichii (0.7 x 10^7 cells/ml). post-thaw assessments of sperm cryopreserved at a target concentration of 10^9 cells/ml revealed high retention of motile sperm in both acropora species across multiple cryopreservation runs (1.5-5.0 x 10^8 cells/ml). p. daedalea displayed markedly reduced post-thaw motility (1.5 x 10^7 cells/ml), a pattern consistent with cryopreservation challenges reported for p. daedalea globally. these results demonstrate that sperm from key red sea coral species can be successfully cryopreserved with substantial recovery of motile cells, establishing a foundational resource for assisted fertilization, selective breeding, and genetic management. as part of the region\u2019s diversifying coral management strategy, the biorepository is integrated within neom\u2019s kaust coral restoration initiative, the world\u2019s largest coral restoration program. embedding cryobanking within this large-scale framework strengthens the red sea\u2019s capacity to secure thermally resilient coral lineages and support interventions that enhance reef resilience under climate change. aligning science with practice: a strategic framework for coral reef restoration at scale in the red sea the accelerating decline of coral reefs worldwide has created an urgent need for restoration approaches that can operate effectively at large scales. meeting this challenge demands tight alignment between scientific evidence and operational decision-making. in this context, kcri (kaust coral restoration initiative) was launched in 2021 in neom (northern red sea) as one of the world\u2019s largest and most innovative coral restoration programs, supporting saudi vision 2030. kcri\u2019s science program was designed to address implementation gaps and inefficiencies by generating targeted knowledge, refining on-the-ground practices, and accelerating innovation in support of a resilient reefscape. we present how >50 focused trials and pilot studies to date directly inform restoration and operational activity, with emphasis on coral health, stress response, sexual reproduction, and genetic management. specifically, our studies provide the empirical foundation needed to optimise species selection, outplanting approaches, and nursery methodologies. in doing so, the rapid development and testing of hypotheses provide a mission-critical, agile feedback loop that improves restoration outcomes whilst strengthening the translation of scientific insight into practical guidance. we also discuss how these solutions are cultivated through >45 diverse academic partnership projects to advance technologies in ecological restoration, assisted reproduction, innovative monitoring tools and frameworks, coral stress detection, and reefscape flow-dynamics modelling. by embedding research, field practice, and technological development into a single program, we enable promising concepts to be evaluated, adapted, and deployed within a unified strategic framework. through integration of field-led questions, targeted experimentation, and strategic innovation, we are building a more resilient, knowledge-driven foundation for coral reef restoration in a rapidly changing environment.",
