          "name": "Laura Beenham",
          "search_text": "laura beenham first identification of vibrio owensii vbm40 strain as the causative agent of rapid tissue loss in acropora c.f hemprichii bleaching and coral disease are escalating globally, yet confirmed pathogen\u2013disease relationships remain exceptionally rare. the complexity of coral microbiomes makes establishing causality difficult, and worldwide, only six studies have experimentally fulfilled koch\u2019s postulates for any coral disease. here, we report the first confirmed identification of vibrio owensii as the cause of rapid tissue loss in acropora cf. hemprichii, making it the 7th verified coral pathogen globally. two symptomatic a. hemprichii colonies exhibiting rapid tissue loss were sampled for bacterial isolation. full-length 16s rrna gene sequencing revealed a single vibrio isolate (vbm40) uniquely associated with diseased tissue. phylogenetic analysis placed this isolate within the vibrio harveyi clade - a group containing several known marine pathogens.\r\nto test pathogenicity, healthy a. hemprichii fragments were exposed to placebo (3.5% nacl solution) or vbm40 (106 cells/ ml) at 25 \u00b0c and 33 \u00b0c. corals exposed to vbm40 developed multifocal lesions characteristic of white syndromes, with disease onset accelerated under heat stress  (21 days at 33 \u00b0c and 30 days at 25 \u00b0c). mortality was significantly higher in vbm40 treatments at both temperatures. whole genome sequencing of the bacteria re-isolated from diseased fragments confirmed>99.97% ani with the original inoculum, fully satisfying koch\u2019s postulates. comparative genomics identified vbm40 as vibrio owensii, sharing >96% ani with the type strain dy05.\r\nthis study provides the first validated report of vibrio owensii vbm40 as an etiological agent of rapid tissue loss in a coral genus other than montipora, more specifically in a. hemprichii, and represents one of only seven confirmed coral disease causations worldwide. our findings underscore the need for targeted pathogen surveillance and proactive reef health management as environmental pressures increase.",
