          "name": "Laura Macrina",
          "search_text": "laura macrina beyond tubipora musica: phylogenomics unveils the overlooked diversity and endemism of the hermatypic octocoral genus tubipora although coral reef framework formation is primarily attributed to scleractinian corals, a few octocoral taxa also play an important role. the genus tubipora deposits hard calcareous skeletons organised in tubes connected by horizontal stolonic platforms constituted of fused sclerites. although the genus is broadly distributed across the tropical indo-pacific, its diversity and evolutionary history remain poorly understood. historically, ten species have been described, yet most recent treatments have recognized only the type species, tubipora musica. here, we used an integrative approach combining morphological and phylogenomic (based on ultra-conserved element and exon loci) analyses of 136 tubipora colonies collected across six marine provinces in the indo-pacific. we delimited 15 morphologically distinct and genetically strongly supported lineages. all lineages exhibited highly restricted geographic distributions, limited to single provinces, highlighting regional endemism. endemic diversification is suggested by sister species restricted to the red sea, madagascar, and eastern australia, as well as regional diversification around the arabian peninsula, in the western indian ocean, and in the western pacific. results show deep diversification within the western pacific, from which the western indian ocean was colonized by a single lineage that underwent radiation there. these findings underscore the need for broader sampling across the indo-pacific to assess tubipora diversity and diversification and highlight the power of genomics in clarifying species boundaries and evolutionary relationships, providing a foundation towards the taxonomic revision of tubipora. accurate species definition is essential for coral reef conservation, enabling precise biodiversity assessments and correctly informing management and restoration strategies, ultimately enhancing our ability to monitor and mitigate the impacts of climate change on vulnerable reef-building organisms.  our findings challenge historical taxonomic assumptions on this group and highlight the need for a systematic revision of the genus through an integrated morphological, genomic, and biogeographic approach. characterising the symbiotic bacterial and algal communities associated with the hermatypic octocoral genus tubipora in the central saudi arabian red sea coral-associated microbiomes are increasingly being recognised as key drivers of reef-building corals' health, resilience, and adaptive capacity to environmental change. while most research has focused on the microbiomes of scleractinian corals, far less attention has been given to octocorals, despite their ecological importance. in particular, the microbiomes associated with reef-building octocorals in the red sea remain largely unexplored. here, we characterize the bacterial and symbiodiniaceae communities associated with two endemic species of the hermatypic octocoral genus tubipora in the central saudi arabian red sea. using high-throughput sequencing of the bacterial 16s rrna gene and the symbiodiniaceae its2 region across 82 colonies collected at three reef sites ranging 0 to 30 m depth, we found that microbial assemblages were broadly similar between the two tubipora species. however, combined species-depth effects significantly influenced both bacterial and algal community compositions, with remarkable species-specificity of its2 type profiles. moreover, tubipora hosted distinct bacterial assemblages compared to co-occurring scleractinian and octocorals species in the same region. our findings provide the first insights into the tubipora-associated microbiomes in shallow-water coral reefs of the saudi arabian red sea. while enriching the inventory of coral-associated bacteria from the basin, this study establishes a baseline for understanding octocoral holobionts in the area and their potential adaptive strategies in response to climate change.",
