          "name": "Laura Marangoni",
          "search_text": "laura marangoni how upwelling-driven nutrient supply, nitrogen metabolism, and oxidative stress shape depth-dependent coral responses during a marine heatwave in the red sea understanding how oceanographic variability regulates coral physiology is essential for predicting reef responses to climate\u2010driven heatwaves. upwelling is widespread on coral reefs, but the physiological interactions between localized cooling and nutrient delivery on coral thermal tolerance are poorly understood. on an offshore reef in the central red sea, we paired 14 months of continuous environmental monitoring with monthly sampling of tagged pocillopora favosa coral colonies across a 5-25 m depth gradient to evaluate how resource supply and thermal stress shape coral physiology. during spring and summer in the red sea, regular upwelling transported cool, nitrate- and plankton-rich waters onto the reef slope, creating strong vertical gradients in temperature, nutrient supply, and food availability. however, a heatwave during july-september 2024 increased stratification of the water column and shut down upwelling, exposing all depths to anomalously warm surface waters. this disruption resulted in depth\u2010specific outcomes, with complete bleaching and mortality at 5-15 m and minimal impact at deeper depths. to investigate the mechanisms underpinning these outcomes, we quantified how upwelling-driven shifts in nitrate availability and food supply influence coral nitrogen metabolism, redox balance, and symbiosis stability using biomarkers of biomass, chlorophyll a, symbiont density, c:n ratios, and protein nitration (pn) - the latter indicating peroxynitrite formation - a highly reactive nitrogen species that can impair cellular function severely under combined heat stress and altered nitrogen metabolism. coral and symbiont c:n ratios reveal depth-stratified nutrient regimes, while pn levels captured how oxidative\u2013nitrosative stress varied across depths and time by integrating thermal driven reactive oxygen species production with nitric oxide derived from nitrogen metabolism. by quantifying natural fluctuations in nutrient delivery and temperature before and during a severe bleaching event, our dataset offers a unique framework for understanding how nutrient dynamics modulate oxidative\u2013nitrosative stress pathways that compromise symbiosis and, ultimately, bleaching patterns across depth.",
