          "name": "Lea Palm",
          "search_text": "lea palm shallow behaviour: isotopic niches and feeding ecology of two coastal sympatric red sea batoids loss of habitat may reduce niche availability and force sympatric species into competition. stable isotope analysis can help to quantify trophic niches and identify partitioning strategies in sympatric species with similar ecologies. for instance, the bluespotted ray taeniura lymma (iucn: least concern) and honeycomb whipray himantura uarnak (iucn: endangered), two functionally similar species, co-occur in the shallow waters and mangrove systems of the saudi arabian red sea. both are benthic bioturbators, using mangroves and neighboring sand and reef habitats as sheltered feeding grounds. while these species broadly overlap in their functional niches, the fine-scale niche partitioning preventing competition is effectively unstudied. this study addresses this gap by specifically focusing on their respective trophic niches, collecting white-muscle tissue from local t. lymma and h. uarnak off of thuwal, saudi arabia and investigating their diets via stable isotope analysis (sia). samples from each species undergo urea and lipid extraction (ule) tests to determine the influence of these treatments on \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3c and \u03b4\u00b9\u2075n values and are standardized to account for these biases. the pre-treated muscle samples, along with specimens from locally available prey, were processed and analyzed to estimate trophic position (tp), dietary contribution of prey (dcp), isotopic niche width (inw), and isotopic niche overlap (ino) to get a holistic view of each species\u2019 diet and better understanding of the relationship between these co-occurring red sea stingrays. this study offers a local glimpse into interspecific dietary partitioning and will help inform conservation of interconnected nearshore habitats to protect these rays in a rapidly changing world.",
