          "search_text": "louis simon the chemical landscape of red sea coral reef taxa: biogenic volatiles as signatures of health and stress coral reefs are undergoing accelerating decline driven by climate change and local anthropogenic stressors, underscoring the need for sensitive, scalable and non-invasive monitoring tools. volatilomics, i.e. broad profiling of biogenic volatile organic compounds (bvocs), has emerged as a powerful diagnostic approach across diverse fields, including medical science (clinical breathomics for disease detection), terrestrial ecology (chemical signalling in plant-insect interactions and defense priming), and food science (quality assurance and spoilage detection). while volatilomics has been increasingly applied to marine science, surprisingly little remains known for coral reefs with only 34 studies to date (systematic scopus search (terms: (volatilomics or \"volatile organic compounds\") and (\"coral*\" or \"coral reef*\")), representing 2.5% of the field and 0.04% of volatilomic studies across all fields. to address this critical knowledge gap, we initiated the first comprehensive volatilomic investigation of red sea reef-building corals to establish a regional baseline and evaluate bvocs as candidate biomarkers of coral health and stress combining field and lab-based approaches. we present the first insights from a three-phase research framework: i) characterizing species-specific bvoc signatures across five coral species and crustose coralline algae integrated microbiome data to assess how microbial community shifts influence bvoc emissions; ii) comparing bvocs from corals grown in in situ and ex situ nurseries over a full year to identify how growth environment tunes chemical markers; iii) characterizing bvoc responses of nursery-grown corals to corallivore predation to develop early-warning indicators capable of distinguishing biotic injury from physiological stress. by generating the first bvoc reference library for the red sea and identifying diagnostic chemical markers, we can establish volatilomics as a meaningful, non-invasive molecular tool for the near real-time assessment, early stress detection, and improved restoration and monitoring strategies.",
