          "name": "Luca Stirnimann",
          "search_text": "luca stirnimann collapse of pelagic subsidies to a coral reef during a marine heatwave pelagic subsidies are increasingly recognized as a vital component of coral reef ecosystem functioning. yet empirical observations of ocean-reef connections are scarce, and much remains unknown about the physical processes that drive subsurface resource supply and the timescales over which reef organisms respond. here, we present one of the first continuous, year-round records of plankton, nutrients, and oceanographic conditions linking offshore processes to a coral reef ecosystem. for 15 months (2024\u20132025), we conducted bi-weekly monitoring of physical and biogeochemical dynamics, nutrient uptake, phytoplankton production, and planktonic community structure on an offshore reef in the central red sea. during winter, the water column is fully mixed to >100 m homogenizing resources across the reef slope. in summer, stratification and wind-driven thermocline dynamics regulate upwelling, which pushes the subsurface chlorophyll maximum onto the reef slope, delivering cool, nitrate- and plankton-rich waters that enhance food availability for zooplankton and benthic suspension feeders. stable isotope analysis (\u03b4\u00b9\u00b3c, \u03b4\u00b9\u2075n) of phytoplankton, zooplankton, and corals revealed how pelagic resource pools vary across seasons and how they are assimilated by reef-building corals along a 5\u201330 m depth gradient. our study highlights that pelagic subsidies are not ephemeral inputs to coral reefs but originate from persistent subsurface resource pools. delivery of these subsidies to shallow reef communities is structured by physical forcing and governs reef functioning across depths and through time. notably, an intense heatwave in the summer of 2024 shut down upwelling, restricting the delivery of cool, plankton-rich waters to shallow depths and resulting in widespread coral mortality. the abrupt halt of upwelling caused by surface warming and increased stratification reveals an emerging threat to ocean-reef connections under climate change. our multidisciplinary approach to ocean observation provides a valuable framework for quantifying the biophysical processes that unite coral reefs with their surrounding seascape.",
