          "name": "Manuel Aranda Lastra",
          "search_text": "manuel aranda lastra symbiotic state reshapes the developmental origin of algal-accommodating host cells in aiptasia the breakdown of the nutritional symbiosis between cnidarians and dinoflagellates underpins the global coral bleaching crisis, yet the cellular mechanisms governing the establishment and maintenance of this partnership remain poorly understood. while metabolic cooperation is well-documented, the identity, origin, and developmental plasticity of specific host cell types pre-adapted to recognize and house symbionts remain unresolved. here, we present a high-resolution single-cell transcriptomic atlas of the model sea anemone exaiptasia diaphana under both symbiotic and aposymbiotic conditions. our analysis reveals substantial cellular heterogeneity and identifies a distinct symbiosis-competent cell population present in both symbiotic and aposymbiotic states, suggesting the host possesses a pre-adapted cellular framework for symbiosis regardless of algal presence. trajectory inference from rna velocity analysis further uncovers a condition-dependent plasticity in the differentiation of these cells. in symbiotic animals, symbiosis-competent cells predominantly differentiate from gastrodermal lineages; conversely, in the aposymbiotic state, they appear to originate from immune progenitors. this lineage switch is accompanied by divergent metabolic and immune signatures, where lysosomal activity acts as a key driver in the transition to symbiosis competence. together, these findings suggest that cnidarian symbiosis is built on a flexible developmental program in which immune and digestive cell fates remain fluid, allowing dynamic modulation of symbiotic capacity in response to environmental and symbiotic cues.",
