          "name": "Nicolas Oury",
          "search_text": "nicolas oury model but complex: cryptic lineages in the widely studied coral galaxea fascicularis (scleractinia: euphylliidae) across the arabian seas and western indian ocean cryptic species challenge the assessment of biological diversity and its distribution. in scleractinian corals, key components of coral reef ecosystems increasingly threatened by global change, the lack of diagnostic morphological characters blurs species boundaries and limits biodiversity monitoring. here, using genomics, we investigated the genetic structure and potential cryptic diversification of the widely studied coral galaxea fascicularis (linnaeus, 1767) across the western extent of its distribution. targeted sequence capture of ultraconserved elements and exons from 202 g. fascicularis colonies collected across the arabian peninsula (ap), maldives, and western indian ocean (wio), was used to delimit evolutionary lineages using phylogenomic reconstructions and species delimitation analyses. genomic, biogeographic, symbiotic, ecological, and morphological differentiations among lineages were further explored to characterise the origin of this diversification. nine genetically distinct, morphologically cryptic, and geographically restricted lineages were identified, with the maldives lineage being the most divergent. four lineages occurred allopatrically in the wio, while four others were sympatric around the ap, for which new genetic barcoding markers were developed to allow reliable identification. significant variation in symbiodiniaceae community composition was detected across localities and lineages, and redundancy in gene functions associated with divergence was also found. ecological differences, including depth distribution, were observed among some lineages. together, these findings reveal extensive cryptic diversity and fine-scale geographic structure within g. fascicularis, reflecting complex evolutionary dynamics potentially driven by a combination of allopatric or peripatric (e.g., following repeated red sea isolation events) and parapatric (associated with depth zonation) speciations. by uncovering this hidden diversity and providing accessible genetic tools for lineage identification, this study advances our understanding of coral speciation and supports more accurate biodiversity assessments and conservation strategies.",
