          "search_text": "jessica zamborain mason towards nutrition-sensitive coral reef food systems coral reef fisheries contribute substantially to the delivery of nutritional benefits (e.g., essential amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins and minerals) throughout the tropics, with over 500 million people, mostly in low- and middle-income countries, estimated to depend on reefs for food and income. however, most reef fish stocks are overfished, and this together with our lack of understanding on how to manage reef-based food systems to enhance nutritional outcomes limits our ability to address malnutrition and other health problems in tropical coastal communities. nutrition-sensitive coral reef management is needed to nourish current and future reef-dependent populations. in this talk, we will (i) define and set the principles that underpin nutrition-sensitive coral reef food systems, (ii) identify the different pathways by which coral reef food systems can become nutrition-sensitive, and (iii) integrate coral reef fish nutrient profiles with reef fish surveys from > 2,000 reefs to evaluate the nutritional status and potential of reef fisheries production systems. overall, our work shows how coral reef food systems can be tailored to support human nutrition and highlights priority locations where nutrition-sensitive fisheries management could deliver the greatest public health benefits.",
