          "name": "Taihun Kim",
          "search_text": "taihun kim from rigidity to collapse: slumping soft corals in the high-latitude asia-pacific marginal distribution zone, jeju island, korea submitter has requested this only be considered as a poster.\r\n\r\nthe coral communities (i.e., soft and hard corals) in korean waters at the northern limit of their high-latitude asia-pacific marginal distribution zone are thriving, exhibiting high resistance and successful adaptation to their comparably stressful environment, specifically the unfavorable winter water temperature. here, by photographic documentation, we report the first demise of soft corals by a severe slumping event. slumping in organisms without exo- or endoskeleton is herein coined as the loss of structural integrity by collapse of their hydrostatic skeleton, leading from drooping to deflation, death, and disintegration. we propose the term \u201cslumping event\u201d to describe the observed phenomenon, which affected at least several species of non-symbiotic octocorallian soft corals of the family nephtheidae. we define four major physiologically abnormal morphological stages involved in the soft coral slumping event, namely the slumping stage, the flaccid stage, the dangle stage, and the disintegration stage. general causes of hydroskeletal deflation are briefly discussed and compared to in-situ, multiple-year-comparison of summer sea surface temperature and salinity data. for future quantifications of salinity conditions during observed soft coral slumping events, we propose the introduction of the metric degree freshening week (dfw) as a measure of hypo-saline stress accumulated over time, similar to the already well-established degree heating week (dhw) measuring heat stress on hard coral reefs. from kelp forests to coral reefs in jeju island, korea: competitive, trophic, and thermal drivers of high-latitude tropicalization environmentally adapted and cold-resistant symbiotic hard coral communities inhabiting the high-latitude asia\u2013pacific marginal distribution zone are flourishing despite strong thermal seasonality and suboptimal light conditions. jeju island, republic of korea, represents a global hotspot of this transition, where temperate, subtropical, and tropical benthic organisms currently coexist. over recent decades, rising sea surface temperatures have driven a regime shift characterized by rapid proliferation of subtropical hard corals accompanied by widespread declines of indigenous kelp forests and soft coral assemblages. here, we integrate long-term field observations, in situ spatial competition experiments, experimental thermal physiology, and stable isotope\u2013based trophic analysis to elucidate the mechanisms restructuring this marginal reef ecosystem. long-term in situ experiments and observations reveal a clear trajectory of regime shift driven by spatial competition, in which hard corals consistently outcompete both macroalgae and soft corals, ultimately occupying most available substrate. thermal performance experiments combined with siber (stable isotope bayesian ellipses in r) analyses demonstrate pronounced species-specific physiological niches and contrasting trophic strategies among key foundation species, indicating that trophic flexibility enhances persistence under high-latitude environmental constraints. predictive modeling based on contemporary coral distributional expansion and long-term seawater temperature trends further validates and forecasts continued transitions toward coral-dominated benthic communities. extreme thermal disturbances amplify these transitions. during the marine heatwave, shallow habitats experienced severe bleaching and high mortality under temperatures exceeding 29 \u00b0c, whereas deeper habitats remained with stable ~22 \u00b0c, and functioned as effective thermal refugia. collectively, our results demonstrate that coral tropicalization in jeju waters is progressing through tightly coupled poleward (boreal), downward (bathymetric), and trophic functional shifts driven by ocean warming, trophic flexibility, competitive dominance, and disturbance-mediated habitat restructuring. these findings improve forecasts of coral expansion, species turnover, and habitat shifts at the geographic limits of coral distributions.",
