          "name": "Yuka Kushida",
          "search_text": "yuka kushida preliminary study of the microbiome of sea pens across depths and phylogeny anthozoans support marine biodiversity and they behave as a holobiont with many microorganisms including bacteria by living in symbiosis. currently, marine organisms face threats caused by environmental changes including anthropogenic impacts. it is needed to understand the microbiome of anthozoans to clarify the role of anthozoans as holobionts that may be tolerant of such stress. however, microbiome studies on anthozoans have biases in both phylogenetic groups and depth ranges. so far, microbiome information has accumulated mainly from shallow-water scleractinians.\r\n sea pens are colonial benthic animals and belong to the class octocorallia (subphylum anthozoa). sea pens inhabit a wide depth range worldwide, from shallow waters  near coral reefs to the deep sea. sea pens possess unique ecological traits among anthozoans, such as bioluminescence, inhabiting soft-sediment environments and active behaviors like burrowing. however, microbiome studies on the superfamily pennatuloidea have not been adequately conducted. because sea pens occupy a wide bathymetric range and have unique ecological traits, clarifying their microbiomes may provide new insights into how habitat depth and host phylogeny shape holobiont organization in anthozoa.\r\n to resolve the holobiont composition of pennatuloidea, we are conducting a comprehensive characterization of bacterial communities associated with sea pens using 16s rrna metabarcoding. in this presentation, we will present preliminary observations based on the 16s rrna gene and discuss holobiont diversity in sea pens.",
