          "name": "Marina Botana",
          "search_text": "marina botana lipid signaling molecular pathways and the elucidation of a new hope for coral reefs survival under warmer oceans coral reefs are among the most diverse ecosystems in the biosphere, yet they face accelerating decline as seawater temperatures rise and marine heatwaves intensify. although the molecular drivers of coral-algal symbiosis homeostasis and heat-driven dysbiosis remain incompletely understood, recent research has suggested that oxylipin mediated lipid signaling may serve as a modulator of symbiosis homeostasis and heat tolerance. here we identify conserved lipid and oxylipin signatures associated with symbiotic stability and heat resilience by integrating physiological measurements, molecular phenotyping, and comparative transcriptome mining in the cnidarian model exaiptasia diaphana and in environmental samples from the reef-building coral isopora palifera during the 2024 great barrier reef el ni\u00f1o heatwave. across both model and field samples, symbiosis with homologous or thermotolerant symbionts was characterized by up to 300-fold enrichment of omega-3 fatty acids and their corresponding enzymatically-derived oxylipins of symbiont origin. these metabolites were consistently enriched in non-bleached vs bleached phenotypes, suggesting that symbiont-derived lipid flux supports metabolic compatibility and influences thermal stability. in the sea anemone model, only hosts colonized with their homologous symbiont exhibited decreased concentrations of oxylipins known to be inflammatory biomarkers in higher metazoans, suggesting that downregulation of the host immune system may be a mechanism of symbiotic homeostasis. in contrast, bleached anemones and corals exhibited lipid profiles marked by oxylipins derived from oxidative stress and enrichment of oxylipin prostanoids associated with inflammation and immune system activation. these signatures aligned with canonical stress-response cascades of higher metazoans, indicating that heat-induced symbiosis breakdown may be mediated by conserved inflammatory and cell-death pathways. our findings reveal conserved oxylipins and lipid-signaling pathways that differentiate stable from destabilized symbioses across taxa and environmental contexts. these molecular candidates represent targets for mechanistic studies and potential biomarkers or intervention points for selective breeding and assisted-evolution strategies aimed at enhancing coral resilience in warmer oceans.",
