          "name": "Mitsuru Jimbo",
          "search_text": "mitsuru jimbo proteomic analysis reveals symbiont-dependent lipid metabolism and transport pathways in juvenile acropora corals corals thrive in tropical and subtropical regions. they harbour symbiodiniaceae in order to obtain nutrients, but the mechanisms involved are not fully understood. when juvenile polyps acquire symbiodiniaceae, the number of symbiosis-related proteins should increase in proportion to the number of symbiodiniaceae cells. in this study, we explored the proteins that increased in line with the number of symbiodiniaceae cells and elucidated their function.\r\nthe juvenile polyps of acropora digitifera and a. tenuis were acquired with cladocopium sp. or durusdinium sp. and reared for several weeks. as the number of symbiodiniaceae cells increased, the polyps were collected and proteomic analysis was carried out. zoxanthellal proteins, peridinin-chlorophyll a-binding proteins (pcp), proportionally increased according to the number of symbiodiniaceae. thus, the proteins correlated with pcp were explored. saposin, apod and collagen \uf061\u03b15(iv) were found in a. digitifera with durusdinium sp. ccmp2556. saposin, long-chain-fatty-acid-coa ligase, and collagen \uf061\u03b12 (iv) were found in a. tenuis with cladocopium and durusdinium. notably, a. tenuis with cladocopium contained proteins related to the energy production pathway. as saposin and apod are related to lipid metabolism and transport, the lipid content of juvenile polyps was examined. juvenile polyps of a. tenuis with cladocopium contained more triglycerides than those with durusdinium, and larger lipid droplets accumulated in the epidermal cells of the former. these results suggest that photosynthates from symbiodiniaceae are transported and stored as lipids in the epidermal cells. collagen (iv) was reported to form holes in the mesoglea to transport substances between gastrodermal and epidermal cells in hydra spp. this suggests that acropora harbouring certain symbiodiniaceae species promote lipid accumulation and transport to epidermal cells.",
