      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Yuta Miura",
          "search_text": "yuta miura advancement of coral measurement and classification method using remote-controlled boat and ai submitter has made a specific request of only being considered for a poster presentation within the session on 21 july.\r\n\r\ncoral reefs play a crucial role in increasing fishery resources by providing habitats for diverse aquatic life. however, their large-scale degradation due to rising sea temperatures necessitates the development of effective, wide-area coral reef conservation and propagation techniques. though monitoring vast coral reefs is indispensable for verifying the effects of these techniques, conventional underwater visual surveys are often impractical when there are constraints on the frequency and period of field work, especially on remote islands. to address this challenge, we developed a method that efficiently photographs seabed surfaces using an underwater camera mounted on a remote-controlled boat, coupled with an ai model for coral classification and coverage measurement.\r\nthis study was conducted in two demonstration fields: the okinawa waters (urasoko bay on ishigaki island) and the okinotorishima waters. we installed 100-meter transects in each field and conducted photographic surveys using the remote-controlled boat. from the acquired images, we classified the corals into two groups: by category, coral and non-coral, and by shape using a deep learning ai model\u2013which recognizes the boundaries of objects such as coral from the images using an instance segmentation method that detects object regions. we subsequently validated its accuracy.\r\nthe validation results confirmed the method\u2019s high efficiency and accuracy. regarding shooting efficiency, the time required for a 10-hectare survey was reduced from 18 days for diver-based video recording to just 3.3 days, a reduction in time to approximately one-fifth. furthermore, the ai model\u2019s classification accuracy was high in both fields, achieving an overall accuracy of 85% to 93%. for class-specific categories, including coral and non-coral, and shape-based (corymbose) classifications, an f-score of approximately 0.8 was obtained. these results suggested that this method is highly useful for wide-area coral distribution measurement and classification.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Advancement of Coral Measurement and Classification Method Using Remote-Controlled Boat and AI",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "04ecfe5d-1c4e-4ffd-81d5-4772cf04da64"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
