          "name": "Jackson Ferber",
          "search_text": "jackson ferber genomic architecture of divergent eco-morphs in sympatric congener corals sympatric speciation was long dismissed due to the homogenizing forces of gene flow and recombination. this was especially true in the ocean, where planktonic larvae and a lack of geographic barriers should promote gene flow. despite this, many sister species of coral co-occur.  we compared genomic differentiation in two such species from the gulf of california, porites panamensis and p. sverdrupi. phylogenetic analysis using snp markers suggested that p. sverdrupi is nested within a paraphyletic p. panamensis, despite its divergent morphology and ecology: p. panamensis forms lobed colonies attached to boulders, while p. sverdrupi colonies are branched, free-living, and associated with rhodolith beds. to identify genomic changes underlying this divergence, we assembled a highly contiguous genome of p. panamensis, with a busco score of 98%, an n50 of 22.2 mb, and an l50 of 8. we then sequenced 50 p. panamensis and 51 p. sverdrupi from sympatric populations to a targeted depth of coverage of 16x, allowing us to characterize the genomic architecture of divergence. we hypothesized that fst outlier regions would include genes associated with p. sverdrupi's free-living lifestyle and environmental perception, such as photoreceptors. to understand the potential origins of these outliers, we searched for historical introgression from other p. panamensis sublineages. understanding the genomic catalysts of this free-living lifestyle will help provide insights into the genomic architecture of this lifestyle transition, which has occurred multiple times in scleractinians.",
