          "name": "Matthew-james Bennett",
          "search_text": "matthew-james bennett influence of reef structure and community composition on early post-settlement survival the survival of coral settlers on a reef is regulated by multiple selective pressures, both biotic and abiotic. coral colonies can impact the physical and biological characteristics of their immediate environment through the structures they develop, thus influencing these selective pressures in complex relationships with other reef taxa. while theory suggests that the coexistence of highly diverse coral species found on tropical coral reefs is the result of classic ecological mechanisms such as niche partitioning and direct spatial competition, the balance between competitive and facilitative processes across life stages and between species in reef-building corals remains poorly understood. here, we explore niche construction on coral reefs in the context of post-settlement survival by analysing the spatial relationships between primary coral settler survival and their surrounding environment. a high-resolution map of a ~150m\u00b2 area of horseshoe reef near jiigurru on the great barrier reef was produced through structure-from-motion photogrammetry and subsequently annotated to spatially locate and identify all coral colonies to the lowest taxonomic group possible. coral gametes from acropora millepora and platygyra daedalea were collected in situ during the annual mass spawning event and resulting larvae were reared and settled at the lizard island research station before settlement devices were deployed across the annotated site. post-settlement survival in both species was monitored repeatedly for 7 weeks following deployment and the survival was modelled against the neighbouring coral community composition and structural elements at expanding spatial scales surrounding each device.  our observations of spatial and temporal variation in post-settlement dynamics for these two species provide informative insight into the community ecology at this vital life stage.",
