          "search_text": "patrick buerger transcriptome-wide snp variation linked to thermal tolerance in heat-evolved symbiodiniaceae to enhance coral thermal tolerance, we subjected cladocopium goreaui to four years (~200 generations) of experimental evolution at increasing temperatures up to 31\u00b0c. the ten heat-evolved (he) strains exhibited lower reactive oxygen species secretion, stable photosynthetic performance, and growth compared to wild-type (wt) strains (maintained at 27\u00b0c). rna-seq analysis of in hospite he (three) vs wt (one) strains at ambient temperature suggested constitutively elevated levels of carbon fixation and reduced photosynthesis rates contribute to thermal tolerance. in addition, we performed a genome-wide snp analysis on the same rna-seq data set and identified 15,640 polymorphic variants across 4,739 scaffolds (covering 36% of the expressed genome). while this approach captures only actively transcribed regions and likely underestimates genomic diversity, most snps were shared across strains with no strain specific private alleles. however, significantly different allele frequencies of 339 snps on 155 scaffolds separated wt and he strains, with 11 additional snps distinguishing among he strains. some of the significant snps affected genes related to photosynthesis and thermal stress, and patterns of allele frequency variation as well as low linkage disequilibrium for a subset of the scaffolds indicated that recombination of standing genetic variation contributed to strain differentiation. these results highlight the evolutionary potential of symbiodiniaceae through recombination of existing genetic variation and suggest that future experimental evolution studies should prioritize genetically diverse starting material to speed up adaptation.",
