          "name": "Remy Oddenyo",
          "search_text": "remy oddenyo optimal placement of voluntary community coral reef closures in kenya: integrating community voices in conservation spatial planning at different scales coral reefs are under high pressure in tropical nearshore fisheries due to overfishing and climate change, putting communities that depend on these ecosystems and fisheries at risk. one of the options available to communities to increase climate resilience and improve ecosystems and fish stocks is to establish voluntary small-scale fisheries (ssf) community closures, otherwise referred to as locally managed marine areas (lmmas). we brought together 86 fishing communities from all five coastal counties in kenya, along with government agencies and ngos, to collaboratively develop a participatory approach to identify optimal locations for ssf closures that will contribute to climate resilience, conservation of coral reef habitats and fisheries recovery. as part of this process, we held national meetings to discuss community needs and ideas around coral reef fisheries management, engaged community members in experiential learning about reefs under different management regimes and evaluated benefits and costs associated with closure placement in a community comanaged area. here we present national feedback from communities on perceptions and needs around ssf closures where >50% highlighted increased income, fish catch and conservation of resources as reasons to establish closures in focus group discussions, lessons learned from engaging fishers in experiential learning, and findings from a consultative workshop to evaluate socioecological tradeoffs in ssf closure placement.  incentives, ecological status and perceptions on coral reef conservation outcomes are variable between communities and integrating community voices is a means of addressing scientific knowledge gaps on socioecological drivers and perceptions at different scales. the process implemented in kenya is a means to catalyze effective community-led management in coral reef habitats and demonstrates a framework to integrate community voices in conservation spatial planning at local and national scales.",
