          "name": "Lucy Gorman",
          "search_text": "lucy gorman gene expression of preferred (acropora hyacinthus) and non-preferred (porites spp.) crown-of-thorns sea star (cots) prey during cots attack crown-of-thorns sea star (cots) outbreaks are a main cause of hard coral cover decline across the indo-pacific, posing a major threat to the resilience of coral reefs. however, the drivers underlying cots feeding on preferred (e.g., acropora spp.) versus non-preferred (e.g., porites spp.) are poorly understood. we have previously shown that these coral genera harbour different sets of venom toxin homologs e.g., jellyfish toxins (cfxs) in porites species versus aerolysin domain pore-forming toxins in acropora species. we further found that two toxins unique to indo-pacific corals were under positive selection, emphasising that their evolution may be influenced by exposure to cots. to investigate these findings in the field, we collected gene expression data from a cots preferred (acropora hyacinthus) and a non-preferred (porites spp.) coral prey species in moorea, french polynesia, during the 2022-24 cots outbreak. we compared transcriptomic responses (rnaseq) of healthy colonies of both species to those under active predation by cots. our findings show that a. hyacinthus altered expression of genes involved in defense, stress responses and metabolic pathways in response to cots predation. specifically, a. hyacinthus colonies under cots predation significantly upregulated genes corresponding to putative toxins we previously identified, demonstrating the potential for coral toxins to aid in cots defenses. in contrast, porites spp. exhibited less metabolic stress and changed expression of genes involved in xenobiotic responses (i.e., response to foreign substances). these findings imply that preferred cots prey exert more metabolic stress during cots predation compared to less preferred prey species. high metabolic stress may result in these species exhausting more energy reserves during cots attack, reducing their ability to repeatedly produce energetically expensive defensive compounds such as toxins to defend themselves.",
