          "name": "Jonathan Jung",
          "search_text": "jonathan jung drivers of atlantic sargassum expansion and demise the great atlantic sargassum belt first appeared in 2011 and quickly became the largest interconnected floating biome on earth. in recent years, sargassum stranding events have caused substantial ecological and socio-economic impacts in coastal communities. sargassum requires both phosphorus (p) and nitrogen (n) for growth, yet the primary sources of these nutrients fuelling the extensive sargassum blooms remain unclear. here we use coral-bound n isotopes to reconstruct n2 fixation, the ultimate source of the ocean\u2019s bioavailable n, across the caribbean over the past 120 years. our data indicate that changes in n2 fixation were primarily controlled by multidecadal and interannual changes in equatorial atlantic upwelling of \u2018excess p\u2019, that is, p in stoichiometric excess relative to fixed n. we show that the supply of excess p from equatorial upwelling and n from the n2 fixation response can account for the majority of sargassum variability since 2011. sargassum dynamics are best explained by their symbiosis with n2-fixing epiphytes, which render the macroalgae highly competitive during strong equatorial upwelling of excess p. \r\n\r\ncomplementary satellite and in situ observations reveal that, in contrast to explosive growth in the tropical atlantic, sargassum biomass in the north sargasso sea has declined sharply since 2015, alongside a collapse in its historic seasonal growth cycle and a switch to advection-dominated supply from the gasb. when considered alongside extended coral-based reconstructions, these contrasting trajectories raise the possibility that recent tropical proliferation may be altering basin-scale pathways of limiting nutrients in ways that affect the sargasso sea.",
