      "search_text": "max planck institute for chemistry alan foreman jonathan jung alan foreman expansion of the indian ocean subtropical gyre over the 20th century subtropical gyres are wind-driven, strongly stratified ocean regions that cover 40% of earth\u2019s surface. these so-called \u2018ocean deserts\u2019 are characterized by nutrient impoverishment and low biological productivity, and their perennial warmth has profound impacts on atmospheric circulation and climate. satellite observations suggest that subtropical gyres have been expanding and migrating polewards since the late 1990s in response to anthropogenic global warming. however, significant uncertainty remains as to the sign and magnitude of such changes because they are easily obscured by interannual and decadal climate variability and because of the relatively short length of the satellite record. \r\n\r\nhere we present records of the 15n-to-14n isotopic ratio of organic matter preserved in the skeleton of shallow-water scleractinian corals from the mozambique channel, extending back 145 years. toward the present, these corals increasingly record the nitrogen isotopic signature of the indian ocean subtropical gyre (iosg), suggesting that the iosg has been expanding since early in the 20th century. while the observed global expansion of the atmospheric hadley cells may have contributed, we find evidence that trade wind enhancement of the south equatorial current acted as a significant control on iosg expansion over the 20th century. such a driver would likely have enhanced the leakage of water from the agulhas current into the atlantic basin over 20th century, which may have worked to stabilize the atlantic meridional overturning circulation in the face of global warming. decadal oscillations in the ocean\u2019s largest oxygen-deficient zone the impact of global warming on the ocean\u2019s oxygen-deficient zones (odzs) is uncertain, partly because of a lack of data on past changes. we report monthly resolved records of coral skeleton\u2013bound nitrogen isotopes (cs-d15n) to reconstruct denitrification in the eastern tropical north pacific (etnp) odz over the last 80 years. the data indicate strong decadal variation in etnp denitrification, with maxima during the cool north pacific phase of pacific decadal variability. the maxima in denitrification (and thus oxygen deficiency) were likely due to stronger upwelling that enhanced productivity leading to greater oxygen demand in the thermocline. prior findings of multidecadal-to-centennial odz trends were likely biased by this variability. odz evolution over the next century will depend on how global warming interacts with the decadal oscillations. jonathan jung drivers of atlantic sargassum expansion and demise the great atlantic sargassum belt first appeared in 2011 and quickly became the largest interconnected floating biome on earth. in recent years, sargassum stranding events have caused substantial ecological and socio-economic impacts in coastal communities. sargassum requires both phosphorus (p) and nitrogen (n) for growth, yet the primary sources of these nutrients fuelling the extensive sargassum blooms remain unclear. here we use coral-bound n isotopes to reconstruct n2 fixation, the ultimate source of the ocean\u2019s bioavailable n, across the caribbean over the past 120 years. our data indicate that changes in n2 fixation were primarily controlled by multidecadal and interannual changes in equatorial atlantic upwelling of \u2018excess p\u2019, that is, p in stoichiometric excess relative to fixed n. we show that the supply of excess p from equatorial upwelling and n from the n2 fixation response can account for the majority of sargassum variability since 2011. sargassum dynamics are best explained by their symbiosis with n2-fixing epiphytes, which render the macroalgae highly competitive during strong equatorial upwelling of excess p. \r\n\r\ncomplementary satellite and in situ observations reveal that, in contrast to explosive growth in the tropical atlantic, sargassum biomass in the north sargasso sea has declined sharply since 2015, alongside a collapse in its historic seasonal growth cycle and a switch to advection-dominated supply from the gasb. when considered alongside extended coral-based reconstructions, these contrasting trajectories raise the possibility that recent tropical proliferation may be altering basin-scale pathways of limiting nutrients in ways that affect the sargasso sea.",
