          "name": "Laura Lardinois",
          "search_text": "laura lardinois gambling on grazers: investigating herbivorous fish-microbe symbioses in a changing ocean host-microbe symbioses are crucial to host and ecosystem health. herbivorous coral reef fishes are widely recognized for controlling algal overgrowth and preserving resilient reefs, but the role of microorganisms in facilitating this has only recently gained attention. herbivorous reef fishes rely on their gut microbiomes to digest complex algal polysaccharides and release metabolites. we investigate how seasonal upwelling alters herbivore microbiomes, microbial function, and host diet. we take a multi-omics approach, integrating dna metabarcoding, metabolomics, and stable isotope analyses (sia), to study host-microbe symbioses in species from two families: damselfishes (pomacentridae) and surgeonfishes (acanthuridae). we sampled fish gut microbiomes across two gulfs in panama\u2019s tropical eastern pacific before, during, and after upwelling. additionally, we conducted fish surveys during the upwelling and non-upwelling seasons to assess whether fish communities changed seasonally. we find that while the composition of fish communities does not change significantly between seasons, our four herbivores\u2019 diets do shift. this includes an enrichment in cyanobacteria in most fishes\u2019 diets during upwelling (23s rrna data), as well as a slight decrease in \ud835\udec515n values (sia), suggesting fishes may be eating at lower trophic levels during this period. contrary to our expectations, the seasonal effect of upwelling on fish microbiome communities is relatively minor compared to the strong effect of host species, with gut microbiomes clustering tightly by species rather than season or region. overall, these findings suggest that herbivorous fishes and their microbiomes are resilient to upwelling-driven environmental shifts: they appear to slightly modify their diets seasonally yet maintain similar feeding ranges and gut microbial communities. seasonal environmental variability may confer greater resilience to environmental disturbances, allowing these fishes and their microbiomes to maintain their control of algal overgrowth. however, as climate change intensifies, it may outpace their adaptive capacity and drive vulnerable reef ecosystems past a critical threshold. sister species an ocean apart: investigating the drivers of coral reef fish microbiome composition across the isthmus of panama three million years ago, the rise of the isthmus of panama caused dramatic environmental shifts that set populations of coral reef organisms on separate evolutionary trajectories. populations separated by the isthmus adapted to different environments, forming pairs of sister species. these sister species each harbour unique microbial communities that may have adapted in tandem with their hosts, creating an unparalleled natural experiment to understand the evolution of host-microbe interactions. we investigate the ecological and evolutionary factors driving microbiome composition using seven pairs of sister reef fish species in panama. we predicted that the fishes\u2019 skin microbiomes should cluster by ocean, being shaped primarily by the surrounding environment, whereas the gut microbiome would be more strongly driven by host species, such that sister fish gut microbiomes would be more similar. we find evidence for the importance of the host in structuring both skin and gut microbial communities, suggesting long-lasting and important symbioses. however, as predicted, gut microbiomes were more strongly linked to host species than skin microbiomes, likely due to the selectivity of the internal gut environment. gut microbiomes were more similar across species within a trophic group and between sister species pairs, indicating that shared host traits play a role in structuring microbiomes. these sister fish species and their distinct evolutionary histories are an ideal system to expand our understanding of host-microbe interactions and, consequently, our ability to protect the diversity of organisms faced with rapid environmental change.",
