          "name": "Hudson Feremaito",
          "search_text": "hudson feremaito blue prosperity vanuatu national coral reef expedition: key findings & management implications the nation of vanuatu relies heavily on coral reefs to support nationally important industries such as fishing and tourism. however, as one of the most disaster-prone countries in the world, vanuatu\u2019s reefs are subject to continuous disturbance from events such as cyclones, earthquakes, and volcanic eruptions, in addition to global stressors such as climate change. until recently, data on vanuatu\u2019s reefs had been somewhat sparse and localized, and no nationwide survey of reef health had been conducted. to fill this gap and support vanuatu\u2019s marine management objectives, the vanuatu government, in partnership with blue prosperity vanuatu, conducted a spatially extensive survey of forereefs across the nation, covering 109 sites across the full latitudinal extent of the country. \r\n\r\nat each site, surveys were conducted to assess fish community structure, benthic percent cover, juvenile coral density, reef rugosity, macroinvertebrate abundance, and water quality. these data, taken together, provide a holistic understanding of the current state of vanuatu\u2019s reefs at the national, provincial and island scales, and comprise an important baseline for understanding reef health across the country. mean nationwide coral cover and fish biomass were moderate for the region, with values of 19.8% and 151.9 g /m\u00b2, respectively. overall, juvenile coral density was high compared to other inhabited pacific reefs. however, commercially important invertebrates, such as sea cucumbers, giant clams, and trochus, were found at low densities, indicating potential overharvesting. no traces of sewage pollution were found on 10m forereefs, though impacts closer to shore warrant further investigation. however, variability across provinces was high, revealing natural gradients as well areas with higher human impacts. understanding this variability is essential for supporting effective marine spatial planning and targeted coral reef management across vanuatu.",
