          "name": "Georgia Lennox",
          "search_text": "georgia lennox leveraging local knowledge to develop micro-fragmentation techniques for community-based coral restoration micro-fragmentation is a methodology that has shown promise for increasing the growth of slow growing corals but is often inaccessible to developing countries due to its high costs. to make the methodology more applicable to all countries, this project is trialling micro-fragmentation techniques with the assistance of community groups in bali, indonesia. we aimed to determine if micro-fragmentation can be completed entirely in-situ, and assess the long-term survival of micro-fragments using cost-effective methods. our methodologies were strongly informed by the experiences of the sea communities team, as well as previous collaboration with them. they provided knowledge on suitable designs for in-situ nurseries, transplant substrates and access to a locally designed, low-cost adhesive called \u2018eka glue\u2019. this approach allowed for the use of locally available, inexpensive materials that require minimal training or expertise, meaning the methodology can be adopted by a wide range of community groups. \r\nthe methodology utilised an underwater hammer drill, hole saw and \u2018eka glue\u2019 to harvest consistently sized fragments and attach them to cement substrates in a nursery table. this allowed the whole process to be completed in-situ. a total of 735 micro-fragments have been harvested from 135 mother colonies across three sites. the first 360 micro-fragments were harvested in late 2024 with preliminary data showing that micro-fragment mortality is 24% and mother colony mortality is 0%. the mother colonies have shown minimal reduction in health, with multiple \u2018wounds\u2019 from harvesting now being undetectable. growth rates of the micro-fragments are being monitored using 3d photogrammetry, with growth rates of up to 6.88 cm2 per month. overall, the preliminary data suggest that depending on the site conditions and genera of coral, micro-fragmentation may be an effective in-situ coral restoration method, making it easier for community groups to implement.",
