          "name": "Luc Feuillerat",
          "search_text": "luc feuillerat reconstructing sedimentary architecture and relative sea-level change from reefal sediment facies and fossil assemblages in the late middle pleistocene minatogawa formation, okinawa island, japan quaternary coral reefs provide key insights into past environmental changes and reef development. on okinawa jima, the minatogawa formation (mf) records relative sea-level fluctuations and reef growth during the late middle to late pleistocene. this formation, up to ~20 m thick, consists of coral and foraminiferal limestones divided into four units separated by unconformities. we examined core sections from 12 boreholes totaling 188 m, including two shore-perpendicular transects (t1: holes 1\u20136; t2: holes 1b\u20135b) and one intermediate hole (c). our objectives are to reconstruct the 3d facies architecture and clarify the depositional, diagenetic, and sea-level history of the mf. we used core logging, x-ray ct imaging, and microfacies analysis to characterize the core material, and bulk-carbonate \u03b4\u00b9\u2078o and \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3c measurements to identify subaerial exposure surfaces. six facies were identified: five coral-rich types (framestone, rudstone, floatstone, grainstone, carbonate mud-rich packstone) and one well-sorted foraminiferal grainstone. coral assemblages differ between transects. t1 is dominated by porites with morphologies ranging from massive near the coast (holes 1 and 2) to laminar and branching in generally finer sediments inland (holes 3 and 4), suggesting a less exposed setting in inland holes. t2 features in situ corals only in holes 1b and 2b near the coast, dominated by robust branching acropora and encrusting merulinidae, suggesting a shallow high-energy environment. bioclastic grainstones contain mainly large benthic foraminifers, and fewer planktic foraminifers likely sourced from older shimajiri group siltstones. stable isotope data, erosional surfaces, and paleosols indicate five subaerial exposure events. coral framestones in holes 1b, 2b, and c preserve three cavity infill stages\u2013carbonate mud, lbf grainstone, and soil-derived clay\u2013reflecting regression, flooding, and modern exposure. our study suggests that sea level change and wave energy control the spatiotemporal distribution of coral assemblages and lithofacies in the mf.",
