          "name": "Marc Humblet",
          "search_text": "marc humblet variation in fossil coral assemblages over the past 500,000 years around hawai\u2019i, based on iodp expedition 389 cores quaternary fossil reefs provide critical insights into climate and sea-level fluctuations and their effects on reef ecosystem, but data from glacial lowstands and transitional periods remain scarce. submerged fossil reefs offer a unique record of these events, particularly along rapidly subsiding margins like hawai\u2019i, where expanded reef sequences have the potential to provide higher resolution records of sea-level and reef growth histories. in 2023, iodp expedition 389 (hawaiian drowned reefs) drilled 35 holes into fossil reef terraces around hawai\u2019i at depths of 132\u20131242 m, penetrating up to 75 m and recovering 425 m of core with 66% average recovery. these cores span the last 500 kyr and primarily consist of reef frameworks composed of corals, coralline algal crusts, and microbialite. we documented the taxonomy, growth form, and context (in situ vs. non in situ) of fossil corals, coralline crust thickness, bioerosion intensity, and vermetid and homotrema abundances. we show porites has been the dominant reef builder for at least 500 kyr with varied growth forms: branching, columnar, (sub)massive, and laminar. the main associated coral taxa include cyphastrea, pocillopora, montipora, and agariciidae. eleven distinct coral assemblages indicate environments ranging from shallow (<20 m) to lower mesophotic (>60 m) depths. thick successions of branching and columnar porites on two distinct leeward terraces suggest similar responses to sea-level rise during climate cycles 200 kyr apart. laminar corals are more common on windward sites, possibly reflecting higher energy or deeper paleowater conditions. small encrusting cyphastrea colonies consistently occur within thick algal crusts, while acropora appears only at one windward site, implying long-term rarity in hawai\u2019i. lithologic and fossil trends at reef tops are complex, lacking a single drowning signature, although several holes on the windward margin and south point are capped by rhodoliths.",
