      "search_text": "nagoya university luc feuillerat marc humblet luc feuillerat reconstructing sedimentary architecture and relative sea-level change from reefal sediment facies and fossil assemblages in the late middle pleistocene minatogawa formation, okinawa island, japan quaternary coral reefs provide key insights into past environmental changes and reef development. on okinawa jima, the minatogawa formation (mf) records relative sea-level fluctuations and reef growth during the late middle to late pleistocene. this formation, up to ~20 m thick, consists of coral and foraminiferal limestones divided into four units separated by unconformities. we examined core sections from 12 boreholes totaling 188 m, including two shore-perpendicular transects (t1: holes 1\u20136; t2: holes 1b\u20135b) and one intermediate hole (c). our objectives are to reconstruct the 3d facies architecture and clarify the depositional, diagenetic, and sea-level history of the mf. we used core logging, x-ray ct imaging, and microfacies analysis to characterize the core material, and bulk-carbonate \u03b4\u00b9\u2078o and \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3c measurements to identify subaerial exposure surfaces. six facies were identified: five coral-rich types (framestone, rudstone, floatstone, grainstone, carbonate mud-rich packstone) and one well-sorted foraminiferal grainstone. coral assemblages differ between transects. t1 is dominated by porites with morphologies ranging from massive near the coast (holes 1 and 2) to laminar and branching in generally finer sediments inland (holes 3 and 4), suggesting a less exposed setting in inland holes. t2 features in situ corals only in holes 1b and 2b near the coast, dominated by robust branching acropora and encrusting merulinidae, suggesting a shallow high-energy environment. bioclastic grainstones contain mainly large benthic foraminifers, and fewer planktic foraminifers likely sourced from older shimajiri group siltstones. stable isotope data, erosional surfaces, and paleosols indicate five subaerial exposure events. coral framestones in holes 1b, 2b, and c preserve three cavity infill stages\u2013carbonate mud, lbf grainstone, and soil-derived clay\u2013reflecting regression, flooding, and modern exposure. our study suggests that sea level change and wave energy control the spatiotemporal distribution of coral assemblages and lithofacies in the mf. marc humblet variation in fossil coral assemblages over the past 500,000 years around hawai\u2019i, based on iodp expedition 389 cores quaternary fossil reefs provide critical insights into climate and sea-level fluctuations and their effects on reef ecosystem, but data from glacial lowstands and transitional periods remain scarce. submerged fossil reefs offer a unique record of these events, particularly along rapidly subsiding margins like hawai\u2019i, where expanded reef sequences have the potential to provide higher resolution records of sea-level and reef growth histories. in 2023, iodp expedition 389 (hawaiian drowned reefs) drilled 35 holes into fossil reef terraces around hawai\u2019i at depths of 132\u20131242 m, penetrating up to 75 m and recovering 425 m of core with 66% average recovery. these cores span the last 500 kyr and primarily consist of reef frameworks composed of corals, coralline algal crusts, and microbialite. we documented the taxonomy, growth form, and context (in situ vs. non in situ) of fossil corals, coralline crust thickness, bioerosion intensity, and vermetid and homotrema abundances. we show porites has been the dominant reef builder for at least 500 kyr with varied growth forms: branching, columnar, (sub)massive, and laminar. the main associated coral taxa include cyphastrea, pocillopora, montipora, and agariciidae. eleven distinct coral assemblages indicate environments ranging from shallow (<20 m) to lower mesophotic (>60 m) depths. thick successions of branching and columnar porites on two distinct leeward terraces suggest similar responses to sea-level rise during climate cycles 200 kyr apart. laminar corals are more common on windward sites, possibly reflecting higher energy or deeper paleowater conditions. small encrusting cyphastrea colonies consistently occur within thick algal crusts, while acropora appears only at one windward site, implying long-term rarity in hawai\u2019i. lithologic and fossil trends at reef tops are complex, lacking a single drowning signature, although several holes on the windward margin and south point are capped by rhodoliths.",
