          "name": "Kyle Morgan",
          "search_text": "kyle morgan field evidence of \u201cdark bleaching\u201d in corals caused by light deprivation from extreme turbidity coral bleaching is widely recognized as a heat-induced stress response under elevated temperature and light. however, bleaching can also occur under severe light limitation, leading to the loss of coral symbionts and pigmentation. despite strong mechanistic understanding from experimental studies, mass bleaching under low-light conditions has rarely been documented in the field. here, we report community-scale dark bleaching on an equatorial reef during a turbidity event that occurred under normal thermal conditions. measurements of seawater optical constituents revealed high chromophoric dissolved organic matter (cdom) and fine suspended sediments prior to bleaching. these conditions compressed the euphotic zone to ~5 m depth and reduced underwater irradiance to near-dark levels across the reef slope. depth-stratified ecological surveys of 836 coral colonies showed clear vertical zonation in bleaching severity that closely matched the light profile. at 3 m depth, most colonies remained healthy (82%), whereas at 5 m approximately 75% of colonies exhibited intense orange\u2013red fluorescence, and at 7 m more than 90% of colonies were fully bleached. repeat surveys after 30 days showed improved underwater light conditions as cdom concentrations declined with the transition between monsoons. corals remained bleached or fluorescent, and only minor mortality was observed. these findings demonstrate that extreme light deprivation can induce mass bleaching comparable in severity to heat-driven events, disproportionately affecting deeper coral assemblages as coastal water quality declines.",
