          "name": "Jen-hong Chu",
          "search_text": "jen-hong chu optimizing land-based culture of the giant clam hippopus hippopus through nutritional technology: a cross-sector innovation pathway to support coral reef restoration giant clams (hippopus hippopus) are ecologically important megabenthic filter feeders in coral reef ecosystems, contributing to nutrient recycling, primary productivity enhancement, and reef resilience. however, seed production and grow-out capacity remain major bottlenecks for reef restoration programs that integrate giant clams as ecological engineers. here, we present a cross-sector collaborative effort linking aquaculture science, feed biotechnology, and conservation practitioners to improve land-based rearing efficiency of juvenile h. hippopus.\r\na 180-day controlled feeding trial evaluated four novel dietary sources\u2013zooplankton, glucose, fish-meal hydrolysate, and microalgae\u2013on the growth, digestive enzyme activity, and survival of juvenile clams (25 g). zooplankton-based feeds significantly enhanced growth performance, clearance rates, protease activity, and overall nutrient assimilation (p < 0.05). nutritional profiling further identified optimal feed characteristics that maximize physiological performance, including particle size (1\u20131.2 \u03bcm), crude protein level (68%), and essential fatty acid composition (epa 4.65%; dha 5.12%).\r\nour findings demonstrate that targeted nutritional engineering can substantially improve hatchery productivity and survival of giant clams, thereby reducing dependence on wild broodstock and supporting scalable reef restoration initiatives. this research highlights a replicable cross-sector model\u2013linking academia, local aquaculture farms, and governmental conservation programs\u2013to accelerate technology access and innovation for coral reef restoration. integrating optimized clam culture into reef intervention strategies may enhance ecosystem-level resilience and biodiversity in a changing climate.",
