          "name": "Hiroki Kise",
          "search_text": "hiroki kise phylogenomic and systematics of zoantharia the integrated application of molecular phylogenetic and morphological analyses has revolutionized systematics and our understanding of evolutionary relationships across many marine taxa. one such group is the hexacorallian order zoantharia, which inhabits a wide range of marine environments, from shallow coral reefs to the deep sea. intensive research over the past two decades has led to the establishment of four macrocnemic families and 16 genera, reflecting a major revision of zoantharian taxonomy and a substantial improvement in the resolution of their phylogenetic framework. nevertheless, these advances have also highlighted the limitations of traditional genetic markers, which frequently lack sufficient phylogenetic resolution to fully resolve evolutionary relationships within zoantharia. as a consequence, support values for several taxonomic groups remain low, and their phylogenetic placements are often unstable. in recent years, ultra-conserved elements (uces) and exon-based markers have become increasingly popular, as they provide a much larger number of informative sites distributed across the genome and allow more robust resolution of both deep and shallow evolutionary relationships in anthozoa. as such, they represent powerful tools for stabilizing zoantharian phylogeny and resolving long-standing taxonomic uncertainties. here, we perform phylogenomic reconstruction based on uces and exonic loci to refine the systematics of zoantharia. we present these results and discuss their implications for the systematics and understanding of the evolutionary history of this group.",
