          "name": "Kodai Gibu",
          "search_text": "kodai gibu comprehensive metabarcoding analysis of symbiotic microorganisms associated with acropora digitifera in the coastal waters of japan reef-building corals form coral holobionts through associations with diverse symbiotic microorganisms, including symbiotic dinoflagellates and prokaryotes. these microbial partners play crucial roles in coral growth and in the response to environmental stress. although high-throughput sequencing has facilitated extensive characterization of coral-associated microbial communities worldwide, such studies remain limited in japanese coastal regions. moreover, coral-associated microbial assemblages are known to be highly sensitive to environmental stressors, such as thermal stress.\r\n  in this study, we aimed to elucidate the microbial community structure of acropora digitifera inhabiting the coastal waters of japan and to determine how these communities relate to thermal stress conditions. we collected a. digitifera colonies along a latitudinal transect across japan, as well as colonies exposed to different levels of thermal stress around sesoko island, okinawa prefecture. metabarcoding analyses were conducted to assess microbial community composition and its relationship with seawater temperature parameters.\r\n across japan, cladocopium was the dominant symbiotic dinoflagellate associated with a. digitifera, whereas durusdinium was detected in regions where environmental pollution has been reported. prokaryotic communities were dominated by the family endozoicomonadaceae, consistent with previous global observations. prokaryotic diversity tended to increase with rising seawater temperatures. no clear correlation was observed between latitude and prokaryotic community composition, and substantial variation in prokaryotic communities was detected even among colonies from the same sampling site.\r\n these results suggest that the structure of prokaryotic communities associated with a. digitifera in japanese coastal waters is influenced by a combination of localized environmental factors, including microhabitat-scale thermal stress.",
