          "search_text": "naoki saito connectivity of coral reefs along the kuroshio current highlights the need for transboundary conservation maintaining connectivity among coral populations through larval dispersal is crucial for their long-term persistence. coral reefs of the yaeyama and miyako islands in japan are rich in biodiversity but have deteriorated rapidly over recent decades. these reefs are expected to receive coral larvae transported by the kuroshio current from regions outside japan. however, previous genetic and oceanographic studies have focused on connectivity among reefs within japan. in this study, we simulated larval dispersal of acropora coral across the northwestern pacific over a 30-year period (1994\u20132023). our aim was to identify the main source regions supplying larvae to the yaeyama and miyako islands. larval survival rates, which decrease with age, were modelled based on past rearing experiments, with a maximum lifespan of 50 days. the results showed that 86% of the virtual larvae reaching the yaeyama and miyako islands were self-recruited. among externally sourced larvae, approximately 70% came from the philippines and 20% from taiwan, whereas less than 5% originated from japan. the kuroshio current functioned both as a corridor promoting dispersal from the northeastern philippines and eastern taiwan, and as a barrier to cross-current or upstream dispersal from northern taiwan and japan. these findings suggest that most external coral larvae arriving at the yaeyama and miyako islands originate from coral reefs outside japan, upstream of the kuroshio current. this study highlights the critical need for transboundary collaboration to understand and conserve coral population connectivity along the kuroshio current.",
