          "name": "Danielle Jayewardene",
          "search_text": "danielle jayewardene pathways to recovery: an integrated framework for indo-pacific threatened corals at this critical juncture for the world\u2019s coral reefs, it is essential that organizations, practitioners, and the public recognize that reversing reef decline and recovering imperiled species remain achievable goals supported by actionable pathways. the u.s. national oceanic and atmospheric administration\u2019s national marine fisheries service (noaa fisheries) is developing a recovery plan for 15 indo-pacific reef-building coral species listed as threatened under the u.s. endangered species act. noaa fisheries is working to support, coordinate, and catalyze conservation efforts across the region, and, where appropriate, implement actions directly. effective coral recovery requires addressing both global and local threats while advancing innovative biological and environmental interventions that reduce extinction risk, enhance resilience, and buy time until global threats are mitigated. in alignment with u.s. priorities, this work integrates ecological recovery with the socio-economic well-being of coastal communities, recognizing that healthy reefs sustain livelihoods, food security, and local economies. to guide decision-making, we apply scenario planning and portfolio efficiency analysis to identify priority activities under multiple plausible futures, highlight spatial priorities across ecoregions, and elevate emerging research, management approaches, and intervention strategies. this approach aims to provide a cohesive framework that blends innovation, practical conservation expertise, and socio-economic considerations to drive systemic change to enable the recovery of threatened indo-pacific corals, promote ecosystem and coastal community resilience, and advance sustainable fisheries and economies.",
