          "name": "Gang Liu",
          "search_text": "gang liu tracking daily spatial and temporal evolutions of mass coral bleaching heat stress at global coral reef locations over the satellite data record of 1985-2025 following the 3rd global coral bleaching event, the world has experienced a multi-year period of regional mass coral bleaching events, culminating with the declared multi-year 4th global coral bleaching event. this global event led to record-setting coral bleaching in florida and the caribbean across 2023 and 2024 due to extreme temperatures, with greatly expanded temporal and spatial coverages, rapid intensification, and increased accumulation of bleaching heat stress. this new paradigm of active and rapidly developing marine heatwaves has prompted the need for a new, near real-time monitoring and assessment tool for coral reef management. this tool must quantitatively monitor the daily change in reef exposure to heat stress and assess spatial and temporal evolutions of heat stress at all relevant scales (daily to annual and local to global) as well as provide concise and easy-to-read summary information for any given day in a historical context. here, we examine temporal and spatial expansions of reef areas exposed to severe bleaching-level heat stress on a daily basis using noaa coral reef watch\u2019s (crw) satellite data record of 1985\u20132025. a daily climatology documenting which coral reef grids have been exposed to select levels of bleaching level heat stress since 1985 (at degree heating week thresholds of 4, 8, 12, 16, and 20 \u00b0c -weeks, a key index of the crw satellite product suite) was developed as the baseline for near real-time tracking of the daily exposure of reef grids. the numbers of reef grids exposed to high stress levels on many individual days of year have dramatically increased over the years, indicating these reef grids now start to experience high stress much earlier and/or stress dissipates later than in the past. this tool will more effectively and efficiently provide coral reef managers and scientists with near real-time bleaching heat stress information.",
