          "name": "Rasheeda Alexander",
          "search_text": "rasheeda alexander beyond biological monitoring: developing a real-time dashboard for public sentiment on coral reef disturbances coral reefs provide economic goods and services worth about $375 billion each year, yet their protection often relies on public support during crisis events. while agencies like noaa have strong systems for monitoring the biological health of reefs, there are few tools available to track social sentiment and how the public feels and reacts during these disturbances. this project fills that gap by using python-based web scraping to track public attention on social media.\r\n\r\nusing a dataset of reddit post titles from 2010 to 2025, we tracked how often people posted about coral reefs during major storm seasons. to ensure we captured actual public conversation rather than academic discussions, we tracked the everyday words people use to talk about storms, like \"hurricane damage\" and \"storm surge\", rather than complex scientific terms. results show that public attention spikes immediately after events like hurricane maria, but these spikes are often very short.\r\n\r\nbuilding on these findings, this project is evolving into an interactive dashboard for resource managers. this tool will allow users to filter data by specific hurricanes or regions to see real-time shifts in public concern. we are also expanding the work to include professional networks like linkedin to compare how the general public reacts versus how professionals respond. this social monitoring tool will help managers time their outreach and funding requests to match the moments when the public is paying the most attention.",
