          "name": "Shay Viehman",
          "search_text": "shay viehman more than a decade of rapid coral community change on u.s. atlantic coral reefs from mass bleaching and disease - insights from the national coral reef monitoring program coral reef ecosystems throughout the u.s. atlantic have continued to decline in the face of persistent chronic and increasingly frequent acute stressors. repetitive heat stress events, including the record-breaking fourth global coral bleaching event in 2023-2024, and widespread coral disease, particularly stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld), have driven significant coral mortality across u.s. atlantic coral reefs. assessments of the impact of these stressors on coral populations and communities used coral demographic data from 2013 to 2025 from noaa\u2019s national coral reef monitoring program (ncrmp), the largest coral reef survey in the u.s.. in the u.s. atlantic, ncrmp uses a stratified random sampling design with in situ surveys to biennially survey hardbottom coral populations in florida, flower garden banks, the u.s. virgin islands, and puerto rico. results show severe declines in hard coral cover across all jurisdictions, with the exception of flower garden banks. overall disease prevalence peaked in southeast florida in 2023, florida keys in 2019, dry tortugas in 2022, and in the usvi in 2021, mostly corresponding with sctld introduction and spread to those areas, which are now all considered to be sctld-endemic. ncrmp data have captured signals of increasingly frequent and severe bleaching, with the highest bleaching prevalence in 2023 or 2024 across all surveyed jurisdictions. coral species that experienced the greatest (>75%) relative declines in density across the ncrmp survey history were dendrogyra cylindrus, meandrina meandrites, dichocoenia stokesii, and diplora labyrinthiformis and the greatest absolute declines were siderastrea siderea, porites astreoides, and orbicella franksi. declines of most species underscore the interacting impact of widespread disease outbreaks and persistent heat stress events to jointly reduce both species density and species diversity, reshaping coral community dynamics and threatening the long-term stability of reef ecosystems across the u.s. atlantic coral reef jurisdictions.",
