          "name": "Alison Moulding",
          "search_text": "alison moulding population management planning for the threatened caribbean elkhorn coral, acropora palmata ocean- and land-based coral restoration nurseries have been established in many locations throughout the greater caribbean and are run by a mix of for-profit, governmental, academic, and non-governmental organizations, each with their own goals, timelines, and restoration plans. despite widespread propagation efforts, the once dominant and currently threatened caribbean elkhorn coral (acropora palmata) has continued to undergo precipitous declines. an effort began in 2020 in florida to coordinate in situ and ex situ conservation efforts for a. palmata among researchers, managers, and restoration practitioners to better manage captive and outplanted population segments in a more collaborative and strategic way. we convened institutions and practitioners maintaining a. palmata and gathered genetic and demographic data on collections, propagation, and outplanting. the goal is to use this information to make recommendations and informed decisions designed to maintain demographic stability and protect existing gene diversity, ultimately safeguarding the species from extinction. through a comprehensive status assessment, we found that there are only about 150 unrelated genets (founders) remaining in florida, the vast majority of which have been extirpated in the wild and are only found in human care. many of these genets were held in a single location in a single institution, but through coordinated efforts, all founder genets are now represented in two land-based gene banks as recommended in annual management guidelines developed as part of the coordination. future short-term recommendations and goals of coordination include distribution of all founder genets among all institutions, equalizing representation of founder genets in human care so that none are over or under-represented, and minimizing mean population kinship through breeding recommendations to minimize risks from inbreeding and loss of diversity important for adaptability. this process for developing scientific population management for a. palmata in florida can be used as a model for other threatened coral species worldwide.",
