          "name": "Jonathan Whitney",
          "search_text": "jonathan whitney from dna to biomass: joint model reveals hidden half of reef fish communities coral reef biodiversity and assessments remain fundamentally limited by what we can visually observe. due to complexities on reefs, standard diver-based surveys systematically underestimate or completely miss substantial fractions of biomass and functional diversity. environmental dna (edna) metabarcoding has emerged as a powerful tool to detect this missing, or hidden diversity, yet its potential for quantifying abundance and biomass in species-rich reef systems remains largely unrealized. we introduce a modeling framework that integrates single-locus edna metabarcoding (mifish) with underwater visual surveys, structure-from-motion photogrammetry, and airborne hyperspectral mapping across coral reefs in the hawaiian islands. using a joint bayesian model, we translate edna read counts into calibrated biomass estimates for 74 reef-fish species (representing >90% of the community), while accounting for amplification biases, detection error, and differences in observation processes. integrating edna with visual count data reveals roughly three-fold higher species richness and double the estimated biomass, indicating that traditional surveys capture only about half of the reef fish biomass present. zooming in on this hidden half, we show that difficult-to-detect guilds\u2013cryptobenthic fishes, nocturnal predators, roaming herbivores, and hyperabundant planktivores\u2013assume redefined and often elevated roles, prompting a reimagining of their contributions to reef trophic structure. more than one-quarter of the most dominant species detected by dna are entirely absent from visual datasets, demonstrating that key functional players can be systematically missed. together, these results show that integrating molecular and visual data uncovers hidden biomass, reshapes ecological inference, and establishes a flexible joint framework that can be continually refined to account for improved estimates of species-specific parameters (amplification, detection, shedding, etc). in this presentation, we will highlight how this integrated approach strengthens biodiversity assessments and provides a scalable template for improving monitoring of coral reef ecosystems globally.",
