          "name": "Halimah Razali",
          "search_text": "halimah razali harvesting sargassum for biofuel applications and reef restoration macroalgae living on coral reefs host a wide range of epibionts. unicellular eukaryotic epibionts like non-photosynthetic diatoms have potential for industrial and biofuel applications due to their metabolic pathways associated with the common and abundant brown seaweed, sargassum. for many applications, sargassum is needed as feedstock and large-scale harvesting may be needed. with over 1,000 metric tonnes of sargassum biomass produced annually in singapore's coastal waters and blooms noted globally, this application for seaweed removal and usage of their epibionts for economic incentives are valuable. however, monitoring and detailed assessment of the associated ecological impacts on coral reefs and epibiotic diversity are needed. this study aims to (1) perform bioprospecting for unicellular eukaryotic sargassum epibionts with potential biofuel applications; and (2) assess the ecological impacts of sargassum removal through benthic transects, canopy assessments, and coral recruitment monitoring with settlement tiles. ongoing bioprospecting efforts reveal ecological differentiation among epiphytic diatom lineages, with distinct oil yields and well-defined spatial and temporal divergences. we hypothesize that different species are associated with sargassum specifically during growth or decay phases. continued seasonal sampling is expected to uncover additional elite strains for potential biofuel development. this study addresses the need to understand the overlooked inhabitants of sargassum and their potential for biotechnology applications, while providing evidence-based guidance for reef management through manual seaweed removal interventions in urbanised tropical reef systems.",
