          "name": "Hazel Oakley",
          "search_text": "hazel oakley a carbonate budget assessment of a degraded southeast asian reef island platform. a multi-site carbonate-budget assessment has been conducted around the reef platform of a small southeast asian island, which has suffered severely from historical bomb fishing. a combination of high-resolution benthic surveys, in situ calcification and bioerosion measurements was used to calculate a standard reefbudget model. a heterogeneous benthic ecosystem with a wide range of carbonate production levels was revealed, including large areas of unproductive rubble, mixed assemblages of macroalgae, sponges and octocorals, as well as a few recovering scleractinian-dominated sites. \r\n\r\nthe results show that benthic reassembly following destructive fishing can result in pronounced spatial divergence with disparate carbonate budgets. the reef-crest zone was predominantly net erosional due to large expanses without any framework-building coral cover, very low levels of crustose coralline algae, and elevated bioerosion from endolithic borers and bumphead parrotfish. the consequences of the platform scale reduction of carbonate production are substantial. the loss of an accreting reef-crest increases the exposure to wave energy, reduces the natural coastal protection and alters the provision of ecosystem services. this study provides an empirical foundation to identify priority zones for restoration in order to maximise the recovery of ecosystem functions and long-term platform stability.",
