          "name": "Jenny Fong",
          "search_text": "jenny fong different strategies in the competitive ability of crustose coralline algae against newly settled coral spat scleractinian corals and crustose coralline algae (cca) are the primary reef builders and their ecological interactions have generally been viewed through the lens of positive, facilitative species interactions. in addition to creating stable frameworks for corals to grow, cca are known to induce the settlement of coral larvae. fewer studies, however, have explored the interactions between cca and coral larvae following settlement. to investigate this, we first assessed the ability of four common cca species (crustaphytum sp., lithophyllum sp., porolithon cf. onkodes, and titanoderma cf. tessellatum) to induce settlement of acropora millepora coral larvae in standard well plate assays. we then monitored the growth and survival of coral larvae that settled directly onto the surface of the four cca species over 10 weeks. our results showed that all four cca species successfully induced >50% larval settlement, although significantly lower settlement was observed in lithophyllum sp. compared to the other three cca species. survival of coral spat that settled on the surfaces of cca fragment was low (<20%) by 10 weeks post-settlement. we also different strategies were employed by cca to kill newly settled spat: 1) t. cf. tessellatum and crustaphytum sp. gradually overgrew coral spat, with crustaphytum sp. exhibiting a faster overgrowth rate than t. cf. tessellatum, 2) p. cf. onkodes formed bulges that pushed spat upwards, resulting in the dislodgement of spat from cca surface, while 3) lithophyllum sp. adopted deep-layer sloughing process, in which thick white patches developed directly underneath settled coral spat and were subsequently sloughed off. overall, this study provides empirical evidence that the interactions between cca and corals are multifaceted and dynamic, from facilitative during the coral larval stage to competitive during the early post-settlement phase. scaling up complexity to maximise the beneficial ecological effects of tropical hybrid shorelines as the demand for coastal protection intensifies in the face of climate change, alternatives to traditional hard engineering such as \u2018hybrid\u2019 shorelines are gaining traction. natural shorelines tend to be structurally complex, yet efforts to actively manipulate and enhance complexity of hard-engineered infrastructure has generally been restricted to small-scale interventions. to maximise the beneficial ecological effects, the design and planning of future coastal defences should consider incorporating complexity across scales ranging from millimeters to 100s of meters, thereby widening the variety of ecological niches available. in this project, we propose a \u2018meandering shoreline\u2019 concept that integrates artificial bays, headlands, coral reefs, and estuaries. our aim is to investigate the potential benefits of scaling up complexity in the context of hybrid shorelines, spanning intertidal zones to subtidal coral reefs. here we present results of surveys for biodiversity present in artificial headlands and bays versus linear seawalls and take a landscape design approach to enhancing coastal complexity through artificial estuaries. we also explore options for combining 3d construction printing with traditional casting techniques for fabricating topographically complex artificial reef units. finally, to evaluate the ecological resilience of tropical hybrid shoreline system, we assess ecosystem functioning, including productivity, carbon and nutrient cycling. overall, findings from this project will provide strategies for increasing the ecological benefits of human-constructed coastal protection systems.",
