          "name": "Jia Jin Marc Chang",
          "search_text": "jia jin marc chang marine biodiversity of southeast asia (mbssea): a call to arms for building hard-bottom cryptobenthic biodiversity baselines in the region the richness and diversity of life remain one of the most fundamental questions in science, yet a precise estimate of it continues to elude us, particularly in lesser-studied animal groups such as marine invertebrates. marine benthic communities, which support a rich biodiversity of such invertebrates, are ubiquitous and major sources of socio-economic importance, but are often understudied in biodiversity research. in particular, southeast asia, where the coral triangle is situated, is the richest hotspot in global marine biodiversity, but is threatened by habitat loss and degradation. against a backdrop of accelerated anthropogenic-induced change, we are potentially losing species faster than we can document and describe them. there is thus a need to extensively document the region\u2019s rich marine biodiversity to better track changes in spatial and temporal patterns in diversity. this presentation will introduce the marine biodiversity of southeast asia (mbssea), an ongoing five-year initiative (2024\u20132029) aimed at characterizing the marine hard-bottom biodiversity of key sites in southeast asia with autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms). arms are artificial units that provide a standardised way of sampling hard-bottom benthic communities. when combined with dna sequencing technology, arms allows for high-throughput assessment of community richness and diversity across local and regional scales. it also presents a yet untapped opportunity to characterise new species and local records. this presentation will cover the objectives and goals of mbssea, provide an overview of the ongoing arms work, and serve as a launchpad to gather interest and collaboration with researchers or stakeholders working in the region and field.",
