          "name": "Samuel Yong Jie Diong",
          "search_text": "samuel yong jie diong same arms, different results? assessing coi primer bias in cryptobiome metabarcoding autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms) provide a globally standardised framework for characterising the cryptobiome of coral reefs. arms studies focusing on the metazoan community rely on metabarcoding based on the cytochrome c oxidase subunit i (coi) gene, yet biodiversity estimates are expected to differ substantially due to variations in amplification conditions. because the mitochondrial coi region is highly variable across marine metazoans, primer\u2013template mismatches may lead to differential amplification, introducing biases in richness estimates, taxonomic representation, and perceived community structure. despite this known issue, few studies have directly compared primer sets on identical arms-derived assemblages, limiting our ability to evaluate the magnitude and implications of primer-driven variation. this study examines how two widely used reverse primers, the inosine-containing jghco2198 and the degenerate lobor1, shape metabarcoding outcomes from arms deployed in singapore\u2019s highly urbanised reef ecosystems. twenty-one arms were installed across seven sites for one year. extracted arms samples are analysed through coi metabarcoding targeting the 313 bp leray fragment (mlcoiintf paired with either jghco2198 or lobor1). we compare primer sets in terms of recovered motu richness, taxonomic breadth, and primer-specific influences on community composition. by characterising these differences on identical arms samples, this study aims to clarify the extent to which primer choice affects biodiversity estimation accuracy in cryptobiome research. our findings will inform methodological refinement for global arms metabarcoding protocols and support more robust, comparable assessments of reef cryptofauna.",
