          "name": "Stacia Loong",
          "search_text": "stacia loong safeguarding manipulated holobionts: microbial risk profiling to prevent pathogen exposure in heat-resilient coral transplants scalable, science-informed restoration increasingly relies on assisting coral evolution through holobiont manipulation. however, the translation of laboratory-reared, heat-resilient genotypes with manipulated holobiont to the reef can be compromised by microbial risks in the receiving environment. to address this, we integrated long-read nanopore sequencing into a multi-criteria decision analysis (mcda) framework to profile transplantation sites in singapore\u2019s urbanized reefs. we characterized the baseline microbiomes (with full-length 16s metabarcoding sequencing) of four target coral species, pocillopora acuta, goniastrea pectinata, pachyseris speciosa and porites lutea, across candidate transplant sites to identify biological risks prior to the introduction of the corals. moving beyond traditional ecological monitoring, this high-resolution genomic approach allowed us to detect specific pathogen reservoirs. analyses revealed elevated latent disease risk at specific sites that may compromise transplant survival. these microbial risk profiles were weighted alongside ecological viability (e.g., coral cover change over time, genetic connectivity, etc), environmental suitability (e.g. temperature, turbidity, etc.) and socio-economic factors, to generate a site suitability heatmap. we demonstrate that pre-transplantation risk profiling is a critical step in future-proofing restoration, ensuring that climate-adapted corals are excluded from zones where microbial dysbiosis threatens their resilience.",
