          "name": "Sze Hui Foo",
          "search_text": "sze hui foo diet of the urchin diadema setosum on urbanised reefs established through dna metabarcoding sea urchins are key herbivores on coral reefs, particularly on degraded systems. however, the limited taxonomic resolution of existing urchin dietary studies hinders comprehensive assessments of reef trophic dynamics in response to their population fluctuations. here, we present a highly resolved diet characterisation of diadema setosum on the degraded reefs in singapore based on coi and 18s metabarcoding, assessing among-population variation across seven reefs. we detected 31 phyla of algae, small invertebrates, and other metazoans in their diet, with an overwhelming abundance of macro- and microalgae, indicating a strong herbivorous preference in this species. marked spatial variation in dietary profiles was observed among populations, largely reflecting the on-site algal distribution. sargassum emerged as the dominant diet item in most urchins, challenging prior reports of aversion by d. setosum. additionally, our results suggest preferential feeding on lobophora by the urchins at a site with one of the highest hard coral abundances in singapore, implying potential top-down control on this allelopathic alga and highlighting the urchin\u2019s role in mediating coral-algal competition. the importance of bacillariophyta (diatoms) in their diet was also evident. given the widespread use of sea urchins as biomonitors in pollution studies, we additionally explored the relationship between diet composition and pollutant loads in urchin tissues, including seven heavy metals and microplastics previously quantified from the same individuals. significant correlations between metal loads in urchin gonads/spines and the read abundances of four diatom species, as well as the rhodophyte hypnea, suggest a dietary pathway for metal uptake in d. setosum. together, these findings provide critical insights into the ecological role of d. setosum on degraded reefs and illuminate potential routes of pollutant transfer within coral reef ecosystems.",
