          "name": "Wen Xin Sng",
          "search_text": "wen xin sng testing green concretes and 3d construction printing as options to enhance coral recruitment on artificial reef structures artificial reefs can contribute to the recovery of degraded coral reefs by providing stable structures that enhance biodiversity and promote coral recruitment. most artificial reefs are constructed using ordinary portland cement (opc)-based concrete due to its durability and low cost. however, opc has a high carbon footprint. sustainable alternatives such as green concrete are increasingly being explored, but their influence on coral larval settlement remains poorly understood. further, concrete artificial reef structures are traditionally fabricated using casting techniques, which yield smooth surfaces that are suboptimal for coral larval settlement. 3d construction printing (3dcp) can be used to fabricate complex geometries, while its inherently textured surfaces may better facilitate coral recruitment. to evaluate the potential of green concrete and 3dcp to improve artificial reef designs, we first explored the settlement preference of pocillopora acuta coral larvae on four concrete types (opc and three green concretes) and four surface textures in two ex-situ experiments. results revealed comparable settlement rates between opc and a green concrete made of ground-granulated blast furnace cement, while 3dcp-produced texture (rough-grooved surface) had significantly higher larval settlement compared to other textures. building on this knowledge, we are expanding the investigation to an in-situ experiment to evaluate how the performance of green concrete and 3dcp-produced textures affects coral larval settlement in a fully crossed experiment. twelve tile types will be fabricated and subjected to two conditioning durations before being deployed at two reef sites. 288 tiles will be deployed and retrieved after singapore\u2019s annual multispecific synchronous spawning to determine coral recruitment rates. findings from this study will provide insights into the potential of green concrete and 3dcp in promoting coral recruitment, which will help inform future artificial reef design and construction.",
