          "search_text": "zhi ting yip spatiotemporal edna monitoring of marine biodiversity in a hyperurbanised coastal environment monitoring marine biodiversity in complex and vulnerable habitats such as coral reefs is critical, yet traditional survey methods often struggle to capture cryptic taxa and fine-scale community turnover. environmental dna (edna) offers a promising solution, but its adoption in coral reef science methodology has been slow due to optimisation of assay capabilities, and the incompleteness of reference databases which constrains taxonomic resolution. using edna metabarcoding of the cytochrome oxidase subunit i (coi) gene to target invertebrates, and the 16s rrna gene for vertebrates, we examined temporal and spatial patterns of \u03b1- and \u03b2-diversity along the coastline of singapore over one year. results revealed differences in marine metazoan communities driven by high spatial and temporal turnover without strong directional trends across sites, including a coral reef. these patterns reflect distinct, cohesive communities with limited seasonality, characteristic of equatorial climates. there were also specific taxa that showed monsoon-associated distributions. this demonstrated that existing edna approaches are versatile and effective in reef settings, recovering diverse assemblages of invertebrates and vertebrates across multiple phyla, including rare and endangered species. while further refinement\u2013such as primer sets tailored to reef taxa and more mid- to long-term surveys \u2013will enhance precision and elucidate the community of resident species, current methods already provide a cost-effective and scalable addition to the coral reef monitoring toolbox. importantly, edna samples can be archived in biobanks, establishing baselines for future surveys, and enabling retrospective analyses as technologies advance. by integrating edna into reef monitoring, managers and scientists gain a powerful tool to safeguard biodiversity in urban seascapes by using it for long-term ecological monitoring and conservation planning.",
