          "name": "James Guest",
          "search_text": "james guest the coralassist plug: overcoming bottlenecks for sexual coral propagation one of the most promising techniques for reef rehabilitation is sexual coral propagation, which enables the production of genotypically diverse offspring and supports assisted evolution. however, this approach is limited by high costs, labour intensity, and low survivorship, restricting the current scale of application. there are no commercially available \u201coff-the-shelf\u201d devices specifically for settling, rearing, and outplanting sexually propagated corals that can be applied at scale across multiple species and environmental conditions. the lack of effective, scalable tools for coral sexual propagation is a major barrier to advancing reef restoration and assisted evolution. to address this, we have developed the coralassist plug (caps), a directly attached device designed to enhance the efficiency and scalability of coral sexual propagation. caps incorporate key innovations: efficient handling for settlement and rearing, built-in microrefugia to reduce early mortality from grazing, and a design that allows rapid, adhesive-free attachment to reefs. this allows a three-person dive team to outplant ~120 caps in one 90-minute shallow dive (just over two minutes per cap per person). long-term field trials in palau, micronesia using the corymbose coral acropora aff. digitifera have demonstrated high rates of survivorship. with long ex-situ nursery durations (6-months) it was possible to achieve 36% yield (i.e., proportion of devices with a surviving coral) 4-years post-outplant. with ex-situ nursery durations shortened to 1-month, we were able to attain 16% yield four-years post-outplant. microrefugia significantly enhanced post-outplant survivorship leading to an 11% increase in yield 4-years post outplant compared to devices without microrefugia. outplanted corals had reached adult size, were self-attached and were reproductively mature after 4 years. here we will discuss our vision to make caps available to a wide range of end users as a practical tool for applying coral sexual propagation at scale as part of reef restoration and assisted evolution efforts. gearing up for large-scale coral reef rehabilitation: a modular framework for efficient sexual propagation workflows combining the coralassist plug with coralclip coral sexual propagation carries great potential for large-scale coral reef rehabilitation, but feasibility rests on overcoming key bottlenecks in efficiency, infrastructure, and deployment. we developed an integrated framework to streamline production and outplanting using coralassist plugs (caps); these are coral settlement devices with microrefugia that have demonstrated enhanced juvenile coral survivorship on the reef. a modular framework of pvc trays (50 x 30 x 2 cm) with threaded rods was designed for use in all stages of the production workflow. each tray holds 500 caps and can be used sequentially for conditioning, settlement, grow-out in rearing tanks (10,000 caps per tank), transportation in coolers, and in trays for reef deployment, whilst minimizing handling and improving workflow continuity. the modular rods enable rapid microscope assessment for coral recruits and can be mounted on nursery trees for in-situ rearing. to increase outplant efficiency, we engineered two modified coralclip\u00ae designs for attaching caps to the reef: a single-bend design and a looped design to prevent caps from spinning. the performance of both coralclip\u00ae devices was compared to commonly used attachment methods: cement, epoxy, adhesive, nails, nylon push mounts, and self-drilling screws. the looped coralclip\u00ae was preferred by divers for ease of use, with the fastest attachment method (35 \uf0b1 2 seconds per cap), and achieved the highest overall yield (77%) with 92% retention and 80% survivorship after four months on the reef. these results demonstrate that rapid attachment methods can also deliver high yields. as outplanting juvenile corals remains the most resource-intensive step in coral sexual propagation, improvements in logistical and deployment efficiency directly translate to reduced costs and increased rehabilitation output. our modular framework and optimized attachment method demonstrate a scalable, field-tested solution for applying coral sexual propagation to reef rehabilitation at meaningful scales.",
