          "search_text": "ni wayan purnama sari depth matters: species-specific growth and survival of sixteen target species to drive sustainable coral mariculture in indonesia indonesia plays a leading role in the global ornamental coral trade, exporting over two million coral fragments annually from 71 species. sixteen species are particularly favoured in the industry and are classified into three growth-rate categories: fast (harvestable in 3\u20136 months), medium (8\u201312 months), and slow-growing (over 24 months). however, these classifications are based solely on time and lack biological validation. this study evaluated the growth and survival performance of 16 species across two depths (10\u202fm and 20\u202fm) in an in-situ husbandry facility. coral growth was assessed using linear and vertical extension metrics over a 13-month period. depth had no significant effect on overall survival or growth across most species, though cynarina lacrymalis showed notable sensitivity. growth performance varied significantly among categories, with fast-growing species achieving \u2265\u202f7.5\u202fcm/year in linear and \u2265\u202f6\u202fcm/year in vertical extension. medium and slow-growing species exhibited consistently lower rates. these biologically derived growth thresholds can be used to refine indonesia\u2019s national coral mariculture guidelines, ensuring that harvest decisions are based not only on time but also on species-specific growth performance, supporting more sustainable and standardised coral farming practices. \u201cunder pressure: how coral harvesting reshapes population structure in the heart of the coral triangle\u201d southeast sulawesi lies in the heart of the coral triangle and serves as indonesia\u2019s primary source region for the coral harvesting trade. yet despite its global ecological importance, the population-level consequences of intensive coral extraction remain poorly quantified. this study provides the first empirical assessment of how harvesting pressure influences the population structure of ten commercially targeted coral species across reefs under contrasting management regimes. understanding these impacts is essential for evaluating the sustainability of indonesia\u2019s coral trade and for safeguarding the ecological integrity of coral reef ecosystems in their global epicentre. underwater photo surveys were conducted at six harvested and six non-harvested reef sites using 150 m\u00b2 belt transects. high-resolution images were processed in imagej using the sizeextractr software to extract standardised metrics of colony presence, frequency, and size distribution. these indicators of population structure are being compared across management zones to detect potential signatures of extraction, including reduced colony abundance, truncated size classes, or shifts in species representation on harvested reefs. preliminary image assessments reveal spatial variation in colony occurrence, and ongoing analyses aim to quantify the magnitude and patterns of population differences attributable to harvesting pressure. by integrating image-based monitoring with a comparative design across indonesia\u2019s most heavily exploited coral source region, this study provides novel, urgently needed evidence on the ecological costs of coral extraction. the findings will provide a scientific basis for improving harvest regulations, optimising mpa design, and informing sustainable management strategies within the coral triangle, where maintaining coral population resilience is vital for global biodiversity, fisheries, and reef conservation.",
