          "name": "Sophie Carolan",
          "search_text": "sophie carolan constructing ecological networks for caribbean reefs: functional consequences of the 2023 tela bleaching event ecological networks map the interactions that link species and functional groups, providing a systems level view of how ecological processes are organised and how they respond to disturbance. although they are powerful indicators of functional change, ecological networks remain uncommon in marine research and are entirely absent for caribbean coral reefs. the pronounced community shift detected on the turbid reefs of tela bay following the 2023 bleaching event provides the empirical basis for establishing these networks for the first time.\r\nthe primary aim is to construct an interaction matrix that synthesises established ecological relationships, including functional roles, trophic pathways, and spatial competition patterns, among the coral, algal, benthic, and targeted reef fish taxa monitored across caribbean reef systems. once completed, this matrix will serve as a regional framework for developing site specific co-occurrence, functional, and disturbance focused ecological network models.\r\nco-occurrence networks will provide a baseline view of community organisation by highlighting patterns of species association. functional networks will illustrate how species interact through grazing, predation, competition, and habitat provision, helping to identify which ecological functions are maintained by which taxa and which roles may weaken as communities reorganise after bleaching. disturbance simulations will assess the vulnerability of these pathways to bleaching-related disruption and will identify where key ecological roles become concentrated in very few species.\r\nwith climate driven disturbances reshaping marine ecosystems rapidly, ecological network approaches offer a valuable advance beyond traditional monitoring by detecting early shifts in functional structure. the caribbean provides an ideal model region, where well documented disturbance histories and contrasting reef conditions allow network reorganisation under climate stress to be examined. this context also enables key functional processes such as grazing, habitat provision, or coral settlement support to be identified as drivers of recovery, which can help inform management strategies. decline of a turbid caribbean reef previously considered as climate-resistant turbid reef systems remain comparatively understudied across the caribbean. tela bay, honduras, is an inshore turbid reef system that historically supported unusually high coral cover for the caribbean, leading it to be regarded as a regional bright spot. however, in 2023, a heatwave caused a mass bleaching event leading to a stark decline in coral cover. the aim of this study was to document how a marginal, turbid reef that resisted past regional heat stress responded to the 2023 heatwave, and to characterise the resulting collapse in benthic community structure. \r\nbenthic surveys at 15 m depth in tela bay and utila showed that tela maintained consistently high coral cover (\u224335-60%) and low macroalgal cover (\u22437-30%) until 2023, when thermal stress caused a sharp decline in coral cover to \u22437% and an increase in macroalgae to \u224335-45%. prior to 2023, z-scores placed tela\u2019s coral cover above the regional mean and showed little year-to-year variation, indicating a stable state. benthic assemblages in tela were consistently dominated by hard corals, particularly agaricia spp, with little change across years. post bleaching, coral cover dropped to below the regional mean and showed greater variability.  benthic community collapsed and gave way to a community composition more typical of contemporary caribbean reefs where soft coral and millepora dominated and coral community become governed by stephanocoenia spp, dichocoenia spp and madracis spp.\r\nthese findings illustrate how a historically stable, coral-dominated turbid reef that had previously demonstrated resistance to regional heat stress collapsed rapidly following the 2023 bleaching event, showing that earlier stability does not ensure continued protection under escalating thermal stress.",
