          "name": "Andrea Carolina Landaverde Ventura",
          "search_text": "andrea carolina landaverde ventura coral immunity across different reef environments in the middle florida keys reef-building corals are increasingly threatened by disease outbreaks, driven by increasing impacts of climate change combined with anthropogenic pressure, highlighting the need to better understand coral immune responses. corals rely on innate immunity, a non-specific defense mechanism that involves mucus barriers, pathogen recognition receptors, phagocytosis, melanin production, antimicrobial peptide production, and other defense mechanisms. recent research suggests that coral immunity is not only modulated by genetic markers but also by environmental and ecological factors, as well as the composition and density of their symbiotic algae. the goal of this study was to evaluate the natural variation in coral immune responses between two species of reef-building corals across a gradient of environmental conditions in inshore, midshore, and offshore reefs in the middle florida keys, and to determine how the symbiont community composition and density influence these responses. we collected coral tissue samples from 2-5 colonies from each of two species (orbicella faveolata and siderastrea siderea) from eight reef sites in june 2024 and conducted transcriptome analyses (rna-seq) and symbiont quantification. preliminary results show clear species-specific patterns in symbiont density and community structure: orbicella faveolata presents relatively stable symbiont densities and largely uniform symbiont community composition across reef types, while siderastrea siderea displays more variable symbiont proportions that shift across the inshore-offshore gradient. additionally, initial rna-seq analyses suggest a subset of genes that may be differentially expressed among reef types, potentially indicating an environmental modulation of immune-related pathways. these findings will improve our understanding of how environmental conditions influence coral immune mechanisms and disease susceptibility across reefs, providing information that can support reef management and conservation efforts.",
