          "name": "Eric Wade",
          "search_text": "eric wade cultural identity, access, and the changing landscape of subsistence fishing subsistence fisheries support the food security, cultural identity, and resilience of millions of coastal households, yet they remain largely absent from fisheries governance and ecological assessments. fisheries governance continues to prioritize commercial and industrial sectors, overlooking the multi-scalar and multi-dimensional importance of subsistence fisheries. at the same time, ecological change, shifts in species abundance, habitat degradation, and increasing competition from tourism and coastal development have begun to erode communities\u2019 access to key finfish species. we examine these dynamics in belize, where subsistence finfish harvests remain deeply rooted in cultural identity and well-being. drawing on household interviews, participatory mapping, and fisheries-dependent and independent data, we explore how access, availability, and use of subsistence species are being reshaped. we found that subsistence fisheries typically target a small portfolio of species, primarily in nearshore habitats. fishers consistently described declining abundance, smaller sizes, and reduced catch reliability, patterns that aligned with ecological monitoring data. households also reported rising prices, reduced fishing effort, and competition from tourism operators, which collectively narrow the space for subsistence harvests. our findings illustrate that ecological trends and social practices are tightly coupled, revealing cross-scale feedback that shapes both vulnerability and adaptive capacity. as countries expand spatial regulations and update coastal management strategies, failing to consider subsistence fisheries may undermine food security, cultural identity, and social cohesion. integrating subsistence fisheries into policy discussions is therefore essential for designing equitable, ecologically grounded coastal governance.",
