          "search_text": "kelly van woesik sea\u2018urchin\u2019 for solutions across scales: carbonate production suppressed by sea-urchin bioerosion in hawai\u02bbi coral reefs provide vital ecological, economic, and social services, yet climate change and local stressors are compromising their functionality. indeed, local fishing pressure can lead to high sea-urchin densities, which erode reef structures. this study estimated carbonate production across depths in h\u014dnaunau bay, hawai\u02bbi using census-based field surveys and utilized airborne imaging spectroscopy to scale estimates across the bay. fishing pressure has led to sea-urchin densities of ~51 individuals m-2 \u2013 among the highest densities on reefs globally \u2013 which are causing substantial bioerosion and suppressing reef-growth potential, particularly in the shallows (3 m). average net carbonate production was ~0.5 kg caco3 m-2 y-1 from 2\u201317 m depth, with values ranging from -2.1 kg caco3 m-2 y-1 (at 3 m) to 2.4 kg caco3 m-2 y-1 (at 6 m). a 2-m resolution map of live coral cover enabled scaling-up of carbonate production estimates. maintaining positive net carbonate production presently requires more than 26% live coral cover. estimated vertical accretion rates of ~0.5 mm y-1 across depths indicate the reef in its present state is not keeping pace with current sea-level rise (3.55 mm y-1) in west hawai\u02bbi \u2013 emphasizing the need for management actions that strengthen resilience of carbonate budgets to ensure a future for coral reefs. the results of this study suggest a critical need for improved fisheries management to increase abundances of carnivorous fishes to help reduce sea-urchin densities and enhance reef growth.",
