          "search_text": "zachary zagon characterizing endozoicomonas aggregates across u.s. coral reefs: unraveling implications for restoration and conservation efforts the coral holobiont is implicated in the health and resilience of corals to a variety of climate and anthropogenic stressors. evidence suggests that elucidating key microbes in the coral holobiont could be leveraged to improve restoration outcomes through microbiome engineering and beneficial microorganisms for corals (bmcs). coral-associated microbial aggregates (camas) are distinct clusters of bacteria within the tissue layers of corals typically dominated by endozoicomonas. significant knowledge gaps remain regarding the functional and ecological roles of camas, especially outside of the indo-pacific. current postulations about camas consider them key secondary symbionts between the coral and symbiodiniaceae as well as important providers of conditional benefits to the coral host during stress events. to expand our knowledge on endozoicomonas-camas and their relationships to global endozoicomonas populations, we are studying u.s. coral species from hawaii to florida. this includes north carolina astrangia poculata and oculina arbuscula that can survive with or without symbiodiniaceae, providing an ideal system for exploring cama dynamics with the coral and other microbes in vivo during simulated stress events. we are also characterizing camas in important species from florida (acropora cervicornis, siderastrea siderea, orbicella spp., etc.) and hawaii (montipora capitata, porites compressa) through histology and sequencing (metagenomic and 16s) to further understand the dynamics between endozoicomonas aggregation, environmental factors, and coral health. together, these studies will improve our understanding of endozoicomonas-camas and can inform restoration practitioners and researchers about the potential to use microbial engineering techniques to target endozoicomonas-camas to improve restoration and conservation efforts.",
