          "name": "Annabel Hughes",
          "search_text": "annabel hughes population genomics of holacanthus angelfishes in the tropical western atlantic studying systems where hybridization occurs between recently-diverged species offers a unique glimpse into the process of speciation and provides a deeper understanding of how species barriers are formed and maintained in nature. in marine systems, hybridization events are common due to a lack of physical barriers to gene flow, especially in coral reef fishes. holacanthus angelfishes in the tropical western atlantic (twa) provide an ideal system to explore the impacts of hybridization and assess the strength of species barriers with continued gene flow. the two sister species in the twa\u2013the queen angelfish, h. ciliaris, and the blue angelfish, h. bermudensis\u2013diverged ~1.5 mya and share a large sympatric range throughout the gulf of mexico. h. ciliaris are found beyond this shared distribution throughout the greater caribbean, whereas the southern extent of h. bermudensis\u2019 range ceases at the onset of the yucat\u00e1n channel. hybrids of the queen and blue angelfishes, previously described as a distinct species (\u201ch. townsendi\u201d), are frequently observed and display intermediate phenotypes relating to body color pattern. the role hybridization plays in introducing genetic variation or, conversely, blurring the lines between parent species through homogenization of genetic material, remains undetermined. similarly, the initial driver of divergence and forces maintaining species boundaries between h. ciliaris and h. bermudensis despite continued gene flow are unknown. for this study, we conducted whole-genome sequencing on sampled populations of both parent species and hybrids throughout their shared and unique ranges to investigate 1) the extent and consequences of hybridization for holacanthus in the twa and 2) the ecological and/or genomic mechanisms driving the origin and maintenance of species boundaries. the findings from this work illuminate the impact of hybridization on the persistence of species barriers separating queen and blue angelfishes and the genetic architecture of speciation.",
