          "name": "Aidan Healy",
          "search_text": "aidan healy from establishment to in situ gamete collection: coral spawning hubs in southeast florida over the last decade, florida\u2019s coral reef has experienced a significant loss in stony coral cover and abundance due to sedimentation, terrestrial run off and marine heatwaves, which led to a stony coral tissue loss disease outbreak, bleaching and ultimately mortality of a large number of corals. the remaining reef-building corals are consequently geographically isolated, reducing the likelihood of successful fertilization during coral spawning events, a phenomenon known as the \u201callee effects\u201d. to address this problem, we established \u201cspawning hubs\u201d. specifically, based on results from a larval dispersal model, ten sites were identified as good potential larval sources in the system. ground truth surveys led to the selection of two sites, deemed the north and south spawning hubs. starting in july 2020, colonies of presumed sexually mature size found dislodged from the substrate were collected from diverse locations throughout the reef system and relocated to these sites. over a five-year period, 288 corals comprised of 15 species have been relocated to the spawning hubs, with 76% survival in the northern hub and 98% survival in the southern hub. as a result of the creation of the spawning hubs, we have now observed spawning of several species in the southern hub in multiple years: diploria labyrinthiformis (2022, 2024, 2025), colpophyllia natans (2025), pseudodiploria strigosa (2025), and pseudodiploria clivosa (2025). gametes that were collected in situ were fertilized, larvae reared and settled, and spats are being grown in an onshore nursery until they reach a size appropriate for outplanting on the reef.  this study highlights the role spawning hubs could play in assisting the natural recovery of the reef while serving as a reliable source of larvae for research and reef restoration.",
