          "name": "Alexander Wheeler",
          "search_text": "alexander wheeler latitudinal variation of intraspecific coral regeneration across temperature and anthropogenic stressor gradients. the regrowth of experimental wounding can serve as a colony health indicator known as the coral regeneration assay. regeneration incurs tradeoffs in allocation of metabolic resources between competing hierarchical systems, including somatic growth, immune response, and reproduction. a multitude of intrinsic (morphology, age, live tissue area, species, genotype) and extrinsic (temperature, light, eutrophication, sedimentation) factors affect growth, therefore a coral's capacity to regenerate from wounding is an indicator of a colony\u2019s health. stressed corals often have depleted metabolic resource pools which increase disease susceptibility and hinders growth and reproduction. understanding the drivers of regeneration and the impact of sampling (disease and predation correlations) are needed for this to be a useful indicator. this project analyzed annual regeneration rates of core lesions on eighty-seven orbicella faveolata colonies across a latitudinal and anthropogenic stress gradient in florida (1,717 cores). most colonies were in a state of repair with 25% of biopsied tissue regrown after three years. disease outcomes from the cores were low throughout the study despite high endemic sctld levels (~11% prevalence). regeneration rates differed by latitude and were positively correlated with colony fecundity and anthropogenic stress. after one year, colonies north of government cut miami regenerated nearly 50% more compared to those in key biscayne and the lower florida keys. disease history, genetic lineage, and predation were not significant in predicting regeneration rate. despite their proximity to anthropogenic stress, higher latitude corals in florida appear healthier than conspecifics further south. these results suggest that higher latitude reefs provide a refuge from thermal stress and highlight the value of conserving these systems for the survival of corals in a rapidly warming climate. hydrographic modeling is underway to determine the contributions of eutrophication from terrestrial-based nutrients.",
