          "name": "Bernhard Riegl",
          "search_text": "bernhard riegl comparable coral decline on artificial and natural reefs demonstrates challenges of in-situ coral restoration (persian/arabian gulf) artificial substrata are common features in the inter- and shallow subtidal of the highly urbanized southern persian/arabian gulf (pag). sometimes placed specifically as artificial reefs, more often primarily as coastal projection, they mostly integrate into the local ecology. we followed change in cover and composition of fauna/flora, focusing on corals, on the outer breakwater of palm jebel ali (pja; uae) and 10 natural reefs in southern pag from 2008-2024. the benthic assemblage on pja from 2008-2010 was characterized by bare rock, algal turfs and increasing cover by corals, bivalves and other invertebrates until 5 years after establishment in 2007, when it became coral-dominated. coral cover first increased until 2014 (pja 68%; pag reefs 40%) and then precipitously declined to very low levels in 2024 (pja 2%, pag reefs 3%) and turf algae cover increased (2024: pja 79%, pag reefs 72%). changes in bivalves and other invertebrates were subtle. coral assemblages on pja changed from dominance by porites spp. in 2008 to merulinid (cyphastraea until 2014, then platygyra). acropora disappeared from 2015 on pja and most pag reefs and has not been credibly recorded in southern pag since 2019. heat exceeded the coral bleaching threshold in 2012, 2015, 2020 and the mortality threshold in 2017, 2018, 2021, 2023, 2024. heat stress correlated negatively with coral survival (glm) and benthic assemblage composition changed along the heat stress gradient (cca) on both pja and pag reefs. since ocean heat caused similar community changes on artificial and natural reefs, the value of artificial reefs and in-situ nurseries for coral restoration appears compromised. the dramatic declines in coral cover and abundance make it harder to assess what management actions could better the fate of corals in pag.",
