          "name": "Ellen Regina Skelton",
          "search_text": "ellen regina skelton modeling and prioritizing sediment-associated and dissolved chemical contaminants of concern in the southeast florida, usa watershed the majority of florida\u2019s coral reef (fcr) is located close to shore and more vulnerable to runoff and pollution than offshore reefs. these local stressors can negatively impact coral\u2019s resilience and ability to recover from periodic stress events (i.e. increased temperatures), as well as reducing the capacity for adaptation to increasing climate change stress.  due to the urbanization of se florida, fcr is subjected to many sources of pollution including a range of chemical contaminants, such as antifouling compounds, hydrocarbons, trace metals, pesticides, herbicides, plastics, personal care products, cleaning products, and general waste from the large urban population. these pollutants can impact coral growth, photosynthesis, metabolism, reproduction and survival.  previous research identified copper, arsenic, and nickel as emerging dissolved contaminants of concern in the water column , with several others found in concentrations above limits recommended by the fdep  and epa.  additionally, high concentrations of numerous chemicals were found in sediments but there is a lack of data examining the effects of individual contaminants in sediment to corals.  in order to mitigate the effects of these contaminants, it is crucial to understand how these contaminants move through the watershed from their terrestrial source(s) to the reef.  \r\nwater and sediment quality data were collected from several databases including the water quality portal from the national water quality monitoring council, noaa national status and trends data, and the south florida water management district\u2019s dbhydro database. contaminants with concentrations above suggested thresholds from the fdep and epa were identified as contaminants of concern and specific locations (i.e. specific inlets or canals) were identified as areas of concern.  this data will allow us to model the flow of contaminants from source to sea and help to inform the concentration and duration of exposure that corals receive to assist in environmental impact assessments.",
