          "search_text": "ian johnson innovating coral sexual propagation ex situ: staggered spawning, integrated larval rearing to early grow-out systems, and sloped grow-out the mass production of new, genetically unique corals via ex situ sexual propagation remains laborious and expensive, hampering the ability to upscale reef restoration. recent innovations to the three phases of ex situ sexual propagation have led to increased production and better use of equipment and labor. spawning of the corals has been staggered to increase year round space and labor efficiency. this was achieved by shifting light and temperature cycles in the different induction systems by 4 and 8 months so spawning occurs every 4 months. creating out of season spawning schedules to have three distinct spawning windows allowed for a year-round usage of the lab space dedicated to the larval rearing, settlement and early grow-out (i.e. the first 3-4 months of culture) and improve labor allocation. an integrated larval rearing, settlement and early grow-out recirculating system allowed hands-free culture from fertilization to one month post-settlement. larvae are reared in recirculating conical tanks equipped with large filters at the bottom to increase water exchange rates, and hartford loops and water spray bars to prevent clumping of larva. when larvae become motile, they are drained by gravity and water flow to a large settlement basket on an adjacent, within-system raceway. the larval settlement baskets were made using a pvc pipe frame with an acrylic bottom tray, wrapped in a 105 \u00b5m mesh bag removable once settlement was complete. once the mesh bag is removed, the settlement tiles remain in the bottom tray of the now dismantled basked, allowing the newly settled corals to remain in the same tank for early grow-out and thus reducing early mortality. late grow-out (3-4 months to a year) is conducted in outdoor tanks in 60\u00b0 sloped racks which increases coral survival and growth (through reduction of algal overgrowth) and doubles tank space available for grow-out.",
