          "name": "Karen Neely",
          "search_text": "karen neely multidisciplinary assessments of a potentially novel acute tissue loss disease coral disease outbreaks are an increasingly common threat to reefs. while coral disease research is expanding rapidly, there are still monumental challenges in diagnosing and differentiating among the different diseases on a reef. we used a collaborative multidisciplinary approach to characterize a potentially novel disease affecting orbicella faveolata colonies in the florida keys. we tagged and fate-tracked individual lesions for progression and cessation rates, reanalyzed in situ photographs of previously monitored corals to determine prevalence and seasonality, and assessed the efficacy of amoxicillin treatments. samples collected from lesions as well as unaffected and healthy controls were used to examine microbiomes, assess nine coral pathological parameters including histological examinations, and measure 19 symbiont-specific physiological metrics using tem. across all analyses, we concluded that the observed disease is unlikely to be stony coral tissue loss disease, which co-occurs at these sites. some, but not all, metrics had similarities to previous descriptions of white plague, but the dearth of quantifiable histology and tem studies on white plague did not allow us to conclusively confirm or refute those comparisons. using the recommended coral disease nomenclature, we define this specific outbreak as \u201corbicella acute tissue loss disease\u201d (oatld). through this multidisciplinary analysis of field observations and laboratory techniques, we provide unprecedented, quantified descriptions across numerous metrics of both diseased and control colonies. we suggest that these data lay the groundwork for future efforts on this disease, a comprehensive set of parameters against which other diseases can be compared, and a model for multidisciplinary disease assessments. too hot to handle? how space, time, species, symbionts, and shading affected corals over three record-breaking years the 2023\u20132025 summers were the three hottest on record for florida keys reefs. the unprecedented 2023 marine heatwave led to 100% bleaching of coral colonies. survival through the event was assessed using an existing semi-monthly fate-tracking program of 4200 brain and boulder colonies across offshore and inshore reefs. the vast majority of colonies experienced no mortality. however, at two inshore sites where cumulative thermal stress reached 20 degree heating weeks, 30% and 43% of monitored corals perished. mortality was strongly species-dependent, with brain corals faring significantly worse than boulder corals. \r\nacross the 200km florida keys reef, spatial patterns in temperature exist both latitudinally and longitudinally, with coral reef watch data since 1985 identifying persistently warmer and cooler reef sites. within a single reef, in situ temperature loggers demonstrated that temperature thresholds were exceeded by a factor of 2 within just 100 meters. we compare this fine-scale variation in temperature to spatial patterns of mortality.\r\nan in situ experiment in 2024 assessed how shade cloths impacted bleaching both visually and via symbiont communities. despite degree heating weeks exceeding 11 (record breaking excepting the previous year), corals exhibited only moderate paling. durusdinium, a heat-tolerant symbiont, almost exclusively colonized these corals, presumably a result of heat stress the previous year. though shades tempered paling visually, they had little effect on symbiont:host ratios during this event. \r\noverall these studies identify widespread survival of brain and boulder corals through a major marine heatwave, but also spatial and species-specific variation. differences in survival as well as temperature exist at both large (100km) and small (100m) spatial scales. temporally, excessive temperatures in 2024 had minimal impact on 2023 survivors, ostensibly because of a shift in symbionts. yet even in this high temperature but mild paling year, in situ intervention through shading likely minimized stress on targeted corals. brick by brick: the efficacy and efficiency of keeping corals alive through disease intervention stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) causes unprecedented damages to infected reefs, including large-scale mortality on over 20 species, reduced coral cover and diversity, and diminished ecosystem services. an in-water topical medicine developed to halt active disease lesions exhibited high efficacy and has been used throughout much of the caribbean to treat affected colonies. treatments, however, are not prophylactic. new lesions can develop on previously-treated corals, though how frequent this is has not been well quantified. by fate-tracking nearly 2,500 treated corals across seven florida keys reef sites, we quantified reinfection rates through the first year of treatment as well as overall survival rates of treated corals. for corals that were part of a regular monitoring program, new lesion development continued to decrease with time, and survival after three years was 86%. the tissue saved from sctld-related mortality through intervention efforts in the florida keys is estimated at nearly 10,000 m2 of tissue, or the equivalent of 3.75 million coral outplants. though there was some variation among species and habitats, 36% of colonies remained disease free for at least a year after just one treatment visit, and 58% remained disease-free if colonies were visited again 1 to 2 months after initial treatment. this suggests that for affected reef sites that can only be visited one or two times, treatments still have substantial positive impacts on coral survival. we use intervention efforts in a remote national park to calculate that 25 days of single-treatment dive operations likely equated to saved tissue equivalent to 547,000 coral outplants. we further outline the pros and cons of concentrated intervention efforts on a small number of sites versus dispersed effort over many reefs.",
