          "name": "Kate Heffner",
          "search_text": "kate heffner assisted emigration of queen conch to the northernmost known mating aggregation in the us queen conch, aliger gigas, were listed as threatened under the united states endangered species act in february 2024 prompting special considerations before coastal construction projects. permitters now require the relocation and monitoring of individuals of every size to avoid take from direct impacts. relocating conch has many challenges including care for the animals during transport, survival, and integration at the receiver site. previous relocations have reported extensive mortality from predation and migration away from the receiver site. here we report on the largest permitted queen conch relocation effort to date in the united states. over the course of seven days, 138 conch were collected from the 8 hectare (80,000 m\u00b2) direct impact area and the 3.8 ha (38,000 m\u00b2) 50 m buffer zone.  each collected individual was tagged and relocated nine miles north to a 9 ha receiver site in pompano beach. weekly monitoring of the 50 m buffer zone yielded an average of 10.6 conch per week migrating into the direct impact area. initial assessment of the receiver site in september found 1,824 conch unevenly dispersed over 9 ha: 54.2% mature adults, 29.2% subadults, and 16.6% juveniles. the most concentrated hectare had 603 conch and another had 336 conch. 29 occurrences of mating, 51 conch laying eggs, and 45 unaccompanied egg masses were documented in september. two weeks after relocation, 27 tagged conch were found in the hectare they were placed and 18 in three randomly chosen adjacent hectares. ten occurrences of mating and six laying eggs were seen in november, well outside of their expected mating season. these results will be used to inform and adapt future conch relocation plans to reduce take during marine construction.",
