          "name": "Kristen Reed",
          "search_text": "kristen reed optimization of settlement and grow-out of two caribbean bouldering corals the sexual propagation of corals ex situ is a successful practice to produce corals for reef restoration, but further optimization is needed to increase production and scale up restoration. a major bottleneck often observed ex situ is low larval settlement and high mortality rates in the early weeks following settlement. using two caribbean bouldering coral species, colpophyllia natans and pseudodiploria strigosa, this study assesses whether colored substrates resembling those of crustose coralline algae (cca), a known settlement-inducing cue, can improve settlement success and whether coral growth orientation contributes to survival and growth. coral larvae were settled and reared in three indoor recirculating systems over two months to (1) compare the proportion of larvae settled on pink, white, vs. mixed-color settlement tiles, and  (2) determine how orientation (diagonal 60\u00b0 and horizontal 180\u00b0) affects post-settlement survival and tissue area growth. c. natans did not show a preference for color scheme, whereas p. strigosa had significantly higher settlement in the pink or white only color schemes (28% and 33% respectively)  compared to the mixed color scheme (22.5%). at two months, corals grown on diagonal pink tiles were significantly larger than those grown on different orientation and tile color combinations, specifically, 50% larger than horizontal pink tiles, 34% larger than diagonal white tiles, and 38% larger than horizontal white tiles. grow-out orientation significantly affected survival regardless of the tile color. corals grown diagonally had a four times greater chance of survival compared to those grown horizontally. these findings suggest that while pink tiles may not increase settlement, they can help improve growth post-settlement, and the combination of pink tiles and a diagonal grow-out orientation can further optimize the ex situ sexual propagation of corals.",
