          "name": "Liam Abrams",
          "search_text": "liam abrams optimization of light spectrum during the early grow-out of caribbean corals reef restoration intends to minimize the ongoing decline of coral populations and accelerate recovery. the sexual propagation of corals adds diversity to the populations. while the techniques to induce corals to spawn ex situ and rear their larvae are relatively well-established, the grow-out, i.e. rearing from newly settled to a size suitable for outplanting, remains yet to be fully optimized. specifically, light spectrum, food quantity and quality, and algal control require optimization. this study investigated the post-settlement survival and growth of the caribbean reef building corals colpophyllia natans and pseudodiploria strigosa under three light spectra: (1) near surface (natural sunlight), i.e. outdoors grow-out at land-based nurseries; (2) 10m reef depth (using radion lights); (3) blue-shifted (using radion lights) peaking between 400-450nm. the experiment was conducted at both nova southeastern university and the florida aquarium using the same larval cohort. all treatments underwent the same irradiance biweekly increase from 25 \u00b5mol photons m\u00b2s\u00b9 at week 0 to 200 \u00b5mol photons m\u00b2s\u00b9 at week 19. after seven months, the blue-shifted light spectrum presented a slight increase in survival rates for both species. pseudodiploria strigosa corals grew similarly under the reef-mimic and blue shifted light spectra, but the reef-mimic light spectrum c. natans reached almost twice the size of the blue-shifted light spectrum. the near-surface spectrum produced smaller corals. this study highlights that light spectra impacts the grow-out of corals, and artificial light spectra has the potential to increase the production efficiency of ex situ coral sexual propagation facilities.",
