          "name": "Michelle Mair",
          "search_text": "michelle mair a spatiotemporal analysis of population change in the caribbean common sea fan, gorgonia ventalina, on florida\u2019s coral reef in contrast to significant stony coral mortality, octocoral density has increased on florida\u2019s coral reef (fcr) over recent decades. specifically, the common sea fan gorgonia ventalina has become a key component of florida\u2019s octocoral community. however, trends that drive g. ventalina population distribution on fcr are unknown. here, we analyzed spatiotemporal changes in g. ventalina density and colony size to investigate population variation and responses to disturbances. from 2013-2024, long-term monitoring was conducted annually along permanent belt transects at 46 sites throughout the three regions of fcr: southeast florida, the florida keys, and the dry tortugas. density and colony height were recorded for all g. ventalina colonies along each transect and analyzed using generalized linear mixed models over time and across regions and habitat types. models were also created to analyze the coefficient of variation, kurtosis, and skew of height data. mean g. ventalina abundance and colony size varied spatially, though all regions experienced similar effects from acute disturbances over the study period. marine heatwaves in 2014 and 2015 and hurricane irma in 2017 did not significantly impact fcr\u2019s g. ventalina populations, revealing resistance to these acute disturbances. from 2019 to 2021, the population experienced significant growth and maintained a greater mean abundance from 2021 to 2023. size distributions shifted towards smaller individuals which likely included recruitment. this was most apparent on shallow habitats in each region, suggesting that environmental variables unique to these habitats may contribute to g. ventalina success. however, the population experienced decline following the 2023 heatwave, highlighting vulnerability in a previously stable population due to unprecedented ocean warming. long-term monitoring efforts of g. ventalina will continue to provide substantial knowledge on the expected trajectory of this species and further analysis is required to determine site-specific characteristics that influence g. ventalina success.",
