          "name": "Morgan Hightshoe",
          "search_text": "morgan hightshoe refining acropora cervicornis coral restoration in southeast florida using multiple long-term outplant studies from 2008-2025 in response to declining coral reefs, restoration programs have been established to promote coral conservation and recovery by collecting, propagating, and outplanting corals to depauperate reefs. despite extensive efforts throughout the region, prolonged evaluations of methodological factors that drive outplant success are limited. here we synthesize the results of five long-term (3-8 years) acropora cervicornis outplanting experiments conducted between 2008-2025 across 21 local reef sites in southeast florida to evaluate the impact of site, outplant density, colony size, and attachment method on survival and change in live tissue area (lta). analysis was limited to the first three years of each experiment to facilitate comparison. from 2008-2018, single a. cervicornis fragments were outplanted using epoxy, nail and cable ties, or cement pucks and survival varied widely (0-100%). beginning in 2019, we transitioned to outplanting multi-fragment clusters using epoxy or cement. mean 3-year outplant survivorship of the clusters, regardless of site and attachment method, was 90% and significantly greater than single fragments (32%). cluster survival remained consistently high for all years, while single fragment survival significantly declined over time. additionally, for multi-fragment clusters, 3-year cement outplant survival (96%) was higher, although not significantly different, than epoxy clusters (87%) across all sites, while change in lta was equal for both methods. furthermore, change in cluster lta differed significantly among sites and is likely limited by partial mortality, such that 63% of clusters did not reach their 3-year expected lta. our results indicate that multi-fragment a. cervicornis clusters attached with cement provide the greatest long-term outplant survivorship and lta increase for southeast florida. following recent bleaching events, efforts are underway to assess nursery and outplant genotypic thermal tolerance, such that this performance metric can be incorporated into local restoration planning and ultimately improve the future viability of restored reefs in southeast florida.",
