          "name": "Patrick Idank Idank",
          "search_text": "patrick idank idank unraveling the mysteries of crustose coralline algae - algal turf dynamics on changing coral reef systems epilithic algal matrices (eam), comprise of short algal turfs (< 2 cm) multispecies assemblages and other non-algal components including detritus, inorganic sediment, and microbes.  eam are proliferating on degrading coral reefs, and may outcompete other benthic organisms, including crustose coralline algae (cca) under specific environmental conditions such as sediment accumulation.  epilithic algae-cca interactions remain poorly understood, but increasing epilithic algae cover and sediment accumulation may negatively impact cca growth and calcification by reducing available light and altering localized conditions.\r\nimportantly, cca play a key role in driving carbonate production on most contemporary coral reefs.  however, it remains unclear how changing environmental conditions and increasing epilithic algal cover may impact cca\u2019s ecological function.  recent evidence suggests that cca can match or even exceed the contribution of hard corals (scleractinia) to the carbonate production.  this may become more relevant as coral cover declines and benthic communities become increasingly dominated by epilithic algae, particularly on marginal, high-latitude reefs where cca may be critical to maintaining reef framework.\r\nthis study will examine epilithic algae-cca interactions, and quantify how epilithic overlay, spatial and seasonal variability, and accumulated sediment influence cca calcification on the florida reef tract (frt) and in the persian gulf.  results will provide the first comparative analysis of epilithic algae-cca interactions across extreme, high latitude reef systems and will advance understanding of carbonate dynamics on marginal reefs.",
