          "name": "Rachel Ionata",
          "search_text": "rachel ionata optimizing coral larval stocking density to increase production for reef restoration coral larval settlement and post-settlement survival are the main constraints to the success of ex situ sexual propagation of corals for reef restoration. to optimize the production of caribbean corals, we assessed the effect of larval stocking density on percent settlement and the effect of initial settler density on post-settlement survival up to eight weeks\u2013 the time-point at which the corals are typically large enough to be isolated and grown on individual tiles. the percent settlement of colpophyllia natans was 46% on average (range 18.7-77.4%) and it increased as the larval stocking density increased, from approximately 3 to 8 larvae/cm^2 of settlement substrate. for orbicella faveolata, percent settlement was 51% on average (range 16.9-80.3%) and was only significantly higher when the larval stocking density was approximately 4 larvae/cm^2 of settlement substrate compared to lower densities (down to 2 larvae/cm^2). post-settlement survival over time was not significantly affected by initial coral settler density. survival was 50% or higher at a density of \u226417 settlers/cm^2 for c. natans and \u22644 settlers/cm^2 for o. faveolata. from a production perspective, the aim is to obtain about 1.5 coral spats/cm^2 at 8 weeks post-settlement, as a density higher than that would prevent different corals from being able to be isolated (cutting of original tile with a band saw or tile nipper would kill part of the corals when attempting to separate two corals in close proximity). to achieve this density for c. natans and o. faveolata, we recommend providing 6 larvae for each cm^2 of settlement substrate since larval settlement was approximately 50% and post-settlement survival at 8-weeks was at least 50%. from a labor efficiency perspective, this larval stocking density would lead to an initial settler density of 3 settlers/cm^2 which is also manageable to clean manually.",
