          "name": "Claire Guillier",
          "search_text": "claire guillier untargeted metabolomics reveals how pocillopora acuta metabolizes organic uv filters note: the abstract submitted here may also be considered for session 5,\r\nsub-theme: pollution (#22). however, prof. mitchelmore, who chairs that session, has a conflict of interest regarding this work. therefore, if you deem it appropriate, i would kindly request not to be redirected to that session, but rather to remain in your session, for which i hope the abstract will be considered relevant.\r\n\r\ndeclining water quality represents an increasing threat to coral reef health. among the myriad of chemical contaminants contributing to this issue, uv filters from sunscreens have been reported to induce deleterious effects on reef-building corals. however, most of the data focus on a limited number of uv filters and their potential to trigger bleaching, leaving metabolic responses, including defensive processes such as xenobiotic metabolism, largely unexplored. in this context, untargeted metabolomics can contribute to a more integrated understanding of coral physiology through the detection of metabolome changes that reflect both stress responses and defense mechanisms, such as xenobiotic biotransformation. our study aimed to characterize the response of pocillopora acuta exposed to 24 organic uv filters, which represent most of the substances authorized in european cosmetic products. corals colonies were exposed to 1 mg/l of each uv filter for seven days, and their endometabolome profiles were analyzed using liquid chromatography coupled with high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry (uhplc-esi+-hrms/ms). most of the differential metabolites were annotated as uv filter biotransformation products, revealing a wide range of transformation patterns spanning expected polar metabolites to novel uv filter derivatives. these results further revealed that xenobiotic metabolism pathways involve both the coral host and its symbionts, highlighting the complex interplay underlying uv filter biotransformation in symbiotic organisms. these findings contributed to the understanding of uv filter metabolism in p. acuta and demonstrate that biotransformation products in their diversity, should be considered as key markers of uv filter exposure in future monitoring programs, alongside the parent compounds.",
