          "name": "Mayu Ishikawa",
          "search_text": "mayu ishikawa larval pre-settling behavior enabling settlement in acropora and isolation of its inducing bacteria with implication to enhancement of coral seedlings production for active restoration of coral reefs, coral seedlings production is one of key ways to propagate coral recruits, however, settlement has been a bottleneck due to its low frequencies such as 1%. elevation of settlement efficiency is an important and urgent issue. it is essential to understand the mechanisms of settlement in order to know the reasons of settlement failure to increase settlement rates. going back to the most basic approach, we tried to record behavioral processes till settlement. time-lapse recording revealed sequential three steps of behavior, random crawling, counterclockwise circling, and upright spinning, on the substrate prior to settlement. the crawling speed on the substrate was extremely slow as 0.016 mm/s, in contrast to the free-swimming speed at around 1 mm/s in the water column. circling was continued around particular sites for long periods as up to 3 hrs. after upright spinning, larvae succeeded settlement. upright spinning is especially important since differentiation of the aboral part to adhesive tissues allows the polyp to adhere onto the substrate. some larvae aborted these behaviors and returned to free-swimming, indicating a stepwise evaluation of substrate suitability. next, we, collected bacteria from positions where pre-settling behavior occurred on conditioned terracotta tiles, and screened bacterial strains inducing these behaviors by culture and assays. four strains induced part of pre-settling behavior, and each strain triggered different behavioral steps, indicating that multiple microbial cues contribute to regulating larval decision-making. these four strains were identified to belong to three genera, alteromonas, pseudoalteromonas, and vibrio. these results may open a gate to the breakthrough method of settlement induction by combining bacteria that induce pre-settling behavior and that trigger metamorphosis, aiming marked improvement of settlement rates in coral seedling production.",
