      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Roger Huerlimann",
          "search_text": "roger huerlimann the brain transcriptional program and behavioural responses of the pajama cardinalfish under multiple environmental stressors much of what we know about how marine organisms respond to multiple simultaneous stressors still comes from laboratory studies on single species, which inevitably misses the ecological complexity of real reefs. nikko bay in palau provides a rare counterexample: a semi-enclosed system with limited connectivity to surrounding waters, where elevated temperature and pco\u2082 and low oxygen have been persistent for decades. this isolation has created a natural setting to explore how reef species adjust to future-like conditions.\r\nwe examined whether environmentally driven shifts in habitat structure inside nikko bay influence the behaviour and brain gene expression of pajama cardinalfish (sphaeramia nematoptera), a coral-associated specialist. we used in-situ observations to assess coral and fish patterns and a reciprocal transplant experiment to move fish individuals between control reefs and inner-bay sites and assess their short- and long-term acclimation potential.\r\ninside the bay, branching porites cylindrica is largely replaced by montipora stellata, accompanied by lower cardinalfish abundance and higher activity levels. furthermore, s. nematoptera change their association from p. cylindrica in the control area to m. stellata in nikko bay. when fish from the control reefs were transplanted into nikko bay and exposed to m. stellata, they showed reduced activity and pronounced upregulation in brain transcriptomic profiles compared to fish kept in their original habitat (both compared to fish associated with p. cylindrica in the control area or fish associated with m. stellata originating from nikko bay). in contrast, transplanting fish from p. cylindrica to m. stellata within the control environment showed a less pronounced reduction in activity and a weak transcriptomic response. these consistent behavioural and molecular responses indicate that habitat restructuring and altered environmental conditions in nikko bay are strong drivers of both ecological and molecular change, offering insight into the limits of plasticity in coral-associated fishes under future ocean scenarios. transcriptomic, physiological, and behavioral responses to ocean warming and acidification in the neon damselfish ocean warming and acidification may profoundly affect marine organisms. to understand how coral reef fish are affected by changing conditions, we used a manipulative aquaria-based experiment to assess the response to warming and ocean acidification in the neon damselfish (pomacentrus coelestis) in shimoda, japan. fish were exposed to one of four treatments: elevated temperature, elevated pco\u2082, combined elevated temperature and elevated pco\u2082, and ambient conditions (control). respirometry, behavioral assays, and brain transcriptomics were used to assess physiological performance, behavioral changes, and gene set enrichment, respectively.\r\n\r\nboth warming and elevated pco\u2082 decreased factorial aerobic scope, and warming alone decreased activity levels. in the combined stressor treatment, there was an increase in activity level relative to the warming treatment, leading to activity levels which were comparable to the ambient temperature treatments. gene set enrichment analysis revealed enrichment of pathways associated with immune response, neuronal activity, metabolic activity, and vision, indicating that these fish may be equipped to acclimate to elevated pco\u2082 and temperature with a robust transcriptomic response. the highest number of enriched pathways was found in the pco\u2082 treatment, followed by the combined stressor treatment, with the lowest number in the warming treatment. \r\n\r\nreduced activity level in the warming treatment indicates that reduced factorial aerobic scope may force these fish to downregulate their activity under warming conditions to conserve energy. however, this compensatory response is disrupted in the multiple stressor treatment, eliminating the behavioral changes seen in the warming treatment. the combined stressor treatment also reduces the number of enriched pathways relative to the pco\u2082 treatment. these results imply that multiple stressors may dilute the response seen in a single stressor treatment, leading to a maladaptive synergistic effect. this study highlights the value of integrative multiple stressor experiments by demonstrating the complexity of coral reef fish response to global environmental change.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "The brain transcriptional program and behavioural responses of the Pajama cardinalfish under multiple environmental stressors",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "69828718-773b-4f2c-be94-49cede1aff5f"
            },
            {
              "title": "Transcriptomic, Physiological, and Behavioral Responses to Ocean Warming and Acidification in the Neon Damselfish",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "c7e8fbe2-e7be-404d-8465-dcdbd8b5db93"
            },
            {
              "title": "Express yourself: Assessing taxonomic diversity and functional gene expression of subtropical shallow water coral reefs based on environmental DNA/RNA",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "5bce231f-8525-4cab-ac41-71eeb5647c66"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Vera Emelianenko",
          "search_text": "vera emelianenko exploring diverse symbiotic microeukaryotes associated with okinawan coral reefs coral reefs are diverse and vulnerable ecosystems that serve as a home for thousands of species in complex interactions with each other. to better understand the biology of okinawan coral reefs, we catalog the diversity of various symbiotic microeukaryotes on the coral reefs and reef flats in okinawa, utilizing edna and broad screening of host samples with metabarcoding and microscopy.\r\nfirst, we focused on symbiodiniaceae. although symbiodiniaceae have been studied extensively in multicellular hosts, their potential free-living reservoirs and diversity of lineages not associated with metazoans remain poorly known. we compared environmental and host-associated communities on the same reef flat at ikei island (okinawa, japan), using the internal transcribed spacer 2 (its2) region as a marker. symbiodiniaceae communities among hosts were different from each other, and seawater samples were distinct from algae and sediment samples. different symbionts were associated with foraminifera of different genera, so the horizontal acquisition of symbionts during sexual reproduction is hypothesized as highly selective. symbiodiniaceae identified from the sediment and macroalgae were the most diverse and contained some potentially free-living lineages such as clades i, h, and j.\r\nto get a more comprehensive picture of the coral reef environment, we have also collected environmental and invertebrate samples from four other reefs in okinawa. this broad screening using non-metazoan primers amplifying the v4 region of the 18s rrna gene is currently in progress. the first set of sequencing data suggest high diversity of microeukaryotes in the environmental samples, including parasitic lineages. approximately half of the animal samples showed amplification of microeukaryotic sequences, with sequences from symbiotic ciliates and apicomplexans present in several samples. our sequencing and imaging results expand our understanding of protist diversity in the subtropical coral reefs.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Exploring diverse symbiotic microeukaryotes associated with Okinawan coral reefs",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "037dd01d-6b9a-46d7-8bce-27797c5c08ba"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
