          "name": "Dan Barshis",
          "search_text": "dan barshis the strengths, limitations, and unknowns of acute thermal stress testing and the coral bleaching automated stress system (cbass) acute thermal stress tests are gaining widespread adoption as a standardized approach for diagnosing thermally tolerant corals for research and restoration applications. the coral bleaching automated stress system (cbass) and short-term thermal stress assay was developed as a portable platform and high-throughput approach to enable rapid thermal tolerance assessments and widespread comparative analyses. however, accurate benchmarking and evaluation of the utility and limitations of the approach are still very much a work-in-progress. here, we will present a mini-review of the strengths, limitations, and remaining unknowns concerning the use of the cbass and similar techniques for coral thermal tolerance assessment. primary strengths center around the portability, low-cost, flexibility in configuration and deployment, high-throughput capacity, limited supporting infrastructure required, and ecological relevance of response differences. limitations have been found in consistency across different equipment types, tank volumes, and flow rates, repeatability of measurements over time, precision of different response variables, and associated cost of high-precision instrumentation. remaining unknowns concern the ecological relevance of results, consistency in acute versus chronic thermal responses, underlying drivers affecting variation in responses, potential tradeoffs to increased thermal tolerance, and contributions of phenotypic plasticity versus adaptation to thermal tolerance differences. some unknowns are technical in nature, while others are biological. we maintain these techniques and approach still hold great promise for research and restoration applications, though argue it is important for the community to recognize the limitations of the various systems and assays when designing experiments and interpreting results. additionally, it is important as a community that we work together in continuing to answer the unknowns in order to optimize and further our understanding of how best to utilize these promising tools.",
