      "search_text": "oldenburg university nicole schroeter nicole schroeter monitoring reef restoration plots using edna analyses in the mafia island marine park, tanzania the coral reefs of the mafia archipelago are among the healthiest in africa and represent a biodiversity hotspot in the western indian ocean. these coral reefs are crucial for the island\u2019s economy, which still depends primarily on fishing and tourism. the corona pandemic caused temporary job losses and a shift away from tourism to fishing. the still enduring economic impact of the pandemic is leading to increased unsustainable and destructive fishing practices, which is causing extensive damage to local coral reefs. additionally, the 2024 global coral bleaching event significantly affected these reefs. elevated sea temperatures caused widespread coral bleaching and mass mortality within the mafia island marine park. compounding this issue, cyclone hidaya struck mafia island in may 2024, causing physical destruction which led to further reef degradation. these combined impacts have caused a rapid decline in coral cover within the mafia island marine park, threatening the biodiversity and ecological functions. at severely impacted reef sites, reef restoration plots have been established since september 2025 to support reef recovery and provide controlled areas for monitoring. environmental dna (edna) sampling, combined with visual monitoring, has been used to evaluate changes in fish and benthic biodiversity, to achieve a comprehensive understanding of species composition and reef health within the mafia island marine park, both at restoration and non-restoration sites. we commenced edna sampling before and following the natural disasters of the past year allowing assessment of baselines and evaluation of biodiversity changes caused by destructive fishing as well as the impacts of the recent mass bleaching event and cyclone hidaya. the insights gained from this study are invaluable for assessing the true impacts of destructive fishing, the recent bleaching event, and cyclone hidaya, as well as evaluating natural reef recovery and the effectiveness of planned reef restoration efforts.",
