          "name": "Caroline Donahue",
          "search_text": "caroline donahue first records of spawning for the gonochoric scleractinian coral coscinaraea monile (forsk\u00e5l, 1775) information on the reproductive behaviour of arabian gulf corals is relatively sparse, especially on the stress-tolerant species, such as coscinaraea monile. although c. monile is among the most thermally resilient reef builders, able to withstand extreme temperatures and high salinities of the arabian gulf, its reproductive biology remains undocumented with no published data on reproductive cycles and spawning timings. here, we present the first dataset on the spawning periodicity of c. monile based on ex-situ spawning observations collected in the first coral hatchery in qatar. we confirmed that the species is a gonochoric broadcast spawner and recorded a total of 53 spawning observations, spread over 25 days, between 10 april 2021 and 6 july 2024, under environmental conditions (temperature, photoperiod and lunar cycle) synchronous with natural seasonal cycles: 6 in april 2021(13-20 nights after full moon); 13 in may 2022 (6-13 nights after full moon); 3 in may 2024 (5 nights after full moon); 20 in june 2024 (5-16 nights after full moon) and; 11 in july 2024 (11-16 nights after full moon). this dataset reveals that, in the arabian gulf, c. monile has a relatively long spawning period, during spring and early summer. because colonies are gonochoric, variation in spawning timings was also observed between male and female colonies. the male colonies usually released sperm (median: 20 minutes after sunset, range: 2-49 minutes) earlier than female oocyte release (median: 36 minutes after sunset, range: 12-88 minutes), a pattern commonly considered optimal for fertilization success in broadcast spawners. these initial records provide a baseline dataset that is of critical importance for future research, conservation and restoration efforts on this species. an industry-academia partnership for coral restoration in qatar: methods, preliminary results, and implications for management through an industry\u2013academia partnership, we are developing and validating active restoration technologies for local coral species to enhance the recovery of degraded reefs in the arabian gulf. we have established the first land-based coral nursery in qatar and are adapting procedures for asexual coral propagation, husbandry, and reintroduction (outplant) to carefully selected recipient sites, as well as their long-term monitoring. all corals used in this study were recovered from subsea construction areas. during phase 1 (2021\u20132023), 10,446 fragments from 10 coral species and 963 donor colonies were outplanted across 4 offshore locations in the eez of qatar. in phase 2 (2024\u2013ongoing), 7,828 fragments from 10 species and 853 donor colonies were outplanted to 4 offshore locations. monitoring will continue for at least five years to assess attachment success, survival, growth, and health status. initial results show consistently high attachment success, with 92% to 99% of outplanted fragments detected during subsequent surveys. survival and health status varied among sites and species, with deeper sites exhibiting significantly higher survival (up to 95% for the first year and 92% for the second year) and better overall condition relative to shallower locations. growth rates also differed significantly among species. cyphastrea microphthalma showed the highest average growth rate, with a mean daily increase of 0.34% in surface area. these results contrast with our ex-situ findings, in which platygyra daedalea grew the fastest (0.21% daily skeletal growth), underscoring the need to understand performance differences between nursery and natural reef environments to guide species selection for future outplanting. overall, the project demonstrates that locally adapted restoration methods can achieve high coral survival and growth in one of the world\u2019s most challenging reef environments, providing valuable insights for future large-scale restoration initiatives.",
