          "search_text": "catherine kim coral-based multi-proxy records from timor-leste: tracing oceanographic and environmental change temperature and ocean warming are one of the key impacts on coral reefs globally with recurring mass bleaching events in the last decade. with such sparse in situ records, natural archives of temperature provide high-resolution data. we assess porites sp. coral cores collected at two sites between shallow depths of 2-5 m along the north coast of timor-leste, beloi and com, by the usaid and national oceanic and atmospheric administration mission in 2012. the cores roughly 30 cm in length, 3 cm diameter were cleaned, slabbed, x-rayed, and uv photographed at the australian institute of marine science. prior to slabbing, the cores were ct scanned. major and trace element analysis was performed on one core per site by an agilent 8900 laser ablation inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometer split stream system at the central analytical research facility at the queensland university of technology. the timeframe spans roughly 1995-2012 with an annual growth rate of 1.5-2.0 cm. thermometry will be determined through assessing sr/ca ratio and correlations with el nino southern oscillation. ga and mn concentrations will give indications of inputs from land such as sediment. timor-leste lies within the indonesian throughflow which has complex oceanography including intrusive upwelling which decouples in situ temperatures from satellite temperature readings during the wet season. the x-rays show sub-annual high-density banding in one core which could be indicative of very localized upwelling or other oceanographic processes. we aim to develop a method to use thermometry and in situ temperature records to correct sea surface temperature from satellites, providing more accurate and robust picture of reef temperature climatologies in timor-leste.",
