          "name": "Charernmee Chamchoy",
          "search_text": "charernmee chamchoy blue carbon potential of macroalgae and marine sediments on underwater pinnacles in thailand for carbon insetting toward net-zero tourism marine ecosystems play a critical role in global carbon sequestration, with macroalgae and marine sediments on underwater pinnacles recognized as important blue carbon storage components. these ecosystems function as nature-based climate mitigation solutions by capturing and retaining anthropogenic co2. however, climate-driven warming and disturbance threaten long-term carbon retention and ecosystem service stability, and these structurally complex pinnacle habitats remain under-quantified in blue carbon accounting. this study assessed macroalgal carbon stocks and sedimentary carbon burial in underwater pinnacles of trang province to evaluate their potential for carbon insetting to enable net-zero tourism transitions. the study found that the macroalgal carbon stock was notably high, with hin samphao chom storing 103.1 mgc ha\u207b\u00b9, exceeding that of hin ta ku at 90.1 mgc ha\u207b\u00b9. among dominant macroalgae, padina australis showed greater carbon storage capacity than turbinaria conoides, reflecting species-specific insetting value. sedimentary carbon stocks were spatially variable (1.20\u201315.41 mgc ha\u207b\u00b9), with an average total carbon storage of 16.29 mgc.ha-1. the highest average carbon storage was observed at hin somphao chom (24.75 mgc.ha-1), while the lowest average was recorded at hin klang (6.19 mgc.ha-1).  these values exceed typical coastal burial estimates reported for degraded tropical shelf sediments, underscoring pinnacles as overlooked carbon sinks. a carbon-insetting scenario suggests that preserving and enhancing macroalgal meadows and structurally complex marine sediments on underwater pinnacles can offset emissions from reef tourism and accelerate net-zero tourism transitions without relying solely on external carbon markets. the findings highlight underwater pinnacles as high-leverage insetting assets, offering measurable carbon, biodiversity co-benefits, and a governance pathway for sustainable tourism development.",
