          "name": "Wiphawan Aunkhongthong",
          "search_text": "wiphawan aunkhongthong coral nursery development as a climate-adaptation pathway to enhance reef tourism resilience and biodiversity recovery in thailand coral reefs are highly productive, biodiverse ecosystems that support coasts, fisheries, and reef tourism, yet are increasingly threatened by ocean warming, pollution, and coastal development. shallow reef flats are key coral reef habitats with high hard-coral diversity, driving reef growth and structural complexity that support many reef-associated species. corals from the shallow reef flat are resilient and adapted to survive in environments with high temperatures, intense light, and high ultraviolet radiation. this study aims to establish a prototype coral nursery for reef restoration by selecting stress-resistant coral fragments from shallow reef flats, and to use the nursery as a key learning resource for promoting sustainable marine ecotourism. coral fragments from dominant species (porites lutea, pocillopora acuta, and dipsastraea favus) were selected to evaluate growth performance on dome-shaped nursery units deployed at ko rang kachiu, chumphon province, in the western gulf of thailand. each coral fragment was collected from the shallow reef flat and reef slope to compare the survival and growth rate on the dome-shaped nursery of transplant. the results showed high overall coral fragment survival rates, reaching 96.9% in porites lutea, 93.2% in pocillopora acuta, and 82.7% in dipsastraea favus. additionally, fragments of these species on the shallow reef flat exhibited higher growth rates than fragments from the reef slope. following transplantation, a major coral bleaching event occurred in 2024, during which fragments sourced from shallow reef flats exhibited only 5% bleaching incidence and showed stronger thermal tolerance than those from deeper reef slopes. the findings demonstrate that corals from shallow reef flat hold strong resilience value for climate-adaptive reef restoration. coral nurseries also function as ecotourism learning hubs, boosting awareness and stewardship among visitors and local stakeholders, reinforcing long-term sustainable reef-tourism engagement.",
