          "name": "Liz Burmester",
          "search_text": "liz burmester the impact of tire pollution on coral nutritional dynamics and wound recovery tires are a ubiquitous source of marine pollution in coastal communities across the globe. tire pollution can enter reef ecosystems in different forms: as chemical leachate released from tires and through tire particles broken down via friction or erosion. understanding the impact of marine pollution on coral reefs requires delineating its impacts on coral nutritional dynamics, which are difficult to study in obligately symbiotic tropical corals. the temperate coral astrangia poculata is a facultatively symbiotic coral, which can range in symbiosis from aposymbiotic (receiving no functional benefit from algal symbionts) to fully symbiotic. this system serves as a natural experiment, allowing the ability to parse apart the relative contributions of both symbiont autotrophy and coral host heterotrophy toward the health and energy demands of a coral colony. this study uses astrangia poculata as a model system to determine the effects of leachate and particle tire pollution on coral health by examining (1) polyp feeding behavior, (2) photosynthetic efficiency, and (3) recovery from small-scale wounds.",
