      "search_text": "red sea global sylvia jagerroos sylvia jagerroos assessing coral health in the al wajh lagoon (northern red sea): disease patterns and stress event responses and coral predator dynamics monitoring understanding patterns of coral disease is essential for predicting coral reef resilience and guiding management actions. this study presents the first assessment of coral disease patterns across four survey periods in the al wajh lagoon, northern red sea. disease signs were recorded in multiple taxa, with acropora, cyphastrea, echinopora, goniastrea, platygyra, pocillopora and porites exhibiting lesions during at least three of the four monitoring periods. several diseases were documented at outbreak levels during the september 2024 global bleaching event, including acropora white syndrome (ws), black band disease (bbd), and porites ws with cyanobacterial bands.\r\nthe data reveal clear patterns of differential disease susceptibility among coral taxa, providing managers and practitioners with valuable information for targeted monitoring during routine conditions and thermal stress events. these insights also support species selection for coral reef restoration and offer guidance for in-situ and ex-situ nursery management by highlighting which endemic lesions are most likely to occur on cultured corals.\r\ndisease prevalence was further compared between natural and artificial habitats and between developed and undeveloped coastal regions. no significant differences were detected across any survey period, suggesting that current levels of coastal development do not yet have a detectable influence on coral disease in the lagoon. in parallel, four invertebrate coral predators were recorded: drupella spp., coralliophila spp., culcita spp., and acanthaster spp.. drupella was consistently the most common predator, underscoring the importance of continued monitoring of this species.\r\noverall, this study establishes a baseline understanding of coral disease prevalence, coral predator presence, and coral reef resilience and recovery patters.  these findings represent a critical first step toward predicting coral reef health trajectories and supporting adaptive management and marine regenerative planning in a rapidly changing red sea environment.",
