          "name": "Derta Prabuning",
          "search_text": "derta prabuning how locals make their voices heard through an inclusive system in indonesian coral reef hub indonesia's archipelago has 41% of worldwide climate-resilient reefs, making it strategically necessary to strengthen active involvement with local communities to ensure long-term conservation success. reef check indonesia provides lessons learned on developing national and sub-national hubs within bio-climatic units to support locally driven and inclusive conservation of climate-resilient reefs. in the archipelago, several unique and successful approaches were found from local and indigenous communities through their traditional knowledge and vision for climate-resilient reef conservation. community in bali and east nusa tenggara with awig-awig and a temporary \"open and close\" principle like sasi (eastern indonesia) and hadi mulung (in alor islands) shows that socio-cultural connection to their reefs, relying heavily on food, income, and traditional practices. however, increasing local stresses on coral reefs stem primarily from direct human activities in coastal areas, and global threats to ecosystems are also leading to declining of \u201cour hope\u201d climate-resilient reefs as well as social and economic decline. to better integrate local knowledge and vision into national coral reef management, supported by the gef7 crr project, reef check indonesia initiated an inclusive national coral reef hub, a platform laid in national government structure where a range of reef stakeholders may collaborate and coordinate, as well as get support from the technical working group in hub, for conserving climate-resilient reefs. an innovative \u201cbuddy\u201d system was approached in hub. partnering hub\u2019s community members with local ngos or entities to enable and encourage inclusive representation in conservation decisions. local remote fisher group in anambas, with anambas foundation improves knowledge on conserving their climate refugee reef. also community in alor with local university and wwf, community group in baranglompo makasar, and negeri ameth ambon. strengthening these systems alongside scientific support and ownership ensures a pathway to more resilient reefs and empowered communities in an archipelagic country like indonesia. near-real-time data and intervention during hot season in the archipelago nation: a case of indonesian coral reef hub with existing limitations of resources, having a vast number of reefs poses management challenges, especially in timing of near-real-time data monitoring for intervention responsiveness. reef check indonesia provides lessons learned from problems that are not alien to developing nations. started in 2010, indonesian coral bleaching network rapidly grew, comprising a range of reef stakeholders, i.e., scientists, agencies, ngos, fishers, hobbyists, local communities, and tourism operators. now it has covered all main islands in indonesia, including some of important climate refugee reefs with around a thousand volunteers. supported by gef7 coral reef rescue project, network is integrated in indonesian coral reef hub and has been improved to expand from a centralized to a regional model in sub-national areas. the hub is a platform where a range of reef stakeholders may collaborate and coordinate, as well as get support from the technical working group in hub. in wake of mass coral bleaching, hub sheds light on how to conduct near-real-time monitoring efforts that cover huge areas. the network\u2019s efficiency can be traced to its innovative coordination using various modes, including social media. a dedicated and experienced team in hub to lead coordination and provide scientific support for network. also, a decentralized approach eventually pays off when a very quick and vast disaster strikes. this hub and its network experience in the 4th global coral bleaching on providing alerts and bleaching information to mpa manager for management intervention. finally, with such high exposure to coral reef issues, the hub can step up its involvement by contributing to the development of an action plan to address the findings. obviously, there is room for improvement to increase the efficacy of this network, especially using information technology to ease reporting and communication processes. with bleak predictions of future coral bleaching events, network is clearly worth investment.",
