      "search_text": "rosenstiel school of marine, atmospheric and earth science caroline dennison caroline dennison high resolution spatiotemporal mapping of flexible algal symbiont communities in large boulder colonies through bleaching events and endemic disease some coral species have highly flexible algal symbiont communities (family symbiodiniaceae) that are known to vary across colonies and over time as a phenotypic response to their location, depth, colony morphology, and light exposure. these varying communities can affect the coral\u2019s susceptibility to thermal stress and disease. this study triannually mapped intra-colony mosaics of symbiodiniaceae taxa in the threatened caribbean coral species orbicella faveolata between 2022 and 2025 to investigate associations between and bleaching and disease on colonies >2 m diameter in southeast florida. five repeatable transects were set up on seven colonies in a radial pattern, where thirty-one biopsies were taken per colony at nine timepoints. quantitative pcr results showed the four northernmost colonies dominated solely by breviolum, a central colony dominated by durusdinium, and two southernmost colonies with a mixture of breviolum and cladocopium. symbiont to coral host cell ratios (s:h) temporally varied within and between colonies, with the lowest corresponding to a severe bleaching event in 2023. five colonies exhibited active sctld lesions. the two colonies without recorded lesions had the lowest proportions of breviolum. intra-colony spatial analyses of colonies with variable symbiont communities showed significant clustering of genera that varied temporally. one colony varied seasonally and shifted s:h distributions during a disease event. although temporally variable, the symbiont communities in the majority of colonies remained consistent except for one colony that shifted, post bleaching event, to be similar to other breviolum-dominant colonies. this work illustrates the spatiotemporal dynamic nature of large corals with flexible algal symbiont communities. high spatiotemporal resolution symbiont community mapping provides a better understanding of the associations between algal symbiont communities and the inter and intra-colony variable responses to bleaching and disease. further associations to shading and light exposure are currently being investigated using 3d sfm modeling. a holobiome approach to \u201ccoral\u201d disease: the russian doll model historically, coral disease research has emphasized characterizing host tissue pathology and pathogen identification, treating surveys of other holobiont members (the dynamic and diverse set of microbes that interact with the coral host and one another) as indicators of disease presence, or as the microbial landscape from which potential pathogen(s) must be distinguished. seldom have these organisms been considered specific targets of the disease agent. recent advances suggest that many coral \u201cdiseases\u201d emerge from disruptions across the microbial consortia, which may interact with the coral host, manifesting as impairment (\u201cdisease\u201d). understanding disease as a breakdown of holobiont function, rather than as a consequence of a pathogenic agent affecting the coral host, offers a more comprehensive framework for explaining variability in disease incidence, transmission patterns, and recovery potential. in this context, we examine coral bleaching and stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) as case studies of holobiont \u201cdisease\u201d. coral bleaching represents an example of holobiont dysbiosis driven by environmental stress on the algal symbiont, modulated by the coral host and manifested as disrupted host metabolism due to symbiont expulsion and loss of photosynthetic inputs. likewise, emerging evidence suggests sctld is an infectious disease of the symbiodiniaceae, modulated by the coral host's immune system, determining the speed and extent of tissue loss. these examples suggest a \u201crussian doll\u201d model of coral disease in which environmental stress or infectious agents can target different (often nested) components of the coral holobiome. together, they demonstrate how coral health outcomes are shaped by interactions among multiple partners rather than by the coral host alone. moving away from coral-centric characterizations and diagnostics toward a holobiont perspective provides a more holistic understanding of coral health and improves our ability to detect early dysbiosis, refine interpretation of disease processes, and support pathogen detection, which remains elusive for many coral diseases. environmentally mediated changes in symbiodiniaceae communities and their role in coral resilience to coral bleaching and disease resilience climate-linked disturbances, including coral bleaching and disease, are becoming increasingly common, emphasizing the need to view resilience as an emergent property of both the coral host and its microbial partners. although the host provides the foundational capacity for immunity and stress tolerance, variation in coral-associated algal symbiont communities (family symbiodiniaceae) can drive differences in host phenotype, shaping thermal tolerance and disease susceptibility. thus, symbiodiniaceae composition can strongly influence coral resilience. here, we reciprocally transplanted corals between inshore (near the port of miami) and offshore (emerald reef) environments off southeast florida to assess how environmental conditions structure symbiodiniaceae communities. we followed 208 fragments of four species evenly distributed between the environments, for one year, collecting tissue biopsies quarterly to track symbiodiniaceae changes and document the 2022 bleaching event. after one year, fragments were returned to the laboratory, where a disease challenge was conducted to understand how environmentally driven symbiont shifts shape disease response. field monitoring shows that corals transplanted to inshore environments shuffled in favor of durusdinium (p<0.05), while those sourced from offshore environments tended to associate more readily with breviolum and/or cladocopium. inshore corals maintained strong associations with durusdinium, whereas offshore corals moved inshore rapidly shifted in favor of durusdinium, suggesting persistent, environment-driven partnerships. corals hosting durusdinium during the summer showed significantly less bleaching than those hosting other algal genera. in the laboratory assay, corals containing more durusdinium showed decreased disease incidence than fragments generated from the same colony associating with breviolum (or cladocopium) (p<0.05). these findings suggest inshore environments select for durusdinium, resulting in higher thermal tolerance and disease resistance, benefits that may persist under changing conditions. leveraging these resilient inshore corals in restoration and intervention efforts may help reinforce vulnerable populations and enhance reef persistence under continued climate stress.",
