          "name": "Rathnayaka Mudiyanselage Gayani Nilupika Thilakarathna",
          "search_text": "rathnayaka mudiyanselage gayani nilupika thilakarathna bioerosion of sea urchin stomopneustes variolaris (lamarck,1816) in two intertidal reefs in a tropical island sea urchins play a crucial role in many shallow marine benthic habitats. as such, they can shape their habitats through grazing and bioerosion. stomopneustes variolaris is the most abundant sea urchin species recorded in shallow coastal reefs in sri lanka. bioerosion rates of s. variolaris across three size classes in two intertidal rocky reefs (ahangama and nilwella) in southern sri lanka, during the northeast and the southwest monsoons, were quantified. thirty urchins were collected from each site for each season to analyze the amount of caco3. gut evacuation rate and population densities were measured for each site and season. calculated gut evacuation rate was 0.432 day-1. during the northeast and southwest monsoons, in ahangama, daily bioerosion rates were 0.21\u00b10.014 g caco3 individuals-1 day-1 and 0.16\u00b10.011 g caco3 individuals-1 day-1, respectively, while in nilwella varied between 0.11\u00b10.009 g caco3 individuals-1 day-1 and 0.14\u00b10.01 g caco3 individuals-1 day-1, respectively.  no significant differences were observed in the caco3 or daily bioerosion rates of urchins between three size classes, either in monsoons or locations (p< 0.05).  calculated annual bioerosion rates exhibited significant variations among size classes, locations, and monsoon periods (p < 0.05). ahangama, the medium samples, had the highest bioerosion rates (749.57 \u00b1 65.37 g caco3 m-2 year-1).  the annual bioerosion rate in ahangama varied between 267.75 \u00b1 16.32 caco3 g m-2 year-1 and 430.29\u00b1 30.51 caco3 g m-2 year-1. variations in annual bioerosion rates ranged from 72.44\u00b19.04 to 110.08\u00b17.76g caco3 m-2 year-1 in the nilwella location. variations in bioerosion rate between locations are mainly attributed to the substrate type. outcomes indicate s. variolaris is an important bioeroder in the southern shallow reefs of sri lanka and emphasize the need for assessment of implications on reef carbonate dynamics.",
