        {
          "name": "Rommel Maneja",
          "search_text": "rommel maneja monitoring coral reef health in the arabian gulf: a multi-temporal sentinel-2 satellite imagery and insitu studies on bleaching events and reef resilience coral reefs in the arabian gulf exist in one of the most extreme marine environments, subject to significant temperature fluctuations that trigger bleaching events resulting in degradation over time. accurate monitoring of coral reef health and recovery is crucial for understanding their resilience and informing conservation strategies. this study presents a robust methodological framework for assessing changes in coral reef cover across multiple years (2016, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2024, 2025) using multi-temporal sentinel-2 satellite imagery and insitu bleaching observations in 2023 in the saudi offshore island of karan. a change detection analysis was conducted using the raster calculator to compute band ratio indices for each date after an iterative relative radiometric normalization procedure allowing for the demarcation of areas with reef loss. the analysis revealed a maximum cumulative transect total of 380 m and 300 m of coral reef loss along the northeastern and northern reef crests, respectively over the study period with varying extent across different zones of the reef. a linear directional progression pattern in coral reef erosion along the northern reef crests suggests susceptibility of degraded reefs to wave action. the largest change was detected in 2019 relative to 2025 with estimated loss of 432,607 m2 and 303,025m2 for the northern and southern halves of the reef crest, respectively. insitu observations along the karan island reef crests in august 2023 reported varying levels of bleaching across several coral taxonomic groups including acropora, porites, platygyra, dipsastraea, and favites. this work underscores the critical importance of combining remote sensing time-series analysis and insitu coral bleaching observations for monitoring coral reef health in challenging environments like the arabian gulf.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Monitoring Coral Reef Health in the Arabian Gulf: A Multi-Temporal Sentinel-2 Satellite Imagery and Insitu Studies on Bleaching Events and Reef Resilience",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "3a842658-a52f-4df4-a7c0-7b3591bf6c16"
            }
          ]
        }
