          "name": "Sophia Zummo",
          "search_text": "sophia zummo nutrient imbalance effects on coral holobiont function across physiological and microbial scales anthropogenic eutrophication frequently introduces nitrogen and phosphorus in unequal amounts, creating nutrient imbalances that destabilize coral holobionts. elevated nitrogen drives rapid symbiont proliferation that outpaces phosphorus availability, pushing the system into phosphorus limitation. under these conditions, photosynthesis becomes less efficient, oxidative stress rises, and symbionts retain more fixed carbon rather than transferring it to the host. this breakdown in metabolic coupling weakens host control of resource exchange and disrupts the tight nutrient recycling normally shared among the host, algal symbionts, and microbial community. \r\n\r\nto examine how nutrient imbalance reshapes holobiont function, we experimentally exposed pocillopora spp. and montipora grisea fragments to controlled nitrogen and phosphorus treatments, followed by \u00b9\u00b3c stable-isotope incubations to quantify carbon fixation and internal carbon cycling within the coral holobiont. preliminary results reveal species-specific sensitivity to nutrient imbalance. pocillopora exhibited elevated \u00b9\u00b3c enrichment and carbon-uptake rates under nitrogen-only enrichment, followed by a decline when phosphorus was co-supplied, patterns consistent with phosphorus-limited symbionts retaining more fixed carbon and reducing host transfer. in contrast, montipora increased carbon uptake only under balanced n+p enrichment, with corresponding decreases in c:n ratios, suggesting a more stable host\u2013symbiont coupling. symbiont densities mirrored these trends: pocillopora proliferated under both n-only and n+p treatments, reflecting rapid loss of nutrient regulation, whereas montipora responded only to balanced enrichment, suggesting greater holobiont stability.\r\n\r\nto assess how these physiological patterns extend to microbial function and internal nutrient cycling, 16s rrna gene and symbiodiniaceae clade sequencing analyses are currently underway to quantify shifts in microbial community structure and symbiont composition. together, these datasets will evaluate how nutrient imbalance propagates through the holobiont, from physiological responses to microbial restructuring, and provide a mechanistic framework for how land-based nutrient loading alters coral function and resilience.",
