          "name": "Stuart Sandin",
          "search_text": "stuart sandin coral reef variability contains insights into ecological potential the world\u2019s coral reefs are heterogeneous, spanning wide swaths of the ocean and myriad habitats across coastlines. the variability across reefs is created by the biodiversity of species as well as the diversity of physicochemical conditions across geographies. further, evidence indicates that coral reef community structure can vary widely through time, driven by external disturbances and ecologically mediated transitions. a challenge thus emerges to develop generalizable ecological insights given the extreme contextual heterogeneity.\r\n\r\nworking with a global community of colleagues and collaborators, we have collected a snapshot of the spatial and temporal variability of coral reefs. our core sampling effort, the 100 island challenge, used a natural experimental approach to consider the independent and interactive effects of oceanographic context, island geomorphology, and anthropogenic forcing on the structure and functioning of reef communities. \r\n\r\nthe data reveal some general patterns that relate well to existing understandings of reef ecology. increased levels of nearshore productivity are linked with increases in biomass growth and standing stock potential. high levels of anthropogenic impact largely reduced biomass of fishes and coverage of reef-building corals. more surprising were the results revealed from time-series analyses. across reef geographies, the modal demographic fate of coral colonies is growth. certainly, impacts such as marine heat waves or predator irruptions can have striking impacts on coral fate. however, under many circumstances the corals respond to impacts by following pathways toward colony recovery or assemblages can demonstrate coral-driven reassembly. \r\n\r\ndescribing geographic patterns of variation in reef structure and dynamics provides a robust platform for next analytical steps. a goal for reef ecology should be to develop predictive models that incorporate geographic and contextual variability, offering insights into how the tremendous biodiversity of these ecosystems may contain mechanisms to meet mounting management challenges.",
