          "search_text": "wesley sparagon metabolites and microbes as water-column indicators of thermal stress in coral reefs global climate change has led to the ever-present and increasing threat of thermal stress on coral reefs. thermal stress can induce numerous physiological changes in the coral holobiont, but how these changes might manifest in the surrounding water column remains an open question. recent mesocosm experiments suggest that thermally stressed corals alter the adjacent water column by releasing compositionally distinct, labile dissolved organic matter (dom), which then enriches copiotrophic and putatively pathogenic bacterioplankton. this mechanism could further harm stressed corals and act as an early indicator of thermal stress in the water column. however, the relevance of this mechanism in situ has yet to be determined. to address this knowledge gap, we sampled the water column of a patch reef in k\u0101ne\u2018ohe bay, o\u2018ahu, hawaii, throughout a thermal anomaly, collecting data on microbial biomass, community structure (16s rdna amplicons), dissolved organic carbon (doc), and dom composition (untargeted metabolomics via lc-ms/ms). we collected temporally resolved depth profiles across the reef and explicitly sampled the boundary layer of corals throughout the thermal anomaly. depth profiles indicated that thermal stress induced a pulse of compositionally distinct, labile doc from the benthos that increased doc concentrations, altered water column dom composition, increased microbial loads, and restructured bacterioplankton communities. boundary-layer sampling further supported this, showing that thermal stress yielded elevated gibbs energies of oxidation and nitrogen content of metabolites in the coral boundary layer. interestingly, longitudinal samples of the coral boundary layer across an entire year revealed a time lag between dom and bacterioplankton shifts. changes in dom occurred immediately at the start of bleaching, while microbial community shifts manifested weeks after peak reef-wide bleaching, suggesting that dom changes, rather than bacterioplankton community shifts, are more useful as early-warning indicators of reefs undergoing thermal stress.",
