          "search_text": "jacinta gigileia jonathan indigenous women bridging tradition and digital innovation for community reef management in papua new guinea (png) across papua new guinea (png) and the broader pacific, indigenous women continue to play a leading role in community stewardship of marine resources. this knowledge, leadership, and daily engagement with reefs and near-shore fisheries form a vital and longstanding foundation for many locally-managed marine areas (lmmas). these lmmas consist of governance rooted in cultural practice, customary tenure, and intergenerational knowledge. the sea women of melanesia (swom), an indigenous, female-led organisation in png exemplifies this leadership through its role in driving community-based reef management across png.\r\nthis presentation highlights how swom is advancing coral reef management by blending indigenous knowledge, practices, community priorities, and innovative digital tools, particularly the use of reefcloud, a cloud-based image analysis and data-sharing platform co-developed in partnership with the australian institute of marine science (aims). reefcloud is locally adaptable because it is being shaped through an ongoing, collaboration between swom and the australian institute of marine science (aims). through this partnership, aims also learns from swom\u2019s field experience, cultural knowledge, and monitoring needs to improve reefcloud\u2019s design and usability. swom-led monitoring programs demonstrate an approach that supports local communities to design and propose new marine reserve areas and management plans, blending scientific reef assessments with community knowledge about fishing practices, fish species, and customary use zones.\r\nwe share four key lessons emerging from this partnership: (1) the importance of co-design; (2) the application of digital tools to meet community-grounded monitoring needs; (3) strengthening data autonomy; and, (4) ensuring that technology supports and does not replace local expertise. we offer recommendations for expanding this model: (1) scaling up women-led monitoring across further lmmas; (2) strengthening training and capacity; (3) improving coordination between communities, ngos, and regional agencies; and (4) continuing to co-develop tools like reefcloud so they remain culturally appropriate, locally usable, and scientifically robust.",
