      "distance_km": 9052.5,
      "search_text": "shantou university cristiana manullang cristiana manullang field investigation of macroplastic embedment and associated chronic stress pathways in underexplored reefs coral reefs cover less than 0.2% of the ocean\u2019s surface yet sustain nearly one-quarter of all marine species, making them among the most biodiverse ecosystems on earth. however, reefs are increasingly threatened by multiple anthropogenic stressors, including ocean warming, acidification, and pollution. among these, plastic pollution has emerged as a pervasive but underexplored driver of coral reef degradation. an estimated 0.8\u20132.7 million metric tons of plastic debris become entangled in asia\u2013pacific coral reefs annually, and this amount is projected to increase by up to 40% by 2025. despite growing awareness, field-based studies investigating chronic plastic exposure under natural conditions remain scarce.\r\nthis study presents an integrated field investigation of macroplastic embedment and associated stress pathways in coral reefs around nanao island, guangdong province, china. combining field surveys, physiological assays, and electron microscopy, we examined coral colonies with and without macroplastic exposure. corals entangled with plastic exhibited significantly reduced symbiont density and chlorophyll a content compared to unaffected colonies, indicating impaired photosynthetic capacity. some colonies were found dead, frequently covered with black algal  that may facilitate microbial infection and disease progression, while others survived with plastic fragments embedded in their skeletons. scanning electron microscopy (sem) revealed clear physical patterns of plastic penetration and skeletal deformation, highlighting long-term interaction between coral tissue and plastic debris.\r\nongoing microbiome and rna-seq analyses will further elucidate how macroplastic embedment disrupts coral-associated microbial communities and activates stress-related molecular pathways. this study provides the first mechanistic field evidence of how macroplastic debris induces chronic physiological stress and structural damage in reef-building corals, contributing essential insights for managing coral health and mitigating plastic impacts in the south china sea.",
