      "search_text": "shoals rodrigues reshad jhangeer-khan reshad jhangeer-khan ecological transformation and recovery limits on a remote indian ocean reef: insights from a decade of community-led monitoring and restoration in rodrigues rodrigues island, in the southwest indian ocean, offers a long-term record of ecological change on a small island fringing reef and lagoon system subjected to centuries of artisanal exploitation and accelerating climate stress. its shallow 240 km\u00b2 lagoon, once characterised by high coral cover and fish diversity, has undergone progressive ecological simplification driven by soil erosion, chronic overfishing, destructive gleaning practices, and most recently by the 2015/16 bleaching event. this marine heatwave caused up to 75% coral mortality across reef flats and slopes, pushing several habitats past functional recovery thresholds and exposing the vulnerability of shallow lagoon systems to thermal extremes.\r\n\r\ndespite high nominal protection coverage from mpas, weak compliance, sustained fishing pressure and damaging fishing practice have constrained natural recovery, favouring fast-growing opportunistic taxa and reducing habitat structural complexity. rodrigues represents a microcosm of indian ocean reef transformation: rapid biodiversity loss, altered trophic structure, and constrained regeneration despite active restoration. our findings highlight the need for stricter enforcement, active and integrative management, sustained outreach and sensitisation that couple cultural ties to sea country, community knowledge, and ecological thresholds to sustain biodiversity and ecosystem services under accelerating climate change.\r\n\r\nsince 2012, some 50,000 fragments of over 20 coral species have been outplanted to mpas through successive nursery, spider frame and direct-transplant programmes involving ngos, local youth, tourism workers and more recently the private sector. survival rates (67\u201394%) highlight that asexual propagation of rodrigues corals retains high short-term restoration potential; however, longer-term monitoring of the marine environment indicates extremely high vulnerability to thermal anomalies, and herbivore depletion that limit sustained ecosystem function. the ongoing restoration programme deploying >100 ocean-based nursery structures, >100 spider frames and direct outplanting efforts offer an unprecedented opportunity to evaluate bleaching-resistant genotypes in rodrigues, community-led stewardship, and socio-ecological feedbacks in a highly climate-exposed system.",
