      "search_text": "smithsonian tropical research center victor lara victor lara ecological, physiological, and genetic analysis of color in the corallimorph ricordea florida in almirante bay, bocas del toro, panama coral reefs are among the most diverse and vibrant ecosystems on the planet. from fish to invertebrates, from corals to algae, color is woven into every level of life. whether for camouflage, conveying a message, reproduction, or adapting to changing conditions, each organism carries its own distinct palette. for this project we are looking into the commercially important, yet little-studied corallimorph ricordea florida (mushroom coral) from a perspective of color, looking at how it could have an influence on their ecology, where first, we surveyed reefs across almirante bay, panama, to document the distribution and abundance of color morphs across sites and depths; its physiology, in which we evaluated whether color corresponds to physiological differences by comparing thermal thresholds and stress responses among morphs; and lastly genetics, where we examined potential genetic and symbiont variation associated with coloration, all this under the premise that there might be undescribed cryptic species of r. florida in the caribbean and the fact that we could potentially have them here in panama. with our ecological surveys we have found clear distinct ecological patterns between color morphs that are being backed up by the results of our physiological analysis and the corals\u2019 response to thermal stress, and we are currently utilizing molecular approaches to further prove our hypothesis. this project not only would expand the limited knowledge we have about the biology of this fascinating group of animals, but will also provide us with valuable insights necessary for the adequate management, protection, and sustainable use of this species, ensuring their persistence in the ecosystems they inhabit and their genetic diversity.",
