        {
          "name": "Zoe Fu-Chen",
          "search_text": "zoe fu-chen the effect of phylogenetic relatedness on scleractinian coral settlement and survival the janzen-connell hypothesis proposes that host-specific enemies reduce the survival of seedlings nearby to adult conspecifics, thus maintaining biodiversity by providing an advantage to heterospecific seedlings. while the janzen-connell hypothesis was originally formulated to explain diversity in tropical forest ecosystems, there is evidence that janzen-connell effects may also be present in coral reefs. to further investigate this, we conducted an in-situ experiment in which larvae of two scleractinian coral species (acropora kenti and acropora aff. hyacinthus) were settled on tiles and placed next to either a closely related (a. kenti) or distantly related (porites cylindrica) adult  on orpheus island inner protected reef, australia. \r\n\r\nafter extracting coral settler metadata and spatial data in qgis from images of settler censuses, we modelled the influence of adult-relatedness on coral larvae settlement success, settler post-settlement survival and settler spatial patterns. we found that both larval species had statistically significant lower settlement when next to a closely related adult  compared to a more distantly related adult. lower settlement was also associated with soft coral cover nearby the tiles. additionally, while phylogenetic relatedness had no effect on settler survival to 41 days, tile settler density (the number of settlers per tile) had a positive effect on settler survival to 41 days. finally, settlers tended to settle near their conspecifics more often than next to heterospecifics. this work indicates that phylogenetic relatedness between adult and larvae may reduce settlement success and deepens our understanding of the ways in which early life stages of coral are affected by adults.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "The effect of phylogenetic relatedness on scleractinian coral settlement and survival",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "be77acca-56a3-4aea-a099-7da20a88dcf8"
            }
          ]
        }
