          "name": "Javier Pardo D\u00edaz",
          "search_text": "javier pardo d\u00edaz coral community dynamics across cumulative anthropogenic and natural stressors in portobelo national park, caribbean panama understanding how coral communities respond to overlapping local stressors is essential for identifying resilient reef sites within marine protected areas. in portobelo national park, caribbean panama, coral assemblages occur under combinations of human and natural pressures. using field-based stressor proxies (tourism intensity, boat traffic, port influence, coastal settlements, river input, wave exposure, and algal cover), we characterised stressor regimes across 16 sites and quantified coral community composition using genus-level surveys conducted between february 2021 and july 2022 to assess spatial variation across stress regimes. a pca based on these proxies revealed a gradient of impact, with a subset of sites strongly associated with port activity, boat traffic, river input, tourism, and elevated algal cover. k-means clustering in pca space identified three stress regimes: low impact, mixed impact, and high impact, and an nmds based on genus-level data showed that coral communities varied across these regimes, although groups overlapped substantially. low-impact sites hosted more diverse assemblages, dominated by stress-tolerant genera such as porites, siderastrea, orbicella, and pseudodiploria. in contrast, mixed-impacted sites are dominated by opportunistic taxa, including agaricia and millepora, which appeared to tolerate altered environments. unexpected patterns emerged, including the presence of diverse coral assemblages at high-impact sites. our results provide one of the first spatially explicit assessments of local stressor regimes and their ecological consequences in portobelo national park. several sites maintain diverse coral assemblages despite persistent human pressure, highlighting their potential value for targeted restoration and long-term monitoring. these spatial patterns offer a baseline for evaluating potential resilience and guiding management efforts in the park.",
