          "name": "Charlon Ligson",
          "search_text": "charlon ligson serial larval enhancement increases coral recruit abundance and species diversity early coral life history demographics and species diversity are key factors in understanding coral recruitment and community ecology. however, the dynamics between previously settled coral recruits and incoming coral larvae of other species remain understudied, largely because these microscopic life stages are very small and cryptic. moreover, the role of increasing species richness in larval settlement cohorts and its influence on overall coral recruitment outcomes has received even less attention. in this study, we assessed the influence of heterospecific coral recruits on early recruit survival and determined how increasing species richness affects overall coral recruitment. we propagated coral larvae from ex situ coral spawning of three species over successive months \u2013 favites colemani in april, f. abdita in may, and acropora verweyi in june 2025. larvae from each species were used in a series of in situ coral larval reseeding deployments on the same settlement tiles, to achieve one-, two-, and three-species combinations\u2013 while the total larvae supplied through time across combinations remained consistent (300 larvae per tile). larval settlement success probabilities exhibited no significant difference between monospecific and heterospecific coral recruits. within each species, coral recruit survival probability declined over time across combinations. interestingly, there was evidence of improved survival in a two-species combination compared with monospecific recruits. two months after the final reseeding, the total recruit abundances across combinations were not statistically different from one another, except for the tiles with monospecific f. abdita recruits relative to the f. colemani\u2013a. verweyi combination. results indicate weak interspecific competition during the early stages of coral life history. the study offers insights into diversifying coral populations through serial larval enhancement via multispecies coral sexual propagation strategies, for improved understanding of coral reef ecology and for application in coral restoration.",
