        {
          "name": "Sheena Suet-Wah Chung",
          "search_text": "sheena suet-wah chung mapping historical extreme temperature event exposure across australia: implications for tropical coral range expansion subtropical and temperate marine ecosystems are increasingly recognised as potential refuges for tropical reef species under ocean warming, yet extreme temperature events, such as marine heatwaves (mhws) and marine cold spells (mcss), can rapidly restructure communities and influence the persistence and establishment of reef organisms across latitudinal gradients. understanding how exposure to these episodic extremes varies among regions is therefore critical for interpreting present-day ecosystem patterns and informing management.\r\n\r\nextreme events differ in duration, intensity, and frequency, yet these dimensions are often assessed independently despite their combined expression shaping ecological impacts. however, interpreting multiple event metrics can introduce unnecessary complexity for stakeholders seeking to interpret these events for planning and management. to address this, we quantified the historical exposure of australian coastal bioregions to mhws and mcss using a novel index that integrates multiple event characteristics, based on 43 years of high-resolution daily sea surface temperature data.\r\n\r\nthe exposure index revealed distinct regional patterns, with mhw hotspots identified in east cape york (queensland), zuytdorp, central west coast and abrolhos island (western australia), batemans shelf (new south wales), and bruny (tasmania). in contrast, tweed\u2013moreton on the east coast exhibited among the lowest levels of mhw exposure despite occurring at a similar latitude to zuytdorp.\r\n\r\nthese spatial patterns provide important environmental context for understanding thermal exposure of tropical species as they progress through stages of climate-driven range expansion. specifically, i will outline how this information is forming the basis for investigating the range-expanding coral pocillopora aliciae along the east coast of australia, where historical exposure to mhws, mcss, and long-term background warming may be influencing its population structure and adaptive capacity.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Mapping historical extreme temperature event exposure across Australia: implications for tropical coral range expansion",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "cd811aba-b0ad-45dc-b63c-5f354bfaf14f"
            }
          ]
        }
