          "name": "Joaquin Yus",
          "search_text": "joaquin yus synthetic tbp (tetrabromopyrrole) coatings and their application in coral restoration coral reef restoration is crucial for mitigating the decline of marine ecosystems due to climate change and other anthropogenic stressors. tetrabromopyrrole (tbp), a bacterial-derived compound, has been recently identified as a potent inducer of coral larval settlement. however, natural sources of tbp are limited, necessitating the development of synthetic alternatives. \r\nhere, we present the synthesis of tbp and its incorporation into engineered surface coatings that can be applied to a wide range of substrates used in restoration. these coatings are designed to stabilize tbp, control its release, and extend its functional lifespan, ultimately providing a practical method for delivering settlement cues in the field.\r\nin this project, we evaluate tbp\u2019s efficacy in inducing larval settlement under controlled laboratory conditions. additionally, we assess the influence of tbp on survival rates across different coral species. our findings provide insights into the potential of synthetic tbp coatings as a scalable and reliable tool for coral restoration initiatives.",
