          "name": "Alexandra Khrizman",
          "search_text": "alexandra khrizman reef-scale thermal variability: implications for conservation planning in large, remote marine protected areas thermal stress is a primary driver of coral reef decline and is commonly considered in climate risk assessments used to inform reef conservation and management. in practice, regional and global assessments of thermal exposure rely largely on satellite-derived surface temperature products, which provide valuable long-term coverage but cannot resolve reef-scale thermal conditions or subsurface variability. as coral reefs increasingly operate near their thermal limits, understanding how reef temperature exposure differs from surface metrics has become increasingly important for interpreting thermal risk and resilience. using long-term in situ temperature observations from multiple reef sites across the chagos archipelago, we quantify spatial and temporal variability in reef-scale thermal exposure in a large, remote reef system undergoing renewed conservation planning, including establishment of a new marine protected area and discussions of island resettlement. the observations reveal strong heterogeneity among sites, driven in part by episodic internal-wave-mediated cooling that substantially alters thermal conditions at reef depth and is not resolved by surface temperature products. we further compare in situ reef temperatures with commonly used satellite temperature products to evaluate how surface metrics represent reef-scale thermal exposure across sites and timescales. this comparison highlights strengths and limitations of satellite-based approaches and provides insight into how surface temperature data can be more effectively interpreted in the context of reef processes. by characterizing reef-scale thermal variability across a system undergoing renewed conservation planning, and by evaluating how satellite products relate to in situ conditions, this work provides physical context that can support interpretation of thermal risk, inform monitoring strategies, and improve understanding of potential reef resilience in large, remote marine protected areas.",
