          "name": "Catherine Lee Hing",
          "search_text": "catherine lee hing designing incentive structures for sustained coral reef management after crisis response stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) has reshaped coral reef management across the caribbean, catalyzing rapid emergency responses, novel scientific collaborations, and unprecedented management interventions. yet as sctld transitions from an acute ecological shock to a chronic, persistent stressor, many reef systems now face a familiar governance challenge: how to sustain effective management once the initial crisis attention, funding, and political urgency have waned. we examine how coral reef policy and management frameworks can move beyond short-term disaster response toward durable, incentive-aligned governance capable of addressing ongoing ecological decline.\r\n\r\nbuilding on empirical and policy insights from sctld response efforts, this study analyzes the institutional, financial, and behavioral incentives that shape reef management decisions in the post-crisis phase. it asks three central questions: which best practices introduced during sctld response, such as coordinated monitoring, rapid decision-making, and cross-sector collaboration, are most vulnerable to erosion over time?  what misaligned incentives undermine long-term coral stewardship once emergency funding and media attention recede? and thirdly, how can incentive structures be redesigned to embed adaptive management, compliance, and local stewardship into routine reef governance?\r\n\r\nusing a comparative policy analysis informed by conservation finance, marine spatial planning, and disease ecology, we propose an incentive-based framework for sustaining coral reef management under chronic stress. we highlight the need to shift from crisis-driven interventions toward governance systems that reward prevention, learning, and long-term ecological outcomes. by reframing sctld not only as a disease outbreak but as a stress test for coral reef governance, this research offers actionable insights for policymakers, managers, and funders seeking to build resilient reef management institutions in an era of compounding threats.",
