          "name": "Courtney Klepac",
          "search_text": "courtney klepac coral futures academy: capacitating local leaders on climate-smart restoration in micronesia, western pacific as marine heatwaves continue to intensify, coral restoration programs must incorporate multiple species of genetically diverse, heat-resistant corals. however, the current costs and technical capacity required to identify heat-resistant colonies are not scalable, and research-driven restoration projects often struggle to engage local community members and agencies in project design and implementation. community participation is crucial for building public buy-in and long-term support, especially in remote or developing nations. to address these challenges, the coral futures academy has adopted a set of training programs that streamline the identification of bleaching-resistant corals, provide low-cost testing equipment and simple experimental designs, and offer local capacity-building opportunities for establishing heat-resistant coral nurseries. by ensuring that every component is affordable, robust, and feasible in remote settings, where most coral reefs are found, the coral futures academy aims to deliver significant global impact. our approach has been successfully piloted in palau in the western pacific. using these methods, more than 900 heat-tolerant corals from 24 species have been identified and maintained in local nurseries by local researchers, supplying colonies for restoration efforts tailored to local needs. we have also empowered local leaders at our project sites to adopt additional restoration strategies appropriate to their contexts through knowledge exchange within our network. this scalable framework, first implemented in palau, is now being expanded to new sites in majuro (marshall islands) and pohnpei (federated states of micronesia), with plans to reach other regions. through these efforts, we aim to support communities in proactively sustaining their reefs and the ecosystem goods and services upon which they depend. this network also serves as a foundation for expanding collaborative restoration and other scientific efforts across the region. furthermore, the lessons we have gained from applying this model offer important information on how it performs within this regional context. testing the test: validating low-tech coral bleaching experiments for integration into reef restoration programmes worldwide as global coral cover continues to decline under increasing heatwaves, reef restoration practitioners are pressed to find effective and scalable solutions to improve long-term coral survival. moreover, there is added benefit to streamline scientific advances in coral resilience methods to be adopted and effective for local practitioners in remote areas. critical to restoration success is integrating climate resilience, where one promising approach is the identification and incorporation of bleaching resistant coral individuals via thermal assays. although some experiments have shown that the response to experimental bleaching aligns with natural bleaching stress, and that some bleaching tolerance is retained after nursery or outplanting stages, more careful exploration of these questions in multiple systems and species is needed. to determine the efficacy of bleaching assays and tolerance metrics in a restoration setting, using multiple coral species, we compared a four-day increasing heat pulse against 1) a slow two-week hold exposure, 2) retesting after one year, and 3) natural bleaching responses of nursery corals. we observed similar individual coral responses between the short and long-term heat stress across multiple bleaching traits. additionally, our pre-tested corals with the highest thermal tolerance maintained their heat resistance durability after one-year in a common-garden nursery and had higher survival following the 2024 bleaching event, compared to corals with the lowest tolerance. this is the first encouraging demonstration in a restoration setting that short-term heat pulse exposures can recapitulate ecologically relevant thermal stress and offers a rapid, accessible way to scale up stocking coral restoration nurseries for future climate resilience in restoration.",
