          "search_text": "francesca cohn a genetic method for distinguishing french polynesian cryptic pocillopora species without sequencing cryptic species of pocillopora coral inhabit distinct ecological niches in reef systems and may be key to developing protective portfolio effects that buffer reefs from stress events. the ability to quickly and accurately identify pocillopora to species is critical to correctly estimate reef biodiversity and understand how communities of morphologically indistinguishable species affect the robustness of reefs against future environmental challenges. in recent years, genomic sequencing and phylogenomics have validated a standardized barcoding method using the mitochondrial open reading frame, termed mtorf, and a histone region, termed pochistone, as species-level markers for pocillopora. however, even sanger sequencing the mtorf and pochis regions may be prohibitively expensive for the high number of samples required for reef monitoring and is frequently infeasible in some reef locations. here, we present in silico and in vitro validated methods that adapt and expand upon a restriction fragment length polymorphism (rflp) digest and gel assay developed for cryptic pocillopora in hawai\u2019i (johnston et al. 2018). our approach leverages interspecific nucleotide differences of the regional mtorf and pochis haplotypes to accurately identify the six french polynesian pocillopora species without sequencing: p. acuta, p. cf. effusa, p. grandis, p. meandrina, p. tuahiniensis, and p. verrucosa. as cryptic species are increasingly recognized across coral genera, the development of cost effective, high-throughout protocols to identify corals to species will be critical to support reef conservation and management.",
