      "search_text": "state of hawai'i christina jayne kira hughes christina jayne a decade of development: lessons from innovative land-based restoration practices for hawai\u2018i\u2019s unique coral reef challenges as the most geographically isolated island chain in the world, with urbanized coastlines and nearshore fringing reefs, hawaiian coral reefs are extremely unique and face distinct challenges for coral restoration. unlike most other reef ecosystems in the pacific and around the globe, hawaiian reefs exhibit low species diversity, high endemism, and lack dominance by fast-growing branching corals. these characteristics have required hawai\u2018i-based restoration practitioners to create novel techniques for working with slow-growing massive corals, rare species, and restoration in environments with heavy tourism and human use. to address these hurdles, the state of hawai\u2018i division of aquatic resources (dar) established the hawai\u2018i coral restoration nursery (hcrn) in 2015 to pioneer multiple innovative approaches to expedite the growth of hawaiian coral species to large sizes in a significantly shorter duration compared to natural growth rates. \r\n\r\nas the first land-based coral restoration nursery in hawai'i, the hcrn initiated a combination of microfragmentation and advanced aquarium techniques to produce large corals for restoring damaged and degraded reef sites and enhancing natural ecosystem services and functions. the 1-2 year process produces 40-cm and 1-m coral colonies which represent 20-100+ years of natural growth. with over a decade of developments, dar will share lessons learned, experimental results, and novel techniques from the nursery\u2019s process and history. from microfragment sizing and species-specific light regimes to site-specific acclimation techniques, the hcrn has tested and proven a suite of methods to improve coral growth and outplant success. these protocols were developed through small scale experiments, trial and error, and collaborations with researchers and practitioners. dar aims to share lessons learned from the evolution of the hcrn to support information sharing, best practices, and collaboration between coral restoration practitioners across our oceans. kira hughes designing a watershed and water quality program to reveal connectivity between land-based impacts and aquatic resource health healthy watersheds and water quality are critical to aquatic ecosystem wellbeing in hawai\u02bbi. however, the compartmentalized nature of state agencies in hawai\u02bbi has made it challenging to address water quality issues that span entire ahupua\u02bba (traditional ridge to reef land divisions in hawai\u02bbi, often compared with watersheds). to remedy this gap in understanding and lack of interagency coordination to improve watershed health and water quality, the division of aquatic resources has developed a watershed program and he wai e ola, a water quality strategy. through partnering with the department of health, the commission on water resource management, the division of forestry and wildlife, and other nongovernmental and community organizations around the state, dar has committed to these interagency efforts to incorporate watersheds and water quality into our management of aquatic resources. to gather feedback and multiple perspectives to guide these programs, dar convened (1) a panel discussion featuring both managers and scientists at the 2024 u.s. coral reef task force meeting in saipan to discuss the merits of co-locating water quality data with marine monitoring data, (2) a workshop gathering dar staff focused on streams, muliwai, and anchialine pools to discuss current watershed-related efforts and future program interests in fall 2025, and (3) a workshop gathering managers, community representatives, and academic scientists from around hawai\u02bbi to discuss what statewide collaboration surrounding water quality should look like. this talk will focus on the process taken to arrive at these collaborations, share results from these discussions, and present paths forward to continue implementing these important collaborative efforts. through these programs, dar will be able to more directly adjust our management of aquatic resources to incorporate the water and watershed ecosystems that feed into them, improving outcomes for the unique fishes, limu, coral, and invertebrates that call hawaiian watersheds home.",
