          "name": "Elena Quintanilla",
          "search_text": "elena quintanilla contrasting tolerance to low oxygen reflected in the physiology and microbiome adaptability of two caribbean coral species acute episodes of low oxygen are increasing worldwide due to global warming and eutrophication, but their impact on reef-building corals and their microbiomes is largely unknown. here we experimentally exposed two reef-building caribbean coral species to in situ low oxygen for 2 days in bah\u00eda almirante, panama. we identified changes in their microbiomes by sampling 16s rrna genes before treatment, during low oxygen exposure, and again three days after treatment, along with their photo-physiology and metabolic rates. we observed (i) the rise of a hypoxia-associated microbial consortium during the initial response to deoxygenation in both coral species comprising primarily of fusibacteraceae, arcobacteraceae, nitrincolaceae, desulfovibrionaceae, marinifilaceae, and vibrionaceae. this assemblage may serve as a reliable microbial bioindicator of hypoxia stress in corals. additionally, we found (ii) marked species-specific differences in host physiology and microbiome signatures following a short 72-hour recovery period. in agaricia tenuifolia, microbiome shifts were accompanied by visible necrosis in most colonies and complete mortality in 66% of the colonies. in contrast, most siderastrea siderea colonies bleached (90%) but none died. all bleached colonies regained algal symbionts with full photosynthetic capacity and metabolic capacity within 4 days. the s. siderea resilience was coupled with colonies regaining endozoicomonas. together, these results reveal that hypoxia tolerance differs across coral species in both the holobiont\u2019s physiological responses and in the microbiome composition, with s. siderea demonstrating more tolerance than a. tenuifolia. results imply that coral microbiomes may play an active and important role in determining the resilience of corals under deoxygenation.",
