          "name": "Thinesh Thangadurai",
          "search_text": "thinesh thangadurai growth anomalies dominate coral disease landscape in oman reefs: microbial evidence and spatial trends. coral diseases are an increasing threat to reef ecosystems, yet their prevalence and host susceptibility remain understudied in oman. surveys conducted across ten northeastern reef sites recorded five disease types\u2013black band disease (bbd), white syndromes (ws), endolithic hypermycosis (eh), growth anomalies (ga), and focal bleached patches (fbl)\u2013affecting eight of 21 coral genera. disease abundance was generally low (0.12 cases m\u207b\u00b2), with the highest occurrence at inner island (1.2 cases m\u207b\u00b2). growth anomalies were the most common lesions (82%), followed by tissue loss (6%), focal bleaching (5%), and bbd (3.6%). porites, platygyra, and acropora were the most susceptible genera. given the dominance of gas, bacterial communities associated with acropora faraonis were analyzed using 16s rrna gene sequencing. while alpha diversity was similar between healthy and ga tissues, compositional shifts were observed. endozoicomonas remained dominant in both, but no known pathogens were detected, suggesting non-microbial origins. this study provides the first baseline of coral disease diversity and microbial patterns in omani reefs, highlighting low disease prevalence but the need for continued monitoring to track emerging coral health threats.",
