          "search_text": "sangeeta mangubhai climate resilience attributes of indigenous multi-species fisheries enhancing the climate resilience of fisheries food systems is critical to safeguarding food security and supporting local livelihoods. this study adapts and applies a global resilience framework to investigate the effects of climate change on indigenous fisheries. we used 15 climate resilience attributes under five resilience domains (i.e., assets, flexibility, social organization, learning, agency) spanning ecological, socioeconomic, and governance dimensions to assess the resilience of multi-species coral reef fisheries that are important to itaukei (indigenous) fishers from three villages in fiji. we found local fishers were significantly impacted by climate change, and that rapid-onset climate events accelerated adaptation and resilience-building processes. our findings suggest that the  perceived immediacy and the acute nature of an event may be a strong driver of socio-ecological transformations for long-term resilience. the resilience framework provided valuable insights on the resilience of indigenous fisheries; however, socioeconomic attributes were easier to interpret compared to ecological and governance. livelihood diversity and opportunity, social capital, agency, traditional ecological knowledge, learning capacity, and a strong resilient mindset contributed to socioeconomic resilience in fishers. we also examined resilience attributes through a gender and broader social inclusion lens. while we found culturally embedded agency empowered indigenous women and enabled them to contribute to their personal, family and community wellbeing, their participation in decision-making was low or absent. our research offers insights into how the resilience framework can be adapted for local contexts, with potential applications for other coral reef-dependent communities globally.",
