          "name": "Liat Biniuri",
          "search_text": "liat biniuri abundance and spatial distribution of boring bivalves in scleractinian coral hosts along a depth gradient in the gulf of eilat/aqaba (goe/a) symbiotic interactions are a defining feature of coral reef ecosystems, which function as biodiversity hotspots and critical habitats that sustain diverse marine life. among reef organisms, bioeroders play a pivotal role as ecosystem engineers, contributing to the balance between reef construction and degradation. bivalves of the genus leiosolenus (family: mytilidae) bore into the skeletons of living scleractinian corals through combined chemical and mechanical processes. these interactions exemplify the dual nature of symbiosis: while bioerosion weakens coral frameworks and may reduce structural integrity, bivalve activity may also provide benefits, such as ammonium enrichment of the surrounding coral boundary layer.\r\nin the gulf of eilat/aqaba (goe/a), leiosolenus spp. are recognized as associates of various coral taxa; however, their prevalence, host range, and spatial variability across environmental gradients remain poorly understood. to address this gap, we investigated the abundance, distribution, and ecological significance of boring bivalves along a depth gradient (2\u201330 m) at two reef sites differing in environmental stress: the interuniversity institute of eilat (iui) and the adjacent eilat nature reserve (nr). using standardized transect surveys, coral colonies were identified, measured, and assessed for bivalve inhabitation through borehole counts and in situ photography. complementary analyses included morphological and genomic identification of leiosolenus species, quantitative comparison with an unpublished historical dataset from the 1970s, and a reciprocal shallow-to-deep translocation experiment with stylophora pistillata to evaluate settlement rates and long-term colonization potential.\r\npreliminary findings revealed pronounced depth and site-related variations in both the prevalence and intensity of bivalve inhabitation, alongside several newly recorded coral hosts. these results suggest that leiosolenus\u2013coral associations are more widespread and ecologically significant in the goe/a than previously recognized, underscoring their potential influence on coral skeletal integrity, nutrient cycling, and overall reef resilience under changing environmental conditions.",
