          "name": "Mika Doron",
          "search_text": "mika doron the hidden engineers: how boring bivalves influence the coral diffusive boundary layer coral\u2013bivalve symbioses are widespread yet poorly understood. in the branching coral stylophora pistillata, populations of the boring bivalve leiosolenus lessepsianus inhabit coral skeletons, generating micro-currents through their exhalant siphons. these currents occur within the coral\u2019s diffusive boundary layer (dbl), a narrow, laminar zone that regulates solute exchange between coral tissues and seawater, thereby acting as a physiological bottleneck for gas transfer. extreme diel oxygen fluctuations frequently occur within this layer, characterized by daytime hyperoxia and nighttime hypoxia, which impose metabolic stress on corals. we hypothesize that the pumping activity of boring bivalves enhances ventilation of  the coral surface, attenuating these fluctuations and potentially enhancing coral resilience, particularly under low-flow conditions typical of deeper reef environments. to test this hypothesis, oxygen dynamics were quantified in colonies of s. pistillata with high (>100 individuals/colony) and low (<10 individuals/colony) bivalve densities using optical oxygen microsensors (pyroscience oxrob10-sub). preliminary laboratory experiments under controlled flow (3 cm s\u207b\u00b9 \u2013 10 cm s\u207b\u00b9) revealed that corals hosting bivalves exhibited narrower diel oxygen ranges compared to uninhabited colonies, indicating dbl stabilization by siphonal jets. building on these results, ongoing in situ measurements at 5 m and 30 m depths near the interuniversity institute in eilat combine high-resolution oxygen logging with the gypsum dissolution technique to characterize intra-colony flow patterns. this study provides the first direct quantification of oxygen dynamics within coral dbls in relation to boring bivalve activity. by linking microscale hydrodynamics with coral physiology, it sheds light on context-dependent symbiotic interactions that may shift along the parasitism\u2013mutualism continuum, thereby contributing to coral reef resilience under changing ocean conditions.",
