          "name": "Noa Ben Ami",
          "search_text": "noa ben ami environmental drivers of soft coral reproduction: a comparative study across seas and decades rising sea temperatures have accelerated the poleward expansion of tropical species, driving the ongoing 'tropicalization' of temperate marine ecosystems. nowhere is this process more pronounced than in the mediterranean sea, where lessepsian migration (i.e., the movement of red sea species through the suez canal) has already introduced hundreds of non-native tropical species. in 2022, a new lessepsian migrant was documented in the mediterranean: the indo-pacific soft coral dendronephthya hemprichi, first observed on gas rigs off the israeli mediterranean coast. soft corals are known for their invasive capabilities and, accordingly, d. hemprichi has rapidly proliferated in the eastern mediterranean, forming an exceptionally dense marine animal forest within a single year. the long-term establishment of any non-native species highly depends on its capacity to survive and reproduce in the novel environment. here, we provide the first comprehensive comparison of the reproductive biology of d. hemprichi between its native red sea ecosystem and its new mediterranean range and evaluate changes relative to historical reproductive data collected from the red sea three decades ago. through monthly sampling, dissections, and histological analyses, we characterized sex ratio, gametogenesis, reproductive seasonality and fecundity across regions. histological examination confirms the presence of both male and female colonies in the mediterranean. we found that while the red sea population retains its already known presence of year-round mature gametes, the mediterranean population exhibits a markedly shorter reproductive window, with a clear seasonal peak in the summer (august). our findings demonstrate that d. hemprichi has established a sexually reproducing population in the mediterranean and is likely to continue expanding its range which undoubtedly indicated tropicalization of the east mediterranean. these results will be discussed in the context of environmental differences between the mediterranean and red sea that may drive shifts in coral reproductive biology.",
