          "search_text": "makeda mills the microbial diversity of deep-sea precious corals on an unexplored seamount octocorals in the genus of corallium are commonly known as precious corals due to their striking red-pink hues and elaborate branching structures. for millennia, these corals have held economic significance across the globe, serving as jewelry and ornaments. while traditionally harvested using coral nets through the 1800\u2019s, the shift towards trawling methods has led to a decline in their mesophotic populations particularly evident in once-thriving habitats in the mediterranean and the seamounts near japan. yet, the increase in trawling efforts is linked to the growing demand for precious coral, primarily for the high-end jewelry industry. given the increasing pressures for precious corals, it is likely that precious coral populations will continue to decline. \r\nthe o\u2019brian seamount, located in the northwest pacific ocean, is an unstudied seamount that shows no visual signs of trawling disruption. it is home to corallium japonicum, a precious coral species that boasts hues of deep and dark reds. this species is commercially harvested from depths of 80 to 300 meters off the coast of japan. although, the species remains in high demand, studies on the health of the coral holobiont are limited, especially for those at greater depths. \r\nby characterizing an apparently unimpacted population, a baseline understanding of associated microbiota could serve as a critical reference point for future comparisons with harvested or trawled seamounts. thus, the goal of this study is to investigate the microbial ecology of c. japonicum inhabiting the undisturbed o\u2019brian seamount at depths ranging from 1398 to 1995 m. fragments of c. japonicum (n=17) were collected in the fall of 2023. ongoing metagenomic analyses will characterize the taxonomy, community structure, and functional composition of associated microbiota. \r\nestablishing a baseline understanding of c. japonicum-associated microbiota is imperative, as it unveils the functional dynamics and ecological structure within these understudied yet overexploited coral ecosystems.",
