          "name": "Samantha McClain",
          "search_text": "samantha mcclain short-term improvements in coastal acidification and eutrophication along a maui coral reef during the covid-19 pandemic as global ocean acidification and warming intensify and threaten corals, mitigating local stressors, such as land-based pollution, becomes increasingly important to the future survival of reefs. an example of land-based pollution occurs on the west coast of maui, hi, where the lahaina wastewater reclamation facility (lwrf) injects treated wastewater into the groundwater aquifer. injected wastewater travels to the coast and emerges in acidified, eutrophic submarine groundwater discharge (sgd) onto a fringing reef within the kahekili herbivorous fish management area. here, sgd has been linked to decreased live coral and increased algal coverage. during the covid-19 pandemic, visitation to maui drastically decreased (98%) when hawai\u02bbi's governor enacted travel restrictions. as a result, significantly less (>50%) wastewater was injected by the lwrf. during this time, we observed reduced acidification and eutrophication of sgd (ph: +0.32; \uf044[no\u2083-]: -104.12 \u03bcmol l-1) and reef water (ph:+0.12; \uf044[no\u2083-]: -0.33 \uf06d\u03bcmol l-1), as well as increased reef daytime net ecosystem production. trends reversed after travel restrictions were removed. additionally, daytime net ecosystem calcification did not seem to respond to changes in sgd chemistry, potentially due to the effects of additional stressors (e.g., bleaching). we will discuss how short-term reductions in acidification and eutrophication led to generally improved reef health. this work is especially important and timely considering that the lwrf is redefining injection protocols, including injected wastewater quality targets, following the 2020 supreme court case in which the county of maui was found in violation of the clean water act. therefore, we will provide recommendations for wastewater management and injected water quality targets (e.g., limits to injected total nitrogen), and compare targets to those recently proposed by the hawai\u02bbi department of health.",
