      "search_text": "texas a&m university - corpus christi david armstrong eleanor tenbrink david armstrong decoupling carbonate availability and proton concentration reveals the primary control on coral calcification reef carbonate dynamics underpin coral reef accretion and ecosystem resilience, yet the physiological driver of coral calcification under altered carbonate system conditions remains unresolved. here, we experimentally decouple carbonate availability and proton concentration to identify the dominant physiological driver of calcification.\r\n\r\nwe conducted ten independent 30-day outdoor flow-through mesocosm experiments over two years, spanning summer and winter seasons. using controlled manipulations of total alkalinity (ta) and ph\u2013applied independently and in combination\u2013we decoupled carbonate ion availability from proton concentration while replicating natural temperature and irradiance regimes. experiments were performed on two ecologically important hawaiian coral species, montipora capitata (branching and plating morphologies) and pocillopora acuta, enabling evaluation of interspecific and morphological sensitivity to carbonate system perturbations.\r\nacross species, morphologies, and seasons, calcification responses consistently tracked proton concentration rather than bulk carbonate ion availability, identifying proton accumulation as the primary constraint on skeletal growth. variation in symbiont density, chlorophyll concentration, tissue protein content, and other physiological metrics did not explain observed calcification patterns. high-resolution ph and o\u2082 microsensor measurements revealed that elevated pco\u2082 intensified chemical gradients within the coral concentration boundary layer, particularly in p. acuta, indicating heightened vulnerability relative to the more resilient m. capitata. in contrast, morphology and skeletal porosity\u2013which influence boundary-layer thickness and the flux of materials (e.g., oxygen and protons)\u2013emerged as key traits governing microenvironmental buffering capacity.\r\n\r\ntogether, these results identify proton flux as the primary mechanistic driver of coral calcification, linking microscale carbonate dynamics at the tissue\u2013seawater interface to species-specific sensitivity under altered carbonate chemistry and informing predictions of coral performance under ongoing ocean acidification scenarios. ocean acidification modulates material flux linked with coral calcification and photosynthesis coral reefs are essential for the foundation of marine ecosystems. however, ocean acidification (oa), driven by rising atmospheric carbon dioxide (co2) threatens coral growth and biological homeostasis. this study examines two hawaiian coral species\u2013montipora capitata and pocillopora acuta to elevated pco2 simulating oa. utilizing ph and o2 microsensors under controlled light and dark conditions, this work characterized interspecific cbl traits and quantified material fluxes under ambient and elevated pco2. the results of this study revealed that under increased pco2, p. acuta showed a significant reduction in dark proton efflux, followed by an increase in light o2 flux, suggesting reduced calcification and enhanced photosynthesis. in contrast, m. capitata did not show any robust evidence of changes in either flux parameters under similar increased pco2 conditions. statistical analyses using linear models revealed several significant interactions among species, treatment, and light conditions, identifying physical, chemical, and biological drivers of species responses to increased pco2. this study also presents several conceptual models that correlate the cbl dynamics measured here with calcification and metabolic processes, thereby justifying our findings. we indicate that elevated pco2 exacerbates microchemical gradients in the cbl and may threaten calcification in vulnerable species such as p. acuta, while highlighting the resistance of m. capitata. therefore, this study advances our understanding of how interspecific microenvironmental processes could influence coral responses to changing ocean chemistry. eleanor tenbrink mitigation to management: evaluating ocean alkalinity enhancement as a tool for coral reef restoration coral reefs are diverse and valuable ecosystems made up of complex calcium carbonate structures created by corals and calcifying algae. unfortunately, the persistence of coral reefs is threatened by elevated atmospheric co\u2082 levels that alter seawater chemistry. as such, intervention strategies to reduce co\u2082 are needed to address this threat to coral reefs and conserve them for future generations. ocean alkalinity enhancement (oae) is an emerging strategy that could simultaneously mitigate seawater acidification and enhance long-term oceanic carbon storage. by adding alkaline substances to seawater, oae increases the buffering capacity and availability of carbonate ions, which may promote favorable conditions for coral growth and reef accretion. however, the ecological implications of oae remain unknown, particularly regarding how different alkaline sources and concentrations interact to affect the growth of corals and calcifying algae. therefore, this project evaluates acute response to oae by measuring the impact of different alkaline agents (olivine, calcium oxide, and sodium hydroxide) at varying concentrations (+1000\u20133000 \u00b5mol kg\u207b\u00b9) on reef-builder calcification rates. specifically, we aim to identify the optimal level of enhancement of different alkaline agents on three representative reef-building species in hawai\u02bbi (coralline spp., montipora capitata, and pocillopora acuta). initial findings indicate that elevated alkalinity generally enhances calcification, but with species- and agent- specific responses. m. capitata exhibited maximal calcification rates at +1000 \u00b5mol kg\u207b\u00b9 across all three agents, whereas p. acuta displayed a lesser, more variable calcification response with maximal rates at +2000 \u00b5mol kg\u207b\u00b9, particularly under olivine enhancement. these results suggest that oae may be a viable carbon mitigation option for coral reef conservation based on coral physiological response.",
