          "name": "Marissa Nuttall",
          "search_text": "marissa nuttall a thriving coral reef defying the odds: 32 years of stability at east and west flower garden banks national marine sanctuary, usa the widespread decline of biologically and economically vital coral reefs, often resulting in phase shifts to macroalgal dominance, necessitates the study of resilient outliers. the reefs of flower garden banks national marine sanctuary (fgbnms), located 190 km offshore from the texas and louisiana coasts in the united states, have demonstrated exceptional stability over decades. a long-term photographic monitoring program, initiated in 1989, has produced a continuous dataset critical for assessing community-level trends and fine-scale benthic changes. we analyzed a 32-year time series (1992\u20132024) of benthic composition (coral, macroalgae, cca, sponge) using point-count analysis from both random and permanent monitoring stations at east and west flower garden banks (efgb and wfgb). our findings reveal sustained high coral cover and growth. key results show efgb maintained stable mean coral cover (46% in 1989 to 50% in 2024), while wfgb exhibited a significant increase from 47% to 62%. species composition remained consistent, indicating increasing cover was due to sustained growth of the existing community. macroalgae showed interannual variability and a slight increase (efgb: 18% to 24%; wfgb: 13% to 17%), but not degradation-level phase shifts. these results contrast sharply with widespread western atlantic/caribbean reef degradation, establishing the reefs of fgbnms as a critical example of sustained coral dominance and reinforcing the value of consistent, photo-based, long-term monitoring. crucially, fgbnms represents a globally significant reference site to actively study mechanisms of resilience against thermal and anthropogenic stress. in turn, the data directly inform decision-making and policy, producing status and trend condition reports, vulnerability assessments, and setting the foundation for the sanctuary's next management plan, directly addressing 21st-century management needs. management of invasive lionfish in the era of establishment: impact of top-down control by native predators lionfish (pterois volitans/miles) are considered established throughout the northwestern atlantic ocean, caribbean sea, and gulf of mexico, but their invasion front continues into the mediterranean sea and southern atlantic. at the onset of the invasion, lionfish were thought to lack native predators with populations largely released from top-down regulation. while divers have reported successfully feeding speared lionfish to sharks, eels, grouper, and snapper, studies examining natural control of lionfish by predators are limited. in regions where lionfish are considered established, a better understanding of the processes that control lionfish densities is needed. using acoustic telemetry, we deployed novel predation tags and examined changes in movement patterns to evaluate the frequency of predation on tagged lionfish within a complex, healthy, coral reef system. predation on lionfish was remarkably high, with movement patterns suggesting that both reef-associated and pelagic species preyed upon lionfish. while it is unclear at what point in the invasion native predators began consuming lionfish as prey, predation clearly plays an important role in population control in their established range. these findings further support the important role that native predators play in regulating the population dynamics of invasive species and maintaining healthy ecosystems .",
