          "name": "Juan Pablo D'Olivo Cordero",
          "search_text": "juan pablo d'olivo cordero integrating growth and geochemistry to track emerging stressors on caribbean coral reefs caribbean coral reefs are facing escalating pressures at unprecedented scales. in the mesoamerican reef system, these include intensifying local anthropogenic impacts driven by rapid population growth and inadequate wastewater treatment, recurrent and increasingly severe thermal stress event, most notably the extreme bleaching event of 2023, and the growing influence of sargassum influxes, whose impacts on reef health and water quality remain insufficiently quantified. here, we assess multi-decadal variability in coral skeletal growth and apply a suite of geochemical proxies to reconstruct environmental change, with particular focus on the recent 2023 thermal stress event. by combining high-resolution growth measurements with trace-element ratios and nitrogen stable isotopes, we aim to link variations in calcification to interacting drivers including temperature, nutrient enrichment, and possible sargassum-related inputs. our results aim to provide new insight into how local and global stressors are shaping reef resilience in one of the caribbean\u2019s most threatened reef systems.",
