      "search_text": "universidad san francisco de quito margarita brandt margarita brandt evaluating ecosystem function through sound: acoustic responses to 7,000 pocillopora transplants in the gal\u00e1pagos. coral reefs in the gal\u00e1pagos, though spatially limited, are critical habitats sustaining high marine biodiversity. after most structural reefs were lost during the 1982\u201383 el ni\u00f1o, natural recovery has been slow and fragmented. to help these reefs recover more quickly, we are combining ecological knowledge with new technologies and local capacity building.\r\n\r\nover the past three years, more than 7,000 pocillopora fragments have been transplanted in isabela. this project is the first mid-scale coral restoration effort in the archipelago and aims to speed up recovery while creating monitoring systems that can be scaled and shared. in 2023, before transplanting the corals, we recorded soundscapes from three contrasting habitats: a degraded reef, a non-degraded reef, and the outplant zone. in this presentation, we will show differences in acoustics and biotic activity across these habitats, providing an early baseline for how the restored reef may evolve acoustically over time.\r\n\r\nin addition, and to understand how restoration affects reef functioning, we will be using continuous passive acoustic monitoring with both low-cost recorders and research-grade hydrophones. we will also be testing an acoustic enrichment system that broadcasts healthy reef sounds to the restored habitats to attract larvae. in parallel, settlement tiles with different materials and textures will be deployed to explore how substrate type and sound interact to shape ecosystem recovery. our monitoring approach combines ai-supported analysis, gopro footage, and visual surveys, allowing us to track changes efficiently and at lower cost than traditional methods.\r\n\r\nfinally, a key part of this project is strengthening local expertise. we will run two acoustic workshops in the gal\u00e1pagos and replicate the acoustic monitoring and analysis at a second restoration site in santa cruz island, led by the gal\u00e1pagos national park. altogether, the project offers a practical and scalable model for community-grounded restoration across the eastern tropical pacific.",
