      "search_text": "universidade do estado do rio de janeiro joel creed larissa pires teixeira joel creed the marine aquarium trade as an emerging vector of introduction of reef-derived invasive species in brazil the marine aquarium trade (mat) acts as a significant vector for the introduction of invasive species primarily through the intentional or accidental release of organisms and the improper disposal of aquarium water. marine biological invasions are increasing globally and cause or are likely to cause ever more economic, environmental or human health harm. in brazil all mat species are imported, most are tropical in origin and the number of aquarium-derived introductions have increased. the aim of the present study was to investigate the mat in terms of propagule pressure (number, frequency and types of species imported), commercial practices and the relationship between imports, sales and introductions. data from the siscites/ibama system, which gathered requests for import of species into brazil between 2020 and 2023 were analyzed. furthermore, a survey was conducted on brazilian aquarium websites to record traded species. during the period, 15,053 requests for animal imports were registered in brazil, with marine invertebrates accounting for approximately 82% of the total. seven phyla (porifera, cnidaria, platyhelminthes, annelida, mollusca, arthropoda and echinodermata) account for nearly 12,323 import requests, an average of 262 per month. the stony corals (scleractinia) were the most imported marine invertebrates (24.9% of imports; 124 species) followed by sea stars (valvatida) and sea anemones (actiniaria) (14.5% and > 1.3 million specimens each) and globular sea urchins (camarodonta, 6.2%). brazil imported from 40 countries but half of imports came from indonesia. in the national ornamental aquarium trade, 1,951 morphotaxa of marine invertebrates were listed. hexacorallia was most commercialized in the mat, mainly scleractinia, zoantharia and corallimorpharia. reef-derived nonnative species were dominant in the brazilian mat, were easily purchased online and thus represent an important vector of introduction with significant propagule pressure, but hidden local practices such as fragging may provide a mismatch between imports, sales and introduction. larissa pires teixeira inclusive and accessible ocean literacy: university extension as a tool for transformation. inclusion in education is a legally guaranteed right and a pathway toward a more equitable society. within the context of marine conservation\u2013particularly the protection of reef environments\u2013strengthening dialogue between universities and coastal communities is essential. the project \u201cpara que serve? o que faz um cientista que trabalha com o mar?\u201d (what\u2019s it for? what does a marine scientist do?) promotes inclusive and participatory knowledge exchange, introducing students to themes associated with marine ecology, reef conservation, and the protection of marine species. to support accessible learning, we developed playful activities and multisensory educational materials designed to be inclusive for students with different visual abilities. we evaluated the effectiveness of these science activities in enhancing the understanding of basic ecological concepts, including those related to reef environments and ecosystem conservation. six sessions were conducted in two schools located in a coastal city in rio de janeiro. all activities incorporated multisensory materials handcrafted with common, low-cost materials. twenty-nine students between 5-17 took part; 36% were blind or low-vision, and 64% were sighted. across all sessions, multisensory materials elicited higher engagement and facilitated comprehension for both groups, demonstrating their value in supporting learning about marine and reef ecosystems. nonetheless, educator mediation played a critical role, highlighting the need for trained facilitators for inclusive conservation education. by integrating accessibility, hands-on learning, and marine conservation themes, this project strengthened the relationship between academic and coastal communities and fostered early awareness of reef ecosystem importance. we hope these results stimulate the development of more inclusive outreach initiatives, broaden public understanding of marine and reef environments, and inspire actions that promote scientific knowledge beyond traditional academic boundaries. eight years after invasion: changes in a benthic community following the establishment of a non-native octocoral. invasive species are considered one of the main threats to marine biodiversity, and biological invasions are becoming increasingly frequent. the aquarium trade is emerging as an important source of non-native species with the potential to invade aquatic habitats. the octocoral latissimia ningalooensis was first detected in 2017 on tropical rocky reefs of praia vermelha, angra dos reis, brazil. it is native to australia and was probably introduced through aquarium release. since then it has spread and increased in abundance. this study aims to quantify the change in distribution and abundance of l. ningalooensis in the region over eight years since its introduction and to examine the change in the structure of the benthic community. we used a relative abundance scale (dafor: dominant, abundant, frequent, occasional, rare) to examine spread and quantified the benthic community with photoquadrats. in the years following its introduction, the structure of the benthic community shifted, with negative effects on native biodiversity, including decreased species richness and a decline in native mat-forming species. l. ningalooensis increased its distribution area, expanding into nearby coastal zones including a marine protected area. our results reinforce the potential that invasive marine species have to modify the biodiversity of host communities and raise an alert the ornamental species trade is an important vector which needs integrated strategies for management, including regulation, continuous monitoring, community education, and rapid eradication actions to contain biological invasions and protect vulnerable coastal ecosystems. settlement and substratum preference of invasive azooxanthellate corals (tubastraea). the number of benthic marine species that are becoming invasive worldwide is increasing. azooxanthellate corals of the tubastraea genus are recognized globally as invasive marine species and have caused significant environmental, economic, and social impacts. these corals have two distinct phases: the larval phase, in which they are mobile, and after recruitments, the sessile adult phase. the success in the recruitment phase which is influenced by the reproductive rate, substratum type and inclination and the composition the receptor community are crucial for the establishment of their population. here we investigated the settlement of tubastraea spp. onto submerged acrylic plates (artificial substrate) at different angles using as well as the abundance of adult colonies on different rocky shores in a marine protected area in brazil. we also related the settlement of tubastraea to the encrusting benthic community and the topographic complexity of the rocky shore. there was an exponential growth in the number of individuals settling throughout the experiment, which lasted one year and tubastraea settled at all four angles of inclination of the artificial substrate. adult colonies on natural substrate were also found at all available angles on the rocky reef, but mostly vertically. on the natural substrate, the abundance of tubastraea spp. was lower in the presence of mat-forming species. on the artificial substrate, the number of settled individuals was lower in the presence of encrusting benthic species, such as ascidians and crustose coralline algae. although the host community may offer some biotic resistance, the high reproductive rate and the ability to grow in different orientations make these invasive corals successful.",
