      "search_text": "universite de la polynesie francaise chlo\u00e9 pozas-schacre damien carrasquet chlo\u00e9 pozas-schacre host genetics shapes fire coral holobiont structure across distinct reef habitats coral holobionts emerge from complex associations between the cnidarian host and diverse symbiotic partners, yet the relative influence of host identity and environmental conditions in shaping holobiont structure remains difficult to disentangle. strong host fidelity is often observed, but resolving host identity at the species level may overlook fine-scale structuring driven by intraspecific genetic variation. in this context, hydrozoan reef-builders (milleporidae) remain particularly understudied despite their key ecological roles in tropical reefs. here, we investigated host\u2013symbiont associations in three co-occurring millepora species (millepora cf. exaesa, m. tenera, and m. cf. platyphylla) across three environmentally distinct habitats on r\u00e9union island (inner reef flat, outer reef flat, mid-reef slope), combining microsatellite genotyping of coral hosts with its2 and 16s rrna metabarcoding of symbiodiniaceae and prokaryotic assemblages. symbiodiniaceae communities were largely dominated by symbiodinium (99.2%), primarily from a3 and a16 lineages. cladocopium occurred at very low abundance (0.8%) and predominantly associated with m. cf. platyphylla and m. tenera. most symbiodinaceae profiles were restricted to particular species and genotypes within habitats. similarly, prokaryotic assemblages were primarily structured by host genotypes, though to a lesser extent than symbiodiniaceae. millepora hosts consistently associated with the spirochaetaceae, kiloniellaceae and desulfobacteraceae bacterial families across all habitats, suggesting key evolutionary-maintained associations. within habitats, each millepora species harbored a distinct microbiome, yet both symbiodiniaceae and prokaryotic assemblages also shifted along the habitat gradient, reflecting flexibility of host-symbiont associations and potential acclimatization or adaptation to local environmental conditions. our results indicate that fire coral holobionts are primarily structured by host-driven processes, with the environment acting as a secondary selective layer for symbiont acquisition. this study provides the first microbiome description of m. tenera and m. cf. exaesa, and sheds light on how host identity and environment jointly shape unique host-symbiont associations contributing to the ecological success of these overlooked coral holobionts. damien carrasquet ai-based stereo-vision system for automated benthic identification and monitoring in french polynesia effective management of polynesian coastal ecosystems requires reliable, repeatable, and large-scale monitoring of key benthic communities. among these, sea cucumbers (holothuroidea) are of particular interest due to their commercial value and ecological role. however, current survey methods, including conventional diver-based surveys, are labor-intensive, risky, and restricted in spatial and temporal coverage. to address these limitations, we developed an integrated imaging-and-ai pipeline for  automated, rapid, and scalable evaluations of holothurian population abundance and density.\r\n\r\nto this purpose, we designed a modular stereoscopic system with paired gopro cameras and adjustable baselines, enabling depth estimation through triangulation similar  to human vision. this compact platform records continuous underwater video suitable for object detection, segmentation, and size reconstruction. using an annotated dataset of several thousand images representing five polynesian holothurian species, we fine-tuned recent deep-learning-based detection models and obtained high performance in identifying individuals across variable reef environments. furthermore, to capture fine-scale morphology and derive biomass-relevant measurements, detections were passed to a segmentation module that extracts masks from bounding-box prompts. by coupling stereo geometry with keypoint correspondence between left and right frames, we obtained accurate estimates of holothurian body length and elongation even under variable poses. further refinements involve combining visual outputs with morphometric and species-level trait data to minimize prediction errors and improve species identification.\r\n\r\nby integrating underwater stereo-vision, deep-learning-based detection and segmentation, and geometric size reconstruction, we established a foundation for scalable, automated holothurian surveys across polynesian lagoons, supporting ecological assessments and sustainable harvesting., the system is versatile, operable by divers, and can potentially be adapted to other platforms (e.g., rovs, auvs) enabling participatory monitoring and providing data to support adaptive co-management of benthic resources.",
