      "search_text": "university college london cameron white-spunner lewis a. jones cameron white-spunner investigating the effects of island-invasive rats on coral reef biodiversity using edna the invasion of tropical islands by rats has not only devastated terrestrial biotas, but has severely impacted the surrounding coral reefs. invasive rats have been implicated in the declines and extirpations of seabirds on 90% of island groups globally. rats predate on seabird eggs, chicks, and sometimes adults, causing catastrophic population declines that interrupt the flow of seabird-derived nutrients to nearby coral reefs. as a result, affected reefs suffer from reduced productivity and functioning, as well as reduced growth rates and lower overall biomass in reef fishes. however, no study has investigated how coral reef community composition is impacted by invasive rats, including how taxonomic, functional and phylogenetic diversities may be affected by the loss of seabird nutrient input. to address this knowledge gap we focused on the seychelles archipelago, which provides numerous replicates of both rat-infested and rat-free islands allowing robust comparisons to be made. we employed environmental (e)dna metabarcoding to quantify and compare coral reef biodiversity between five rat-invaded and five rat-free islands. we collected >130 edna water samples across these islands, supplemented by high frequency temporal samples taken every two hours from sunrise and sunset to capture diel variation in community composition. all samples were sequenced using 12s (mitofish) primers targeting ray-finned fishes and elasmobranchs. paired traditional visual survey data (obtained by scuba) was also collected at a subset of sites for comparison and validation of edna data. by integrating edna-derived species diversity with evolutionary and functional trait data, this study provides a multi-faceted assessment of how invasive rats may restructure reef fish communities via loss of seabird-derived nutrients. our findings will offer new insights into how invasive rats are impacting coral reef ecosystems and will inform island restoration efforts. lewis a. jones shining a light on dark data: towards a sustainable community repository for historical reef imagery historical underwater photographs provide rare windows into the past condition of coral reefs. these archives offer empirical site-specific data for reconstructing ecosystem baselines and improving the evidence base available to managers and policy makers for adopting conservation strategies. despite their inherent value, these \u2018dark data\u2019 are being lost to the ages due to a lack of suitable infrastructure and community effort to organise, curate, and preserve these archives. as part of a new initiative to build a sustainable, open-access repository for legacy reef imagery and metadata, we showcase a digitised collection of 1,480 photographs taken by eileen graham in the mid-1960s near discovery bay, jamaica. these images\u2013now freely accessible through the uk natural history museum\u2019s data portal\u2013include precise spatial and temporal information and document reef communities before their widespread decline in the 1980s and prior to the establishment of long-term caribbean monitoring programmes.  these archives range from fine-scale close-ups of individual corals to broad reefscape views. the collection captures ecologically meaningful features such as benthic assemblage composition, coral cover, and structural complexity. we propose developing a global network of historical reef-image repositories, capitalising on the unique opportunity presented by the first generation of scuba-enabled scientists who are now retiring and seeking long-term stewardship for their photographic records. our experience highlights the importance of working directly with photographers to document site locations, dive histories, and contextual details while this knowledge is still accessible. finally, we emphasise the essential role of museums and archives as enduring custodians of these materials\u2013unlike short-term research projects, these institutions can provide long-term stability, dedicated curation expertise, and open-data infrastructures to ensure that historical reef imagery remains accessible for future scientific, conservation, and educational use. morphological traits as proxies of fossil coral photosymbiotic state recent advances in geochemistry have enabled the detection of photosymbiosis in fossil corals using geochemical proxies (e.g. 15n/14n). while promising, its application requires rarely available exceptionally-preserved specimens, particularly for aragonitic scleractinian corals. consequently, upscaling its application to all extinct coral taxa is unfeasible. in contrast, morphological characters are documented for nearly all described taxa. here, using the full diversity of extant corals (>1700 species) for which photosymbiotic state is known, we test whether objectively measured morphological traits (e.g. corallite width and integration, coloniality, growth form) can reliably predict photosymbiotic state across taxonomic levels. our analyses extend previous work with far greater taxonomic, spatial, and environmental coverage. we present a preliminary classification framework for inferring the photosymbiotic state of extinct corals and evaluate its validity using independent evidence from the published literature, including available geochemical proxy data. together, our results offer insight into the capacity of morphological traits to serve as a robust and scalable approach for reconstructing photosymbiosis in fossil scleractinian corals across evolutionary timescales. the edith project: elucidating diversity dynamics in cenozoic marine tropical hotspots global marine biodiversity is concentrated in the indo-australian archipelago today, where corals and other marine organisms achieve their greatest species richness. however, the fossil record suggests at least four marine biodiversity hotspots existed throughout the cenozoic, waxing and waning in response to global environmental changes: (1) western tethys (eocene\u2013oligocene); (2) arabian (eocene\u2013miocene); (3) caribbean (oligocene\u2013pliocene); and (4) indo-australian archipelago (miocene\u2013recent). while broadly recognised, fundamental gaps in our understanding of these biodiversity hotspots exist including the timing and drivers of their origin, persistence, and ultimate decline. understanding these long-term dynamics is crucial for anticipating how modern biodiversity hotspots may respond to accelerating environmental change over the next century. through the edith project, we are investigating the diversity dynamics within cenozoic marine tropical hotspots to reconstruct their macroevolutionary history and identify their long-term drivers. through comparison across multiple marine biodiversity hotspots, we will identify\u2013if any\u2013general drivers of such concentrations of biodiversity. here, we present initial work focused on reconstructing the evolutionary history of the caribbean marine biodiversity hotspot. using a comprehensive dataset of fossil scleractinian coral occurrences and sampling-standardisation approaches, we first estimate alpha, beta, and gamma diversity for the region. extending upon these analyses, we apply state-of-the-art bayesian modelling to estimate diversification rates throughout the cenozoic. our results provide an evolutionary baseline for the caribbean marine biodiversity hotspot, enabling comparison with other cenozoic marine biodiversity hotspots.",
