          "search_text": "sophie wong fluorescent proteins in corals interact with symbiont photophysiology and holobiont bleaching response coral hosts and algal endosymbionts have evolved mechanisms to deal with the stressors of the reef environment while maintaining high photosynthetic productivity. fluorescent proteins (fps) produced by the host, which capture light and re-emit it at lower-energy wavelengths, alter the light environment in host tissues and likely play a large role in the coevolutionary dynamics of coral-algal symbioses. to understand the role of coral fps in maintaining optimal light environments for algal photo-symbionts (family: symbiodiniaceae), we developed a nondestructive custom fp fluorometer that uses six excitation wavelengths to individually target cyan, green, and red fps and capture quantitative emission spectra with a high-resolution spectrometer. we paired data from this instrument with photo-symbiont light use characteristics via chlorophyll-a fluorescence measurements to create highly detailed phenotypes of host and symbiont bio-optical characteristics. together, these tools were used to analyze hundreds of individual coral colonies from four different genera (acropora, pavona, pocillopora, and porites) from nearshore environments in fiji and guam. using network analyses, we then looked for correlation between host fp and symbiont photophysiological mechanisms and mapped those to differences in bleaching response. across species, photo-physiological mechanisms appear correlated to various host fp signatures and may reflect co-evolutionary stress response mechanisms that are unique to each host/symbiont combination. next, a combination of acute and chronic experimental bleaching assays were used to connect these complex bio-optical signatures with thermal tolerance. coral-algal symbioses are central to the evolution of scleractinian corals and success of coral reef ecosystems across the globe. our approach using bio-optical tools to characterize highly connected traits within both the host and symbiont provides unique and novel perspective on ecologically and coevolutionarily important physiology.",
