          "name": "Adhithi Balaji",
          "search_text": "adhithi balaji towards quantifying reef flat growth: comparing close-range and drone photogrammetry on a fringing reef in tongatapu. direct measurement of reef flat vertical accretion in the field remains rare, with most estimates inferred from geological or ecological proxies of vertical accretion. wave-dominated reef flats pose logistical challenges for deploying instruments capable of monitoring elevational change over time. yet, understanding whether reef flats can keep pace with rising sea levels is critical. close-range structure-from-motion (sfm) photogrammetry offers millimetre-scale vertical accuracy for monitoring growth of reef calcifying organisms, but close-range applications are rare and typically limited to individual colonies and small quadrats. it remains challenging to overcome limitations in co-registering sfm models across time and scaling up spatial coverage of models, while maintaining high elevational accuracy. \r\nthis study evaluates the potential of drone-based sfm to overcome these limitations by comparing digital surface models (dsms) derived from close-range and aerial imagery of a fringing reef flat in tongatapu. close-range dsms were generated from handheld digital camera surveys of 1 m\u00b2 quadrats at ~1 m elevation. aerial dsms were produced from drone imagery captured with a dji mavic 3e at 10\u201315 m elevation, covering a much larger area of reef flat, while overlapping with the location of the close-range quadrats. all orthomosaics and dsms were processed in agisoft, with ground control points surveyed using a leica total station to ensure a consistent and accurate vertical datum across time.\r\nwe present a comparative analysis of accuracy, spatial coverage, and repeatability between the two approaches, assessing their suitability for detecting elevational changes of the reef flat\u2019s morphologically differing calcifying organisms and substrates through time. findings highlight the capabilities and limitations of drone-based sfm for scaling up reef monitoring while maintaining the precision required to quantify vertical growth. these insights inform the development of cost-effective, repeatable monitoring strategies essential for predicting reef flat resilience under accelerating sea-level rise.",
