          "name": "Ivonne Paola Rojas Martinez",
          "search_text": "ivonne paola rojas martinez first evidence of trace elements bioaccumulation in the mediterranean coral balanophyllia europaea (scleractinia, dendrophylliidae) the concurrent action of climate change and the accumulation of anthropogenic pollutants represent a major threat to coastal marine environments. in this context, the effects on scleractinian corals are particularly relevant as they sustain marine biodiversity. corals, as long-living benthic organisms, are capable of bioaccumulating heavy metals in response to environmental factors and anthropogenic impacts. their skeletons incorporate these metals by absorbing those dissolved in seawater, which are then retained indefinitely as they become encapsulated within the overlying carbonate formed during new skeletal growth. this characteristic makes corals valuable sentinels for monitoring environmental contamination and its potential ecological consequences. most studies quantifying trace-element concentrations in scleractinian corals have been conducted in the red sea, the persian gulf, and coastal regions of australia; however, analogous studies in the mediterranean sea are scarce.   \r\nthis study explores trace element accumulation in mediterranean scleractinian corals, using the zooxanthellate solitary coral balanophyllia europaea as a reporter species. populations from eight sites along the italian coastline, encompassing a gradient from heavily impacted to relatively pristine conditions, were sampled to assess the status of this species across the mediterranean sea. coral samples were separated into three biological compartments, host tissue, symbiotic algae, and skeleton, to characterize the compartment-specific distribution of trace elements. concentrations of al, as, cd, cr, cu, fe, k, mg, mn, na, ni, pb, se, sn, and zn were measured using inductively coupled plasma - optical emission spectrometry (icp-oes). intra and inter-population comparisons provide new insights into differential metal accumulation patterns among coral compartments and highlight the potential of b. europaea as a bioindicator of heavy metal pollution in the mediterranean sea.",
