          "search_text": "jessica hodge artificial substrate characteristics outweigh the influence of acoustic enrichment in determining larval settlement outcomes a key mechanism by which coral populations are replenished and recover from disturbance is through the arrival and settlement of larvae. coral larvae orient themselves towards settlement sites on the reef using multiple cues, including acoustics produced by soniferous organisms on the reef and chemical cues from crustose coralline algae and biofilms. to investigate whether acoustic enrichment could alleviate the need for long periods of conditioning of artificial substrates, we played back recorded healthy local coral reef soundscapes to coral larvae. we assessed settlement and metamorphosis in comparison to no playback control treatments in the presence of substrates conditioned for various amounts of time. initial observational studies of phonotaxis in broadcast spawning corals were made on seven species in florida. following this, controlled experiments were conducted on acropora palmata in cura\u00e7ao and diploria labyrinthiformis in mexico in contained environments, either small-scale, ex situ laboratory setups or large-scale in situ enclosures. we found limited evidence that healthy reef sound altered levels of settlement in coral larvae of both species in the experimental setups. in line with previous research, preconditioning of settlement material had a much larger influence on larval settlement success. therefore, acoustics may be more influential at larger, reef scales as larvae circulate in the water column searching for a healthy reef, while finer-scale chemical cues may become more important when larvae search for a suitable settlement habitat at centimeter scales. these findings add to an emerging area of research into acoustics cues and coral larval settlement. these data contrast with those of existing work on brooding corals, highlighting differences between species and experimental context. understanding these cues, and how they interact, may help to boost the settlement of new individuals onto the reef and support coral reef recovery.",
