          "name": "Rosie Rallings",
          "search_text": "rosie rallings tiny tyrants: artificial models of aggressive damselfish reduce corallivory on nearby corals corallivory poses a major challenge to coral reef restoration, particularly during the early stages of transplantation when juvenile corals are most vulnerable to predation. territorial damselfish stegastes nigricans exhibit aggressive behaviour that can influence surrounding reef communities and potentially deter roaming corallivores. this study compared the influence of both live s. nigricans and \u2018scarecrow\u2019 s. nigricans models on coral predation at port ghalib in the egyptian red sea. bite densities on established pocillopora verrucosa colonies were quantified across three treatments: within live s. nigricans territories, in non-territory areas, and alongside model s. nigricans. additional experiments measured the survival of transplanted acropora humilis fragments placed alongside each treatment. we found that live s. nigricans did not provide consistent protection to corals, and often acted as antagonists, increasing coral tissue damage through farm-maintenance behaviours that promoted algal overgrowth. a. humilis fragments were actively removed (55% success rate over 4 weeks) or rapidly overgrown by filamentous algae characteristic of damselfish farms. in contrast, scarecrow models of s. nigricans significantly reduced corallivory on p. verrucosa without the associated damage of territory maintenance, supporting a new strategy of model-based deterrence. although the deterrent effect plateaued over time, likely due to corallivore habituation and biofouling of the models, these findings represent the first empirical evidence that artificial fish models can reduce coral predation in a natural reef setting. the use of s. nigricans scarecrows offers a novel, low-cost approach to protecting vulnerable corals during the initial stages of restoration, avoiding the damaging effects associated with live territorial damselfish. while their effect was transient, refinements to model design, such as enhanced realism, movement, or multisensory cues, could improve efficacy and longevity. this study demonstrates the potential of behavioural deterrents as practical tools for coral restoration and highlights the value of integrating ecological behaviour into coral reef restoration.",
