      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Anthony Bonacolta",
          "search_text": "anthony bonacolta the ecology and evolution of coral-infecting apicomplexans (corallicolids) corals from shallow tropical reefs to deep, cold waters are infected with apicomplexans known as corallicolids. apicomplexans are prolific parasites of metazoans, with the most well-known being the malaria pathogen, plasmodium. corallicolids are unique amongst the apicomplexa in their retention of the chlorophyll biosynthesis pathway within their non-photosynthetic plastid. they also appear to be in a commensal relationship with corals, contrary to the \u201cparasitic\u201d label commonly attached to apicomplexans. recent research on these peculiar coral symbionts has expanded our knowledge on their ecology and evolution, enlightening their role in coral ecosystem health and resiliency. metabarcoding studies have expanded their geographic distribution to cold water anthozoans and have even identified another host and potential vector for these symbionts, the bearded fireworm (hermodice carunculata). as these corallivourous fireworms are invasive in warming marine ecosystems, climate change has the potential to facilitate corallicolid transmission to new reefs. laboratory heat-stress experiments have also shown that the relative abundance of corallicolids can negatively impact coral host thermal tolerance, underscoring their role in coral reef resiliency to climate change. lastly, phylogenomic analyses have clarified the evolutionary position of the corallicolids within apicomplexa, shedding light on how the chlorophyll biosynthesis pathway has been co-opted in this lineage. although these recent advances have illuminated key aspects of corallicolid ecology and evolution, much of their biology remains a mystery. further work on the corallicolids will not only advance coral conservation efforts, but also offers the potential to uncover new and exciting biological innovations within this second most prevalent coral protist.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "The ecology and evolution of coral-infecting apicomplexans (corallicolids)",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "e27bf550-487b-4465-8ade-59541f5507ff"
            },
            {
              "title": "A systematic multi-omics framework to characterize and differentiate infectious white diseases across the coral holobiont",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "2f120fb2-1fb8-4be1-a106-730629943d56"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Virginie Bornarel",
          "search_text": "virginie bornarel quantifying global biases in coral bleaching severity using bayesian ordinal models and satellite-derived heat stress accurate long-term monitoring of coral bleaching is essential for understanding climate-driven changes in reef ecosystems and informing management strategies. yet global bleaching databases rely on in situ surveys that are rarely conducted at the peak of thermal stress. therefore, bleaching severities recorded too early\u2013before peak heat stress\u2013or too late\u2013after partial recovery\u2013may underestimate the true severity experienced by corals. despite the extensive use of these datasets in global assessments and climate-risk analyses, the magnitude of this possible timing bias remains unquantified. here, we assess the potential underestimation of bleaching severity in a global database. we matched 21,008 bleaching observations (1963\u20132017) to noaa crw 5-km daily dhw values at the survey date, and extracted all daily dhw fields within \u00b16 months to reconstruct each site\u2019s thermal stress trajectory. from this window, we obtained the maximum heat stress reached during the event (max_dhw), and the time offset between the survey and max_dhw. using these metrics, we developed two bayesian cumulative ordinal regression models: a pre-peak model relating severity to dhw at the survey and a post-peak model relating severity to max_dhw and time since the thermal peak. these models quantify how the probability of each severity category shifts with these metrics. using four rule-based criteria for identifying reports that underestimate bleaching severity, preliminary results show that 20\u201327% of reports were consistently flagged. underestimation increases when surveys occur \u226542 days before or \u226580\u201390 days after the heat-stress peak. globally, mild and moderate bleaching appear systematically under-reported. by identifying when and where underestimation is most likely \u2013 and by establishing severity-dhw probability relationships \u2013 this work provides actionable guidance for field monitoring programs, strengthens interpretation of long-term databases, and enhances the reliability of large-scale assessments supporting 21st-century reef management.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Quantifying global biases in coral bleaching severity using Bayesian ordinal models and satellite-derived heat stress",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "44530b2b-c0d5-4a3f-b2db-8ecc388ef707"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
