          "name": "Rachel Carlson",
          "search_text": "rachel carlson island-wide mapping of groundwater microclimates in coral reef ecosystems coral reefs in high island ecosystems are heavily influenced by freshwater inputs including submarine groundwater discharge (sgd), which can alter carbonate chemistry, temperature, nutrients, and pollution exposure on reefs. sgd may also create microclimates where the effects of heatwaves and ocean acidification vary. for example, sgd in tropical islands is typically colder than ambient seawater, potentially providing localized cooling during heatwaves, while, conversely, sgd often contains low-ph waters that may exacerbate ocean acidification. however, these important microclimates are difficult to quantify because the magnitude and quality of sgd vary widely over space and time. prior research on sgd has primarily focused on single seeps or case studies that do not capture coast-wide spatial variability in sgd influence. to address this gap, we are developing low-cost, island-scale remote sensing methods using unmanned aerial vehicles (uavs) to map sgd plumes across the coastline of mo\u02bborea, french polynesia. we demonstrate the feasibility of island-wide sgd mapping using thermal uav imagery within monitoring schedules and cost limitations appropriate to public agencies or local managers. results reveal: 1) a high risk of undersampling groundwater discharge when relying solely on in situ measurements, 2) key physical parameters (e.g., altitude, wind speed, tidal height, and solar conditions) required for groundwater mapping at island scales, 3) new insights into the spatial distribution of sgd seeps, and 4) future directions and limitations of aerial measurements of sgd in reef systems. overall, we aim to provide a scalable framework for monitoring groundwater influences on coral reef ecosystems, improving predictions of reef responses to climate stressors, and informing integrated land\u2013sea conservation and restoration. opportunities and challenges for engaging coastal tourism in financing reef restoration. coral reefs provide critical social and economic benefits to coastal communities, supporting tourism and protecting shoreline infrastructure during large storms. given the high economic value of coral reefs to coastal tourism, new initiatives such as coral insurance programs have begun to engage the private sector in funding coral restoration. however, it is unclear whether coastal businesses - key beneficiaries of reef ecosystem services - perceive coral reefs as an asset to business activities. in addition, further research is needed to identify common features of businesses that will likely participate in restoration initiatives like coral insurance. we surveyed coastal tourism businesses on o\u02bbahu and hawai\u02bbi island, hawai\u02bbi, to measure business perception of coral reef health and value. we also identified variables correlated with business willingness to pay for coral restoration through a coral insurance. our results indicate that many coastal businesses, even those operating on the ocean, see limited economic value in protecting coral reefs. however, most businesses showed strong willingness to pay for coral reef insurance. pro-nature/stewardship norms, concern for business reputation, and native hawaiian ethnicity were significantly correlated with willingness to pay for coral insurance, while length of employment was correlated with decreased payment. we find that appealing to stewardship norms, social networks, and reputational concerns can motivate tourism businesses to participate in coral restoration. finally, survey results indicate that high transparency, democratic decision-making, and leadership by well-known and trusted community groups are critical to ensuring that private-sector coral financing is successful. based on follow-up interviews with key decision makers in coral insurance, we discuss considerations for the design and implementation of the policy, as well its present outlook for renewal.",
