          "name": "Anya Brown",
          "search_text": "anya brown can coral diversity increase resistance to thermal stress? genotypic diversity leads to increased resistance to disease in coral populations. however, it is unknown how increased genotypic diversity influences coral responses to other important global stressors, such as high temperatures. one underlying mechanism underlying increased resilience under stress is contributions from the coral microbiome. we hypothesize that when corals are in diverse mixtures, their microbiomes shift towards increased bacteria associated with thermal resistance. we tested how genetic diversity influences coral growth, thermal stress, and microbial responses in a coral nursery in rarotonga, cook islands.  we arranged genets of acropora hyacinthus corals from five sites around the island of rarotonga in monocultures (single genotypes) and polycultures (mixtures of genotypes). corals grew in these arrangements for one year, and following one year, they were exposed to an acute heat stress experiment. our results suggest that the net biodiversity effects of polycultures depend on coral sensitivity to thermal stress: sensitive corals grow more in polyculture. for most coral responses (e.g., physiological characteristics, such as lipid content, carbohydrate content), the effects of polyculture depended on genotype. continuing work will assess if mixtures of coral genotypes lead to a) an increase in thermal stress resistance and b) convergent microbial communities associated with lower thermal stress. our results provide insight into whether population diversity can buffer thermally sensitive corals to stress, and if a mechanism underlying this response is due to adaptive shifts in the coral microbiome. we expect this research to inform coral restoration practices by quantifying the individual, population, and ecosystem benefits associated with increasing coral genotypic diversity.",
