          "name": "Benjamin Moran",
          "search_text": "benjamin moran pacific-wide population genomics in the acropora hyacinthus complex: speciation, gene flow, and local adaptation growing evidence suggests that many morphologically defined coral species contain multiple genetically distinct groups, or cryptic taxa. these complex patterns of genetic diversity present a challenge to traditional conservation, but also an exciting opportunity to reveal the fundamental processes by which species diverge genomically and ecologically. the table coral, acropora hyacinthus, is one such cryptic complex, with recent population genetic and phylogenetic work describing numerous cryptic sympatric groups, some of which may represent independent species. however, previous studies have been limited in geographic extent, sample size per location, or in the number of genetic markers surveyed. as such, the relative strength of within-location population structure vs. isolation by distance at the oceanic scale, the extent of historical and contemporary gene flow between taxa, and the degree to which cryptic taxa may share adaptive genetic variation, remains unclear. i will present the work of a collaborative network combining 1300 new and existing a. hyacinthus samples from over 40 sites throughout the species\u2019 pacific range. we will subject these sequences to a standardized whole-genome sequencing and genotyping pipeline, and use this unique dataset to create a clearer and broader view of diversity within the a. hyacinthus complex. we will distinguish cryptic population structure from the effects of allopatric divergence, and estimate effective migration rates across the range of each cryptic taxa. we will also identify admixed individuals and infer the history of gene flow across the complex, revealing the degree of long-term reproductive isolation between taxa. finally, we will perform genotype-environment association within each taxa to infer loci involved in local adaptation, and quantify the extent to which adaptive alleles are shared across taxa. together, our work will reveal the evolution of this ubiquitous coral at the pacific scale, and create a rich resource for future a. hyacinthus research and conservation.",
