          "name": "Rachael Bay",
          "search_text": "rachael bay what factors facilitate or limit coral adaptation under future warming? adaptive evolution of coral thermal performance under future ocean warming will depend on both the standing genetic variation on which natural selection can act and evolutionary constraints to adaptation. while many studies over the last decade have made progress toward short-term or high-throughput measurements of thermal tolerance, we are just beginning to understand the evolvability of thermal tolerance as a trait. in this talk, we will draw from multiple datasets to discuss the forces that facilitate or limit the evolution of thermal tolerance. first, we discuss evolutionary constraints due to genetic architecture, heritability, and tradeoffs. we measured traits related to symbiosis, energetics, and response to heat stress for ~150 colonies of acropora hyacinthus from moorea, french polynesia, finding both heritable variation in traits that might enhance adaptation (positive correlation with thermal tolerance) or constrain adaptation (negative correlation with thermal tolerance). using whole genome sequencing and a multi-trait framework we identify genomic regions associated with variation in these traits to build an understanding of mechanisms governing the co-inheritance of traits and limits to future adaptation. next, because standing variation in thermal tolerance is highly dependent on gene flow from populations harboring adaptive alleles, we pair our estimates of genetic architectures with fine-scale maps of gene flow in a. hyacinthus across the pacific. for this, we have established an international network of collaborators and accumulated ~1500 samples for whole genome sequencing of multiple \u2018cryptic species\u2019 of a. hyacinthus. using this data, we can map the movement of putatively adaptive alleles among populations, predicting capacity for evolutionary response to future warming scenarios.",
