          "search_text": "vivian li creating art with local and indigenous collaborators: lessons from a personal journey in art and science this is a story of a little girl who had no idea coral reefs existed. this little girl lived inside of a polluted metropolitan city. politics were strife with corruption, society focused on economic growth, the concept of nature was foreign. how does this little girl who can barely swim, who gets severely seasick, grow up to fall in love with the ocean? the answer - art, science and community. symbionts of symbionts: pangenomes of symbiodiniaceae-associated bacteria. lab cultures of cnidarian endophotosymbionts (symbiodiniaceae) harbor distinct microbiomes but also host a number of bacterial genera that are consistently shared between most symbiodiniaceae cultures. labrenzia (alphaproteobacteria) and marinobacter (gammaproteobacteria) have been consistently found in most symbiodiniaceae cultures regardless of differences in isolation method, culture maintenance practices, growth media, and growth conditions. we hypothesize that labrenzia and marinobacter are common in symbiodiniaceae cultures as they perform functions that support the physiology of symbiodiniaceae such as promoting growth rate and nutrient acquisition. assuming the microbiomes in symbiodiniaceae lab cultures could act as host surrogates for symbiodiniaceae, a better understanding of the ways in which bacteria support symbiodiniaceae physiology in culture could fill knowledge gaps about the nutritional requirements of symbiodiniaceae in hospite, thus informing coral conservation strategies. \r\n\r\nwe investigated the core pangenomes of symbiodiniaceae-derived labrenzia and marinobacter isolates to identify the genes that make labrenzia and marinobacter common and beneficial to symbiodiniaceae cultures. we identified gene functions and metabolic pathways that are enriched in labrenzia and marinobacter sequenced from symbiodiniaceae cultures compared to coral hosts, and other hosts/environments. adaptations unique to symbiotic symbiodiniaceae, free-living symbiodiniaceae and coral hosts were found and could be further studied to inform our understanding of coral-symbiont-microbe interactions.",
