          "name": "Sophia MacVittie",
          "search_text": "sophia macvittie the impact of host domestication on microbial functional potential in the model exaiptasia diaphana long-term cultivation of model systems within the laboratory can lead to divergence in host-associated microbiomes from natural host populations. this can limit our capacity to evaluate the functional potential of microbial members in lab-based experiments and translate our findings to natural systems. over the past 20 years, a global community of researchers have studied clonal populations of the sea anemone, exaiptasia diaphana (aiptasia), to understand cnidarian-algal symbioses. however, it is unclear how the domestication of aiptasia impacted the composition and function of their microbiomes.  considering that researchers are beginning to use this model to investigate the role of the microbiome in supporting host-algal symbiosis, it is essential to determine how lab cultivation has affected the functional potential of the microbiome. here, we sought to answer two questions: 1) what are the core microbial functions of the aiptasia microbiome, and 2) how does lab cultivation change the taxonomic and functional composition of the community?  to address these questions, we collected individual aiptasia from four field sites along a 500km stretch of the coast of florida, and individual polyps of the cc7 clonal line from four different laboratories. using full-length 16s rrna sequencing, we found that the taxonomic composition and diversity of host microbiomes across laboratory and field sites varied widely, suggesting that domestication of the microbiome is shifting the overall community composition. to explore functional changes within the community, we sequenced from bacteria-enriched samples and obtained metagenomically assembled genomes (mags). using our mag library, we identified core functions present across samples and documented which metabolic functions are lost during host domestication. overall, our results allowed us to identify changes in the metabolic potential of the aiptasia microbiome, which will inform future studies aiming to explore how microbial communities support cnidarian health.",
