      "search_text": "university of california - merced hailey hatch-west sophia macvittie hailey hatch-west reintroduction of bacteria to gnotobiotic hosts alters aiptasia host-microbiome interactions and metabolism cnidarians, including anemones, jellyfish, and reef-building corals, form dynamic and complex symbioses with a range of microbial species, collectively creating a metaorganism. interactions between these microbial taxa and their hosts directly impact the health, longevity and survival of the metaorganism. however, the functional roles of many microbial partners remains unknown, and it is unclear how their interactions support metaorganism metabolism. here, we experimentally manipulated the composition of the exaiptasia diaphana (aiptasia) microbiome to investigate the roles of three bacteria species on host and symbiont metabolism. for our study, we chose two species of sulfur cycling bacteria (labrenzia aggregata and ruegeria mobilis) and a putative pathogen (vibrio alginolyticus), all cultured from aiptasia. individual aiptasia were first treated with antibiotics to remove their native microbiome. then, we inoculated individual anemones with either single bacterial species or a synthetic community of all three species at equal densities. when the bacteria were introduced to the host as single inoculates, our qpcr data showed that they successfully colonized the host and were maintained throughout the experiment. however, when we inoculated individuals with all three species at once, we only observed successful colonization by l. aggregata and r. mobilis, which suggests these two species outcomplete the putative pathogen v. alginolyticus for space within the host. full-length 16s rrna sequencing data confirmed that the inoculation of the bacterial isolates directly shaped the overall structure and development of the microbiome as the aiptasia were recolonized following the antibiotic disturbance. metatranscriptomics results further revealed that the inoculation of each bacteria significantly altered key metabolic pathways. together, our results demonstrate how l. aggregata and r. mobilis modulate aiptasia metabolism and influence overall assembly of the aiptasia microbiome, highlighting the functional importance of specific microbial partners in cnidarian metaorganisms. sophia macvittie the impact of host domestication on microbial functional potential in the model exaiptasia diaphana long-term cultivation of model systems within the laboratory can lead to divergence in host-associated microbiomes from natural host populations. this can limit our capacity to evaluate the functional potential of microbial members in lab-based experiments and translate our findings to natural systems. over the past 20 years, a global community of researchers have studied clonal populations of the sea anemone, exaiptasia diaphana (aiptasia), to understand cnidarian-algal symbioses. however, it is unclear how the domestication of aiptasia impacted the composition and function of their microbiomes.  considering that researchers are beginning to use this model to investigate the role of the microbiome in supporting host-algal symbiosis, it is essential to determine how lab cultivation has affected the functional potential of the microbiome. here, we sought to answer two questions: 1) what are the core microbial functions of the aiptasia microbiome, and 2) how does lab cultivation change the taxonomic and functional composition of the community?  to address these questions, we collected individual aiptasia from four field sites along a 500km stretch of the coast of florida, and individual polyps of the cc7 clonal line from four different laboratories. using full-length 16s rrna sequencing, we found that the taxonomic composition and diversity of host microbiomes across laboratory and field sites varied widely, suggesting that domestication of the microbiome is shifting the overall community composition. to explore functional changes within the community, we sequenced from bacteria-enriched samples and obtained metagenomically assembled genomes (mags). using our mag library, we identified core functions present across samples and documented which metabolic functions are lost during host domestication. overall, our results allowed us to identify changes in the metabolic potential of the aiptasia microbiome, which will inform future studies aiming to explore how microbial communities support cnidarian health.",
