          "search_text": "ahmi cacapit contextualizing long-term monitoring trends through baseline demographic processes coral populations persist through processes of survivorship, growth, and recruitment, which together describe demographic trends. many recent studies document coral demographic patterns under extreme external forcing, such as responses to marine heat waves that result in widespread mortality. however, such reports often fail to address baseline patterns of populations under natural, stochastic change. new advances, particularly photogrammetry, enhance our capacity to investigate colony-specific change across reefs. in this study, we used large-area imagery products to track 1,000 coral colonies spanning a 10-year interval across five geographies: cura\u00e7ao, the florida keys, maui, millennium atoll, and palmyra atoll. coral colonies were digitized in taglab, an ai-assisted software, to quantify radial change across four timepoints. these demographic trends then enabled us to contextualize overall changes in the benthic cover of major functional groups. benthic communities remained stable across all locations; however, the autogenic pathways contributing to that stability varied between regions. through this approach, we demonstrated distinct pathways of reef persistence. most colonies survived and continued to grow through multiple transition periods of the study. with the exception of millennium atoll, corals experienced a 90% survivorship rate. additionally, radial growth rates varied among major coral taxa and regions, yet colony-specific changes showed notable similarities in their distributions across years. this study highlights the variability of baseline coral growth and survival through a multi-region analysis of long-term monitoring initiatives. together, these insights exemplify the natural capacity for reef persistence during a time of rapid environmental change.",
