          "name": "Catherine Mullenmeister",
          "search_text": "catherine mullenmeister submarine groundwater discharge alters the biogenic production of dissolved organic matter by reef calcifiers in nearshore coral reefs, submarine groundwater discharge (sgd) delivers freshwater with distinct physical and chemical properties that can alter the physiology of reef calcifiers and influence ecosystem function. modification of coastal watersheds through human land use can result in the transport of elevated concentrations of inorganic nutrients and anthropogenic contaminants into reef habitats. this study employs untargeted metabolomics to characterize the composition of groundwater-derived organic matter and track its transformation along a spatial gradient across a fringing reef in moorea, french polynesia. groundwater entering the fringing reef is cooler, fresher, lower in ph, and enriched in humic substances, creating distinct chemical conditions that elicit strong physiological responses in calcifying reef producers. these primary producers release a substantial fraction of their photosynthetically fixed carbon as dissolved exometabolites, stimulating microbial activity in the water column. thus, physiological responses to groundwater can reshape biogeochemical gradients by shifting the types and quantities of metabolites released into the environment, reflecting stress or response to excess nutrient exposure. to test whether groundwater exposure drives differential metabolite release, a mesocosm experiment was conducted to capture and isolate exometabolites exuded into the seawater by foundational reef calcifiers under both high and low groundwater exposure. the content of organically bound nitrogen and the release of proteinaceous compounds were altered under groundwater exposure in both coral and crustose coralline algae treatments. together, these results illustrate how metabolomic fingerprints of submarine groundwater discharge, combined with organism-level responses to groundwater exposure, can strengthen environmental monitoring efforts and support more informed management of nearshore coral reefs.",
