          "name": "Hannah Lyford",
          "search_text": "hannah lyford evaluating short-term porewater dynamics and tracing carbon storage across a seagrass-fringing reef interface in moorea, french polynesia. the influence of coastal seagrass meadows on fringing reef communities is not well understood,  particularly in the tropical indo-pacific. despite this region being a biodiversity hotspot, there is limited published data on the biogeochemical connectivity between coral reefs and seagrasses. in moorea, french polynesia, seagrass is found nearshore, adjacent to fringing coral reefs. as ocean acidification becomes a growing threat to corals, seagrasses may buffer impacts by increasing ph and aragonite saturation states, thereby sustaining reef calcification.  here, we take a multidisciplinary approach to investigate organic and inorganic carbon cycling in the seagrass and fringing reef areas of moorea with high spatial resolution sampling across an individual fringing reef and via island-wide surveys. we use elemental and isotopic analyses of carbon and nitrogen of seagrass and fringing reef sediments, and grain size analysis to better understand organic carbon storage and sources of carbon across the seagrass meadow into the fringing reef. we also conduct short-term porewater sampling to measure total alkalinity and dissolved inorganic carbon within both habitats to investigate the ability of seagrasses to increase ph and the aragonite saturation state, which may improve calcification in nearby fringing reefs. our results show universally high carbonate (wt %) present across the fringing reef and seagrass samples, however; we find elevated organic carbon content (wt% c) in the seagrass sediments. smaller grain sizes were a reliable predictor of organic carbon of surface sediments in moorea. we expect to find elevated alkalinity levels in porewater and increased ph within the seagrass meadow compared to the fringing reef. our study provides the first estimates of seagrass-fringing reef biogeochemical connectivity in moorea, a critical step towards understanding the biogeochemical connectivity between tropical reef ecosystems and associated habitats.",
