          "name": "Lauren Enright",
          "search_text": "lauren enright post-disturbance macroalgal dynamics in moorea, french polynesia top-down (e.g. consumer pressure) and bottom-up (e.g. nutrients) pressures are strong determinants of succession after disturbances. changes to these pressures may result in shifts in the abundance or composition of primary producers. macroalgae and corals compete directly for space, and each can suppress the other when abundant. given this, priority effects might have a strong influence in some environments. understanding the biotic and abiotic conditions that facilitate the establishment and proliferation of macroalgae can help determine when priority effects play a greater role than top-down or bottom-up processes in shaping benthic community composition. here, we used an exclosure experiment on the north shore of moorea, french polynesia to quantify how occupancy of macroalgae on coral reefs varies with consumer pressure and nutrient enrichment among algal taxa. we tracked benthic cover over 4 years to investigate: 1) how consumer pressure and nutrient enrichment influence the establishment, abundance, and temporal occupancy of three macroalgal taxa, and 2) how the order of arrival of species affects the temporal occupancy of macroalgae. we found that top-down and bottom-up forcing had different effects on the establishment, abundance, and temporal occupancy of three macroalgal taxa, and found some evidence indicating that priority effects impacted these associations. while lobophora spp. generally established early regardless of consumer pressure, halimeda spp. and turbinaria ornata established later under higher consumer pressure. the abundance of all three taxa was lower under increased consumer pressure. in the absence of consumers, we found that the temporal occupancy of lobophora was dependent on the order of arrival of taxa, with temporal occupancy decreasing when coral anteceded lobophora. however, priority effects did not impact the temporal occupancy of halimeda and turbinaria. our findings suggest that taxon-specific responses of macroalgae to changes in top-down and bottom-up drivers impact benthic successional dynamics following disturbance.",
