          "name": "Mackenzie Kawahara",
          "search_text": "mackenzie kawahara nutrient and consumer pressure legacies influence developing coral microbiomes microorganisms are essential for the development of their hosts. perturbations from ambient conditions change this host-microbe relationship, and multiple stressors often occur in tandem over long periods of time. however, few studies have tracked these microbiome shifts under experimentally manipulated conditions. in moorea, french polynesia, we allowed acropora spp. recruits to settle and develop under various experimental conditions, then tracked their microbiomes over three years. across 32 replicate plots at 10m depth, we built a consumer (herbivorous and corallivorous fishes) pressure gradient to alter access to the benthos. additionally, plots were randomly assigned either to ambient or enriched conditions created via osmocote placed in pvc pipes. this produced \u2018environmental legacies\u2019 of varying nutrient and consumer pressure gradients. using 16s rrna analysis, we see that nutrient enrichment primarily influenced the microbiome regardless of the consumer pressure gradient. alpha diversity and beta dispersion declined over time in both environmental legacies and were not significantly different from one another in the first two timepoints. however, one year after ending the nutrients, the previously enriched plots had significantly higher alpha diversity and beta dispersion compared to the ambient plots. this suggests that the juvenile corals may have adjusted to the elevated nutrient levels and were experiencing stress after those conditions ended. to our knowledge, this is the first study tracking the microbiomes of naturally settled juvenile acropora spp. exposed to four years of two different environmental stressors and one year post ending the nutrient enrichment. this data can aid coral restoration management in the decision of nursery location as it is common to have nurseries close to shore where they are often influenced by local inputs. in marine organisms, these changes in local community dynamics may disrupt the developing animals\u2019 microbiomes, thereby resulting in shifts in holobiont ontogeny potentially leading to host compromised development.",
