          "search_text": "madeleine ward dietary partitioning of herbivorous fishes differs across coral to algal gradients tropical coral reefs support some of the highest diversities of herbivores, and prevailing theories of competition and coexistence suggest that niche partitioning has promoted their diversification and long-term coexistence. herbivorous fishes exert a strong top-down impact on algae, which suggests that food may be a limiting resource for these species. moreover, herbivory provides an important ecosystem function by promoting coral reef resilience against macroalgal overgrowth. however, dietary partitioning among herbivores remains poorly defined due to the difficulty of differentiating the taxonomic identity of the food items they consume. this information is especially needed now\u2013a time when habitat destruction is increasing and the ecological roles of herbivores remain poorly understood in the context of changing reef conditions (e.g., shifts in the abundance of corals and algae). fortuitously, recent developments in dna metabarcoding allow us to better discern the taxonomic identity of food items. in this study we (1) define the trophic niches of the primary herbivorous species across an algal-dominated and coral-dominated reef in moorea, french polynesia by using a combination of behavioral feeding observations and dna metabarcoding, and (2) determine how the trophic niche of each species shifts with changing reef conditions. preliminary results from behavioral data show that, in general, there is interspecific dietary portioning on coral dominated reefs, but diets shift to incorporate more turf algae and macroalgae on algal-dominated reefs, suggesting that dietary niches may converge as macroalgae increases. more specifically, diets of species that feed primarily on high-relief, rocky substrate, such as chlorurus spilurus, shift more than those of species that feed mostly on sand and rubble, such as scarus psittacus. we anticipate that our dna results will reveal further taxonomic differentiation among species and individuals which will help elucidate the true degree of niche portioning versus convergence on algal-dominated reefs.",
