          "name": "Madison Weise",
          "search_text": "madison weise temporal and spatial patterns of coral edna across reef sites in moorea, french polynesia environmental dna (edna) represents a powerful, non-invasive tool for monitoring coral reef biodiversity and detecting distributions of biodiversity across spatial and temporal scales. however, its application to understanding coral population structure in complex tropical reef systems remains underexplored. here, i present preliminary work from an edna-based study conducted across five reef sites in moorea, french polynesia, designed to evaluate how well dna recovered from seawater can capture fine-scale patterns in biological diversity and community composition. at each site, surface water samples were collected during morning and evening periods to explore potential diel variation in edna abundance and taxonomic representation. in parallel, benthic invertebrate surveys were conducted to provide an independent measure of community structure for comparison with edna signals. the primary goals of this work are to assess (1) how consistently coral taxa are detected across sites and sampling times, (2) whether spatial variation in edna signatures aligns with benthic survey patterns, and (3) whether the diversity and quantity of coral edna may be sufficient to inform future population-level analyses. while bioinformatic workflows are ongoing, early findings suggest that edna can capture meaningful spatial differences in reef community composition. this study contributes to growing efforts to integrate edna into coral reef monitoring and provides an initial framework for evaluating the feasibility of using water-derived dna to investigate coral diversity and connectivity.",
