          "name": "Molly Brzezinski",
          "search_text": "molly brzezinski regeneration and wound healing in indo-pacific corals corals are susceptible to sublethal predation, which results in a range of tissue and skeletal damage patterns. although they possess extremely high regenerative abilities to overcome this damage, it is not well understood why some colonies can withstand and recover from predation, while others exhibit partial mortality. in this study, we experimentally wounded juvenile colonies from three common genera in the indo pacific, acropora, pocillopora, and porites, using two standardized methods that mimic natural corallivory: scraping (tissue and skeletal damage) and deep tissue removal (near total tissue removal with no skeletal injury). we characterized the morphological changes between wound methods, observed native rfp fluorescence at wound margins, and used histological approaches to understand the role of algal colonization in regeneration failure. over four weeks, all scraped wounds regenerated whereas deeper tissue removal resulted in algal colonization and regenerative failure. healing performance also varied across genera. pocillopora fragments regenerated more slowly and frequently retained open lesions, while acropora and porites often achieved full closure. to test whether skeletal damage facilitated regeneration, we crossed tissue removal with skeletal damage in the perforate porites. regardless of skeletal damage, deep tissue removal enabled algal colonization in the wound, which delayed or prevented complete regeneration. although tissue regenerated at the margins in both scraped and deep wounds, algal competition significantly delayed lateral regeneration in deep tissue wounds. additionally, rfp signaling in regenerating tissue was transient in scraped wounds (4-5 days), whereas signaling persisted along algae-colonized wound beds for over four weeks. chronic signaling seen only in the tissue adjacent to algae suggests that algae disrupts the normal transition from inflammation to proliferation and remodeling. the results of this study provide a mechanistic understanding of wound healing under two corallivory pressures, and clarify the impact of natural predation on colony regeneration and survival.",
