          "search_text": "rebecca vega thurber identifying routes of horizontal transmission of the disease-associated, parasitic bacterium, aquarickettsia rohweri, to the endangered coral acropora cervicornis in florida, the endangered coral acropora cervicornis is often dominated (up to 99% relative abundance) by the parasitic intracellular bacterium aquarickettsia rohweri, and this bacterium is associated with decreased host fitness and increased disease susceptibility. despite its intracellular nature and host dependence, a. rohweri is not vertically transmitted, making it essential to identify vectors and mechanisms of its horizontal transmission. to evaluate potential vector-mediated routes, we utilized na\u00efve coral outplanting studies, ex situ transmission studies, and short- and full-length 16s rrna gene sequencing to track a. rohweri in coral hosts, potential vectors, and the water column. we found that when na\u00efve corals were outplanted, only corals within 50m of an infected coral acquired a. rohweri. further, we explored whether this transmission was potentially due to vectoring via the corallivorous snail coralliophila abbreviata or via infectious coral host mucocytes. our results suggest that a. rohweri is only transiently present in the snails, highest 24-hours after feeding (4% relative abundance), and almost undetectable after a two-week depuration. although a. rohweri does not become an established microbiome member in c. abbreviata, snails may still vector this bacterium via direct feeding or fecal transmission. we therefore conducted a transmission trial in which snails fed on a. cervicornis sexual recruits \u2013 containing little to no a. rohweri \u2013after feeding on high-aquarickettsia corals. mucus from recruits was repeatedly sampled and screened by qpcr to detect a. rohweri acquisition. to uncover dynamics of waterborne transmission, the water from coral only and snail tanks was collected and treated with a bacterial viability dye to determine whether live a. rohweri bacterial cells are shed into the water from either snails or infected hosts and if they retain their infectivity. such information can inform restoration strategies and guide future investigations into other routes of transmission in vulnerable a. cervicornis populations.",
