          "name": "Sadie Cwikiel",
          "search_text": "sadie cwikiel physical-ecological dynamics shaping coral community structure in the back reef of moorea\u2019s lagoon understanding small-scale, high-frequency ocean temperature variability and its physical drivers is important for coral reef conservation and restoration because this variability influences corals\u2019 exposure and response to heat stress. as thermal stress events increase in intensity and frequency, these dynamics will shape the benthic communities of future reefs. accordingly, we seek to investigate whether small-scale differences in daily temperature range (dtr) correspond to differences in coral community structure across the back reef of the lagoon surrounding moorea, french polynesia. here we present an analysis of continuous temperature data (2021-present) from back reef sites 1 km apart surrounding moorea, courtesy of the moorea coral reef long term ecological research program. these data exhibit within-island variability in dtr (~1\u00b0c difference across sites) but minimal difference in mean temperature, presenting an opportunity to assess how fine-scale thermal dynamics influence coral community structure within a single habitat type. to explore potential drivers of thermal variation, current velocity in the back reef was measured via surfzone drifters. percent benthic cover, coral genus diversity, coral colony size, and rugosity were estimated through benthic surveys in the austral winters of 2024 and 2025. differences in dtr across the back reef are likely influenced by differences in water residence time and flow variation across shallow reef flats. preliminary analysis indicates that sites with higher dtr exhibit higher percent coral cover and larger median coral colony sizes, consistent across genera. juvenile coral diversity increased with structural complexity but decreased with average dtr. these results illustrate that the influence of temperature regimes on coral community structure is complex and varied. moorea\u2019s back reef exhibits heterogeneity in both coral community structure and physical dynamics, illustrating the importance of identifying local drivers of dtr and investigating how thermal variation impacts coral community variation at a kilometer scale.",
