          "name": "Scott Miller",
          "search_text": "scott miller fine-scale spatial processes interact with herbivore foraging preferences to influence coral recovery coral reefs are subjected to different types of disturbances, and the type of disturbance can have significant impacts on reef recovery. prior modeling and empirical work have found that coral bleaching, a disturbance where coral skeletons remain on the reef, may decrease coral recovery potential compared to disturbances that remove relict reef structure, such as cyclones. however, fine-scale spatial differences, such as the size and spacing of pre-disturbance corals, or herbivore preferences for different types of benthic macroalgae may further influence the ability for coral to recover post-bleaching. here, we created a cellular automaton model, parameterized with data from the fore reef of moorea, french polynesia, that simulates coral and macroalgae competition to investigate how spatial processes influence coral recovery under different disturbance types. to explore the importance of pre-bleaching conditions, we varied the initial spatial distribution and sizes of living coral colonies in our simulated reef area. our model showed that coral bleaching increases the likelihood of a reef being overgrown by macroalgae relative to a cyclone of similar intensity. additionally, we found that when the pre-disturbance state consisted of larger coral colonies more densely packed together, the likelihood of a macroalgal regime shift was even higher due to the ability for macroalgae to rapidly expand across adjacent dead skeletons. when herbivores preferred to forage on macroalgae growing on dead coral skeletons rather than macroalgae growing on exposed substrate, recovery to a coral state is further reduced; by focusing herbivory pressure on dead skeletons, macroalgae rapidly proliferates on the exposed substrate between corals and spreads to living colonies. our study highlights the importance of fine-scale spatial processes in influencing regime shifts following disturbances, and by considering these processes, our model reveals that seemingly healthier pre-disturbance conditions can lead to reduced recovery under certain disturbance contexts.",
