          "search_text": "jaelyn bos multi-scale genetic variation in a western indian ocean acropora coral heat tolerance depends heavily on coral genetics and varies at multiple spatial scales. here, we use whole genome sequencing, in situ temperature loggers, and bleaching observations to examine how seascape-level genetic variation relates to heat tolerance in an ecologically vital yet understudied environment on the coast of northern mozambique. we collected corals from a regionally common but unidentified acropora species for genetic analysis from multiple microclimates at two sites 400 km apart, allowing us to examine genetic variation at both broad and fine spatial scales. from 2023 to 2024 we collected 315 acropora samples which we sequenced to approximately 10x coverage with low coverage whole genome sequencing. 250 of these samples were collected during the 2024 western indian ocean bleaching event, allowing us to relate genotype to bleached status. we also collected 12 months of data from in situ temperature loggers at each location. \r\n we use this data to test for specific loci under selection, both within populations and across the entire samples, and to relate these to previous study of genome areas correlated with heat tolerance in acropora. we also conduct a genome wide association study to identify snps associated with bleaching status. we then examine the prevalence of snps under selection and snps associated with bleaching at multiple spatial scales to test for evidence of heat adaptation at fine or broad spatial scales. this data not only improves our baseline understanding of seascape-level genetic variation in a key east african acropora taxon but also informs ongoing coral restoration work in mozambique.",
