          "name": "Kayle Stremler",
          "search_text": "kayle stremler hawaii coral growth response to rapid sea-level change during the last two glacial cycles from iodp expedition 3893c3c3c coral reefs provide a critical archive of past sea-level variability because framework-building corals grow within narrow depth ranges and can be precisely dated across glacial\u2013interglacial cycles. the drowned reefs surrounding the island of hawai\u2018i offer an exceptional opportunity to reconstruct coral growth history in relation to rapid sea-level change, as the island simultaneously experienced steady volcanic subsidence and repeated reef drowning events. international ocean discovery program expedition 389 recovered an unprecedented suite of reef cores spanning the last ~500 kyr. this study focuses on corals collected from the two youngest reef terraces, h2 and h1 which drowned during the penultimate (133-134 ka) and last deglaciation (12-14 ka).\r\n\r\nwe quantify annual skeletal extension using high-resolution x-ray computed tomography (ct) and the coralct analysis framework to measure density banding in porites corals. these measurements are compared to independently derived sea-level reconstructions and atmospheric pco2 concentrations to assess extension rate variation across sea-level conditions. we test the hypothesis that corals exhibit faster extension during intervals of accelerated sea-level rise (\u201ccatch-up\u201d), slower growth during highstands (\u201ckeep-up\u201d), and growth failure during extreme rises (\u201cgive-up\u201d).\r\n\r\nseventeen minimally altered coral cores from h1 and h2, collected from leeward and windward island settings, allow evaluation of spatial and temporal variability in coral growth response. resulting extension histories will refine estimates of the rates of sea-level rise these reefs could tolerate before drowning, and illuminate the environmental thresholds governing reef survival. preliminary results suggest that corals from both h1 and h2 have an average extension rate (0.95 cm/yr) lower than modern-day hawaii porites spp. (1.09 cm/yr), regardless of sea level. however, extension rates are lowest during periods of rapid change relative to periods of stable sea level. this work ultimately advances understanding of how coral reef systems respond to rapid, climate-driven sea-level change past and future.",
