          "name": "Carla Hoge",
          "search_text": "carla hoge towards a unified characterization of the evolutionary history of acropora corals in combating rapid declines in coral populations, a major challenge is to link relevant information across species over spatial and temporal scales. understanding whether patterns of genomic diversity and demographic histories inferred in studies of individual coral populations result from common evolutionary processes requires a unified framework of analysis. in this respect, comparative population genomics techniques, which study variation across related species to learn about shared evolutionary processes, have been underutilized in corals. we therefore set out to unify genomic analyses across studies and species, focusing on the reef-building species-rich acropora genus, which have been the focus of many analyses. we collected high coverage whole genome resequencing (wgs) data from a. millepora colonies sampled from across the great barrier reef and analyze these data alongside recently published wgs datasets from five additional acropora species and high-quality reference genomes from twelve acropora species. these species include acropora from around the world, estimated to have diverged >50mya, as well as closely related species with overlapping ranges. we generated a multi-species alignment and called variants using the same pipeline for all datasets and in so doing, developed a data-driven approach for quality control that we expect will be useful for other coral genomics studies. we characterized levels of genetic diversity, linkage disequilibria patterns, and population structure within each species and visualize genetic connectivity patterns across their ranges, identifying signals of asymmetric and long-range historical migration in each population and species. additionally, we inferred a new phylogeny from whole genome data, identified genomic regions for which the genealogical history deviates from the species tree, and quantified putative introgression between species. together, these analyses begin to paint a picture of the evolutionary history of the acropora genus, enabling further cross-species analyses and identifying shared and unique patterns of evolution within the clade.",
