          "name": "Andrew Altieri",
          "search_text": "andrew altieri deoxygenation triggers a shift in the microbiome of caribbean corals when combined with ocean warming submitter has requested a 15min oral presentation.\r\n\r\nthe microbiome plays a critical role in the resilience of corals to global change stressors including warming temperatures, and we are just beginning to understand the coral microbiome response to ocean deoxygenation. even less is known about the potential response of the microbiome to the combined stressors of warming and deoxygenation, despite these stressors commonly co-occurring. here, we employed an experimental approach that compared the response of two caribbean reef-building corals, acropora cervicornis and orbicella annularis, to elevated temperatures and lowered oxygen. we exposed both species to high temperature (31.5 degc) and low oxygen (0.5 mg/l) conditions simultaneously in a fully factorial lab experiment that include control conditions (29.0 degc and 6.0 mg/l, respectively). we found that a. cervicornis, the species recognized as more vulnerable to deoxygenation, had its greatest shift in microbiome community composition when exposed to a combination of both stressors \u2013 a response that is less apparent in o. annularis. moreover, a. cervicornis had more differentially abundant microbial taxa than o. annularis in the combined stressor treatment, including several potential coral pathogens. we also tested whether sequential exposure to stressors would affect the microbiome response by having conducted the above lab experiment with corals that had been previously outplanted over the summer at either of two reefs in the florida keys that differed in thermal regime. we found that prior exposure to more stressful reef temperatures muted the subsequent response of several microbial taxa to warming and deoxygenation, suggesting a \u201cstress hardening\u201d response that carried over from thermal to deoxygenation stress. our work reveals the importance of testing deoxygenation in a multiple-stressor context, and that differences between coral species can be altered by the history of stress exposure.",
