          "name": "Andrew McInnis",
          "search_text": "andrew mcinnis contemporary drivers of pacific atoll coral reef communities in the anthropocene the republic of the marshall islands (rmi) is an atoll nation which spans some of the most remote, pristine coral reefs to some of the most densely populated and heavily used reef systems. this study utilizes data collected sporadically over the past 10 years across 12 atolls in rmi. we evaluated how environmental and geomorphologic gradients interact with emerging anthropogenic stressors, including increasing human population density and climate\u2010driven thermal indicators, to assess trajectories of coral communities, benthic condition and net carbonate accretion. environmental gradients remained important with low wave exposure reefs exhibiting higher proportions of slow growing heterotrophic corals, with faster growing autotrophic corals representing a consistently higher proportion of communities on high exposure coasts. atolls with minimal direct local human pressure typically retained higher coral cover, greater representation of fast-growing taxa, and more favorable overall calcified benthic composition. atolls that have experience more frequent and severe thermal stress had consistently lower coral cover, selective loss of fast-growing sensitive taxa, and a higher proportion of small weedy corals. in the most extreme cases, when thermal stress was paired with high human pressure, a noticeable a shift in benthic substrates from coral and crustose coralline algae dominated communities toward fleshy macroalgae and other non-calcified substrates became evident. our results show that (1) low human impact can buffer, but not fully prevent, climate-driven degradation, (2) high thermal stress alone can drive reef transitions to slightly diminished calcification states, (3) the combination of intense local use and repeated thermal events produces phase shifts to degraded, net erosional reefs. at the global level this study highlights the urgency of reducing local stressors through effective management while simultaneously advocating strong global action on climate change to sustain coral reef atolls into the future.",
