      "search_text": "university of geneva elias samankassou elias samankassou orbitally forced warming versus co\u2082 forcing in regulating carbonate factories: lessons from mis 11c and mis 5e the relative roles of temperature and co\u2082 in regulating carbonate production during past warm intervals remain insufficiently resolved. many experimental studies are necessarily short-term and focus on modern taxa, while geological reconstructions often integrate multiple forcings (including changes in sea level, nutrient supply and circulation) and may conflate temperature- and co\u2082-driven signals. previous studies have focused on coral reef accretion, large-scale carbonate budgets or stable isotope reconstructions from sediment cores, but comparatively few studies have systematically compared interglacial periods representing opposing climatic extremes with contrasting atmospheric co\u2082 levels. such comparisons are crucial, particularly given that future warming is projected to coincide with atmospheric co\u2082 concentrations far exceeding those of any quaternary interglacial.\r\nin this study, we analyse samples from the international ocean discovery program (iodp) expedition 359, site u1467 17, which was drilled in the inner sea of the maldives and, examine two pleistocene interglacials that are identified in the records as representing temperature extremes, but which differ substantially in terms of their atmospheric co\u2082 content. this contrast enables us to disentangle co\u2082-driven effects from temperature-driven changes in carbonate systems and to evaluate their combined influence on carbonate productivity and preservation. using the high-resolution g. ruber \u03b4\u00b9\u2078o (along with the mg/ca ratios of selected samples) as an indicator of temperature and global ice volume and the bulk sr content as a proxy for aragonite abundance, we can reconstruct the timing, magnitude and volume of carbonate production during these intervals. by integrating these datasets from iodp site u1467 from the maldives, we provide new insights into how elevated co\u2082 shapes the dynamics of the \u201ccarbonate factory\u201d, sediment accumulation and mineral preservation. these palaeo-constraints highlight the importance of evaluating carbonate system resilience not only in terms of thermal stress but through the combined lens of temperature, co\u2082 forcing, and carbonate saturation state.",
      "connection_count": 1
