          "name": "Zoe Ariellius",
          "search_text": "zoe ariellius effects of eutrophication on the microbiome and resilience of guam\u2019s key scleractinian corals coral reefs face mounting pressures from local and global stressors, yet the role of the complex coral microbiome network in mediating holobiont resilience remains underexplored. this study investigates how eutrophication (e), a chronic local stressor in guam, influences coral holobiont resilience by examining the microbiome community assemblages of different functional groups in two reef-building scleractinian species \u2013 porites cylindrica and pavona decussata \u2013 which differ in life history traits and environmental sensitivity. this research utilized a one-year in situ reciprocal transplant experiment to assess how nutrient exposure (e) influences coral-associated microbial communities. coral fragments were collected from two reef sites with contrasting nutrient levels: tumon bay (\u2191e) vs. luminao (\u2193e) in western guam, characterized using isotopic analyses of \u03b4\u00b9\u2075n levels. microbiome structure was assessed using molecular techniques, including rbcl gene barcoding for ostreobium algae, its2 metabarcoding for symbiodiniaceae algae, and 16s rrna metabarcoding for bacterial communities. coral health and fitness were assessed using ecological metrics, such as partial mortality. while symbiodiniaceae associations remained highly specific and inflexible in both coral species, in situ monitoring indicates that p. decussata exhibits greater resilience with lower mortality and faster bacterial microbiome stabilization (dominance of endozoicomonadaceae), whereas p. cylindrica experienced prolonged bacterial dysbiosis under similar environmental conditions. in particular, p. cylindrica corals transplanted from luminao (\u2193e) to tumon (\u2191e) showed lower resilience, suggesting sensitivity to nitrogen enrichment. in contrast, skeletal-associated microbiome communities (ostreobium) remained compositionally and functionally stable under nutrient stress. together, these findings highlight that coral holobionts respond to environmental stress in distinct yet interconnected ways. tissue-associated bacteria are sensitive bioindicators of stress, while symbiodiniaceae and ostreobium display compositional stability but differ in functional responsiveness. this underscores the need to view the holobiont as a dynamic system to better predict coral resilience.",
