          "name": "Jonathan Martinez",
          "search_text": "jonathan martinez evaluating the sub-lethal impacts to coral from the overgrowth of an invasive algal-mat forming species on a hawaiian coral reef. invasive organisms can negatively affect marine ecosystem structure and function, particularly native species. the impacts as well as the associated underlying mechanisms affecting native species often remain unclear. this study assessed the effects of the invasive red macroalga, gracilaria salicornia, to the physiology of the scleractinian coral, porites compressa, in k\u0101ne\u2018ohe bay, hawai\u2018i. paired studies showed that coral colonies did not die when covered by the algae for as long as seven weeks. however, corals were visually bleached within two weeks of being overgrown by algal mats. in response to shading, chlorophyll a, c2 and total carotenoids increased in remaining zooxanthellae. there was no evidence of oxidative stress or anaerobic respiration despite diurnally fluctuating hypoxic and hyperoxic conditions. when management intervention was simulated by removal of algal mats, pigment quantity recovered in coral tissues after three weeks; zooxanthellae density recovered within five weeks. our results suggest that g. salicornia mats imposed substantial physiological stresses on the symbiosis and photosynthetic capacity of the coral porites compressa and that recovery can be detected within three weeks if the alga is removed. further, we demonstrated that the removal of mats is an effective management action leading to recovery from short-term stress. thus, short-term management efforts that include removal of algal biomass are likely to enhance restoration success on reefs in k\u0101ne\u2018ohe bay and elsewhere.",
