          "name": "Megan Carney",
          "search_text": "megan carney a comparison of thermal stress responses of porites lobata to heterogeneous thermal regimes marine heat waves are threatening corals worldwide. on hawai\u2018i island, a thermal gradient exists due to predominant wind patterns, reflected in a difference of sea surface temperatures (sst) between the leeward coast experiencing higher temperatures when compared to the windward coast of the island. while limited thermal acclimatization has been shown in other reefs across the hawaiian islands, the potential for resistance to heat stress in hawai\u02bbi island coral populations remain understudied. in this study, porites lobata fragments from both leeward and windward coasts of hawai\u2018i island incubated in flow-through aquaria at temperatures set to an ambient 24\u00b0c and elevated  28\u00b0c treatments. responses to temperature treatments were assessed via calcification (buoyant weighing), planar area change (imagej), metabolic responses (oxygen flux respirometry), and maximum photochemical efficiency (pulse amplitude modulation fluorometry) after a four-week incubation. maximum photochemical efficiency was significantly reduced between collection locations (leeward: 0.612 \u00b1 0.007 windward: 0.440 \u00b1 0.014; mean \u00b1 se). no significance was seen in origin, treatment, or the interaction of treatment based on calcification rate, planar area change, and metabolic response. the results show a potential of acclimatization of corals in both the leeward and windward coast of hawai\u2018i island. the interaction between the results of the maximum photochemical efficiency and planar surface show traits of acclimatization in corals from leeward coast, hawai\u2018i island. this study provides an understanding of acclimatization of hawai\u2018i island corals serving as a way to understand potential thermal resistance to future heating events. this information can be used by restoration efforts to better incorporate stock of corals that are more resistant to rising sst.",
