      "search_text": "university of hawai'i - hilo john burns megan carney john burns harnessing the geometry of nature to uncover hidden patterns in coral reefs conventional statistical methods provide powerful tools for understanding patterns in nature, but they often rely on assumptions that ecological systems routinely violate. coral reefs are shaped by nonlinear interactions, interdependent processes, and complex spatial heterogeneity. when such data are analyzed with conventional models, subtle yet important patterns in ecosystem function can remain hidden. this study applied topological data analysis, a mathematical framework that examines the geometric structure of complex datasets. this approach uses principles of topology and homology to detect multidimensional features that persist across scales, allowing meaningful ecological signals to be distinguished from background noise. when applied to coral reef datasets, these tools reveal how ecological functions emerge from the interplay of many interconnected species and habitat features. to test the applicability of this approach, benthic composition, three dimensional habitat metrics, and fish community composition and functional trait data were integrated across a broad spatial gradient in west hawaii. topological methods were then used to characterize the shape and structure of their multivariate relationships in high dimensional space. by mapping sites according to intrinsic similarity, the analysis uncovered natural ecological clusters and transitions that remain undetected through traditional approaches. to identify early indicators of stress and resilience, nonlinear smoothing was applied to the derived coordinates, and the first and second derivatives of the resulting response curves were evaluated. threshold points were identified where small changes in habitat complexity or functional biomass correspond to rapid shifts in ecological state, occurring at locations of maximal curvature that mark an acceleration in ecological change. these threshold locations provide quantitatively defined reference points that can serve as indicators of ecosystem function, emerging stress, and potential recovery trajectories. by integrating geometric data structure with nonlinear threshold detection, this approach identifies actionable ecological indicators that support ecosystem assessment, management, and monitoring of coral reef resilience. megan carney a comparison of thermal stress responses of porites lobata to heterogeneous thermal regimes marine heat waves are threatening corals worldwide. on hawai\u2018i island, a thermal gradient exists due to predominant wind patterns, reflected in a difference of sea surface temperatures (sst) between the leeward coast experiencing higher temperatures when compared to the windward coast of the island. while limited thermal acclimatization has been shown in other reefs across the hawaiian islands, the potential for resistance to heat stress in hawai\u02bbi island coral populations remain understudied. in this study, porites lobata fragments from both leeward and windward coasts of hawai\u2018i island incubated in flow-through aquaria at temperatures set to an ambient 24\u00b0c and elevated  28\u00b0c treatments. responses to temperature treatments were assessed via calcification (buoyant weighing), planar area change (imagej), metabolic responses (oxygen flux respirometry), and maximum photochemical efficiency (pulse amplitude modulation fluorometry) after a four-week incubation. maximum photochemical efficiency was significantly reduced between collection locations (leeward: 0.612 \u00b1 0.007 windward: 0.440 \u00b1 0.014; mean \u00b1 se). no significance was seen in origin, treatment, or the interaction of treatment based on calcification rate, planar area change, and metabolic response. the results show a potential of acclimatization of corals in both the leeward and windward coast of hawai\u2018i island. the interaction between the results of the maximum photochemical efficiency and planar surface show traits of acclimatization in corals from leeward coast, hawai\u2018i island. this study provides an understanding of acclimatization of hawai\u2018i island corals serving as a way to understand potential thermal resistance to future heating events. this information can be used by restoration efforts to better incorporate stock of corals that are more resistant to rising sst.",
