          "name": "Justin Berg",
          "search_text": "justin berg restoring corals from sediment to symbionts: a novel microbiome approach in olowalu, maui coral restoration programs increasingly rely on outplanting nursery-propagated colonies, yet the ways in which local environmental conditions reshape the holobiont during early post-outplanting remain poorly understood. additionally, sedimentation is a pervasive stressor that can influence coral health and microbiome stability. however, the potential for microbiome research to inform restoration practices remains largely untapped. olowalu reef, maui, a priority reef for conservation in hawaii, provides a practical study system with historically documented sedimentation variability, allowing investigation of coral holobiont responses across spatial and temporal gradients.\r\nthis project examines microbiome dynamics in two hawaiian corals, porites compressa and porites rus, collected from olowalu, fragmented, and re-outplanted to 20 sites (10 shallow, 10 deep) along the reef\u2019s sediment gradient. biopsies were collected from each coral prior to outplanting and again at six-month and one-year intervals to assess changes in the microbial community. fine-scale water-quality monitoring of turbidity, light, temperature, and conductivity were paired with quarterly estimates of sediment deposition, providing an environmental framework for interpreting shifts within the holobiont. to allow repeated sampling of individual colonies, we developed a novel 16-gauge syringe-tip biopsy technique that is minimally invasive and enables resampling of the same colonies over time\u2013an approach not previously applied in coral restoration that enhances the resolution of holobiont dynamics. \r\nmetagenomic analyses will reveal how sedimentation shapes microbiome assembly, stability, and divergence in restored corals. we expect to identify microbial signatures associated with resilience along the gradient, providing new criteria for selecting parent colonies based on holobiont traits rather than solely physiological measures or bleaching history. by integrating restoration, environmental monitoring, and high-resolution microbiome sampling, this work advances understanding of how habitat-forming holobionts respond to sediment stress and provides critical insight for improving restoration outcomes in sediment-impacted reefs.",
