          "name": "Luisa Meister",
          "search_text": "luisa meister environmental dna (edna) metabarcoding reveals distinct mesophotic coral reef communities on caribbean banks mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces), particularly those in remote locations such as seamounts and banks, remain among the least explored reef habitats due to limited accessibility. with growing anthropogenic pressures, understanding the taxonomic and functional composition of their communities, and the ecological services these habitats may provide, has become increasingly important. in this study, we applied a multi-marker environmental dna (edna) metabarcoding approach (co1, its2, 12s) to seawater and sediment samples to compare the diversity of benthic and fish communities on shallow (4\u201310 m) and upper mesophotic (43\u201350 m) coral reefs of two caribbean offshore banks, and two nearby islands in the cayman islands. we found higher benthic (cnidaria and porifera) diversity on upper mesophotic reefs from the banks compared to the islands. benthic and fish community compositions varied significantly (1) along the depth gradient from shallow to upper mesophotic coral reefs as well as (2) among the two banks and between each bank and the islands. the banks\u2019 unique community composition was partially driven by higher relative abundance of pelagic and deep-sea species, suggesting higher productivity and a function as aggregation points for fishes. the presence of reef building scleractinian corals along with overexploited and vulnerable fish species detected on the banks indicates the urgency of extending conservation measures already implemented on the islands to these offshore ecosystems.",
