          "name": "Anna Fiesinger",
          "search_text": "anna fiesinger signatures of genomic and symbiont selection in the coral porites harrisoni from the persian/arabian gulf coral reefs are facing catastrophic losses under accelerating ocean warming, with many species already living close to their upper thermal limits. to investigate the genomic basis of thermal tolerance in reef-building corals from extreme environments, this study examined population structure, specificity of host-algal associations, and candidate genes under positive selection and their expression in the coral porites harrisoni from two locations in the persian/arabian gulf (pag) and one in the gulf of oman (go). we sampled 119 colonies (n = 79 from the pag, n = 40 from the go) and subjected them to whole genome sequencing. population genetic analyses revealed overall low genetic differentiation with a high level of inbreeding between the pag and go, although the pag population traces back to an ancestral lineage distinct from that of the go. host genetic variation correlated weakly but significantly with algal symbiont assemblage, indicating that a combination of host genetics and environment shapes symbiont specificity. we identified two genomic regions under positive selection in p. harrisoni populations from the pag, containing highly differentiated variants driven to near-fixation, arguing for their essential role in the persistence of genotypes carrying these alleles in this extreme environment. gene expression analysis of the associated candidate genes under selection revealed distinct transcriptional responses between pag and go populations under ambient and heat stress conditions, supporting the functional relevance of these genes for thermal resilience. our findings suggest that strong directional selection acting on a small set of variants has disproportionately shaped the genomic architecture of p. harrisoni in the pag, highlighting the importance of conserving regional coral genetic diversity. this study is the first step in identifying genomic targets for future intervention strategies (e.g., selective breeding) to enhance coral resilience, while emphasizing the reduced genetic diversity associated with coral adaptation under environmental extremes.",
