          "name": "Christian Voolstra",
          "search_text": "christian voolstra global search for genetic regulators of coral resilience to thermal stress rapid ocean warming continues to intensify mass bleaching events, yet some corals survive marine heatwaves better than others. understanding the biological mechanisms underlying this resilience\u2013and determining the scales at which they operate\u2013is essential for informing conservation and restoration action. the \u201cglobal search\u201d project funded by the paul g. allen family foundation with support from oceankind, noaa, and the dfg was a coordinated, four-ocean effort spanning the great barrier reef, tropical central pacific, arabian seas, and caribbean with the aim to apply standardized, field-deployable acute thermal stress assays (cbass and seasim-in-a-box) in conjunction with systems biology approaches to elucidate genetic regulators of coral resilience to thermal stress. here we report on current and projected outcomes of this global endeavor. \r\n\r\nover the course of four years, we quantified thermal tolerance thresholds for hundreds of genets across eight coral species with subsequent analyses of gene expression, algal association, microbiome assemblage, and population-genomic variation. we identified transcriptional splicing as a key mechanism reflecting the severity of thermal stress impact. further, specific coral-algal pairings were predictive of thermal resilience, while climate bleaching metrics aligned with differences in heat stress phenotypes. we could show that both conserved and region-specific molecular signatures are associated with heat tolerance. comparative analyses across the ocean basins will reveal the biological scales and universal applicability of genetic predictors of resilience\u2013from individual genets to highly tolerant regional populations. \r\n \r\noutcomes from this effort will hopefully further our understanding of thermal resilience at ecologically relevant scales to provide biomarkers active intervention. for instance, universal molecular signatures associated with resilience will provide evidence-based criteria for selecting broodstock and designing selective-breeding or assisted-migration strategies. regional contrasts can further highlight where naturally tolerant populations occur, offering a window into what shaped past heat tolerance and how best to protect and utilize already resilient populations. the tara coral expedition (2026-2027): elucidating the factors underlying coral reef resilience to global warming the tara coral expedition builds on the tara pacific expedition (2016\u20132018) that surveyed 32 pacific archipelagos using standardized protocols to generate contextualized datasets linking coral physiology with detailed in situ and environmental measurements. tara coral complements the previous expedition by focusing on reefs in the coral triangle, the only region globally where hard coral cover has thus far remained stable, despite ocean warming trends that closely mirror global temperature trajectories. this striking divergence between global reef decline and regional stability highlights a critical scientific question: why do corals in this region appear resilient to warming while reefs elsewhere deteriorate?\r\nthe overarching objective of the expedition is to elucidate the factors underlying coral reef resilience to global warming using a transdisciplinary approach integrating climate, environmental, physiological, and genomic research. current hypotheses suggest that historical exposure to high maximum sea surface temperatures or strong interannual variability may have pre-adapted these corals to present-day conditions. however, alternative explanations involving reef macro- and coral microbiome composition, physiological plasticity, or lineage-specific evolutionary processes remain largely untested. understanding the mechanisms will contribute to predictions for coral reef futures worldwide and inform conservation and active intervention.\r\nthe presentation will outline the scientific framework, international partnerships, and operational objectives of the expedition, emphasizing how an integrated design can contribute to the understanding of processes sustaining coral reef functioning under climate stress. the tara coral expedition seeks to provide an open-access, multidimensional reference framework for a mechanistic understanding of coral reef resilience to generate predictive indicators that can be operationalized for conservation planning, environmental monitoring, and future restoration approaches. ultimately, tara coral aims to shift the trajectory of coral reef science from documenting decline to decoding the biological foundations of persistence\u2013providing critical knowledge to help safeguard reef ecosystems in a rapidly warming world.",
