          "name": "Evelyn Abbott",
          "search_text": "evelyn abbott single cell approaches resolve algal symbiont diversity of coral holobionts single-cell genomic techniques would improve resolution of fine-scale symbiodiniaceae diversity across the landscape of a coral colony, yet most current approaches rely on bulk sequencing that masks heterogeneity across individual symbiont cells. we developed and applied a single-cell typing framework to characterize algal symbiont diversity at genotype resolution to elucidate colony-associated algal diversity at the sublineage level. using fluorescence-activated cell sorting coupled with high-throughput amplicon and metagenomic shotgun sequencing, we resolve single-cell profiles from the standing population of symbiont cells associated with a colony.\r\n\r\nour aim is to understand the presence and level of within-colony heterogeneity that is otherwise undetectable in bulk datasets. this will allow to assess structured variation in symbiont assemblages across polyps and tissue compartments suggestive of spatially distinct microhabitats within the host. across coral species, it will allow the detection of low-abundance lineages with potential functional relevance for stress response phenotypes.\r\n\r\nthese planned analyses will address the unresolved diversity that persists below canonical lineage or species boundaries and will demonstrate the value of single-cell approaches for revealing symbiont dynamics relevant to coral physiology, adaptation, and resilience. the resulting framework will enable the integration of fine-scale symbiont diversity into holobiont models and improve predictive understanding of coral responses under rapid climate change.",
