          "name": "Jan Paulini",
          "search_text": "jan paulini achieving climate-smart reef restoration through thermal pre-screening, microbiome transfer, and long-term monitoring coral bleaching is devastating coral cover globally with projections of losing >90% by 2050 if global warming exceeds 2\u00b0c above pre-industrial levels. the global tipping points report 2025 has highlighted that coral reefs passed their tipping point, emphasizing the urgent need for action to protect these vital ecosystems. current coral reef restoration is criticized due to the limited evidence supporting their long-term success and a lack of long-term monitoring. a rational design of approaches to assess restoration success based on research-informed criteria is a critical gap in supporting biodiversity conservation and restoration. as such, their efficacy could be significantly enhanced through evidence-based, science-informed strategies, especially in regions with limited access to advanced technologies.\r\n\r\nin collaboration with seachange indonesia, we have established a model restoration site in gili asahan, where we assess the impact of thermal pre-screening and resilient donor-based microbiome transplantation on long-term survival and restoration success. we are further developing a standardized monitoring framework. this framework incorporates growth/survival tracking, automated underwater image analysis, and edna-based approaches to measure long-term increase in reef biomass, micro- and macro-biodiversity, and ecosystem services. \r\n\r\nour goal is to develop an adaptable and scalable framework for coral reef restoration grounded in science. by prioritizing practical, real-world transformative, and evidence-based interventions aiming at a high return of investment, we hope to build a replicable model that can contribute effectively to supporting reef preservation at scale through adapting research-based coral conservation and restoration to local community stake holding.",
