          "name": "Patricia Rodr\u00edguez Garc\u00eda",
          "search_text": "patricia rodr\u00edguez garc\u00eda building reef resilience through science-industry collaboration caribbean coral reefs are undergoing accelerated decline due to climate-driven thermal stress, altered hydrological regimes, and cumulative local pressures, highlighting the need for restoration approaches that are both science-informed and scalable. the zapata swamp national park in southwest cuba, a comparatively well-preserved and hydrologically distinctive wetland system, offers a valuable reference site for developing future-proof coral reef restoration strategies. this initiative integrates cutting-edge ecological research with industrial innovation to establish a comprehensive framework for resilience-based reef restoration. a zero-emission research platform operating entirely on renewable energy provides the infrastructure for high-resolution environmental monitoring, physiological assessments of coral stress tolerance, and in-situ experiments aimed at identifying adaptive mechanisms that can inform restoration at larger scales. industrial partners contribute through the deployment of modular, renewable-energy-powered water treatment systems that address freshwater scarcity in nearby communities while reducing land-based pollution and other watershed-derived stressors that indirectly affect coastal reef health. in parallel, the co-development of sustainable ecotourism models generates stable income streams that are reinvested into scientific monitoring, restoration operations, and community-led conservation programs, establishing an economic\u2013ecological feedback loop that enhances long-term project viability. capacity-building efforts, including training, knowledge exchange, and participatory environmental governance, ensure that restoration practices remain locally embedded and adaptable to shifting environmental baselines. collectively, this integrative science\u2013industry approach aligns with un sustainable development goals 6 (clean water and sanitation), 11 (sustainable cities and communities), 13 (climate action), 14 (life below water), and 15 (life on land), while contributing to sdg 4 (quality education) and sdg 8 (decent work and economic growth). by coupling emerging scientific insights on coral adaptation with scalable industrial technologies and socio-economic mechanisms, this model provides a robust pathway for future-proofing coral reefs in the face of accelerating environmental change.",
