          "name": "Daniel G\u00f3mez-Gras",
          "search_text": "daniel g\u00f3mez-gras mapping reef coral extinction risk in functional space corals are foundational ecosystem engineers that sustain the biodiversity and functioning of tropical coral reefs worldwide. yet, with nearly half of reef-building species currently threatened with extinction, concerns are growing over the potential consequences of species loss for the maintenance of coral functional strategies. here, we combined occurrence data for ~800 reef-forming coral species with trait information and the most recent iucn extinction risk assessments to: (i) map extinction risk within the global coral functional space, (ii) test whether extinction risk is randomly distributed across trait axes, and (iii) simulate extinction scenarios to predict changes in trait diversity at global and regional scales. preliminary results indicate that extinction risk is not evenly distributed across trait space. instead, specific functional strategies, particularly those associated with large, branching species characterized by low skeletal density, high growth rates, large surface-area-to-volume ratios, large interstitial space, and narrow corallites, are globally more threatened. in addition, extinction simulations suggest that the high functional redundancy (species with overlapping trait combinations) within coral fauna may buffer against widespread loss of functional strategies globally. however, this buffering effect is weaker at regional scales, where functional erosion is projected to occur, varying markedly among regions. overall, our findings suggest that key coral functional strategies underpinning critical ecological processes are already at risk, underscoring the need for conservation efforts explicitly considering trait diversity.",
