          "name": "Emily Croasdale",
          "search_text": "emily croasdale biogeographic gradients in coral growth: a meta-analysis coral growth is an important demographic process and mediator of ecological function on coral reefs; growth rates drive reef accretion and develop structural complexity, which in turn determines habitat availability for a range of reef taxa. however, coral growth has been measured using many different methodologies with many existing datasets being composed of lower-dimensional proxies for coral growth. we combined photogrammetric techniques and meta-analysis to develop a standardised comparison of coral growth rates globally from the early 20th century to present day. we used photogrammetry-derived models of individual coral colonies with a range of morphological characteristics to generate conversions from lower to 3-dimensional measures of growth (change in volume and surface area through time). we applied these conversions to more than 1200 published observations of lower dimensional proxies for growth rate from 127 (sub-)tropical coral species to facilitate standardised comparison of  growth rates. we sought to examine which parameters drove variability in coral growth rates at a macro ecological scale. we found that interspecific variation in growth rate was not significantly greater than intraspecific variation, despite pronounced gradients in maximum growth rates across species. we also observed decreasing growth rate with latitude and distance from the coral triangle (relative longitude). when controlling for longitudinal or latitudinal location, we detected a bimodal distribution of coral growth rates driven by life history strategy. we did not observe any effect of time on coral growth rates. with this meta-analysis, we have established a baseline for biogeographic patterns in coral growth rates. this will facilitate future work on growth as an ecosystem function at the community, ecosystem, and regional scales.",
