          "name": "Joana F. Couceiro",
          "search_text": "joana f. couceiro leptogorgia sarmentosa diversity assessment by cultivation-dependent and independent methods: culturable bacterial diversity under three different oxygen conditions, aerobic, microaerophilic and anaerobic octocorals play pivotal roles in benthic ecosystems and rely on microbial symbionts to thrive. indeed, coral-associated bacteria are rich chemical reservoirs for innovative pharmaceutical and biotechnological applications. however, most microbial cultivation attempts use nutrient-rich media and aerobic conditions, not corresponding to the habitat provided by the coral host, wherefore many coral symbionts remain uncultured.\r\nthis study aimed to expand the cultivable diversity of bacteria associated with the octocoral leptogorgia sarmentosa and assess their secondary metabolite biosynthesis potential. octocoral specimens were processed and bacterial symbionts cultured under aerobic, anaerobic and microaerophilic conditions, in diluted marine agar, and minimal medium supplemented with coral extract. the resulting culture collection was screened for polyketide synthase (pks) genes. coral fragments and microbial pellets were also subjected to 16s rrna gene amplicon sequencing for analysis of the total prokaryotic community.\r\nthe metabarcoding approach reveals a large intra-species variability of microbiome composition and a dominance of endozoicomonadaceae, mycoplasmataceae and spongiibacteraceae, typically difficult to cultivate. in contrast, culture-dependent approach favoured rare taxa (<1% relative abundance), primarily aquimarina, vibrio and shewanella.\r\nthe collection of isolates included 328 strains, spanning 38 genera and 37 isolates not classifiable at genus-level. fourteen genera had not been previously cultured from corals, including desulfotalea, halodesulfovibrio and propinigenium, exclusively obtained under anaerobic conditions. of 322 screened isolates, 36 carried pks-genes. the aquimarina genus stood out with 15 positive strains spanning four phylotypes.\r\nour findings demonstrate how cultivation techniques often select for rare yet versatile bacteria and that manipulating oxygen regimes broadens the recovery of taxonomically distinct coral-associated bacteria, providing valuable new resources for blue biotechnology. molecular quantification and imaging reveal the aggregate-forming, intracellular lifestyle of candidatus gorgonimonas (endozoicomonadaceae) in octocorals the microbiome of octocorals is frequently dominated by endozoicomonadaceae. however, most studies rely on relative abundance data from metabarcoding and metagenomics and lack spatial resolution across coral body parts. imaging-based evidence of microbial organization within the host and absolute abundance estimates of octocoral-associated bacteria remain largely unavailable.\r\nhere, we quantify and visualize endozoicomonadaceae symbionts and other key bacterial groups in the gorgonian leptogorgia sarmentosa using qpcr, and fluorescence in situ hybridization (fish), and resolve the co-phylogeny of dominant endozoicomonadaceae phylotypes through 16s rrna gene amplicon data and metagenome-assembled genomes (mags). we developed new primers and fish probes to detect endozoicomonadaceae phylotypes of the candidatus gorgonimonas lineage, uniquely associated with temperate gorgonians.\r\nour results reveal substantial intraspecies variability in 16s rrna gene copy numbers, with differences up to 2\u20133 orders of magnitude among host specimens. specimens with the highest estimated bacterial abundances were enriched in uncultivated gorgonimonas (1.98\u00d710\u2077 copies/\u00b5g dna), whose gene copy numbers largely exceeded those of other endozoicomonas phylotypes (4.24\u00d710\u2074 copies/\u00b5g dna). in individuals depleted of their main gorgonimonas symbiont, no other bacterial taxa filled this niche, including mycoplasmataceae that appeared dominant in 16s rrna gene relative abundance datasets. this highlights the limitations of compositional data for identifying dominant phylotypes.\r\nfish imaging showed gorgonimonas cells forming host intracellular aggregates (~10 \u03bcm diameter) in polyps. these structures were absent in specimens with low estimates of gorgonimonas abundance. mags indicate that these symbionts affiliate at the same-species level (ani>95%) with l. sarmentosa-derived gorgonimonas leptogorgiae mags sampled a decade earlier and at genus level (ani>85%) with mags from eunicella host species. phylogenetic analyses reveal a distinct clade of gorgonimonas across multiple mediterranean and northeast atlantic gorgonians, suggesting strong phylosymbiosis.\r\noverall, our study identifies gorgonimonas as intracellular, aggregate-forming symbionts of temperate gorgonians, while raising the possibility of a facultative association for the host.",
