          "name": "Maria Dornelas",
          "search_text": "maria dornelas distributing coral species along the fast-slow life history axis life history rates determine resistance to as well as the pace of recovery from disturbances. the main axis of variation in life histories is the fast-slow continuum: organisms are sorted according to investment in recruitment (a fast life history associated to quick recovery from disturbance events) vs. investment in survival (a slow life history whereby organisms withstand disturbances). simultaneously quantifying life history variation among coral species through multiple disturbance events, allows mapping coral species into the fast-slow continuum and provides essential information to allow forecasting reef futures. assisted by orthomosaics obtained through structure-from-motion photogrammetry, we have tracked the recruitment and survival of over 70,000 coral colonies, in 21 sites around jiigurru (also known as lizard island) in the northern great barrier reef, australia from 2016 to 2025. here we partition variation in these two demographic rates, across the >300 species recorded, as well as across life stages. we examine evidence for trade-offs in the two demographic rates, and the distribution of species along the fast-slow axis of life history variation. finally, we contextualise these demographic differences with variation of the same rates in space and time. our quantification of demographic variation in coral assemblages can be used to scale up modelling frameworks to predict future trajectories of coral reef ecosystems.",
