          "name": "Allen Place",
          "search_text": "allen place polyketides made by symbiodiniaceae we catalogued  the abundance of polyketide synthase domains in dinoflagellate transcriptomes (williams, bachvaroff and place, evolutionary bioinformatics 17: 1-24, 2021) and found that all members of the symbiodiniaceae are capable of making polyketide toxins.  this was substantiated by the polyketides zooxanthellamides and zooxanthellatoxins being isolated from two free-living symbiodiniaceae\u2013 symbiodinium pilosum (strain no. y-6) and s. natans (strain ha3-5)\u2013 but whether symbiotic species also produce these bioactive molecules has remained largely unexplored. to address this research gap, we isolated material from free-living s. natans ccmp 2548 as well as symbiotic species s. linucheae (ssa01) and breviolum minutum (ssb01) via a standard methanol extraction and a methanol lc gradient elution used for sterolysin purification. hplc fractionation revealed a high abundance of material absorbing at uv 225 nm in each of the 60% meoh fractions. time-of-flight mass spectrometry analyses further revealed the most abundant neutral monoisotopic masses produced by s. natans were peak 1 2781.37 amu (c127h220n2o59s2) and peak 2 2935.52 amu (c141h238n2o57s2). ssa01 was found to produce two smaller polyketides with parent masses peak 1 920.53 amu (c49h80n2o10s2) and peak 2 964.55 amu (c51h84n2o11s2). ssb01, however, does not appear to produce any major polyketides.  we are extending this analysis to the  sea anemone exaiptasia diaphana (\u201caiptasia\u201d) infected with ssa01 or ssb01 to see if the same findings exist in hospite.",
