          "name": "Brooke Sienkiewicz",
          "search_text": "brooke sienkiewicz the role of microbiomes in resiliency to stony coral tissue loss disease corals\u2019 endophotosymbionts and microbial community provide essential roles in immune system support and are important contributors to holobiont evolution. in recent decades, there has been a rise in coral stressors, including increasingly prevalent coral diseases. stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) emerged in 2014 and has been devastating coral reefs in the caribbean. the cause of sctld is unknown, but it is transmissible and affects over 20 species of scleractinia coral with varying degrees of susceptibility. we collected tissue samples pre- (2019) and post-sctld (2022) from tagged coral colonies in belize that span the susceptibility range: meandrina meandrites and pseudodiploria strigosa (highly susceptible), orbicella annularis and o. faveolata (intermediately susceptible), montastraea cavernosa (low susceptibility), and porites astreoides (highly resistant). following the outbreak (~2020) in 2022, 60% of tagged colonies had been infected or died from the disease. using shotgun sequencing, we are comparing the microbiomes of all resistant and susceptible colony microbiomes pre and post sctld outbreak. our goal is to identify microbes associated with disease resistance across and within host species, providing insights into the microbiome's role in holobiont disease resistance. from preliminary analysis, we have identified 11 microbial taxa that are significantly associated with resistant m. cavernosa colonies and 1 taxon that was significantly associated with susceptible p. strigosa colonies. this study is crucial for understanding the coral microbiome\u2019s role in differential resistance to sctld, and informing targeted probiotics development for preserving functionally resistant coral populations.",
