          "name": "Shakeel Yavan Jogee",
          "search_text": "shakeel yavan jogee a holobiont-integrated diagnostic framework for infectious coral diseases in the western indian ocean more than 65% of coral disease studies still depend on underwater identification guides, despite evidence of inconsistency and frequent misclassification. although existing morphologic lesion terminology provides an essential foundation, the field still lacks a comprehensive diagnostic framework that integrates macroscopic morphology, skeletal pathology, physiological impairment, microbial etiology, and disease progression. this gap constrains accurate diagnosis, early detection, and effective disease response.\r\nwe applied a holobiont-integrated diagnostic framework to seven infectious coral diseases across the republic of mauritius: white band, white plague, white syndrome, brown band, black band, skeletal eroding band (seb), and yellow blotch. lesion characterisation coupled with scanning electron microscopy revealed significant alterations in corallite diameter, septal width, and micro-architecture in white band, seb, and yellow blotch (p < 0.05) - signatures not discernible via field-based methods.\r\nphoto-physiological assessments using diving- and imaging-pam fluorometry showed consistent reductions in fv/fm, retrmax, and npqmax across all diseases, demonstrating substantial impairment of symbiont photochemistry in infected tissues. molecular pathogen screening confirmed hallmark etiological agents: scuticociliatia ciliates in brown band and seb, and vibrio coralliilyticus, vibrio shilonii, and aurantimonas corallicida in white plague lesions. disease progression measurements revealed rapid advancement, with white band (0.64 \u00b1 0.25 cm/day) and white plague (0.54 \u00b1 0.02 cm/day) spreading fastest, followed by brown band, black band, and seb.\r\naccurate identification of each infectious syndrome and assessment of prevalence levels during surveys was only possible after adopting the diagnostic framework, particularly morphological characterisation, which enabled consistent lesion staging, differentiation among visually similar conditions, and detection of significant spatio-temporal differences in prevalence around mauritius island. by integrating morphology, microscopy, physiology, molecular diagnostics, and progression rates, this study provides one of the first holistic infectious coral disease diagnostic frameworks for the western indian ocean, substantially improving diagnostic accuracy and supporting earlier, more effective management strategies. artificial shading improves photophysiological performance and mitigates bleaching of nursery corals during the 2025 extreme bleaching event in mauritius, western indian ocean the 2025 thermal anomaly in mauritius exposed lagoonal reefs to temperatures exceeding 33 \u00b0c, with noaa coral reef watch dhw values >16, creating conditions for mass bleaching. to assess bleaching\u2011mitigation strategies within undp\u2011funded coral nurseries operated by eco\u2011sud (mauritius), we tested whether artificial shading can improve coral photophysiology during peak heat stress. eight coral species commonly used in restoration were examined: acropora abrotanoides, acropora aculeus, acropora hyacinthus, acropora muricata, pocillopora damicornis, pocillopora verrucosa, porites cylindrica, and pavona cactus.\r\n\r\nfragments from each species were placed on paired identical nursery tables at two sites (cn4, cn5): a shaded treatment using woven polyethylene screens and a control treatment exposed to natural sunlight. shading reduced incoming light by 89%. pam fluorometry showed clear divergence between treatments: y(ii) increased or stabilised under shading but declined under full\u2011light controls during peak heating.\r\n\r\np. cylindrica exhibited the strongest physiological benefit, with y(ii) increasing by ~0.07\u20130.12 within two weeks (p < 0.001), whereas controls declined before partially recovering as temperatures dropped. p. cactus, p. damicornis, and p. verrucosa also showed significant y(ii) improvements under shading (+0.05 to +0.10; p < 0.05), contrasting with fluctuating or depressed values in controls. acropora aculeus responded positively only at cn4, where shaded y(ii) rose by ~0.06 while control y(ii) declined (p < 0.05). in contrast, a. muricata, a. hyacinthus, and a. abrotanoides exhibited near\u2011zero y(ii) and suffered total mortality in both treatments by week 3, confirming extreme thermal vulnerability. shading also reduced visible bleaching, with >2\u2011tone colour improvements for p. cylindrica, p. cactus, p. verrucosa, and a. aculeus.\r\n\r\noverall, artificial shading moderated bleaching progression in several nursery species, though it failed to protect the most sensitive acropora under extreme heat. these results highlight shading as a low\u2011cost but partial resilience tool that should be combined with cooler\u2011water refugia and thermotolerant genotypes.",
