          "name": "Yashvi Nundlall",
          "search_text": "yashvi nundlall live coral cover and recruitment patterns for four years between 2008 and 2024 at a mauritian reef: implications for resilience to bleaching-induced mortality impacts severe bleaching events leading to mass coral mortality are increasing in intensity and frequency worldwide. consequently, live coral cover decreases and it is unclear whether this is directly related to an induced imbalance in the population dynamics of reef-building corals. this study investigated live coral cover using lit surveys (n=3) and recruitment patterns, determined by 1mx1m quadrats placed on alternate sides along a 30m transect (n=3), at the belle mare reef of mauritius island in october/november months in years 2008, 2012, 2020 and 2024. the live coral cover percentage was 85.67\u00b18.80, 55.07\u00b15.50, 57.22\u00b113.98, and 83.00\u00b111.00 at belle mare reef in 2008, 2012, 2020 and 2024, respectively. total coral recruit density (individual m-2) was higher in 2008 (6.37\u00b10.93) and 2012 (7.42\u00b10.90) compared to 2020 (2.53\u00b10.25) and 2024 (1.60\u00b10.36). recruitment was mostly dominated by acroporidae followed by pocilloporidae family and then variably by poritidae and other coral families. belle mare reefs have known to be severely bleached and suffered mass coral mortality due to thermal anomalies in years 2009, 2016 and 2019. these findings imply that following mass bleaching events in 2009, 2016 and 2019, the coral cover has recovered in 2024. in 2012 recruitment remained high following the 2009 bleaching event and though it was less than half its density in 2020 and 2024, following the bleaching events in 2016 and 2019, the 2024 coral cover regained its percentage as it was in 2008. these results indicate that belle mare reefs tended to be resilient to bleaching-related disturbances at least partly due to its coral recruitment capacity. further investigations on a yearly basis on natural and potential assisted coral recruitment for a long-term period is warranted to thoroughly understand the role of coral recruitment and subsequently assist in boosting recovery and resilience to climate change-induced mass coral bleaching impacts.",
