          "name": "Akili Ligons",
          "search_text": "akili ligons coral transplantation: quantification of xenogeneic cellular response between acropora cervicornis and pocillopora damicornis stem cell transplantation and cellular therapies are useful tools for increasing health outcomes in many organisms including invertebrates. however, within corals this technique has not been investigated. here, we introduce the concept of cell transplantation between coral species as a potential method to enhance the resilience of corals. in this study, we used fluorescent activated cell sorting to perform allogeneic (between genotypes) and xenogeneic (between species) phagocytosis and cytotoxicity assays to assess whether recipients recognize donors as self or non-self. we hypothesize that this indicates that cellular rejection is low in these species of corals. additionally, we are comparing our cellular assays with whole organism assays, to understand the differences between whole organism and cellular rejection. this research aims to identify the biological level at which corals differentiate between self and non-self to assess the feasibility of transplantation at multiple scales.",
