          "name": "Alexandra Wen",
          "search_text": "alexandra wen exceptional thermal tolerance in a durusdinium-dominated population of caribbean elkhorn coral, acropora palmata, in tela bay, honduras caribbean elkhorn coral (acropora palmata) populations have experienced catastrophic regional declines, yet tela bay, honduras, harbors extensive stands of resilient elkhorn corals that have survived decades of chronic heat stress, regular freshwater inputs, and pollution. here we rigorously assessed the thermal tolerance of these corals, characterized their population genetic structure, and identified their algal symbiont communities (family symbiodiniaceae) using standardized coral bleaching automated stress system (cbass) assays, quantitative pcr, its2 and whole-genome sequencing.  we collected 30 colonies, representing 21 distinct genotypes, from multiple sites near punta sal in the west of tela bay and exposed replicate fragments to temperatures ranging from 30-38\u00b0c.  thermal tolerance varied among genotypes, with ed50 values (temperature causing 50% reduction in photosynthetic efficiency) ranging from 37.0-38.7\u00b0c, significantly higher (+1.4-1.5\u00b0c) than typical florida elkhorn corals at the same time of year. quantitative pcr analyses revealed that tela bay elkhorn corals exclusively hosted the thermotolerant durusdinium trenchii, contrasting with populations elsewhere (including florida) which are typically dominated by symbiodinium fitti. this finding is exceptional, as adult a. palmata rarely maintain d. trenchii associations in natural caribbean populations, with most field-outplanted juvenile corals reverting to the typical symbiont s. fitti over time. population genetic analyses of these and 72 additional samples confirmed that the tela bay population clusters with western caribbean populations (florida, mexico, belize) rather than eastern caribbean sites.  thermal tolerance showed no significant correlation with symbiont load, suggesting symbiont identity and/or host genetics are the primary determinants of higher thermotolerance. differentiating these factors is critical for assessing whether this population might be used for restoration efforts elsewhere.  these findings provide critical baseline data on one of the caribbean's most thermally tolerant a. palmata populations and highlight the potential for how host and symbiont genetics combine to enhance heat tolerance and restore reefs. quantifying increases in thermal tolerance due to hosting the thermotolerant algal symbiont durusdinium trenchii following bleaching-induced shifts or early-ontogeny uptake in seven species of caribbean coral associations with the thermotolerant algal symbiont durusdinium trenchii are recognized as a critical driver of coral heat stress responses, yet the precise magnitude of thermal tolerance enhancement has never been rigorously quantified across multiple species and life stages in caribbean corals. this study quantifies d. trenchii-conferred thermal threshold increases across seven caribbean coral species across adult and recruit life stages using a novel analytical approach employing photochemical efficiency decline curves and adjusted degree heating week (dhw) calculations. to assess the increase in thermal tolerance in adult colonies, corals from five species (montastraea cavernosa, orbicella faveolata, siderastrea siderea, solenastrea bournoni, stephanocoenia intersepta; n = 10 genets per species) were collected and divided, with one half undergoing controlled bleaching to induce symbiont community shifts toward d. trenchii, while control halves retained native symbionts (symbiodinium, breviolum, and/or cladocopium). to assess early-stage recruits, aposymbiotic larvae of colpophyllia natans and pseudodiploria strigosa were inoculated with cultures of either breviolum minutum or d. trenchii. adults endured 70-day exposures to 33.5\u00b0c (>30 experimental dhws), while four-month recruits (n = 200\u2013300 per treatment) experienced 30-day exposures to 32.2\u00b0c (~10 edhws), with maximum quantum yield (fv/fm) monitored via pulse amplitude modulated fluorometry. dose-response curves fitted to photochemical efficiency declines enabled derivation of thermal thresholds through dhw adjustments to enable direct comparison of the increases in thermal threshold due to associations with d. trenchii compared to alternative, native associations. both experiments revealed significant delays in photochemical decline and extended survivorship in d. trenchii-dominated corals, with adults increasing relative thermal tolerance by 1.41\u20132.49\u00b0c, and recruits exhibiting smaller increases of 0.89\u20130.95\u00b0c, perhaps reflecting their lower symbiont loads and/or dependency.  this comparative framework establishes quantitative baselines essential for parameterizing predictive models of reef resilience and provides direct applications to symbiont-based restoration strategies targeting enhanced thermal tolerance  in climate-threatened ecosystems.",
