          "name": "Aliyah True",
          "search_text": "aliyah true determining holobiont response to thermal stress on siderastrea stellata from divergent reef sites in brasil environmental history and thermal priming shapes coral host response to future thermal stress. siderastrea stellata\u2013a thermally tolerant coral endemic to brasil\u2013lives across a range of environments, experiencing large variations in location-dependent stressors including high nutrient input, thermal swings, full aerial exposure and irradiance at low tide. during the 2015 global bleaching event, brazilian reefs experienced 73% bleaching (total coral cover) but less than 3% mortality across species, including s. stellata. thus, s. stellata is a great system for examining resilience to thermal stress over a large species range. in this study, we aim to identify physiological and microbial changes that occur within s. stellata in response to heat stress from three distinct environmental regimes: one tidal pool (0-1m), shallow reef (2-5m), and deep reef (20-25m) site. we hypothesize that tidal pool corals will be more resilient to thermal stress due to environmental priming and access to excess nutrients. we performed a nine day heat stress experiment (32\u00b0c) and examined survivorship, protein content, total antioxidant capacity, symbiont density, and 16s rrna sequencing. our findings suggest that reef sites influence distinct physiological patterns and microbial assemblages within in situ populations, particularly in tide pool corals which had the highest protein stores, symbiont densities, and lowest diversity in microbial communities compared to other sites. symbiont densities and microbial abundances converged as fragments were moved to a common garden indicating the removal of environmentally dependent microbes. following heat stress, deep reef corals suffered the most bleaching but exhibited the highest survivorship\u2013despite having the lowest protein stores and symbiont densities\u2013compared to tide pool corals, which had the highest mortality. these findings add context to our current understanding of how environmental history and the costs and benefits associated with the selection of symbiotic partners affect coral resiliency to heat stress thus affecting survivorship.",
