          "search_text": "sophia lee optimizing restoration using sexual recruits: effect of outplant age and nursery pre-acclimation on the outplant performance of lab-reared caribbean brain corals coral populations on florida\u2019s coral reef have experienced dramatic declines in the past few decades, and natural rates of sexual reproduction have been insufficient to replace lost biomass and maintain genetic diversity in the region. to address these issues and improve recruitment outcomes, managed breeding approaches (ex-situ spawning and settlement, recruit rearing, and outplanting) have been applied to boost the production of native florida coral species, but this approach, although effective, is both costly and labor-intensive. to increase the efficiency of rearing early-stage recruits in land-based facilities while also maximizing outplant survival of key reef-building species, we outplanted lab-reared recruits of three caribbean brain coral species (diploria labyrinthiformis, colpophyllia natans and pseudodiploria strigosa) to reef sites off miami, florida, usa, at 5 and 8 months of age. each cohort was further subdivided to include acclimation periods in an offshore nursery of 0, 1, and 3 months. this design allowed us to test how outplant age and offshore nursery acclimation interact to affect restoration outcomes. juvenile survival, health (i.e., bleaching, disease, predation), and growth were monitored for one year following their release from the land-based facility. this project helps identify minimum viable outplant ages and assess the potential incremental benefit of adding an offshore nursery acclimation stage prior to outplanting, ultimately guiding future strategies for scalable sexual recruit restoration. these results will help guide restoration practitioners and managers in florida and elsewhere on how best to integrate recruit growout pipelines  into ongoing outplanting and restoration efforts.",
