          "search_text": "maren stickley stability and restructuring of coral microbiomes in \u201cflonduran\u201d elkhorn corals (acropora palmata) during bleaching and recovery coral-associated microbial communities play a key role in thermal tolerance, yet the extent to which microbiomes from more heat-resilient coral populations can enhance the resilience of vulnerable populations remains unclear. ongoing assisted gene flow (agf) efforts with elkhorn coral (acropora palmata) from an unusually warm site (tela, honduras) in the caribbean offers an ideal opportunity to test this. in 2024, a. palmata recruits were produced by crossing broodstock from florida (usa) and honduras to generate \u201cflonduran\u201d (florida x honduras) offspring, alongside florida x florida controls. flonduran juveniles were then reared in closed systems with either florida corals or their honduran parents to expose them to different microbiomes. after 18 months of growout, juveniles were fragmented into four replicates and exposed to a controlled heat-stress assay, together with fragments from the honduran broodstock. throughout the experiment, corals remained segregated into floridian or honduran microbiome tank systems to prevent microbial exchange. photosynthetic efficiency was measured every 2\u20133 days to track bleaching progression, and microbiome samples (16s and 18s rrna gene metabarcoding) collected at four key timepoints: pre-stress, post-ramp, peak bleaching, and post-recovery. during recovery, control and heated replicates were reciprocally transferred into the other microbiome systems to test how microbial communities are restructured during recovery when exposed to a different microbial pool. assessing microbial diversity and plasticity is a critical component of ongoing agf efforts and also helps us determine whether microbiomes from resilient populations can enhance resilience in less heat-tolerant populations, helping test whether microbiome-based interventions such as microbial seeding or probiotic applications are viable tools for improving restoration outcomes under climate change.",
