          "name": "Julia Cole",
          "search_text": "julia cole environmental histories from \u201curban\u201d corals: reconstructing regional hydrologic change from coral geochemistry at varadero reef, colombia reefs that lie adjacent to major urban centers experience a high degree of localized anthropogenic stress on top of that imposed by a warming ocean. long-lived corals that persist in these environments preserve a history of these compounding stressors and provide examples of resilience from which we may be able to take useful lessons. on varadero reef (cartagena bay, colombia), a century-long history of dredging in the dique canal to expand and maintain shipping access has resulted in high levels of sedimentation and turbidity. local industry and wastewater further reduce water quality. despite this environmental degradation, corals persist at this site. here we present a 60-year monthly reconstruction of hydrological variability from the geochemistry of an orbicella faveolata colony from varadero reef that reflects the discharge of the magdalena river, which feeds the dique canal. skeletal oxygen isotope and ba/ca ratios both track historical discharge, which is linked to the position of the intertropical convergence zone and the state of the el ni\u00f1o/southern oscillation system. a decreasing trend in skeletal carbon isotope ratios is consistent with reduced water clarity and increasing heterotrophy since the mid-20th century. the skeletal geochemistry provides a continuous environmental context for paired reconstructions of coral holobiont diversity through ancient dna analysis of the same skeletal materials. together, these methods offer new ways of exploring why some corals are resilient to significant environmental degradation.",
