          "name": "Marco Contardi",
          "search_text": "marco contardi engineering silk microparticles in coral bleaching treatment: bioinspired underwater vehicles for curcumin delivery rising sea surface temperatures are a major driver of coral reef degradation worldwide. one key consequence is coral bleaching, triggered by oxidative stress and excessive production of reactive oxygen species (ros) within coral tissues, which disrupts the coral\u2013algae symbiosis and can ultimately lead to coral death. the alarming findings of the fourth global bleaching event reported by noaa in 2024 highlight the increasing frequency and severity of thermal stress events, underlying the urgent need for effective coral bleaching-mitigation therapies. however, delivering antioxidant treatments in the open ocean remains highly challenging, particularly in ensuring controlled drug release without contaminating the surrounding ecosystem. to overcome these limitations, a biodegradable drug delivery system represented by silk-based microparticles (mps) was developed for the administration of curcumin - a natural antioxidant compound derived from the curcuma longa plant \u2013 aimed to enhance coral thermal resistance. mimicking corals\u2019 prey in size and composition, curcumin-loaded mps were extensively characterized in terms of morphology, physicochemical properties, release kinetics, and coral biocompatibility. post-production thermal processing transformed the mps from a water-soluble, \u03b1-helix silk structure into a water-resistant \u03b2-sheet configuration, enabling slow and controlled release of curcumin over time. ingestion studies on heliofungia fralinae corals suggested mps' capacity to reach the gastric cavity and release curcumin locally, while a four-phase bleaching experiment conducted on stylophora pistillata nubbins demonstrated the therapeutic efficacy of curcumin mps. treated corals showed significantly higher chlorophyll content and symbiodiniaceae density compared to untreated controls, suggesting improved thermal stress resistance. these outcomes demonstrate that curcumin-loaded silk mps represent a targeted, biocompatible, scalable delivery platform capable of improving coral resilience, offering overall, a promising therapeutic strategy for mitigating temperature-induced bleaching and supporting reef conservation efforts. functional materials for coral restoration in the scope of advancing coral restoration two different functional materials are presented. first, an environmentally friendly, conductive hardening bicomponent paste was developed to transplant and anchor corals, provide them with a solid growing substrate, and enable mineral accretion technology (mat), to accelerate coral growth. the bicomponent paste consists of bio-based and biodegradable acrylate soybean oil matrix and electrically conductive carbon fillers. the paste hardens through mixing, transitioning from a young\u2019s modulus of \u2243 0.1 mpa to \u2243 60 mpa and reaching a strength of \u2243 5 mpa. rheological tests demonstrate the tunability of the crosslinking dynamics of the paste. the paste exhibits a resistivity of 0.1 w\u2219m, with stable electrical properties for over 40 days in seawater. mat tests show significant enhancement of coral growth rates within two weeks, doubling those of the control group. this paste offers versatility for application in aquaria and nurseries, does not require prone-to-oxidation metallic structures underwater, and can be employed on reefs. the second material is a biodegradable, non-toxic antifouling biocomposite formulated to reduce persistent plastic pollution while providing an environmentally friendly fouling-control strategy. the biocomposite pairs a beeswax matrix with tween as natural antifoulant and bio-fillers to tune rheology and improve stability the material is compatible with conventional thermoplastic processing, including compression molding and 3d printing, and can also be applied as a conformal retrofit coating for existing structures. antifouling action is driven by tween-induced surface hydrophilization, which creates a hydration layer that suppresses protein adsorption and biofilm formation. the biocomposite can be integrated with existing coral restoration tools such as reef plugs, scaffolds, and 3d-printed reef structures, both reducing environmental impact and limiting adverse fouling and algal competition around coral recruits. 3d-printed antifouling coral plugs produced from this formulation supported healthy fragment attachment and early growth while limiting fouling. eco-friendly active film and sealant for underwater drug delivery to diseased corals symbiotic bacteria in corals play a role in regulating coral responses, modifying their distribution and community composition based on the coral conditions (e.g., oxidative stress). however, not all associated bacteria have beneficial effects on corals; some can induce harmful diseases, while others, such as opportunistic strains, can exploit favorable community dynamics to thrive and disrupt the holobiont equilibrium. coral diseases have become a threat to reefs, occurring more frequently and causing more destruction to these ecosystems.\r\nonly a few options to treat such infections have been proposed, involving the mechanical removal of the lesion or the administration of probiotics, phage therapy, or active molecules. however, all these methods present drawbacks that limit their effectiveness. uncontrolled antibiotic administration to treat diseases may produce antibiotic-, meanwhile probiotics and phages tends to be difficult to administrate in nature and are difficult and not advantageous to scale.\r\ninspired by the design of buccal delivery systems to treat human infections in wet environments, we developed a new strategy to treat coral disease. we created an eco-friendly underwater drug delivery system to address bacterial infections in corals. this system consists of an antibiotic-loaded hydrophilic film applied directly onto the infected coral tissue, alongside a hydrophobic bio-based sealant that covers the film. the sealant ensures adhesion and prevents drug leakage into the environment while the film continuously releases the drug at the infected site. the design was optimized to ensure monodirectional drug release to the infected coral tissue within one week of treatment, targeting the infected coral while preventing environmental release. this technology successfully inhibited the growth of vibrio coralliilyticus (a common pathogen for corals, responsible for white syndromes and bacterial-induced bleaching) and halted the progression of tissue necrosis-like symptoms in in vivo tested corals. moreover, 8 months after the application, coral base expansion was qualitatively observed.",
