          "name": "Vittoria Calabretta",
          "search_text": "vittoria calabretta coral microbiome: characterization, spatial variability and interactions with hydrocarbons in the mediterranean sea the coral microbiome represents a fundamental component of the holobiont, actively involved in the coral nutrition, metabolism and response to environmental stresses, contributing to the maintenance of animal health. understanding its composition and functions is fundamental to evaluate the health and resilience of the holobiont and to develop conservation and restoration strategies. this project aimed to characterize the microbial composition associated with five species of scleractinian corals, sampled between june 2023 and july 2024, across seven mediterranean sites. a total of 88 samples were analyzed to assess the variation of the microbiome in relation to three main factors: host species, sampling site and seasonality. additionally, prokaryotic communities from corals collected in hydrocarbon-contaminated areas were compared with those from non-contaminated sites in order to assess the effect of oil pollution on microbial composition. the sequences obtained by pcr and illumina sequencing of the v4 hypervariable region of the 16s rrna gene were bioinformatically processed to generate amplicon sequence variants (asvs) and assign the corresponding taxonomic classification. \r\noverall, microbiomes were dominated by families nitrosopumilaceae and micrococcaceae, as well as unclassified members of classes gammaproteobacteria and alphaproteobacteria. significant differences emerged according to coral species and seasonality. multivariate analyses showed that the most influential variable in structuring the microbiome was the coral species, confirming species-specificity. although hydrocarbon presence did not result in major taxonomic shifts comparing samples collected at the contaminated and non-contaminated sites, it appeared to have enriched the exposed samples with pollutant-resistant or potentially detoxifying taxa, suggesting an environmentally induced selection.\r\nin conclusion, the obtained findings emphasize the central role of the microbiome as a dynamic and functional element of the holobiont, responsive to both natural and anthropogenic environmental stimuli, maintaining a balance that reflects the state of the surrounding environment.",
