          "name": "Federica Siena",
          "search_text": "federica siena playing with corals: football as a gateway towards climate action and marine awareness the republic of the maldives comprises around 1,200 islands originating from corals, highly threatened marine organisms. reefs sustain the livelihoods of the maldivian population through ecosystem services, thus degradation translates directly into socio-economic impacts, including coastal erosion, sea-level rise, and the risk of population displacement. yet, ocean literacy and environmental awareness on local islands are often limited, hampering the creation of a network of ocean guardians for a more sustainable ocean governance.\r\nto address this gap, the community-based project \"playing with corals\u201d was launched in may 2024 in faafu atoll. it combines education, social engagement, and reef restoration using the maldives' most popular sport, football, to raise awareness about marine conservation. football serves as a tool to engage young people, build local capacity (sdg 4), improve physical health and enhance children's resilience (sdg 3), increase climate change awareness (sdg 13) and promote marine conservation (sdg 14).\r\nthe aim is to nurture a new generation of ocean stewards and to build local capacity through theoretical and practical workshops for local trainers, conducted twice a year by a team of researchers from the university of milano-bicocca and trainers from inter campus, the csr department of fc internazionale milano. thereafter, trainers carry out activities independently in their home islands. marine-focused environmental games are integrated into football training sessions, and inter campus's educational principles help develop children\u2019s personalities, promoting marine awareness and more positive attitudes towards the marine ecosystem. in reef restoration, reef stars are used as educational tools to foster a sense of ownership and collective responsibility. the project has propagated around 500 corals, conducted over 150 training sessions, reached 100 children aged 10 to 14, and 20 maldivian trainers. before-and-after questionnaires document changes in children\u2019s environmental knowledge and closeness to the ocean, providing the basis for creating an exportable and replicable model.",
