          "name": "Killian Ver Hoeye",
          "search_text": "killian ver hoeye should i stay or should i go? behavioral responses of mutualistic coral-dwelling crustaceans to host bleaching coral reefs host dense networks of interactions in which many invertebrates depend on living corals for shelter and survival. among these, several decapod crustaceans, like the crab trapezia serenei and the shrimp alpheus lottini, form iconic mutualistic associations with pocilloporid corals. however, the increasing frequency of bleaching events caused by climate change disrupts these relationships. when a coral loses its endosymbiotic algae, resident decapods frequently abandon the colony and move to a healthier host. this raises a key question: how do these symbionts locate and correctly identify a new coral host in the chemical and structural complexity of the reef?\r\nin this study, we first examined whether the location and recognition of a new host occurred randomly, through trials and errors until a suitable one was encountered, or if the process was directed and mediated by specific cues. by conducting migration experiments from bleached to healthy corals, we found that symbionts consistently preferred their original host species even when another suitable species was available, indicating a form of habituation and a non-hazardous relocation process. moreover, we observed that individuals predominantly left bleached colonies at night, likely to avoid predation, as they are colorful decapods. \r\nto clarify the sensory mechanisms involved, we then investigated relocation behavior. through a set of choice experiments, we tested whether visual or olfactory cues could support correct host recognition. let\u2019s see during the talk whether these cues were sufficient or not to guide symbionts back to an appropriate coral host.\r\naltogether, these findings reveal which mechanisms shape host recognition in reef symbioses and provide deep insights into how symbiotic relationships may persist or break down under environmental stress. trophic dependency: exploring host contributions to the diet of coral-dwelling decapods coral reefs are oligotrophic ecosystems where resources are scarce, yet they host nearly one third of marine biodiversity. this paradox is largely explained by the symbiosis between corals and symbiodinium, which sustains high primary production and drives nutrient transfer through the food web. some organisms, however, rely more heavily on this coral-derived production than others. among them are symbiotic decapod crustaceans living between the branches of several hexacorallia, particularly pocilloporids and acroporids, which are obligate associates who depend on their coral host for survival. but is this assumption accurate? do these decapods truly rely on their host as their main food source, and if so, do they feed exclusively on coral tissue or also on other nutrient inputs?\r\nto address these questions, we focused on the pocilloporid coral pocillopora acuta and its two mutualistic ectosymbionts, the crab trapezia serenei and the snapping shrimp alpheus lottini. we first examined the morphology of their mouthparts to infer the types of particles or tissues they could ingest. we then analyzed their stomach contents through light and scanning electron microscopy. to test whether they directly consume coral tissue, corals were experimentally dyed prior to dissecting the decapods in search of dye traces within their digestive tract. finally, stable isotope analyses were performed to integrate dietary information over longer time scales.\r\nour results show that these symbionts do feed on coral material but can also subsist for several days without it, as they ingest suspended particulate organic matter. however, they do not appear to forage outside their colony: neither sediment nor nearby algal material was found to contribute to their diet.\r\noverall, this study clarifies the long-standing assumption that coral-dwelling decapods rely almost exclusively on their host for nutrition and reveals a more nuanced trophic strategy.",
