          "name": "Elizabeth Weatherup",
          "search_text": "elizabeth weatherup pour some sugar on me: how coral mucus carbohydrate composition impacts vibrio coralliilyticus virulence vibrio coralliilyticus (vcor) is a devastating pathogen of reef-building corals, threatening important reef ecosystems. one such relevant vcor strain is ocn008 one of the agents responsible for acute montipora white syndrome (amsw), an acute tissue loss disease that can significantly reduce coral cover during outbreaks. however, there is a lack of information on the effect of host and environmental signals on vcor virulence and how they trigger disease outbreaks. past research has shown that the metabolism of different carbon sources can impact virulence factors and growth in vcor. further, the carbohydrates in the extracellular mucus (ecm) layer of the coral can change in composition due to host physiology or environmental perturbations. therefore, we hypothesize that environmental and host-related factors can influence ecm carbohydrates, which in turn influences vcor virulence. to investigate the role of metabolism in vcor strain ocn008, this pathogen was grown with different ecm-associated carbohydrates. depending on the main carbohydrate available, positive or negative effects on growth were observed as well as important virulence factors such as production of antibiotics, protease, and antibacterial systems, i.e., the type vi secretion system (t6ss). to further assess the role of metabolism in important virulence factors, a deletion of the camp receptor protein (crp) gene was made in ocn008. crp is a global genetic regulator which responds to different carbohydrates. the crp mutant had reduced protease activity, t6ss activity, and antibiotic production. additionally, when introduced to a surrogate clam infection system, virulence of the crp mutant was significantly reduced compared to wildtype. these results suggest that carbohydrate metabolism is critical for vcor virulence and that carbohydrate composition in the ecm may modulate the expression of key virulence factors. future studies will explore how specific mucus-associated carbohydrates influence infection outcomes in live coral experiments, providing insight into host-microbe interactions that determine disease susceptibility.",
