          "name": "Ellyn Darke",
          "search_text": "ellyn darke gene expression profiles of pre-conditioned juvenile coral outplants: an assisted evolution technique for restoration sea surface temperature anomalies are increasing in frequency and intensity. such anomalies are stress-inducing for both the coral and the symbiont, negatively affecting nutrient exchange by inducing bleaching. coral cover has decreased in florida and the caribbean region primarily due to disease outbreaks and regular, large-scale bleaching events. these stress events have reduced genetic diversity and gene flow, making natural recovery difficult. further, natural evolutionary adaptations are likely not possible at the rapid pace of climate change. maintaining genetic diversity provides variations in traits that natural selection can act upon to enable adaptations in changing conditions. investigating mechanisms to accelerate natural selection (i.e., assisted evolution) could revolutionize the state of reefs by selecting traits that may improve the resilience of coral populations and species. thermal pre-conditioning has been evidenced to increase coral thermal tolerance in laboratory settings. however, more data is necessary to explore how ex situ pre-conditioning affects the cellular and metabolic response of coral outplants under natural temperature cycles. \r\n\r\nwe pre-conditioned pseudodiploria clivosa juvenile corals by oscillating temperature exposure to 34\u00b0c across a 6-week experiment, varying exposure time between 4 hours, 12 hours, and 24 hours. these corals were then out-planted in newfound harbor, florida keys, in may 2025 to investigate changes in gene expression during a heat wave. in july and october 2025, photosynthesis and respiration rates were measured via cisme (coral in-situ metabolism) and subsamples were collected for gene expression profiles via bulk rna-sequencing. this study will identify quantifiable outcomes of pre-conditioning at both the physiological and genomic level and investigate the impact of pre-conditioning as a technique to increase resistance and resilience to natural temperature anomalies in juvenile coral outplants.",
