          "name": "Erin Papke",
          "search_text": "erin papke putative nitrogen cycling ultrastructure, but not viral-like particles indicate sctld health states stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) has devastated coral reefs throughout florida and the caribbean. the etiology of sctld is currently unknown, but hypotheses suggest that the pathogen(s) responsible target the coral\u2019s algal endosymbionts and cause dysbiosis in this relationship. previous studies using transmission electron microscopy (tem) identified the presence of viral-like particles (vlps) in sctld samples as well as a decrease in storage granules within the endosymbionts compared to those in apparently healthy corals. to aid in determining the pathology of sctld, tem micrographs of endosymbionts from healthy and diseased colonies were compared. sample groups included endosymbionts from five apparently healthy corals from the field, five suspected sctld disease lesions from the field, five corals naive to sctld held in aquaria, and five paired sctld lesion samples from aquaria transmission experiments. the ultrastructure of 446 endosymbionts was assessed using 37 parameters including the presence of vlps, the number of starches and lipids storage structures, and the presence of cellular breakdown. multivariate analysis indicated that the ultrastructure between field and aquaria corals varied significantly, so health state differences were separated based on sample origin. vlps did not significantly differ between health state for either sample origin; however, the number of starch granules decreased for all sctld samples compared to their healthy controls, which could indicate a decrease in photosynthetic efficiency or activity. additionally, the presence of uric acid crystals significantly increased in diseased samples, which may suggest that nitrogen cycling may play a role in sctld dynamics. these data provide baseline ultrastructure comparisons for both field and aquaria corals that can be used for future coral health and disease studies. tem is a valuable diagnostic tool for understanding morphological changes that provide insight into coral-algal physiology and the potential etiological agent(s).",
