          "name": "Jordan Ruiz",
          "search_text": "jordan ruiz high-throughput discovery and evaluation of probiotic bacteria for mitigating stony coral tissue loss disease in san andr\u00e9s, colombia stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) is devastating coral populations throughout the caribbean, causing unprecedented declines in coral cover and biodiversity. addressing this challenge requires novel, scalable interventions that enhance coral resistance and suppress disease transmission (prophylactics). in this study, we present an application of a high-throughput biotech platform to discover and evaluate coral-associated probiotic bacteria with potential to mitigate sctld. mucus samples were collected from healthy colonies of multiple coral species across san andr\u00e9s island, colombia, which have survived a sctld outbreak. using targeted microfluidic cell sorting, we isolated 6,876 microbial strains for cultivation and screening. of these, 609 strains exhibited in vitro inhibitory activity against disease-associated microbes. forty-nine isolates with strong antagonistic potential were further screened, and ten\u2013comprising five pseudoalteromonas, one stenotrophomonas, and one vibrio species\u2013were selected for coral trials based on their safety, verified using a surrogate galleria mellonella (greater wax moth larvae) model. coral transmission trials were conducted under controlled laboratory conditions. coral fragments were treated with 107 cfu ml\u20131 of the respective probiotic strain, dosed every other day over a one-week period. following treatment, fragments were exposed to donor corals displaying active sctld disease signs. coral health was monitored daily to assess the potential prophylactic effects of these probiotic candidates. surviving coral fragments were intended for outplanting to natural reef sites for long-term health monitoring and evaluation of post-treatment resilience to sctld, which is still present in the environment. our results support the use of a high-throughput platform for the rapid identification of coral probiotics and highlight the biotechnological potential of microbial-based strategies into reef conservation and restoration efforts, contributing to a broader understanding of host-microbe interactions in the context of infectious coral disease dynamics.",
