          "name": "Kerri Dobson",
          "search_text": "kerri dobson advancing our understanding of crustose coralline algae (cca) and peyssonnelid algal crust (pac) diversity in the tropical western atlantic crustose coralline algae (cca; order corallinales) are coral reef foundation species that form calcified crusts cementing reef frameworks together and release chemical cues that encourage coral larval settlement. yet despite their ecological importance, little is known about cca diversity on tropical western atlantic reefs. most previous research within this region has relied on morpho-anatomical identification of species. however, dna sequencing has revealed extensive cryptic diversity within red algae (rhodophyta), meaning that species cannot be identified using morpho-anatomical characters. further, in recent decades peyssonnelid algal crusts (pac; order peyssonneliales) have rapidly spread across shallow western tropical atlantic coral reefs, growing to dominate the substrate in some locations. previous research has shown that pac microbiome communities host lower abundances of pseudoalteromonas\u2013\u2013an epibiotic bacterial genus known to facilitate invertebrate larval settlement and recruitment\u2013\u2013compared to cca. as a result, pac may constrain coral recruitment on reefs where they dominate the benthos, potentially contributing to reduced reef resilience. by combining in situ field observations with dna sequencing, associated microbiome community analyses, and coral larval settlement experiments, we aim to advance our understanding of the diversity and ecological importance of cca and pac within the florida keys national marine sanctuary, usa, and the saba national marine park, dutch caribbean. preliminary results suggest that neogoniolithon solubile is the most dominant cca species at the collection sites within the florida keys. however, neogoniolithon spectabile, sporolithon dimotum, sporolithon spp., hydrolithon spp., lithophyllum spp., lithothamnion spp., peyssonnelia spp., and ramicrusta spp. have also been identified. following no-choice settlement experiments with orbicella faveolata larvae, preliminary results suggest that a hydrolithon sp. induced the highest settlement (~40%), whereas a peyssonnelia sp. induced the lowest (<10%). identification to the species level and microbiome community analyses of florida specimens are ongoing, and dna has yet to be extracted from saban specimens.",
