          "name": "Krista Laforest",
          "search_text": "krista laforest resurrecting a long-term monitoring transect in bocas del toro, panama: have orbicellid spawning populations stood the test of time? coral reefs are facing global decline from anthropogenic-driven climate stressors; most coral species rely on large-scale, synchronous spawning events with high fertilization success to increase the recruitment of genetically diverse juveniles to reefs. the longest observations of caribbean orbicellid coral spawning were recorded at hospital point near bocas del toro, panama. drs. don levitan and nancy knowlton established a transect and monitored three species of orbicellid corals from 1998 to 2013; however, the transect remained unmonitored for the last decade. this study revisited this long-term transect in 2024 to: (1) assess coral cover, (2) determine whether the magnitude and synchrony of coral spawning at hospital point has changed within a species or across years, and (3) record environmental parameters to understand their potential role in long-term reproductive patterns. using historic tags and maps approximately 50% of the corals on the transect were identified, and biomass was assessed using a combination of in situ measurements and extrapolated data from photographs using imagej. orbicella annularis, o. franksi, and o. faveolata were monitored for spawning 2\u20138 nights after the full moon. data collected in 2024 indicates significant partial coral mortality, a heavily reduced magnitude and quality of spawning, and a potential shift in spawning times compared to historic data. these findings could be attributed to environmental factors, such as increased ocean temperatures, changes in light intensity and spectrum from turbidity, and/or the presence of endogenous/exogenous sex steroids. furthermore, the reduction in coral biomass may result in a strong allee effect, in which fertilization success is reduced due to mate or gamete limitation. determining whether changes in the timing and magnitude of annual reproduction events are occurring will help our understanding of how a changing environment and lowered population density will impact the reef ecosystem\u2019s natural recovery.",
