          "name": "Nikki Fogarty",
          "search_text": "nikki fogarty early acquisition of symbiodiniaceae and spectral shifts to blue light increased coral settler survival and growth in an ex situ nursery global coral populations are declining at alarming rates, underscoring the urgent need for effective coral restoration strategies. ex situ coral sexual reproduction offers a scalable pathway to produce coral settlers for reef restoration, yet the optimal light spectrum to maximize survival and growth remains unclear. this study investigated how spectral shifts influence coral post-settlement survival, growth, and algal endosymbiont acquisition. over a 28-week experiment, sets of 36 pseudodiploria clivosa coral settlers were transitioned from a reef mimic spectrum to a blue light spectrum at ten different time points. coral survival and symbiont acquisition were monitored every 2 weeks, while growth was assessed every 4\u20136 weeks. results revealed that shifting from reef mimic to blue light at 4 weeks post-fertilization yielded the highest survival and growth rates. more broadly, spectral shifts within the first three months, particularly by 10\u201312 weeks, significantly enhanced survival. faster acquisition of algal endosymbionts was strongly correlated with improved survival and growth outcomes. these findings provide critical insights into coral photophysiology and highlight the importance of early spectral manipulation in ex situ coral aquaculture. by identifying optimal light regimes, this work supports the upscaling of coral restoration efforts and contributes to addressing the global coral reef crisis.",
