          "name": "Annemarie Selle",
          "search_text": "annemarie selle ocean acidification and the zoantharian holobiont: palythoa tuberculosa microbiome responses at co\u2082 seeps in papua new guinea ocean acidification threatens the stability of reef ecosystems, but whether host\u2013microbe associations reorganize in predictable ways under chronic low ph remains unresolved. co\u2082 seeps provide valuable natural laboratories to better understand future conditions, and among these sites, ambitle island in papua new guinea represents a comparatively \u201cpure\u201d co\u2082 seep environment and thus provides a critical opportunity to generate new comparative data. this study characterized bacterial and symbiodiniaceae communities associated with the zooxanthellate zoantharian palythoa tuberculosa at ambitle under high, medium, and control ph levels. preliminary analyses revealed that bacterial richness showed a tendency to be highest at the mid-co\u2082 site, while bacterial community composition differed significantly among sites with similar multivariate dispersion. notably, symbiodiniaceae at the high-co\u2082 site were dominated by durusdinium or variable cladocopium types, while at all other sites they were dominated by cladocopium c1, which has been seen in other sites across the indo-pacific.  these results, when compared with recent results from the same host species in japan and across the pacific, indicate that perhaps holobiont responses to acidification follow consistent patterns across regions. thus, p. tuberculosa could serve as a useful model for predicting holobiont change under future ocean acidification. the study improves our ability to assess how reliably natural co\u2082 seeps can be used to understand holobiont resilience in high-co\u2082 oceans.",
