          "name": "Gino Valentino Limmon",
          "search_text": "gino valentino limmon drivers of coral reef resilience across managed and unmanaged sites in eastern indonesia understanding reef resilience across managed and unmanaged areas is essential for developing adaptive conservation strategies in indonesia. despite the country\u2019s expansive network of marine protected areas (mpas), direct comparisons of ecological resilience indicators between managed and unmanaged reefs using a unified framework remain limited. this study assessed the resilience of 30 coral reef sites inside and outside mpas in eastern indonesia by applying a set of widely used resilience indicators and established methodological guidelines. resistance metrics, such as temperature variability, coral diversity, and herbivorous fish biomass and richness, along with recovery metrics including macroalgae cover and substrate suitability for coral settlement, were combined into composite resilience scores through a structured normalisation and anchoring process. of the 30 sites surveyed, ten  showed high resilience and nine moderate resilience. principal component analysis (pca) biplots were employed to examine indicator patterns and identify the main drivers of variation across high, medium, and low resilience clusters. herbivorous fish biomass emerged as the strongest contributor to differences in resilience, particularly in analyses that excluded temperature variability. high-resilience sites, including ameth, aboru, rutong, and porto, were characterised by elevated coral cover and diversity, greater herbivore biomass, low macroalgae cover, and reduced temperature variability. conversely, medium-resilience sites were more strongly associated with higher temperature variability and increased macroalgae cover, reflecting heightened ecological stress. coral cover, coral diversity, fish biomass, and coralline algae contributed positively to resilience scores, while algal abundance explained minimal variance due to its narrow range across sites. overall, combining resilience indicators with multivariate analyses provided a clear differentiation of resilience patterns inside and outside mpas, offering a valuable framework for prioritising management interventions in eastern indonesia\u2019s coral reefs.",
