      "search_text": "university of pennsylvania jill ashey katie barott marcelina martynek jill ashey small rnas in coral and cnidarian sperm: implications for epigenetic inheritance and early development small rnas have been proposed as a mechanism for epigenetic inheritance across generations, but their presence and potential function has not been examined in corals. small rnas are crucial regulators of gene expression and genomic stability and are influential in early development of bilaterian species. however, their roles in gametes and early development remain poorly understood in early-diverging (non-bilaterian) metazoans, including corals. in this study, we characterize two major classes of small rnas\u2013micrornas (mirnas) and piwi-interacting rnas (pirnas)\u2013in sperm from three phylogenetically and ecologically distinct cnidarian species: the sea anemone nematostella vectensis, the temperate stony coral astrangia poculata, and the reef-building tropical coral acropora hyacinthus. through comparative analyses, we found that mir-100, the only mirna conserved between cnidarians and bilaterians, was the only shared mirna expressed by all three species. most mirnas were species-specific, but predicted target analysis revealed that approximately half of the targeted genes in each species shared homologs involved in developmental and signaling pathways. pirnas were abundant across the three species, mapping primarily to protein-coding genes with additional overlap on transposable elements, indicating a potential dual role in gene expression regulation and genomic integrity maintenance. indeed, functional enrichment of the genes overlapped by pirnas revealed terms associated with dna binding, repair, and integration, supporting their potential involvement in preserving genomic stability in gametes and through development. together, these findings provide the first evidence that cnidarian sperm carry a rich repertoire of small rnas, including both conserved and species-specific types. their presence suggests that paternal small rnas may contribute important regulatory information to the zygote, implicating paternal small rnas as possible mediators of epigenetic inheritance and early development in corals and their relatives. katie barott changes in seasonal calcification and metabolic rates of reef-building corals following a legacy of recurring marine heatwaves marine heatwaves are reshaping coral ecosystems, driving widespread bleaching, mortality, and physiological disruption of reef-building corals. survival of coral populations facing heatwaves amid chronic ocean warming will increasingly depend on the acclimatization and recovery capacity of surviving individuals. understanding how these survivors perform across seasons can provide key insights into acclimatization stability and long-term effects. here, we characterized seasonal photophysiology and thermal performance of two hawaiian coral species (montipora capitata and porites compressa) that survived recent marine heatwaves (2014, 2015, and 2019). these corals had distinct bleaching histories, with half bleaching (\u201csusceptible\u201d) and the other half remaining pigmented (\u201cresistant\u201d) during prior heatwaves. colonies were monitored over two consecutive non-heatwave years (2023-2025) to assess seasonal calcification, photosynthesis, and respiration rates. in addition, thermal performance curves were generated in fall 2023 and 2025 to estimate temperature optimums for photosynthesis and calcification. across both species, rates of calcification, photosynthesis, and respiration did not differ by bleaching phenotype but varied strongly by season, indicating metabolic recovery of previously bleached individuals. surprisingly, calcification rates during the warmest time of the year were as low as the coldest months, contrasting with historical patterns of peak summer calcification. this likely reflects mild thermal stress, as late-summer conditions exceeded local bleaching thresholds but did not accumulate degree heating weeks. these patterns indicate that, in the absence of marine heatwaves, resistant and susceptible corals experienced similar physiological modulation across seasons. this suggests that previously bleached individuals may have undergone functional recovery or acclimatization since prior heatwave events. crucially, the absence of acute stress during this period means that underlying differences in heat tolerance may not manifest until the next major heatwave. thus, our results highlight both the potential for resilience through physiological acclimatization and the need for future studies to elucidate the legacies and limitations of acclimatization in corals. marcelina martynek when variability helps\u2013or hurts: coral responses to an extreme marine heatwave across a natural thermal gradient coral reefs are declining rapidly, punctuated by the most extreme marine heatwave on record in the southern great barrier reef in 2024. diel temperature variability can \u201cprime\u201d corals for heat tolerance, yet earlier heat stress events resulted in unexpectedly high mortality in the most variable habitats, raising questions about when variability helps or harms. here, we quantified host physiology and microbial communities of the reef-building coral pocillopora damicornis across six distinct thermal environments spanning 2.4\u20137.7 \u00b0c d\u207b\u00b9 and three timepoints: pre- (sept 2022), during (may 2024), and post-heat stress (sept 2024). pre-heatwave, p. damicornis from the shallow lagoon\u2013an intermediately variable habitat\u2013displayed the clearest \u201coptimal priming\u201d phenotype, with elevated host protein content, mycosporine-like amino acids, and  phenoloxidase (ppo) activity, indicating enhanced antimicrobial and immune potential prior to thermal stress. microbial communities from the most thermally variable sites contained a greater proportion of rare and specialized taxa, suggesting  microbiome structuring along the variability gradient. during the heatwave, corals exhibited synchronous declines across most physiological metrics regardless of site, and microbiomes underwent significant beta-diversity shifts while remaining broadly endozoicomonas-dominated. post-heatwave, many physiological metrics rebounded\u2013often approaching or exceeding pre-heatwave levels\u2013yet trajectories diverged sharply between lower-, intermediate-, and higher-variability sites. differential abundance analyses revealed that taxa with putative probiotic and antimicrobial properties became enriched primarily at intermediate variability , a pattern absent at both the least and most variable sites. together, these results reveal nonlinear, site-specific regimes in which variability promotes resilience, induces vulnerability, or both, with direction and effect dependent on the level of thermal variability by coupling natural microbial assemblages with host physiological status across an environmental variability gradient and through an extreme marine heatwave, this work advances mechanistic forecasts of coral persistence and helps prioritize reef habitats most likely to withstand continued ocean warming.",
