          "name": "Claudia Pogoreutz",
          "search_text": "claudia pogoreutz chilling of algal symbionts shapes the cold stress response in a temperate cnidarian photosymbiosis bleaching, the breakdown of the cnidarian-algal symbiosis has been widely documented in response to heat stress. while similar responses have been reported under cold stress, the underlying mechanisms remain unknown. here we investigated the physiological and metabolic responses of the temperate photosymbiotic sea anemone aiptasia couchii to a non-linear cold stress gradually ramped down from 17 to 6 \u00b0c, to mimic a marine cold spell in the western mediterranean. after four weeks of experiment, we observed the onset of bleaching and tentacle retraction along with doubling of host antioxidant activity in cold-stressed anemones. of note, while algal symbiont density and chlorophyll a content decreased significantly in the cold, photosynthetic efficiency (fv/fm) remained largely unaffected. strikingly, this was contrasted by a complete collapse of gross photosynthesis. this apparent de-coupling of light-dependent and -independent reactions of photosynthesis suggests chilling injury of cold-stressed algal symbionts, drawing parallels to higher plants and free-living marine microalgae. untargeted lc-ms metabolomics confirmed the absence of photosynthetic carbon fixation and resulting increases in catabolic activity reflected in a distinct decrease of metabolites involved in energy storage such as lipids and carbohydrate derivatives with a concomitant increase of various dipeptides. overall, this suggests that chilling causes the collapse of symbiotic nutrient cycling, leading to host starvation, similar to what is observed during heat-induced bleaching. taken together, our work highlights that despite contrasting physiological responses to cold and heat stress, both stressors may destabilize the symbiosis through similar mechanisms centred around host starvation. two decades of endozoicomonadaceae research: where are we now, and where are we headed? the bacterial family endozoicomonadaceae is globally prevalent in a diversity of marine animal hosts, with endozoicomonas being the genus most consistently associated with cnidarians worldwide. long considered key mutualists of corals due to their metabolic versatility, evidence of host co-diversification, and their frequent decline during stress, bleaching, and disease, endozoicomonadaceae have become emblematic of coral symbiosis. yet, definitive empirical evidence for their functional roles, interactions within the holobiont, and the breadth of their lifestyles remains limited. emerging reports of parasitic and pathogenic behaviors in other marine hosts further challenge the prevailing assumption of mutualism and highlight the need to re-examine their roles in cnidarian biology. in this talk, we will provide an overview of the state-of-the-art of this rapidly developing field, and showcase recent functional studies aimed at unraveling the biology of this enigmatic and ubiquitous bacterial family. endozoicomonas\u2013cnidarian symbiosis: understanding metabolic interactions in their spatial context endozoicomonas is the most consistently associated bacterial genus of cnidaria, including corals and anemones. functional -omics, their often-high abundances, and the loss of these enigmatic bacteria from the host tissues under host stress, suggest that endozoicomonas may be coral mutualists. however, their functions within the holobiont remain yet to be determined. to elucidate metabolic interactions of endozoicomonas in their symbiotic context, we leverage a newly established temperate photosymbiotic sea anemone model to employ a multipronged approach combining genetic transformation, experimental microbiome manipulation, and correlative high-resolution imaging. using two genetically and morphologically distinct endozoicomonas strains from the sea anemone aiptasia couchii, we here elucidate their ecological niche in the intact symbiosis. first, we will identify their specific localization with fish-imaging in freshly collected anemones. then, we will harness fluorescently-tagged and 15n labelled isolates to quantitate and link their metabolic capabilities to their spatial context at (sub)cellular resolution using a correlative fluorescence microscopy-sem-nanosims imaging approach. thereby, this approach promises to shed light on the metabolic interactions of two distinct endozoicomonas strains, and ultimately their contribution to cnidarian holobiont functioning.",
