          "name": "Ilse Valenzuela",
          "search_text": "ilse valenzuela development of artificial reefs using additive manufacturing and waste materials. promotion of ecological restoration techniques through an educational platform the loss of biodiversity, structural complexity, and biomass in marine organisms has been documented over the last two decades (bracho-villavicencio et al., 2023). artificial reefs have been employed to support the recovery of degraded marine ecosystems, using a wide range of materials, structural designs, and construction methodologies (yoris-nobile et al., 2023).\r\nthis study investigates the potential of waste-derived materials for developing a cementitious mortar suitable for extrusion through 3d printing technologies. several formulations incorporating mussel shells, limestone, metakaolin, brick dust, ceramics, and portland cement were developed to manufacture artificial substrates. these substrates were evaluated in terms of biological receptivity, printability, mechanical performance, and material cost.\r\nin the initial phase, small-scale substrates were fabricated and tested in an aquarium containing tropical coral species. the results showed several positive indicators of biological compatibility, including surface morphology features that promoted coral attachment and growth. however, elevated ph levels were detected in some formulations, highlighting potential risks and the need for material adjustments in subsequent phases.\r\nthese results were considered in the manufacture of a larger 1 m\u00b3 artificial reef unit, anchored in the southern portugal, which is currently being assessed for additional ecological and technical factors. these include the design of environmental functions (refuge zones, benthic colonization areas, complex geometric features, and pathways facilitating fish movement), material durability, and the logistical aspects of manufacturing, deployment, and long-term maintenance.\r\nthis work also contributes to the development and dissemination of ecological restoration methodologies through an educational platform funded by the european union's erasmus+ programme. the initiative aims to develop an interdisciplinary curriculum and e-learning environment that promotes marine conservation, while enhancing the training of erasmus students and marine-related professionals. students from portugal, spain, and croatia will receive theoretical and practical training based on the scientific outputs of this project.",
