          "name": "Joseph Townsend",
          "search_text": "joseph townsend comparison of coral reef carbonate production using in-water and image-based techniques calcium carbonate production constitutes one of the core processes that drive coral reef ecosystem functioning. previously carbonate production has been assessed using in-water census techniques such as reefbudget, and more recently researchers are considering estimations using image-based assessments. this study compares carbonate production estimates derived from image-based coralnet and image-derived modified reefbudget techniques to in-water reefbudget surveys in puerto rico, indonesia, and the chagos archipelago. gross carbonate production estimates were similar between in-water and image-based approaches across all surveys, indicating that the area-normalized scaling of calcification rates, colony size, and rugosity employed in the calcification tables published for coralnet produce comparable estimates to in-water reefbudget assessments of carbonate production. notably, the recent publication of updated area-normalized rates (v2) produced estimates more comparable to in-water reefbudget estimates across all locations and rates. remaining divergences in production estimates between methods are potentially driven by differences in area captured in the surveys (full reef contour vs. planar imagery) and survey effort that were shown to affect calcifier cover estimates, particularly at low coral cover sites. using locally derived growth rates compared to regionally-based growth rates also suggests site-specific characteristics can influence accuracy more than method choice. both in-water and image-based methods have considerations for use, and practitioners should recognize situation-specific differences in methodologies and select the one most suitable for their specific study. however, automated substrate classification algorithms have the capacity to quantify benthic cover from image-based approaches and track carbonate production across larger spatial and temporal scales than was previously possible by diver-based surveys.",
