          "name": "Cheong Xin Chan",
          "search_text": "cheong xin chan genomics-powered insights into diversity, ecology and evolution of coral algal symbionts symbiodiniaceae are a specialised family of dinoflagellates best known for their symbiotic partnerships with corals and many other reef organisms; these relationships are critical for sustaining coral reef health. genome data from symbiodiniaceae are essential for understanding the molecular and evolutionary mechanisms that underpin their symbioses across diverse hosts. genome resources for symbiodiniaceae have expanded in recent years, spanning at least 22 taxa to date. these genomes reveal remarkable divergence, including phylogenetic diversity obscured by near-identical morphologies, and heterogeneous evolutionary patterns shaped by distinct selective pressures across different genomic regions. we recently sequenced multiple isolates of the naturally thermotolerant symbiont durusdinium trenchii (~2 gbp) and the free-living lineage effrenium voratum (~1.2 gbp). comparative analyses incorporating all available symbiodiniaceae genomes uncovered evidence of whole-genome duplication in d. trenchii, along with condition-specific expression of duplicated genes. we hypothesise that increased gene dosage, together with its facultative lifestyle, provides functional redundancy and flexibility that enhanced the efficiency of d. trenchii as a coral symbiont. our analysis of the e. voratum genomes further revealed ancient genome reduction predating the diversification of symbiodiniaceae, and how gene duplication and adaptation to free-living versus symbiotic lifestyles intersect to shape genome evolution of symbiodiniaceae. we also identified key molecular regulatory mechanisms of gene expression in dinoflagellates, including differential rna editing, differential exon usage, and prevalence of long non-coding rnas. in combination, these results demonstrate how genomic approaches are transforming our understanding of symbiodiniaceae diversification and the evolutionary innovations that enable these dinoflagellates to support coral reef health. these data provide foundational resources that now enable emerging hologenomics approaches to study coral-algal symbioses in hospite, advancing our capacity to investigate the in situ diversity and dynamics of \u201cunculturable\u201d coral algal symbionts.",
