          "name": "Chris Roelfsema",
          "search_text": "chris roelfsema 20 years of annual monitoring of coral reef benthos using innovative approaches, heron reef, australia coral reefs are dynamic environments where temporal changes occur within and across reefs as a result of natural drivers and impacts such as bleaching, cyclones and diseases. due to mounting climatic pressures, there is an urgent need to better understand reef dynamics at extensive spatial and temporal scales to aid reef management. recent developments in low cost cameras and gps, machine learning and earth observation have rapidly increased our ability to monitor reefs at various spatial and temporal scales. \r\n\r\nhere we present some of the innovations that let to the analyses of over 20 years of annual coral reef benthic monitoring combining, citizen science, photo quadrates, satellite imagery and machine learning applied at heron reef (28 km2), southern great barrier reef, australia. \r\n\r\nannual surveys included: coralwatch colour health chart surveys, +4000 geolocated photo quadrats covering +30 km of transects, and coincident high resolution multi spectral satellite imagery. the photos were analysed through machine learning using reefcloud/coralnet, and were integrated with high spatial resolution multi spectral satellite imagery, water depth, and waves using object based analysis and random forest classifier to produce high resolution habitat maps. these data sets provided benthic composition at individual photo quadrat, site, geomorphic zone and reef scales over time. analysis gave us further insights into: benthic cover change, carbonate budgets, bleaching-cyclone-disease impacts, and reef resilience. \r\n\r\nour findings highlight recent innovations that increase our ability to monitor reefs at various spatial scales, while also making research methods more accessible, repeatable, and robust. the developed approaches formed the basis for various mapping initiatives locally to globally scales. we also show that coral benthic communities vary within and across reefs over time and space, and site selection is crucial for understanding reef dynamics. future research directions will focus on real time analysis, autonomous monitoring and integration with hyperspectral imagery.",
