          "name": "Christopher L. Lawson",
          "search_text": "christopher l. lawson choosing sampling sites to maximise impact of reconnaissance on decision making reef interventions require strategic decisions about resource allocation, as management efforts typically reach only a small fraction of possible reefs. determining where to intervene requires data on current reef state across vast areas, though not necessarily high-resolution taxonomic detail. recent technological advances have transformed who can contribute such information via improved underwater photography, remote internet connectivity, and artificial intelligence.\r\n\r\nthe great reef census provides the infrastructure for citizen scientists to easily collect standardised survey imagery, capitalising on public interest. images undergo dual analysis: semantic segmentation by deep learning models and verification by online citizen scientists. this hybrid approach achieves over 95% accuracy for key coral morphologies when compared to expert assessments. the most recent annual survey programme documented approximately 380 reefs across the great barrier reef within four months. the platform now operates globally, scaling up into regions including hawaii, red sea, and coral triangle. the programme is operated through a collaborative network spanning universities, not-for-profits, traditional owners, government agencies, and technology companies.\r\n\r\nto harness this infrastructure most effectively, a recent critical innovation optimises where surveys should be conducted, rather than relying on random volunteer deployment. priority mapping algorithms identify reefs where resolving coral cover uncertainty most influences management decisions. this approach accounts for larval connectivity networks, disturbance history, and uncertainty propagation through reef networks. several hundred strategically selected surveys can effectively inform intervention allocation across thousands of potential sites.\r\n\r\napplications include optimising pest control programmes that can reach less than 5% of reefs, targeting restoration efforts toward key larval source reefs, and prioritising fishing compliance enforcement. by combining accessible technology with decision-focused survey design, this approach demonstrates how broadscale citizen science can meaningfully enhance cost-effectiveness and ecological impact of targeted reef interventions.",
