          "name": "Javiera Olivares-Rojas",
          "search_text": "javiera olivares-rojas a management framework leveraging thermal regimes and connectivity to promote evolutionary adaptation across coral reef seascapes coral heat tolerance has gradually increased over recent decades, but the rising frequency of marine heatwaves may well outpace future rates of adaptation. as the climate continues to warm, coral reef conservation and restoration must harness corals\u2019 evolutionary potential. however, there is currently limited operational guidance on how to translate evolutionary principles into actionable, spatially explicit management strategies across reef seascapes. we present a framework that integrates patterns of thermal exposure with larval dispersal networks to classify reefs into functional units with distinct management priorities. our framework addresses two practical questions: (1) which management intervention\u2013among protection, restoration, or assisted evolution via deployment or collection of heat-tolerant corals\u2013is most appropriate for a given reef, and (2) where should each intervention be prioritised to maximise long-term adaptive capacity across reef seascapes? applying the framework to the great barrier reef as a case study, we identified complementary regional priorities: southern reefs emerge as priorities for protection and the deployment of heat-adapted corals, while northern reefs serve as valuable heat-tolerant sources and targets for restoration. we also tested the sensitivity of the classification criteria across three threshold scenarios and found that stricter criteria progressively identify smaller, more confident sets of priority reefs, demonstrating how threshold choices influence management flexibility. in the coming years, our framework can be updated to support adaptive management as new insights become available from the rapidly advancing field of coral evolutionary biology. this framework offers restoration practitioners and reef managers a practical, evidence-based tool for translating evolutionary principles into strategic, climate-informed restoration planning that supports both ecological resilience and long-term adaptive potential as climate pressures intensify.",
