          "name": "Joanna Smart",
          "search_text": "joanna smart a validated benthic habitat classification scheme for hyperspectral remote sensing: integrating ecological relevance and spectral separability. ecologically meaningful field monitoring classification schemes often include subtle distinctions that are not spectrally distinguishable using remote sensing. while remote sensing classifications sacrifice ecological detail due to sensor limitations. this disconnects results in separate schemes for field work and remote sensing, limiting integration between methods. to combat this, we present reefclass, a five-level hierarchical benthic habitat classification scheme that bridges this gap by co-designing ecological relevance with spectral separability. the hierarchy ranges from broad functional groups to detailed morphological and colour distinctions, with explicit compatibility mapping to established monitoring programs. to validate the scheme, we collected in-situ hyperspectral reflectance signatures (400-700 nm) for a set of endmember categories ecologically relevant across three australian reef systems. the signatures were deposited and analysed in a specially created open-access spectral repository called wavelength. linear discriminant analysis demonstrated complete separation of broad functional groups at functional levels, confirming ecologically relevant distinctions are spectrally achievable. random forest classification suggested that where high spectral resolution data is available, classification accuracy can exceed 80%, even at high thematic resolutions. hierarchical clustering revealed that pigmentation, rather than morphology or taxonomy, primarily drives spectral similarity, with coral colour dominating over functional ecology. our results suggest that reefclass provides a validated classification framework for hyperspectral mapping, allowing users to select appropriate classification levels based on data quality and monitoring objectives. the scheme enables integration of existing monitoring data as training datasets while acknowledging realistic limits of spectral discrimination. further work will validate these derived separability patterns against operational hyperspectral satellite imagery to assess performance under real-world atmospheric and water column conditions.",
