          "name": "Julian Gueho",
          "search_text": "julian gueho understanding benthic roughness: how does spatial scale affect the measurement of reef structural complexity? structural complexity underpins critical ecological functions on coral reefs and is central to multiple quantitative metrics used in reef assessment and monitoring. it is associated with high biodiversity, enhanced wave attenuation, and increased ecosystem resilience. concerningly, coral reefs worldwide are experiencing an unprecedented decline in structural complexity and are shifting from live coral to rubble-dominated states due to the increasing frequency of harmful disturbance events. the geo-ecological health of a coral reef, defined as its capacity to maintain structural integrity, is closely linked to benthic structural complexity and represents an important metric in reef monitoring. however, complexity is often measured within a narrow range of spatial scales and methodological approaches, despite its strong scale dependence, and frequently overlooks the contribution of dead and mobile components to seabed rugosity. in this study, we investigated how structural complexity varies across spatial scales and assessed the contribution of key benthic classes to overall seabed rugosity. structure-from-motion photogrammetry was used to generate sub-centimetre-resolution orthomosaics and digital elevation models for two contrasting reef environments at heron reef: one dominated by rubble and one dominated by complex branching coral. seabed structural complexity was quantified using the vector ruggedness measure across nine spatial scales, and benthic classes (including coral rubble as a distinct class) were classified using object-based image analysis. results revealed that roughness increased logarithmically with spatial scale. higher-magnitude roughness at coral-dominated locations occurred at finer scales, whereas the highest roughness values at rubble-dominated locations occurred at broader scales. although the greatest structural complexity was associated with coral-dominated areas, coral rubble contributed between 18% and 48% of total benthic roughness across all scales. these findings demonstrate that rubble can contribute substantially to reef structural complexity and highlight the importance of including non-living and mobile components in future assessments of reef structural change.",
