          "name": "Kailash Cook",
          "search_text": "kailash cook marine heatwaves disrupt the culling of crown-of-thorns starfish on the great barrier reef crown-of-thorns starfish (cots) predation poses an enduring threat to coral health on the great barrier reef (gbr), driving substantial investment in research and control efforts. however, how cots respond to heat stress in-situ remains uncertain, limiting our ability to optimise control efforts. we analysed 33 years of cots control records, including pre-culling surveys (manta tows) and cull data, with satellite-derived thermal stress metrics to investigate how heat stress influences cots detections and detectability patterns across the gbr. cots detections on pre-culling surveys declined significantly with cumulative heat stress, decreasing by 22% per \u00b0c-week increase in degree heating weeks (dhw) to a maximum absolute decrease of 95% at 12\u00b0c-weeks. cull data showed 9\u201314% fewer cots culled per dive per \u00b0c-week depending on size. these patterns underpin a seasonal response: cots culling efficiency peaks in winter and declines in summer, with deeper and longer summer troughs under higher annual heat stress. we incorporated these empirical relationships into an ecosystem model of the gbr (reefmod-gbr) to investigate their effects on coral cover benefits from the cots control program. simulations indicated that these heat stress effects on cots detectability reduced predicted coral cover benefits up to 2040. however, these reductions were mitigated when adopting strategies that when culling effort was redistributed away from heat-stressed reefs, highlighting opportunities to refine cots management in a warming climate.",
