          "search_text": "katrina mcguigan data mining and data archiving to support management of coral resilience climate-smart reef restoration depends on identifying coral genotypes and phenotypes with high resilience to warming, yet collecting the necessary survival and reproduction data is resource-intensive. in this talk, we propose that a substantial body of published data already exists that could be used more effectively to support restoration decisions, including donor colony selection, site prioritization, and planning for adaptive potential.\r\n\r\nwe review the current landscape of resilience-relevant coral data, highlight common limitations in experimental design and curation, and propose simple, low-cost changes that restoration projects can implement to rapidly build more informative datasets. through re-analysis of published studies, we will show how integrating these data may reveal new insights into coral thermal tolerance directly applicable to climate-smart restoration. we seek to make data management recommendations that offer practical, scalable tools to help restoration practitioners embed climate resilience into their current workflows.",
