          "name": "Mandy Cheung",
          "search_text": "mandy cheung ocean warming fragments coral connectivity on the great barrier reef ocean warming disrupts coral larval connectivity through two major mechanisms, including altered larval development in response to warming and reduced reproductive output from a shrinking coral population. the extent to which these combined impacts can subsequently alter dispersal patterns across the vast coral reef ecosystems has not been extensively studied. using a 1 km coral larval dispersal model, climate model projected sea temperatures during spawning months, and a spatially-explicit model of coral population dynamics, we examine how climate change disrupts the connectivity of the great barrier reef (gbr) from 2025 to 2100 under four climate scenarios. under the highest emission scenarios of ssp5-8.5, the combined impacts reduce the number and strength of source-sink connections by 49.7% and 99.6%, respectively, by the end of the century. the mean number of reef clusters increases by 6%, while the mean size of clusters decreases by 46%, suggesting potential habitat fragmentation. conversely, under the lower emission scenario of ssp2-4.5, the number and strength of connections are predicted to be reduced by 12.4% and 74.4%, respectively, and the clustering of reefs remains consistent by 2100. the weakening of larval connectivity and the increasingly isolated reef patches will potentially reduce the exchange of genetic materials between coral populations and the potential for rescue effects of larvae after local extirpation of corals from disturbances. we have also identified key source reefs, mainly distributed in the northern and southern regions, that have a high potential to replenish many other reefs consistently in over half of the hydrodynamic scenarios, given that larval production remains stable. conservation actions should be prioritised in these reefs that maintain connectivity to increase the post-disturbance recovery potential of the gbr.",
