          "name": "Maria Kottermair",
          "search_text": "maria kottermair shoreline changes in 2d versus 3d: case study from ulithi atoll, micronesia reef islands are small, low-lying landforms composed of loose reef-derived sediments, making them highly dynamic and susceptible to climate-driven coastal hazards. previous research has largely concentrated on planimetric (2d) shoreline changes to assess island persistence, leaving an important gap in understanding how these changes translate into three dimensions (3d). this study addresses that gap by mapping and monitoring reef island shorelines in both horizontal and vertical dimensions and examining whether 3d changes correspond with 2d trends. field surveys were conducted on ulithi atoll (micronesia) using structure-from-motion (sfm) photogrammetry and mobile laser scanning. shoreline indicators such as vegetation line, beach berm, and beach volume were analyzed to compare 2d and 3d changes. findings reveal that 3d changes do not consistently mirror 2d patterns and vary across islands and indicators, indicating that long-term planform studies reporting stability or accretion may need to be interpreted with caution. this research demonstrates that 3d shoreline dynamics can be effectively quantified using drone-based sfm and laser scanning, though each technique involves trade-offs in acquisition time, cost, resolution, and coverage. the results refine conceptual and methodological approaches for monitoring reef island evolution and underscore the importance of selecting appropriate shoreline proxies. a better understanding of 3d changes improves predictive models of island futures and informs climate adaptation strategies, critical for atoll nations facing severe climate uncertainty and limited relocation options.",
