          "name": "Matt Glue",
          "search_text": "matt glue resilient reefs, resilient communities: advancing marine conservation through education & innovation the coral reef rescue (crr) project: resilient reefs, resilient communities, funded by the global environment facility (gef), and executed by the university of queensland (uq), is implemented in six countries that host the majority (~68%) of the climate-resilient coral reefs worldwide, as identified in the 50 reefs project. to support in-country interventions, the gef crr team at uq, along with partnering ngos, and academics, collaborated to develop a series of free online courses tailored for government officers and ngo personnel working in marine conservation roles without a formal marine science background, as well as high school students and undergraduates. covering topics including the sustainable blue economy, coral reef ecology, coral reef data & monitoring, and climate-resilient communities, participants can learn in english or bahasa indonesia, with french coming soon. the courses aim to improve the effectiveness of marine managers and decision-makers and can be integrated within government training programmes through a bespoke certificate awarded upon course completion. \r\n\r\ncomplementing these courses, the uq team has developed a decision support portal to help practitioners select the most suitable, publicly available monitoring tools and datasets, dependent upon their requirements. with the vast array of resources available, pinpointing the best fit for specific marine management needs can be difficult. this dashboard simplifies the process by consolidating 70+ tools and datasets into a single system, allowing users to enter their project goals and receive customised recommendations.\r\n\r\nthe tools developed by the gef crr project support the ongoing efforts of project partners in each country, including the establishment of national hubs to foster greater collaboration among multi-sectoral stakeholders and the development of national action plans for coral reef conservation. by addressing the issue from multiple perspectives, the gef crr project is creating enabling conditions for effective local actions that promote the conservation of climate-resilient coral reefs.",
