          "name": "Nicole Leonard",
          "search_text": "nicole leonard utilising u-th dating to assess past reef growth, future reef potential and acute mortality events in the face of challenges to coral reefs it is important to understand responses to climatic and environmental changes at multiple spatial and temporal scales. yet, there is currently a disconnect between understanding past reef growth, modern disturbance regimes, and the potential of future reef growth. to bridge the gap between biological and geological timescales we here present the results of ~500 u-th dated corals obtained from reef matrix cores, death assemblages and fossil microatolls from eleven continental island fringing reefs in the keppel islands, southern great barrier reef (gbr). data from reef flat cores show that reefs initiated prior to 7500 years before present (yr.bp-1950), although not contemporaneously among sites. reef accretion rates following initiation were comparable to reefs elsewhere on the gbr, but rapidly outpaced average rates reported for central and northern inshore gbr sites in the subsequent 1500 years when sea levels were higher, and sea surface temperatures (sst\u2019s) were ~1\u00b0c warmer. reef slope cores show limited net accretion from ~500 yrbp to present at two reef sites suggesting these reefs have reached their geomorphological limits. u-th dates from coral death assemblages were highly variable until a multi-site peak centered around the 2006 bleaching event, suggesting unprecedented modern regional scale mortality. death assemblage data was also used to spatially interpolate past coral mortality and compare this data to in situ observations of mortality following known bleaching events. \r\ndata obtained from the keppel islands provides evidence for optimal growth phases, site specific geomorphological limitations and underestimation of acute mortality events when compared to observational reports. this information can be used to prioritise active reef restoration efforts and guide regional management strategies and expectations.",
