          "search_text": "carlos prada coral recruitment and fragment survival in mesophotic coral communities of the mississippi-alabama shelf the 2010 deepwater horizon oil spill impacted marine benthic communities across a wide geography centered on the mississippi-alabama continental shelf and slope to a depth of 1800 m. habitat-forming octocorals were heavily affected, prompting restoration efforts, starting with active fragmentation of corals, and passive recruitment of corals. the work used rovs to demonstrate ex-situ fragmentation of three dominant species occurring deeper than 50 m on the mississippi-alabama shelf: swiftia exserta, thesea nivea, and muricea pendula. corals were collected by rov, recovered to the surface, fragmented, and subsequently glued on to cement-based racks that were returned to sea. of 190 fragments monitored for 500 days, survivorship reached 57%, with strong variation by species, size class, and substrate. large fragments (\u22658 cm) placed on hard substrate had 82% survival. s. exserta performed best, with 89% survival and mean growth of 2.1 cm year\u207b\u00b9 (\u00b10.5 sem). mortality during the first six months was low, indicating high resilience to fragmentation. significant losses due to burial or overgrowth emerged later, when corals were surveyed at day 355. simultaneously, we performed recruitment studies at a 52-m mesophotic site (dsr7) and deployed 16 arrays, each containing twelve 20 cm square limestone and concrete settlement tiles for 724 days. across 131 tiles, 100 coral recruits settled (92 scleractinians, 8 octocorals), yielding a rate of 4.6 corals m\u00b2/year. highest recruitment (13.0 corals m\u00b2/year) occurred on upper surfaces of horizontal limestone tiles over hard substrate. recruitment responded significantly to substrate beneath tiles, tile material, and orientation, with no significant interactions. despite the predominance of octocorals at the site and near-absence of scleractinians, octocoral recruitment was unexpectedly lower than that of scleractinians. our findings  suggest that restoration of nearby deeper deepwater horizon-impacted sites will require active propagation techniques to supplement the slow and episodic recruitment in natural and artificial substrates. speciation across depth gradients in reef corals ecological speciation through adaptation to different habitats can readily occur without strong geographic isolation when the same traits underlie both ecological divergence and reproductive isolation. in light-dependent corals with environment-mediated spawning, adaptation to varying light regimes across depths provides opportunities for such speciation. we show that depth-related distributions are common among sister lineages of corals within the photic zone. we then investigated molecular drivers of depth-associated adaptive divergence by analyzing sequence variation in proteins related to environmental sensing in depth-segregated and light-dependent lineages in the orbicella species complex. specifically, we analyzed 1) two genetically divergent ecotypes of orbicella faveolata across a depth gradient, and 2) two depth-segregated sister species, o. annularis and o. franksi, with different spawning times following sunset. genome-wide analyses indicate divergence across depths occurred through adaptation via positive selection on g-protein-coupled receptors (gpcrs). these molecules mediate chemo/photo/thermo-reception, enhancing physiological adaptation across environments, and are also involved in reproductive isolation via differences in spawning time. our study proposes a molecular mechanism for the origin of depth-segregated coral species, common across anthozoans, in which ecological divergence operates at spatial scales smaller than their larval dispersal potential, and highlights avenues contributing to generating biodiversity in the sea.",
