          "name": "Hollie Putnam",
          "search_text": "hollie putnam contrasting temperature-induced gene network rewiring and isoform switching underlie relative thermal tolerance of coral species organismal thermal tolerance will shape coral reef diversity and abundance of the future, as ocean warming drives extensive coral bleaching and mortality through increasingly frequent marine heatwaves. coral thermal tolerance varies markedly among species, reflecting differences in both physiological and molecular regulatory mechanisms. gene expression plasticity enables dynamic transcriptional adjustments to environmental stress, while alternative splicing and isoform switching further expand molecular adaptability by altering transcript diversity and abundance. because genes function within combinatorial networks, changes in gene connectivity and in the identity of highly influential genes, or hub genes, can reveal higher-order biological reorganization under stress. yet, the contribution of isoform switching to gene network dynamics remains unknown in corals. here we integrate photo-physiology, gene network topology, hub genes dynamics, and isoform switching patterns across three hawaiian corals, porites compressa, montipora capitata, and pocillopora acuta to identify the molecular processes shaping thermal performance. we show that the interplay between expression plasticity, hub gene rearrangement, and isoform switching drives species-specific thermal tolerance. network analysis uncovered extensive rewiring and temperature-responsive hub gene turnover regulated by switches in isoform usage. the most thermally tolerant species, p. compressa, shows high energy reserves, stable constitutive gene expression and continuous isoform adjustments across temperatures, while fine-tuning thermal response through dedicated hub genes. in contrast, p. acuta shows the lowest holobiont thermal tolerance, with broad transcriptomic shifts and isoform switching controlling the reorganization of network leadership at high temperatures. these findings identify isoform switching as a newly recognized regulatory mechanism in coral thermal resilience, linking isoform diversity to gene regulatory hierarchies and establishing its role in the evolutionary toolkit of reef-building corals and as a predictor of resistance and environmental resilience.",
