          "name": "Zoe Dellaert",
          "search_text": "zoe dellaert dissecting coral biology: tissue- and cell-specific insights into holobiont thermal stress reef-building corals are complex holobionts, containing cross-kingdom symbioses, somatic genetic diversity, and internal microenvironments that differ substantially from the overlying seawater. their two tissue layers are composed of diverse cell types with specialized roles in environmental sensing, biomineralization, and supporting photosynthetic endosymbionts. traditional whole-organism gene expression analyses provide broad signatures of how corals respond to environmental perturbations, yet can obscure spatial context and cell-specific biology. this limits our ability to capture heterogeneity in biological baselines and also the spatial and cell-level complexity of the cascades leading from heat stress to coral bleaching. in light of these challenges, we present a series of experimental approaches to test the hypothesis that the processes that result in coral-algal dysbiosis originate in different cell types, and are therefore spatially partitioned. we demonstrated that coral tissues have spatially distinct gene expression profiles, and then applied bulk and spatially resolved approaches to study coral and symbiont gene expression under control and acute heat-stress conditions for three species representing diverse tissue morphologies. laser capture microdissection and rnaseq revealed >1,500 differentially expressed genes involved in biomineralization, environmental sensing, and molecular transport between oral epidermis and aboral tissues in pocillopora acuta. we generated single-nuclei expression atlases for p. acuta, montipora capitata, and porites compressa and identified that, in control conditions, key heat stress genes from the literature are expressed more substantially in certain cell types rather than uniformly across tissues. lastly, our analysis of both bulk and spatially-resolved assays of an acute heat stress time series identified both shared and distinct expression patterns between these three coral species, with many heat stress genes showing spatial variation and species-specific differences in the timing of response initiation. our high resolution approaches generate spatial and temporal information essential to disentangling the coral holobiont stress response and resilience.",
