          "name": "Andrea Toso",
          "search_text": "andrea toso heat at depth: unprecedented thermal anomalies of summer 2024 trigger mass mortality of paramuricea clavata (risso, 1826) in the mediterranean sea global warming is severely affecting the mediterranean sea, determining mass mortality events in long-lived benthic organisms. among the most vulnerable is the purple sea-whip, paramuricea clavata, a key habitat-forming octocoral that mostly thrives on coralligenous reefs and supports several ecosystem functions. while shallow populations, <40 m, are typically the most exposed to thermal anomalies (ta), showing mass mortality events in several locations, deeper populations have not yet been affected by comparably extensive events. here, we report the effects of an unprecedented ta affecting p. clavata at 50 m depth at scilla italy, during summer 2024.\r\nsurveys conducted via video transects at depths of 35, 40, and 50 m revealed a dramatic deterioration in colony health following the 2024 summer ta. in january 2024, severe necrosis affected 46.9% of the colonies at 35 m, while its incidence was markedly lower at 40 m (2.9%) and 50 m (2.1%). after the ta, in september 2024, severe necrosis of p.clavata became widespread across all surveyed depths, reaching 94.5% at 35 m, 98% at 40 m, and 48.5% at 50 m.\r\non-site underwater temperature loggers recorded 28 distinct ta events in 2024 at 50 m depth, during which sea temperatures reached or exceeded the species\u2019 physiological threshold of 25 \u00b0c. global ocean reanalysis data (obtained from copernicus marine service, 1994\u20132024) were used to identify the marine heat waves in the period 2011-2024 at 35, 40 and 50 meters. the result show that the summer 2024 produced an unusually prolonged (64-day) warm anomaly at 50 m depth, highlighting an unprecedented downward penetration of thermal stress.\r\nour findings provide the first evidence of a thermal anomaly in waters deeper than 40 m causing extensive necrosis in p. clavata in the central mediterranean sea, and highlight that deep thermal refugia for this species may be compromised.",
