      "search_text": "university of salento andrea toso francesca necci andrea toso heat at depth: unprecedented thermal anomalies of summer 2024 trigger mass mortality of paramuricea clavata (risso, 1826) in the mediterranean sea global warming is severely affecting the mediterranean sea, determining mass mortality events in long-lived benthic organisms. among the most vulnerable is the purple sea-whip, paramuricea clavata, a key habitat-forming octocoral that mostly thrives on coralligenous reefs and supports several ecosystem functions. while shallow populations, <40 m, are typically the most exposed to thermal anomalies (ta), showing mass mortality events in several locations, deeper populations have not yet been affected by comparably extensive events. here, we report the effects of an unprecedented ta affecting p. clavata at 50 m depth at scilla italy, during summer 2024.\r\nsurveys conducted via video transects at depths of 35, 40, and 50 m revealed a dramatic deterioration in colony health following the 2024 summer ta. in january 2024, severe necrosis affected 46.9% of the colonies at 35 m, while its incidence was markedly lower at 40 m (2.9%) and 50 m (2.1%). after the ta, in september 2024, severe necrosis of p.clavata became widespread across all surveyed depths, reaching 94.5% at 35 m, 98% at 40 m, and 48.5% at 50 m.\r\non-site underwater temperature loggers recorded 28 distinct ta events in 2024 at 50 m depth, during which sea temperatures reached or exceeded the species\u2019 physiological threshold of 25 \u00b0c. global ocean reanalysis data (obtained from copernicus marine service, 1994\u20132024) were used to identify the marine heat waves in the period 2011-2024 at 35, 40 and 50 meters. the result show that the summer 2024 produced an unusually prolonged (64-day) warm anomaly at 50 m depth, highlighting an unprecedented downward penetration of thermal stress.\r\nour findings provide the first evidence of a thermal anomaly in waters deeper than 40 m causing extensive necrosis in p. clavata in the central mediterranean sea, and highlight that deep thermal refugia for this species may be compromised. francesca necci coral-sponge joint transplantation enhances restoration of degraded benthic communities the current global rate of biodiversity loss and habitat degradation calls for innovative approaches to ecosystem restoration. despite receiving growing attention, multispecies approaches to habitat restoration remain poorly explored in marine environments. this study was carried out in the porto cesareo marine protected area (italy), where illegal harvesting of endolithic date mussels caused dismantling of shallow rocky bottoms and the development of extensive barren grounds.\r\nthe aim was to improve restoration outcomes by transplanting propagules of two species that naturally co-occur in the same habitat: the mediterranean reef-building coral cladocora caespitosa and the yellow massive sponge aplysina aerophoba. we established three experimental treatments: coral fragments transplanted alongside sponge fragments (multispecies), coral fragments transplanted in pairs (coral, monospecific), and sponge fragments transplanted in pairs (sponge, monospecific). we tested the hypothesis that the joint transplantation of coral fragments with their co-occurring sponge could enhance the survival and growth performance of both species.\r\nfor a. aerophoba, mono-species transplants exhibited an 84% survival rate, while survival in multispecies treatments was 64%. after one year, the mean surface area increased from comparable initial values to 15.8 \u00b1 1.0 cm\u00b2 in the mono-species treatment and 23.2 \u00b1 3.2 cm\u00b2 in the multispecies treatment, with trajectories diverging significantly between treatments (p < 0.05). \r\nfor c. caespitosa, survival rates were comparable between mono-species (65%) and multispecies (63%) treatments. after one year, the mean number of living corallites reached 14 \u00b1 2 in mono-species transplants and 19 \u00b1 2 in multispecies assemblages, resulting in significantly different final measurements between treatments.\r\nour findings are consistent with the initial hypothesis that both species benefit from being transplanted together showing growth performance in multispecies assemblages. these results provide new insights into the advantages of incorporating positive ecological interactions into restoration strategies, thereby supporting more effective ecosystem recovery.",
