          "name": "Luna Mayura da Fonseca Bauer",
          "search_text": "luna mayura da fonseca bauer a trait-based framework for kickstarting advanced functional ecology investigations in symbiodiniaceae understanding how symbiodiniaceae influence coral resilience is fundamental for predicting the future of reef ecosystems under accelerating climate change. although recent taxonomic advances have revealed extensive genetic diversity within this family, ecological knowledge has not kept pace. in particular, the absence of standardized functional trait data has prevented the integration of symbiodiniaceae into trait-based models widely applied to corals, fishes, and other reef taxa. this gap limits our ability to quantify functional diversity, redundancy, and vulnerability\u2013key elements for assessing ecosystem resilience. here we present a trait-based framework designed to initiate the development of functional trait databases and ecological models for symbiodiniaceae. we synthesize current knowledge on physiological, ecological, and evolutionary variation within the family, emphasizing how differences among lineages in photosynthetic performance, oxidative stress response, nutrient use, host specificity, and environmental tolerance shape holobiont functioning. building on this synthesis, we propose a suite of 17 operationally measurable traits spanning six core functional dimensions: photosynthesis, growth, energy reserves, symbiotic interaction, ecological plasticity, and thermal tolerance. these traits were selected for ecological relevance, methodological feasibility, and their potential to predict symbiont contributions to holobiont performance. we additionally identify three major barriers that currently limit trait-based approaches in symbiodiniaceae: (i) the lack of standardized trait definitions and measurement protocols, (ii) scarce large-scale, open-access trait datasets, and (iii) the need for modeling frameworks that incorporate the host\u2013symbiont\u2013environment interaction. overcoming these barriers will enable the integration of symbiodiniaceae in global functional ecology and strengthen predictions of coral reef trajectories under climate change. we outline pathways forward, including refined taxonomy, systematic trait measurements in hospite and ex hospite, and the creation of a centralized symbiodiniaceae trait database. by establishing the conceptual and methodological basis for trait-based symbiont ecology, this work provides a necessary foundation for incorporating symbiodiniaceae functional diversity into models of reef resilience.",
