          "name": "Mariana Marcondes",
          "search_text": "mariana marcondes shaded habitats are not universal microrefugia for sw atlantic corals: insights from the 2025 heatwave marine heatwaves and high irradiance drive coral stress, highlighting the importance of climate refugia for species persistence. we investigated the physiological variability of the reef corals montastraea cavernosa and mussismilia hispida in a cross-shelf gradient and distinct micro-habitats (reef tops vs. walls) in the abrolhos bank during the 2025 marine heatwave. we hypothesized that light attenuation in vertical walls and turbid coastal sites would function as climate refugia, sustaining higher symbiont densities and pigments compared to high-irradiance tops. specifically, we predicted this effect would be maximized in coastal walls and for the low-light specialist m. cavernosa. data from 104 colonies were analyzed using generalized linear models (gamma distribution). results revealed context-dependent responses rather than a universal refuge pattern. for symbiodiniaceae density, we observed a significant species \u00d7 site \u00d7 habitat interaction. starkly contrasting our hypothesis, m. hispida in clear midshelf waters exhibited ~6 times higher symbiont density on reef tops compared to walls. this suggests that the large depth disparity at this site (top ~7.7m vs. wall ~18.9m) likely imposes light limitation on the deep walls rather than protection, with m. hispida preferring moderate irradiance levels found on tops. conversely, coastal sites showed negligible differences between habitats, likely due to limited depth variation homogenizing the light environment. unexpectedly, m. cavernosa defied the refuge hypothesis, displaying physiological stability across all habitats. chlorophyll-a concentration was influenced by a species \u00d7 habitat interaction and positively driven by depth; the latter explained ~11% of the pigment variability, suggesting active photoacclimatization where deeper colonies accumulated more chlorophyll. we conclude that reef walls do not serve as universal refugia, as their protective capacity is strictly modulated by local bathymetry, turbidity, and species-specific strategies. to fully unravel the drivers of physiological resilience, future analyses will incorporate remote sensing data (e.g., sst, dli) alongside additional ecological metrics.",
