          "name": "Michelle Amario",
          "search_text": "michelle amario are coral restoration sites on the right track? thermal stress episodes, turbidity, and depth as criteria for the assessment of the suitability of current coral restoration sites over the past decades coral reef systems have been suffering from anthropogenic climate-induced environmental changes. coral bleaching events have become more frequent, severe, and widely distributed, leading to a decline in coral cover and reef health. in response, active coral reef restoration has been widely adopted to reverse reef decline. however, the effectiveness of such initiatives remains limited, especially when environmental conditions are not adequately considered in site selection. our hypothesis is that there is a fundamental mismatch between the location of reef restoration sites and their environmental suitability, as many initiatives are located in shallow, clear-water habitats that are highly susceptible to thermal anomalies and bleaching events. we assessed conditions at 920 coral reef restoration sites worldwide using historical satellite-derived data on thermal stress (degree heating weeks), turbidity (kd490), and local bathymetry. we found that 74% of sites occur in shallow waters (<20 m), 71% in clear-water environments (kd490 \u2264 0.1), and 80% in areas highly exposed to recurrent thermal anomalies. since 1985, more than 80% of sites have experienced at least five bleaching-level events (\u22654 \u00b0c-week dhw), and a similar proportion has encountered at least one severe bleaching event (\u22658 \u00b0c-week dhw). these findings confirm our hypothesis. as it is, the current site selection criteria\u2013often based on accessibility, cost, and operational resources\u2013do not adequately account for climate-related vulnerabilities, raising concern about the long-term viability of current restoration. without a shift toward evidence-based planning that incorporates environmental resilience, restoration efforts risk falling short of their intended conservation goals. therefore, to improve outcomes, it is essential that environmental history and vulnerability become integral components of site selection. our study provides a data-driven framework to identify more climate-resilient restoration sites and refine conservation strategies under future warming scenarios, highlighting the potential of remote sensing technologies to support this process.",
