      "search_text": "university of south carolina - chapel hill stephanie peak stephanie peak cell type-specific gene expression programs define the transition from aposymbiotic to symbiotic states in a cnidarian host coral reef ecosystems are sustained by the metabolic partnership between cnidarian hosts and intracellular symbiodiniaceae, which inhabit a specialized subset of host gastrodermal cells dedicated to symbiont maintenance and nutrient exchange.  disruption of this relationship (coral bleaching), commonly through prolonged heat stress, can lead to coral mortality due to their dependence on algal symbionts for essential nutrition. as climate change drives rising seawater temperatures and increases the frequency of bleaching events, a detailed understanding of coral-algal symbiosis dynamics is urgently needed. recent single-cell transcriptomic studies in reef-building corals have begun to illuminate host cell type diversity and symbiont-associated states; however, the cellular and molecular mechanisms that govern symbiosis establishment and maintenance remain largely unresolved. in particular, the transition from symbiont-free gastrodermal cells to symbiont-containing cells, along with the gene expression programs that define these states, are still poorly characterized. bulk transcriptomic approaches obscure cell state distinctions by averaging gene expression across heterogeneous tissues, masking the cell-type-specific processes central to host\u2013symbiont interactions. here, we apply single-cell rna sequencing (scrna-seq) to the cnidarian model sea anemone exaiptasia diaphana (aiptasia) to resolve host cellular diversity in both symbiotic and symbiont-free states. this approach enables us to compare transcriptional landscapes across individual host cells and characterize multiple distinct cell populations within both gastrodermis and epidermis tissue layers. we identify the cell state associated with intracellular symbiont occupancy and uncover transcriptional features that differentiate this state from symbiont-free gastrodermal cells. these findings provide a prioritized set of candidate genes and pathways for functional testing and offer new mechanistic hypotheses for how different host cell states support the establishment, stability, and breakdown of cnidarian-algal symbiosis.",
      "connection_count": 1
