        {
          "name": "Cesar Garcia Carreon",
          "search_text": "cesar garcia carreon from brains to reefs: exploring the evolution of neurotransmission in corals understanding how early-diverging marine invertebrates regulate development and environmental responses at the molecular level is crucial to identifying mechanisms of resilience under rapid environmental change. neuropeptide signalling is one of the earliest-evolving systems of intercellular communication, it regulates a large diversity of physiological processes, in many animals, yet its role in corals remains largely unknown.\r\n\r\nthis project investigates the evolution of neurotransmission using the coral stylophora pistillata, a scleractinian cnidarian. the research builds upon previous studies in the cnidarian nematostella vectensis, where neuropeptides and their receptors were identified and functionally characterized, laying the groundwork for exploring these mechanisms in reef-building corals. the main goal is to identify and functionally characterize neuropeptides and their g protein-coupled receptors (gpcrs) in s.pistillata, marking the first such exploration in corals.  we identify coral neuropeptides and their gpcrs using bioinformatic prediction and phylogenetic analyses, followed by molecular cloning and heterologous expression to perform deorphanisation assays and characterise ligand\u2013receptor interactions.\r\n\r\napplying candidate neuropeptides to live coral tissues and coral larvae, we test their roles in processes fundamental to early-life success, including larval settlement, growth, reproductive physiology, stress responses, and symbiosis. linking these pathways to larval fitness and stress tolerance will provide mechanistic insight into how signalling networks shape developmental and ecological outcomes.\r\n\r\nthis work introduces neuropeptide signalling as an overlooked regulator of coral biology and connects evolutionary molecular mechanisms to phenotypic resilience. by integrating evolutionary biology, comparative genomics, molecular physiology, and functional assays, the project provides new perspectives on conserved pathways underlying early-life robustness and identifies potential molecular targets relevant to coral adaptation and conservation under global climate change.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "From Brains to Reefs: Exploring the Evolution of Neurotransmission in Corals",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "4fa8e993-3145-4b6f-9e60-f84a27ab510b"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Finley Chadwick",
          "search_text": "finley chadwick structure and dynamics of prokaryotic communities under temperature and salinity stress in a heat tolerant coral holobiont coral reefs are increasingly threatened by bleaching driven by rising ocean temperatures. understanding the mechanisms underlying temperature resilience in reef building corals, such as porites lobata, is crucial for safeguarding these ecosystems. the persian/arabian gulf (the gulf) experiences extreme heat and large seawater temperature fluctuations that would be lethal to coral elsewhere, suggesting the presence of unique resilience mechanisms. in addition to high temperature, gulf corals are exposed to unusually high salinity, raising the question of whether tolerance to temperature and salinity is linked.\r\nprokaryotic communities play essential roles in coral holobiont functioning, including nutrient cycling and disease resistance. but the extent to which these communities contribute to coral thermal tolerance is poorly understood. here we use 16s rrna metabarcoding to determine how the combined effects of temperature and salinity stress, under controlled aquaria conditions, influence the prokaryotic communities associated with heat tolerant p. lobata from the gulf. we studied microbial community shifts under natural and reduced salinity conditions, and under moderate and high thermal stress, allowing an assessment of the independent and interactive influence of these two important stressors. \r\nthis presentation will show that temperature and salinity stress induce significant restructuring of the coral associated prokaryotic communities, including shifts in the relative abundance of key taxa. these findings enhance our understanding of how gulf p. lobata holobionts may have adapted to extreme environments and provide insight into the potential for microbiome mediated resilience in future warming seas and across salinity regimes. this work contributes to understanding how environmental change shapes microbiome dynamics and coral holobiont adaptation.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Structure and Dynamics of Prokaryotic communities under Temperature and Salinity Stress in a Heat Tolerant Coral Holobiont",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "70f59987-f02f-4e01-ab42-6902ef036bde"
            }
          ]
        }
