          "name": "Maya Gomez",
          "search_text": "maya gomez genotypic variation in light-stimulated morphological plasticity and calcification in acropora cervicornis the complex branching morphology of acroporid corals contributes to reef rugosity and local hydrodynamics, rendering them foundation species on reefs worldwide. in the caribbean, acropora cervicornis morphology dynamically changes as a function of both host genotype and the environment. this differential capacity for plasticity allows some genets to optimize their response to changes in the local environment, contributing to the differential success of outplants. however, the underlying molecular and physiological mechanisms giving rise to these genet-specific changes in morphology remain undefined. to target this, we stimulated morphological plasticity in ten genotypes of a. cervicornis under controlled light and flow conditions over seven weeks and quantified genet-specific changes in host and symbiont physiology, skeletal structure, and gene expression. on average corals exhibited greater linear growth and weight gain under high light compared to low light treatments, yet the degree of the response varied by genet \u2013 some genets grew considerably more under high light than low light compared to others. investment into axes of growth also varied by genotype, with certain genets gaining more weight relative to their linear growth, a finding that could be explained by the significant genotypic variation in skeletal thickness and porosity revealed with micro-ct. internal skeletal frameworks are also plastic: newly generated skeletons in corals grown under low light were significantly thicker than their high light counterparts. though an effect of flow was not clear in morphological or physiological traits, it did appear in the molecular signature, suggesting that corals also sense and alter their gene expression in response to perceived flow. taken together, our results indicate that coral perceive and respond to these environmental cues, but the magnitude of the response varies among genets, which may underpin the variation in coral morphology on, and therefore function of, natural reefs.",
