          "name": "Natalie Villafranca",
          "search_text": "natalie villafranca understanding the impact of international policy frameworks on coral research and restoration in the caribbean effective collaboration between countries is essential for the conservation, protection and restoration of coral reefs. coral researchers and restoration practitioners often collaborate internationally to exchange genetic material or live corals for research and conservation purposes. however, because stony corals are protected under national and international law, stakeholders must obtain various permits to transfer these types of samples between countries. specific regulatory frameworks governing international exchange of coral include the convention on international trade in endangered species of wild fauna and flora (cites), ratified in 1975, and the nagoya protocol on access and benefit sharing which was initiated in 2010. coral researchers and restoration practitioners are increasingly concerned that these regulatory processes will delay or complicate urgently needed interventions such as assisted gene flow and live biobanking. we are conducting surveys and interviews of coral researchers, restoration practitioners, and governmental stakeholders across the caribbean to understand experiences with the process of obtaining/approving cites and nagoya permits for exchanges. in parallel, we undertook a literature review to elucidate where exchanges are occurring and to recruit participants. results to date from interviews with 12 researchers representing 12 countries of export indicate that all respondents were required to obtain a cites permit; however, only two acquired nagoya permits despite most of the countries of sample origin having ratified the nagoya protocol. further, experiences with obtaining cites permits were highly variable \u2013 some respondents reported ease in acquiring permits, while others reported significant barriers including specific administrative processes, time delays, and financial challenges. preliminary findings indicate substantial variation in experiences with permitting processes, and through continued analyses we aim to elucidate how these processes may impact the implementation of novel restoration interventions in the caribbean. finally, we aim to synthesize recommendations for stakeholders to facilitate a regulatory environment that facilitates use of novel interventions.",
