          "name": "Ruiqi Li",
          "search_text": "ruiqi li integrative transcriptomic analysis reveals conserved neurogenic responses to heat stress in reef-building corals rapid changes in the marine environment, including rising sea surface temperatures, are reshaping ecosystems worldwide. coral reefs, which rely on a delicate symbiosis between corals and their algal partners (symbiodiniaceae), are particularly vulnerable. elevated temperatures disrupt this relationship, triggering mass bleaching events that threaten one of the ocean\u2019s most critical biodiversity hotspots. while the impacts of coral bleaching have received considerable attention in recent years, the fundamental neurobiological pathways by which corals sense environmental stressors, regulate homeostasis during stress, and manage their symbiotic relationships remain largely understudied. understanding these processes is crucial for addressing the underlying mechanisms driving coral resilience to climate change. \r\nin this study, single-cell and bulk rna-seq datasets were integrated to investigate neurogenic responses to heat stress using stylophora pistillata as a model system. differentially expressed genes (degs) associated with thermal stress were first identified from bulk rna-seq data. to pinpoint genes specifically related to neuronal processes, these degs were cross-referenced with single-cell transcriptomes, focusing on genes highly or uniquely expressed in neuronal cell clusters. this integrative approach revealed several strong candidates, including aplp2, chrna7, glucl, and hvcn, which are implicated in synaptic signaling, neurotransmitter reception, ion transport, and ph regulation. these processes are likely essential for coral stress perception and cellular communication. the expression of these neuro-related genes was further validated using independent rna-seq datasets from acropora cervicornis subjected to comparable heat-stress conditions. orthologs showed consistent expression patterns associated with thermal stress, suggesting conserved neurobiological pathways mediating coral stress responses across species. together, these findings demonstrate that integrating single-cell and bulk transcriptomic data enables fine-scale resolution of stress-associated molecular mechanisms and provides new insights into the cellular basis of coral resilience in a warming ocean. this open-access computational pipeline will also allow researchers to explore their own questions at fine cellular resolution beyond neuronal processes.",
