          "name": "Alexander Waller",
          "search_text": "alexander waller extreme climatic stress reshapes coral communities in a mangrove habitat nearshore reefs at environmental margins, shaped by turbidity, variable water chemistry, and proximity to mangrove habitats, offer natural laboratories for understanding coral responses to future ocean conditions. pioneer bay, orpheus island, is one such multi-stressor system, where corals inhabit chronically turbid, mangrove-influenced waters and experience large diel fluctuations in temperature, ph, oxygen, and salinity. in early 2025, this already variable environment was subjected to an acute, extreme disturbance: record-breaking rainfall (3,116 mm) coinciding with a thermal anomaly.\r\n\r\nusing three consecutive years of reef-crest surveys (2023 to 2025), we quantified how coral abundance, cover, richness, and benthic composition shifted before and after the event. coral abundance and richness peaked in 2024 but collapsed in 2025, with richness declining by up to 82% at some sites. bleaching and mortality affected all major taxa, resulting in the near complete loss of branching corals and significant declines in the health and abundance of massive genera. this loss of stress-sensitive corals led to a significant shift in community structure, accompanied by rapid turf-algal colonisation of newly dead skeletons, with cover increasing from 40% in 2024 to 83% in 2025. \r\n\r\nflood-plume exposure produced salinity as low as 10 ppt, far below natural diel variation for this system, and likely acted synergistically with elevated temperature, turbidity, and sedimentation to precipitate widespread bleaching and mortality. the event also caused 100% mortality of a nearby giant clam population established more than 40 years ago, underscoring its severity and ecological reach.\r\n\r\nthese results demonstrate how acute, superimposed climate extremes in naturally variable, marginal-extreme habitats can drive rapid community reorganisation, offering insights into the limits of coral persistence under future multi-stressor regimes. as global change intensifies, long-term observations from such extreme systems are essential for understanding coral acclimation potential, identifying thresholds of collapse, and anticipating ecosystem trajectories in the anthropocene.",
