          "name": "Bess Ruff",
          "search_text": "bess ruff archetypal trajectories of socioeconomic change in coral reef fisheries coral reef ecosystems face increasing and diversifying impacts from both environmental and anthropogenic pressures, with devastating outcomes for reef-associated fisheries. climate change is driving widespread mortalities across reef-building corals, resulting in a loss of diversity and changes to fish community composition. further, overfishing threatens the function and service provision of coral reef ecosystems as well as the livelihoods and food security of millions of people. these environmental and ecological changes across coral reefs and their fisheries have been intensively evaluated and continue to receive considerable, and imperative, attention across research and management contexts. however, very little consideration has been given to changes across socioeconomic dimensions and their relationship to sustainability and resiliency in coral reef fisheries. elucidating these dynamics is critical to developing a multidimensional understanding of socioecological transformation across coral reef ecosystems and their fisheries. to address this gap, we characterize archetypal trajectories of socioeconomic change across the world\u2019s tropical coral reefs between 2000 and 2020. using spatial and national-level data, we employ a global-scale cluster analysis across eight indicators of distal human pressure on coral reef fisheries: gdp, population density, urbanization, deforestation, agriculture, regulatory quality, marine aquaculture, and marine protected areas. our results identify 6 archetypes of socioeconomic change and determine the relative importance of our indicators in differentiating these archetypes. this work contributes to our broader understanding of coral reefs as complex social-ecological systems and supports the development of theories of change for coral reef fisheries\u2013which can inform innovative, interdisciplinary fisheries management decisions that are applicable across geographies while remaining responsive to local context.",
