          "name": "Brigitte Sommer",
          "search_text": "brigitte sommer understanding and managing species range shifts: first mixed-species outbreak and coral predation by crown-of-thorns starfish (acanthaster brevispinus and a. cf. solaris) on subtropical reefs climate change is forcing scientists and natural resource managers to contend with species range shifts and altered species interactions. these range shifts are difficult to detect and can have unexpected consequences, especially when range expanding predators feed on resident species. on tropical western pacific coral reefs, outbreaks of the coral-eating crown-of-thorns starfish, acanthaster cf. solaris, cause widespread coral loss. here we document the poleward range expansion of its close relative acanthaster brevispinus, often a non-coral-eating species, to the subtropics of eastern australia (solitary islands marine park, 30\u00b0s). in this region, corals grow directly on rock and persist in marginal conditions at their poleward range limits. we recorded the first mixed species outbreak of a. brevispinus and a. cf. solaris (91 and 39 starfish, respectively) where both species preyed on a broad diet of primarily endemic and high-latitude corals. the unusually broad diet of the two predator species compounded the detrimental effects on vulnerable high-latitude coral assemblages, which host many long-lived and slow-growing coral species that will be slow to recover (e.g., paragoniastrea australensis, micromussa lordhowensis, astrea curta). both crown-of-thorns species also preyed on other cnidarian species including zoanthids and corallimorpharians abundant on subtropical reefs. the broad diet of the predatory starfish has potential cascading effects on benthic dynamics and associated biodiversity in this region where tropical, subtropical and temperate species overlap. we show that collaborative efforts across marine resource managers, researchers, dive operators, and citizen scientists facilitated adaptive management that led to the control of the outbreak to protect vulnerable high-latitude coral assemblages. this highlights the success of cross-jurisdictional, cooperative management in adapting to the challenge of species redistribution under climate change. decadal demographic shifts and size\u2010dependent disturbance responses of corals in a subtropical warming hotspot long-term studies are critical to detect and predict ecosystem change, especially in naturally dynamic subtropical regions exposed to episodic severe disturbances amplified by climate change. species respond differently to such disturbances, and interspecific and size-dependent variation in demographic performance can provide valuable insights into the idiosyncratic effects of environmental stress. focusing on subtropical reefs in eastern australia, we examine trends in the size\u2010structure of corals with contrasting life\u2010histories and zoogeographies surrounding the 2016 coral bleaching event (2010\u20132019) to determine their resilience and recovery capacity. we document demographic shifts, with disproportionate declines in the number of small corals and long\u2010term persistence of larger corals. the incidence of bleaching (pocillopora, turbinaria) and partial mortality (acropora, pocillopora) increased with coral size, and bleached corals had greater risk of partial mortality. while endemic pocillopora experienced marked declines, decadal stability of turbinaria despite bleaching, coupled with abundance increase and bleaching resistance in acropora indicate remarkable resilience of these taxa in the subtropics, despite complex multi-stressor regimes. declines in the number of small corals and variable associations with environmental drivers indicate bottlenecks to recovery likely mediated by inhibitory effects of thermal extremes for pocillopora (heat stress) and acropora (both heat and cold stress), and stimulatory effects of food availability in high chlorophyll\u2010a conditions for turbinaria via heterotrophic feeding. disparity in population dynamics suggest that subtropical reefs are ecologically distinct from tropical coral reefs. although our study reveals signs of resilience, it foreshadows the vulnerability of subtropical corals to changing disturbance regimes that include marine heatwaves. notably, disproportionate abundance and bleaching of large corals combined with recruitment limitation of corals at high latitude have the potential to decimate subtropical coral populations under severe heat stress and affect their recovery.",
