          "name": "Christopher Cooney",
          "search_text": "christopher cooney the spatial demography of the coral pocillopora aliciae at its poleward range limit in sydney (34\u02das), australia range shifts and altered dominance patterns of habitat-forming species are changing the seascape structure of temperate marine ecosystems. yet, the role of intraspecific interactions in mediating establishment and growth of newly arriving populations at range-edges remains poorly understood, limiting predictions of persistence in marginal habitats. we examined the spatial demography of the coral pocillopora aliciae at its poleward range limit in sydney, australia (34\u00b0s), quantifying the spatiotemporal dynamics of adults and juveniles over five years to identify demographic mechanisms underpinning coral establishment and local expansion on temperate rocky reefs. structure-from-motion photogrammetry was applied between 2019 and 2024 to quantify changes in density, size structure, and spatial configuration of corals. corals were segmented from large-area images and spatial structure assessed using point pattern analyses with null model comparisons. over five years, p. aliciae expanded its cover from 26 m2 to 73 m2 (180% increase) by forming dense clusters of large corals, with mean coral size increasing more than threefold (57 cm2 to 201 cm2). this growth led to space limitation within clusters and reduced juvenile abundance. spatial associations between adults and juveniles reflected episodic recruitment and subsequent competitive exclusion by larger corals, with fusion likely contributing to cluster consolidation. expansion was driven primarily by the persistence and growth of established corals rather than continuous recruitment. these findings suggest that rapid growth, brooding reproduction, and spatial clustering likely promote p. aliciae establishment on temperate reefs of eastern australia, highlighting the importance of intraspecific interactions in shaping poleward range-edge dynamics. comparative energy acquisition strategies of high-latitude coral species pocillopora aliciae and plesiastrea versipora. energy acquisition is fundamental for corals to meet metabolic demands of survival, growth and reproduction. corals can acquire nutrients from both autotrophic and heterotrophic sources (mixotrophy), an evolutionary advantage enabling them to occupy diverse ecosystems from shallow oligotrophic waters to mesophotic depths. pocillopora aliciae is a small-range subtropical endemic that is expanding at its poleward range limit in coastal waters near sydney. in contrast, the subtropical and temperate coral plesiastrea versipora occurs across australia's east and west coasts, extending from the tropic of capricorn (23 \u00b0s) to tasmania (40 \u00b0s). the range overlap of these species around sydney provides a unique opportunity to compare energy acquisition strategies in two high-latitude coral species with distinct distributions and life-histories. using bulk stable isotope analysis and photosynthetic measurements of coral-associated symbionts, we quantified carbon and nitrogen exchange between hosts and symbionts during summer and winter 2023. changes in symbiont density, chlorophyll content and isotopic signatures revealed that p. versipora maintained strong nutrient exchange (autotrophy) between symbiont and host year-round. in contrast, p. aliciae exhibited elevated heterotrophy in summer and host-symbiont dysbiosis in winter. this demonstrates that the widely distributed p. versipora maintains a stable autotrophic strategy regardless of season, while dysbiosis during winter in p. aliciae suggests this narrow range endemic relies on mixotrophy to persist through the cool, light-limited, conditions when occurring at its southern range limit near sydney. a shift towards mixotrophy during suboptimal conditions may constrain further poleward range expansion and highlights how energy acquisition strategies can influence distributional limits under variable environmental conditions.",
