          "name": "Will Figueira",
          "search_text": "will figueira tracking the fate of corals and coral-dependent fishes through a major marine heatwave coral reefs are experiencing an alarming increase in the frequency and intensity of widespread disturbance events which impact both the habitat forming corals as well as associated assemblages. while our understanding of the drivers and consequences of these events continues to grow, there appears to be considerable variability associated with environmental and geographic context.  one tree reef, located on the southern end of the great barrier reef, experienced a marine heat wave in the austral summer of 2024 which prompted wide-spread coral bleaching. this study used a combination of large-area 3d mapping of permanent sites, underwater visual census surveys and remote underwater video to track the fate of thousands of coral colonies at multiple time points through the event and evaluate the effects of this change in benthic cover on the associated abundance and behaviour (feeding rates and aggression) of corallivore assemblages (chiefly butterflyfish). colony fate tracking indicated overall high levels of mortality across the sites but with taxa-specific state-transition dynamics. butterflyfish assemblages were generally resilient to the effects of the change in benthic cover though changes in abundance and feeding behaviour were most pronounced for the obligate corallivores when compared to the facultative ones. overall, this study provides an extremely robust and high-resolution assessment of the effects of a major marine heat wave on the fate of individual coral colonies and suggests a reasonably high level of resilience of butterfly fishes to these changes, at least over the course of approximately one year from the onset of the event.",
