          "name": "Max Rintoul",
          "search_text": "max rintoul things that go2 bump in the night \u2013 physical drivers of nighttime increases in ph and dissolved oxygen diel cycles in coral reef ph and dissolved oxygen (do) are typically driven by benthic photosynthesis and respiration, producing daytime highs and nighttime lows. however, nighttime increases in ph and do have been reported globally and remain poorly explained, adding uncertainty to coral reef oxygen budgets and metabolism estimates. in this study we evaluated the drivers of these increases by combining field observations from a fringing reef in onna-son, okinawa, japan, with a novel one-dimensional hydrodynamic\u2013biogeochemical model. we show that higher nighttime flow speeds and water depths dilute the effects of benthic respiration, reducing chemical drawdown and producing apparent increases in ph and do. furthermore, we find that coupled hydrodynamic\u2013biogeochemical interactions allow hydrodynamic conditions to be inferred from chemical measurements, and vice versa. these results show that nighttime increases in ph and do can arise without changes in biological activity or external sources of water, and that they encode hydrodynamic information. collectively they improve our ability to interpret chemical time series, and provide new tools to more accurately assess coral reef function under changing environmental conditions.",
