      "search_text": "university of tasmania max rintoul sterling tebbett max rintoul things that go2 bump in the night \u2013 physical drivers of nighttime increases in ph and dissolved oxygen diel cycles in coral reef ph and dissolved oxygen (do) are typically driven by benthic photosynthesis and respiration, producing daytime highs and nighttime lows. however, nighttime increases in ph and do have been reported globally and remain poorly explained, adding uncertainty to coral reef oxygen budgets and metabolism estimates. in this study we evaluated the drivers of these increases by combining field observations from a fringing reef in onna-son, okinawa, japan, with a novel one-dimensional hydrodynamic\u2013biogeochemical model. we show that higher nighttime flow speeds and water depths dilute the effects of benthic respiration, reducing chemical drawdown and producing apparent increases in ph and do. furthermore, we find that coupled hydrodynamic\u2013biogeochemical interactions allow hydrodynamic conditions to be inferred from chemical measurements, and vice versa. these results show that nighttime increases in ph and do can arise without changes in biological activity or external sources of water, and that they encode hydrodynamic information. collectively they improve our ability to interpret chemical time series, and provide new tools to more accurately assess coral reef function under changing environmental conditions. sterling tebbett trajectories of coral reef change, algal turfs, and ecosystem functioning globally, ecosystems are being reconfigured by a range of intensifying human-induced stressors. coral reefs lie at the forefront of this environmental transformation, bringing the need to understand trajectories of reef change and associated links to ecosystem functioning to the fore. while it is widely accepted that one of the key trajectories of reef decline is the replacement of hard corals by algae, emerging evidence has revealed that in the majority of cases this is not manifesting as coral-macroalgae transitions, but rather coral-turf transitions. yet, turfs remain widely overlooked and poorly quantified on reefs, despite now being the single most abundant benthic cover, on average, on coral reefs globally. here i will synthesise my recent research to explore how turfs on coral reefs are inherently linked to coral reef decline, the functioning of coral reef ecosystems, and the capacity of coral reefs to recover from disturbance. in doing so, i will reveal the marked variability in turf composition at the scale of millimeters, how this variability is linked to other reef organisms (juvenile corals and herbivorous fishes), and why this variability matters for understanding reef functioning and reef recovery potential. moreover, i will draw on a recently developed metric, the epilithic algal ratio, to explore the environmental drivers related to variation in turf composition from local to global scales. ultimately, i will highlight the need for broader quantification of variability in turfs if we are to comprehensively understand temporal changes on coral reefs. this need arises as turfs represent a critical ecological interface through which the effects of human-induced stressors may reverberate up through coral reefs, compromising reef resilience and functioning.",
