          "name": "Bronte Connolly",
          "search_text": "bronte connolly nutritional ecology of sydney corals at their range limits under changing environments sydney is a biogeographic transition zone where temperate and subtropical corals overlap, providing a unique natural system for investigating their range shifts in response to environmental changes. two coral species: pocillopora aliciae and plesiastrea versipora, present a range overlap but diverging responses: p. aliciae, that approaches its southern range limit, exhibits metabolic flexibility under short-term warming, while p. versipora, near its northern limit, shows limited physiological adjustment. although p. versipora is a stronger competitor under current temperatures, this advantage disappears under ocean warming scenarios. these shifts in species-specific and community interactions are likely to affect the composition of sydney\u2019s transitional waters, helping forecast future community trajectories and prioritise sites for targeted monitoring. yet, the precise locations and in situ environmental contexts of current coral communities remain unknown, limiting our capacity to predict how these species will shift under changing conditions.\r\n\r\nhere, we combine in situ surveys, citizen-science observations, and environmental datasets to produce the first fine-scale distribution map of these species across sydney\u2019s northern beaches. at each site, coral presence, relative abundance (colonies m\u207b\u00b2), and colony size were quantified and paired with a comprehensive suite of environmental data, including organic and inorganic nutrient content, turbidity, and chlorophyll a content. we assessed benthic community composition and neighbouring organism cover from photo-quadrats to evaluate potential cross-kingdom competitive influences on coral persistence. together, these datasets informed species-specific nutrient-landscape models within a species distribution modelling framework, identifying coral \u201chotspots\u201d and currently unoccupied environmentally suitable habitats.\r\n\r\nthis is the first study in sydney\u2019s biogeographic transition zone to explicitly link coral occurrence with ecological and nutrient measurements. not only the resulting habitat-suitability maps provide practical guidance for evidence-based marine park area planning, but improved understanding of species resilience at thermal margins, offering new insights into how nutritional ecology shapes coral range dynamics in a warming ocean.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Nutritional ecology of Sydney corals at their range limits under changing environments",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "738c3a32-41c5-4377-8449-23429ef01ac9"
            }
          ]
