          "name": "Hannah Kish",
          "search_text": "hannah kish just keep swimming: how coral larvae change their vertical position in response to flow and chemical cues coral larval dispersal is a fundamental mechanism of a functioning reef ecosystem as it shapes genetics, population dynamics and recovery after disturbances. although modern dispersal models include fine scale hydrodynamics and comprehensive water quality layers, they struggle to replicate observed successional patterns. one likely explanation is coral larvae are treated as passive particles due to poor horizontal swimming capacity, yet vertical positioning is most influential for dispersal trajectories.  larvae remain near the surface of the water column past the normal point of when models simulate settlement. crustose coralline algae (cca) is assumed to attract larvae due to its role in settlement and metamorphosis. however, its impacts on larval movement are not well documented. despite the implications, larval behaviour and drivers behind behavioural change is poorly understood. therefore, we aim to demonstrate the swimming behaviours of coral larvae to chemical cues (chemotaxis) from cca via individual larval ciliary flow dynamics and how flow impacts any chemotactic response. we first assessed larvae chemotaxis through particle imagery velocimetry and in-situ chemical assays to test the following hypotheses: 1.) larvae adjust cilia to swimming and searching behaviour when exposed to chemotactic settlement cues and 2.) larvae alter swimming trajectories to swim towards settlement cues. we then used stereo-imagery to test if larvae adjust their vertical position in response to flow and chemotactic settlement cues. we found larvae increase swimming and searching behaviour at the cilia level and actively swim towards settlement cues. additionally, larvae move away from high flow environments and towards chemical cues. these results showcase the chemotactic strategies of coral larvae and suggest active participation in dispersal. integrating active swimming behaviours and sensing capacities into dispersal models would significantly improve model performance and thus the predictive capacity of reproductive strategies in reef ecology.",
