          "search_text": "sanna eriksson high-throughput phenotyping of two key symbiodiniaceae species under variable n:p ratios the symbiodiniaceae family contains unicellular dinoflagellates which commonly form symbioses with reef-building corals, sharing photosynthetic products to their coral host, and receiving inorganic nutrients (e.g., nitrogen and phosphorus) in return. nutrient cycling within the symbiosis allows corals to thrive in oligotrophic tropical oceans, and breakdown of this nutrient exchange can lead to coral bleaching and death. there is evidence that different symbiodiniaceae species have unique nutritional requirements and therefore unique biogeochemical niches, which could have significant impacts on coral symbioses. however, we don\u2019t yet have a comprehensive understanding of the full range of variation in nutritional requirements across symbiodiniaceae species. the current lack of information in this area is partly because symbiodiniaceae laboratory experiments generally use flask-based culturing methods, which are relatively time consuming and therefore limit the number of nutrient environments to explore. when considering the impact of different nutrient environments, both the absolute amount of each element (e.g., nitrogen and phosphorus) and the stoichiometry of the elements (e.g., n:p) have significant impacts on microalgae functioning. here we present data on a developed high-throughput phenotyping platform for symbiodiniaceae using 96-well plates. our approach allows different concentrations and stoichiometries of nutrients to be created, and use of spectrophotometry and fluorometry to assess cell physiology in a high-throughput fashion, over a two-week growth period. we apply this high-throughput phenotyping approach to assess the effects of different nitrogen (nitrate and ammonium) and phosphorus (phosphate) concentrations on the performance of two common symbiodiniaceae species (cladocopium proliferum and durusdinium trenchii), under both ambient and heat stress conditions. our results provide new insights into the fundamental nitrogen and phosphorus requirements of these two competing symbiodiniaceae species and furthers our understanding of their optimal biogeochemical niche.",
