          "name": "Wyatt Million",
          "search_text": "wyatt million aerobic physiological plasticity to marine deoxygenation; evidence for resilience or susceptibility in coral? deoxygenation, or the loss of biologically available oxygen, is a pervasive environmental stressor on coral reefs that can arise as acute, near-complete oxygen loss (hypoxia/anoxia), moderate multi-day low-oxygen events, as well as the decadal downward shift in oxygen availability associated with climate change. while extreme hypoxia events can lead to immediate bleaching and mortality in tropical reef-building corals, accumulation of sublethal effects during less extreme, prolonged deoxygenation can arise through hindrance of aerobic energy production, which is predicted to limit coral stress response, growth, and reproduction in future oceans. \r\nto address gaps in knowledge around prolonged deoxygenation effects, we exposed corals with differing aerobic respiration maintenance (i.e., oxyregulation) strategies to long-term sublethal deoxygenation to uncover potential consequences, tradeoffs, and/or acclimatization under hypoxia stress. specifically, pocillopora damicornis (strong oxyregulation) and hydnophora rigida (weak oxyregulation) were exposed to nighttime deoxygenation (4.57 or 2.61 mg o2/l) or constant normoxia (6.53 mg o2/l) for five weeks in controlled experimental aquaria. aerobic thresholds, heat stress tolerance, and cilia behavior evaluated post-exposure reveal fixed and plastic intraspecific variation associated with transcriptomic changes that suggest acclimatization to stress. \r\nthe capacity for oxyregulation offers physiological strategies to maintain energy production in the short-term but is hypothesized to accrue tradeoffs in other pathways if oxygen stress persists. here, shifts toward reduced oxyregulation highlight the potential adaptive benefits of metabolic plasticity to avoid tradeoffs under deoxygenation. exploration of the outcomes of long-term sublethal deoxygenation on coral reefs will be necessary given this form of low oxygen stress is forecasted to be a perennial factor on future reefs. together, the multivariate phenotypic responses described here help characterize the resilience and susceptibility of coral to sublethal deoxygenation.",
