          "name": "Daniela Gutierrez Andrade",
          "search_text": "daniela gutierrez andrade role of conserved and rapidly evolving gene families in sctld susceptibility and resistance across species variation in coral host resistance and susceptibility to stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) has been observed at the individual and species levels, yet the biological mechanisms underlying these processes are poorly understood. here, we examine the relationship between gene expression variance and evolutionary history to evaluate how expression of conserved genes and rapidly evolving gene families impacts the response to sctld across species. in april 2022, samples from six coral species with varying susceptibility to sctld were collected from reefs in the united states virgin islands (usvi). tissue was sampled from apparently healthy colonies (hh) and from diseased colonies, both adjacent to active lesion sites (dd) and from areas of apparently healthy tissue (hd).  to characterize shared responses across species, we identified single-copy orthologs and conducted differential expression analysis. across the three health states, 74 differentially expressed orthologs (deogs) were identified. conserved genes involved in various processes were downregulated in dd tissue, including deogs involved in immune responses to external stimuli. to identify lineage-specific and highly variable responses within conserved genes, we applied the expression variance and evolution (eve) model. we identified 48 significant lineage-specific genes that represent potentially evolved differences in susceptibility to sctld. these were enriched in intracellular transport, cellular localization, cellular organization, and transcription factor and protein binding processes. the model also identified 39 highly variable genes that are responsive to sctld and involved in the innate immune response and translation initiation complex. to complement the eve analysis, we identified rapidly evolving gene families using the computational analysis of gene family evolution (cafe5) model to determine if investment in the expansion or contraction of these gene families impacts susceptibility to sctld. understanding the mechanisms underlying susceptibility will improve our ability to respond to future epidemics and tailor our strategies to the differing needs of each species.",
