          "name": "Hannah Swain",
          "search_text": "hannah swain molecular and ecological drivers of species-specific coral disease responses in a multi-species outbreak located in the gulf of mexico, the flower garden banks national marine sanctuary (fgbnms) is an isolated mid-depth to mesophotic reef system with historically low disease prevalence. however, in 2022 a multi-species acute white syndrome outbreak occurred, and in 2023 coral samples were collected from three scleractinian corals\u2013porites astreoides, orbicella franksi, and pseudodiploria strigosa\u2013across three health states: tissue from an apparently healthy colony (hh), tissue from the healthy side of a diseased colony (hd), and tissue from the disease lesion (dd). differentially expressed genes were identified for each species, and patterns closely mirrored their susceptibility to disease: p. astreoides showed minimal transcriptional change, p. strigosa exhibited moderate changes, and o. franksi had the highest number of degs. the expression variance and evolution (eve) model was used to distinguish genes that represent disease responses from those with lineage-specific expression differences that contribute to species-level susceptibility. across species, diseased tissues were characterized by inflammation, metabolic collapse, and hypoxic stress. these lineage-specific baseline differences help clarify why species vary in susceptibility: low-susceptibility corals (p. astreoides) upregulated early immune mechanisms, including signaling kinases and ubiquitin ligases, while high-susceptibility corals (p. strigosa and o. franksi) downregulated these same genes, indicating a failure to activate early protective responses. high-susceptibility species also upregulated stress and damage pathways\u2013such as apoptosis, rna editing, and protein trafficking\u2013whereas low-susceptibility species downregulated these pathways. to integrate molecular patterns with ecological context, large-area reef imaging will be used to quantify physiological and disease-ecology metrics (e.g., colony size, extent of tissue loss, and neighborhood disease prevalence), enabling correlations between gene expression and spatial disease dynamics. this novel integrated molecular\u2013ecological framework provides new insight into species-level disease trade-offs and supports improved disease monitoring and management.",
