          "name": "Laura Mydlarz",
          "search_text": "laura mydlarz machine learning for coral disease gene expression: highlights, headaches, and the path forward coral disease outbreaks are increasing worldwide, yet the molecular processes driving susceptibility, resilience, and recovery remain poorly understood. gene expression studies generate large, multidimensional datasets on coral responses to stress and pathogens, but these datasets are often noisy and hard to interpret without appropriate analytical tools. machine learning can help resolve this noise and reveal biologically meaningful patterns. we have applied several machine learning approaches to coral disease datasets with three main goals: 1) reduce noise to identify signals contributing to coral disease resistance; 2) integrate multilayered datasets, including multi-omics and clinical data; and 3) identify host gene biomarkers that distinguish diseases with similar phenotypes or health states. using models such as support vector machine recursive feature elimination (svm-rfe), we reduced noise and identified signatures of dysbiosis, and metabolic and immune shifts associated with stony coral tissue loss disease. partial least squares discriminant analysis (pls-da) was used to build a predictive model that differentiates white plague from sctld, showing that host gene expression profiles can reliably discriminate between these diseases. multi-omics integration further connects symbiont and host expression with microbiome data to reveal coordinated networks underlying phenotypes such as disease resistance. while these approaches have yielded strong results, the challenges and bottlenecks to full implementation will also be discussed. advancing these methods will allow the development of models that leverage existing and new datasets in innovative ways to help bridge the gap between molecular biology and emerging technologies for coral management. comparative transcriptomic signatures of caribbean coral diseases: insights into white plague and stony coral tissue loss disease white plague (wp) and stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) are two detrimental diseases in the caribbean that affect many of the main reef-building corals. wp has produced sporadic outbreaks for about 40 years, while sctld emerged in florida in 2014 and rapidly spread through the caribbean. because the diseases can co-occur and share similar phenotypes, understanding their molecular similarities and differences may improve early diagnosis and differentiation. since 2017, the mydlarz lab has generated numerous datasets from corals with wp and sctld from the usvi and the flower garden banks, including two parallel transmission experiments across multiple coral species and field-collected disease samples. synthesis of these datasets using comparative transcriptomics and machine-learning approaches reveals important similarities and differences in host and symbiont responses. wp and sctld share core molecular responses, including induction of immune, signaling, and extracellular matrix\u2013related (ecm) genes, and reliance on intercellular protein trafficking that underpins species-level resistance. in both diseases, the autophagy\u2013apoptosis axis is a key determinant of susceptibility, with exposed but uninfected corals upregulating autophagy and susceptible species shifting toward apoptosis. the major differences reflect disease severity. wp triggers hypoxic stress, inflammation, and ecm-related changes, but with comparatively moderate immune and metabolic disruption, consistent with a more generalized immune response. in contrast, sctld shows dysbiosis between host and algal symbiont, metabolic collapse, and intense immune activation, along with pronounced symbiophagy driven by photosystem disruption. these qualitative and quantitative comparisons will be presented in this poster in the context of identifying targets for field assay development to support disease identification and treatment.",
