          "name": "Alexa Huzar",
          "search_text": "alexa huzar investigating the effects of nutrient pollution and turbidity on the healthy reefs of tela bay, honduras tela bay, honduras is home to coral reefs that are thriving with preliminary research suggesting that coral cover is between 48-62%. this is despite the fact that the bay has been exposed to decades of sedimentation and nutrient pollution from the ul\u00faa river, the most polluted river in honduras due to the lack of wastewater treatment. these factors create a highly turbid and nutrient rich environment which is usually detrimental to corals. however, during an exploratory trip to the bay, we observed healthy corals across the bay with many of the healthy corals being partially covered by a thick biofilm. additionally, recent studies have shown that turbidity can partially protect corals from bleaching. these studies along with our observations led us to hypothesize that rather than harming the corals, the turbid waters may reduce light stress along with providing nutrients for beneficial microorganisms. to investigate this hypothesis, we collected sediment and biofilm from across the bay as well as sediment from roatan, honduras, a nearby island that experiences less turbidity and sedimentation. whole genome libraries were prepared to classify the metagenomic communities associated with the different environments. the field data will be bolstered by satellite derived data to measure turbidity using the diffuse attenuation coefficient kd(490). this will provide quantitative data to the impacts of pollution on the bay as well as provide insights if there are potential beneficial microorganisms present. additionally, it marks one of the first genomic studies in this bay and starts the work of characterizing this environment. these reefs have been largely unstudied until recently making it vital to understand these marginal yet healthy reefs to potentially uncover new ways to bolster declining reef systems. population genomics of symbiodiniaceae across spatial scales the symbiosis between corals and algae from the symbiodiniaceae family is essential for coral survival, health, and thermal tolerance. many previous studies have documented how symbiont communities vary spatially and by host as well as how different communities confer different benefits. however, nearly all studies rely on identification utilizing only the its2 gene. this has led to a lack of resolution to the clonal and sometimes even the species level with many results being complicated symbiodiniaceae variant profiles. currently, many corals are thought to be dominated by a single symbiont species but clonal level identification could reveal previously unrecognized diversity. to enable efficient clonal level genotyping of symbionts, we designed a hybridization capture probe set targeting 850 cladocopium snps, based on the previously published whole genome sequencing data for acropora millepora from the central great barrier reef. we chose non-coding snps that were the most diagnostic of the cladocopium genetic structure observed among these samples. we also supplemented the set with 150 probes targeting durusdinium snps. the hybridization capture libraries prepared from whole holobiont dna extracts contained mostly the targeted symbiont genomic regions and required very few reads to achieve good depth of coverage. we then applied our probe set to investigate symbiont clonal distribution and population structure across spatial scales and host species. for this study, we sampled a.millepora in replicated, closely clustered groups (\u201cpatches\u201d) of eight across three reefs near orpheus island, australia, plus one patch-of-eight of another coral species, a. hyacinthus, from one of these reefs. including an additional species allows us to investigate the possible genetic break across coral host species. we report symbionts\u2019 genetic structure and clones\u2019 distribution across three spatial scales: meters (within a patch-of-eight), tens of meters (between patches-of-eight at the same reef site), and hundreds of meters (between reef sites).",
