          "name": "Aranya Dhibar",
          "search_text": "aranya dhibar deep-time population and microbiome dynamics of acropora palmata revealed by hybrid-capture ancient dna3a the critically endangered caribbean coral, acropora palmata, has experienced severe declines due to global change and anthropogenic stressors, yet its long-term evolutionary responses to environmental change remain poorly understood. while population genomics of present-day samples has revealed contemporary population structure, ancient population level patterns and host\u2013microbiome dynamics are largely unexplored because of challenges in recovering endogenous ancient dna (adna) from reef cores. these challenges include poor preservation of dead coral tissues in dynamic marine settings and overwhelming co-extracted microbial dna that often obscure low abundance host sequences. \r\nto overcome these barriers, we utilized hybridization capture sequencing targeting approximately 5,000 loci previously shown to resolve population structure in modern a. palmata - thus enabling enrichment of host nuclear adna while retaining important genomic information. we studied ancient coral fragments from florida and us virgin islands reef cores, spanning two-time intervals bracketing a \u201creef gap\u201d dated to ~3300\u20132900 years before present. by comparing the ancient and extant coral population structure, we showed the shifts in a. palmata population structure through time (before and after the time gap) happened differently in these two different regions. additionally, we leveraged the abundant non host reads to study the ancient coral associated microbiomes and showed that geographic location, rather than core age, is the primary driver of microbiome diversity across samples, although specific reef sites exhibited notable temporal changes in microbial composition. together, this study demonstrates a scalable capture-based approach for retrieving coral adna from reef cores, establishes a foundation for future palaeogenomic work in corals, and illuminates how a. palmata and its microbiomes had responded to past environmental variability, a knowledge that may inform conservation effort under ongoing climate change.",
