          "name": "Gildas Todinanahary",
          "search_text": "gildas todinanahary biodiversity seeding with arms enhances reef recovery and community well-being in southwest madagascar coral reef degradation in the semi-enclosed bay-of-ranobe, southwest madagascar threatens both ecological functioning and the well-being of coastal communities. to accelerate recovery and strengthen local resilience, we developed an integrated restoration approach that combines limestone-aggregation artificial reefs with biodiversity seeding using autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms). in this system, arms serve a dual function: documenting cryptic biodiversity and early colonisation processes across habitats, and transferring these assemblages onto newly built artificial reef modules to stimulate ecological succession. arms units rapidly accumulated diverse cryptic communities, and when translocated to artificial reefs, they enhanced fine-scale structural complexity and facilitated the early establishment of key benthic functional groups. ecological analyses from 33 sites revealed faster development of microhabitats in seeded modules, while in situ observations indicated favorable shifts toward crustose coralline algae and increased herbivore visitation\u2013conditions that support coral settlement and long-term ecosystem regeneration. a strong participatory component underpinned this work. through a series of over 15 community workshops and 5 governance agreements, fishers co-designed restoration sites, contributed to reef construction, and directly participated in ecological, fishery and human health monitoring, along 14 villages. this co-design structure strengthened stewardship, reinforced ecological literacy, and linked ecological recovery with local aspirations for food security, livelihoods, and well-being. building on these results, the restoration program is now expanding into a multi-trophic, multi-habitat framework. ongoing efforts integrate coral larval enhancement, herbivore restocking, and the introduction of ecosystem engineers such as sea urchins and sea cucumbers to accelerate functional recovery. additional innovations include community-built coral nurseries and \u201coyster arks\u201d as new microhabitat-forming reef units. parallel governance strengthening aims to translate these ecological gains into long-term, community-led management. by merging biodiversity seeding, ecological engineering, and participatory governance, this model offers a scalable pathway for climate-resilient coral reef restoration and improved socio-ecological well-being across madagascar and the western indian ocean.",
