      "search_text": "university of victoria alexander van nynatten julia baum kristina tietjen mareike de breuyn alexander van nynatten marine heatwaves transform coral symbioses with enduring effects climate change-amplified extreme weather events are reshaping ecological communities globally. on coral reefs, heatwaves typically disrupt the obligate coral\u2013algal symbiosis, with symbiont identity a prime determinant of coral resilience to these thermal extremes. yet, whether heatwaves have long-term effects on coral symbioses remains unclear due to a lack of longitudinal symbiont data. \r\nhere, we report on a decade-long symbiont survey from kiritimati atoll (2013\u20132023), spanning the most prolonged tropical marine heatwave on record (2015\u20132016) and its aftermath. using its2 dna metabarcoding data from 856 samples belonging to 551 coral colonies and benthic surveys from 37 forereef sites along a gradient of chronic local human disturbance we document wholesale transformation of the symbiont assemblages in two coral species, montipora aequituberculata and pocillopora grandis, the legacy of which was persistent for seven years post heatwave. the recovery of coral cover and pre-heatwave symbioses was negligible in both species for at least three years after the heatwave (2017-2019) and by 7.5 years after the heatwave (2023) was only observed in p. grandis and not m. aequituberculata, a species driven close to local extinction by the marine heatwave. we also observe significant changes to the incidence and abundance of the heat tolerant generalist symbiont taxa after the marine heatwave, and in p. grandis, higher durusdinium abundance associated with colonies that survived the heatwave and colonies at sites exposed to high human disturbance. \r\nour study demonstrates that marine heatwaves can have long-lasting impacts on symbioses, making the recovery of symbiont communities in some corals increasingly unlikely due to the protracted duration of the recovery of pre-heatwave symbionts, the potential for local extinctions of host-adapted symbiont species, and the additional delays in recovery associated with chronic stressors, raising concern for coral resilience to future heatwaves. julia baum coral resilience to climate change and our collective responsibility to advance solutions kristina tietjen coral community in recovery on a remote atoll following a mass mortality event isolated coral reefs are predicted to recover slowly due to a reduced supply of coral larvae from neighbouring islands. the increased rate and extent of marine heatwaves will likely exacerbate this problem as neighbouring islands more frequently experience similar impacts and recovery periods shrink. the remote coral atoll of kiritimati (republic of kiribati), and all nearby islands surveyed, experienced overall coral mortality exceeding 89% following the 2015-2016 el ni\u0144o induced marine heatwave that peaked at 27 degree heating weeks on kiritimati. we tracked coral recovery at kiritimati over 7 years (2017, 2023) post-mass mortality using 1469 benthic photoquadrats from 26 sites around the atoll, collected as part of the long-term monitoring program that began in 2007. in september 2023, about 7.5 years after the end of the heatwave, there was a significant increase (342%; 2017: 3.48 \u00b1 0.7 sd, 2023: 15.41 \u00b1 7.05 sd) in hard coral cover across the island. however, coral recovery remains incomplete and is 32.5% below 2007 coral cover (22.8 \u00b1 9.33 sd). the chronic local human disturbance gradient present on kiritimati\u2019s reefs significantly impacts hard coral recovery, with medium exposure sites demonstrating the highest recovery (422.05%), followed by sites with the lowest exposure (357.15%). sites at the highest exposure had the lowest recovery (119%). of the two corals that had 100% loss during the heatwave, corymbose acropora has significantly recovered (1380.2%) across the island, exceeding the pre-heatwave cover (152%). in contrast, foliose montipora has had no detectable recovery (0%). porites spp. and platygyra spp. were both winners during the heatwave, but in recovery, only porites has had an increase in cover. this study reveals that recovery is possible on remote reefs that have experienced mass coral mortalities, but local disturbance and coral genus both have an impact on the rate and extent of these processes. mareike de breuyn low symbiodiniaceae diversity across acropora hosts on the central pacific atoll kiritimati a decade after the third global mass-bleaching event, coral-reef recovery trajectories remain heterogeneous: while some reefs remain degraded, others have regained coral cover but often exhibit taxonomically simplified host and symbiont assemblages. environmental gradients can further modulate these recovery trajectories by promoting unique coral assemblages and symbiont associations, even among closely related corals. on kiritimati (republic of kiribati), coral cover declined by 89% on the atoll\u2019s shallow reefs during the event, driving a major ecological shift in coral and symbiont assemblages across a strong gradient of human disturbance. identifying how certain abiotic factors (wave exposure, human disturbance) and biotic variation in symbiodiniaceae community composition collectively modify post-disturbance recovery trajectories is key to understanding why certain coral species dominate recovering ecosystems. we investigated the spatial distributions of two acropora species with differing growth forms (tabulate and corymbose) across 37 sites in 2023 spanning a human-impact gradient and using its2 dna metabarcoding on 109 co-collected tissue samples from 13 sites, to characterize host\u2013symbiont relationships between symbiodiniaceae communities and their hosts. acropora dominance shifted predictably with disturbance: tabulate corals dominated sites exposed to low levels of pollution, coastal development, and fishing pressure, whereas corymbose corals dominated sites where these pressures were high. although symbiont communities partially overlapped, each species hosted distinct its2 lineages identified by indicator-species analyses. unifrac dissimilarity confirmed significant host-specific structuring of symbionts and revealed differences across the human-impact gradient. these results suggest that the dominance of tabulate acropora on previously pristine reefs one decade after mass coral mortality is shaped by interactions between species-specific traits and local disturbance regimes. the ecologically restricted recovery of both species, and their growth-form-specific symbionts, may reduce resilience to future stressors. these findings show that human impact and symbiont-community structure jointly predict which acropora species and holobiont structures are likely to dominate future reefs.",
