          "name": "Natalie Rosser",
          "search_text": "natalie rosser timing is everything: reproductive phenology as a driver of coral biodiversity and allochronic speciation understanding the ecological and evolutionary processes that generate coral biodiversity is essential for predicting reef resilience in a rapidly changing ocean. while geographic isolation has traditionally been viewed as the primary mechanism driving allopatric speciation, emerging evidence from western australia highlights the important role of reproductive phenology in promoting allochronic speciation. many western australian reefs display both autumn and spring spawning periods, yet the extent to which local environmental conditions shape these patterns remains underexplored. here we show that fine-scale environmental gradients can produce markedly different reproductive outcomes at adjacent sites. ningaloo reef is a high-energy fringing reef exposed to indian ocean swell and persistent south-westerly winds, whereas nearby bundegi reef in exmouth gulf is a sheltered and turbid embayment protected by the north-west cape. despite their proximity, coral reproductive phenology differed sharply in spring. at bundegi, 31% of species exhibited mature gametes, compared with only 5% at wave-exposed ningaloo, while autumn spawning remained similarly high across both sites. these contrasting patterns reflect clear differences in hydrodynamic conditions. low wave energy and wind-driven turbulence at bundegi likely enhance spring fertilisation, whereas strong winds and higher wave energy at ningaloo appear to suppress it. this case study demonstrates that local environmental conditions can generate pronounced phenological divergence over small spatial scales. across western australia, differences in spawning time are increasingly linked to genetic structuring and incipient speciation. sympatric autumn- and spring-spawning cohorts of several acropora species show strong genomic divergence, revealing that allochronic speciation is an underappreciated contributor to cryptic and overall reef biodiversity. recognising and characterising these phenological patterns is essential for accurate biodiversity assessments, interpreting population connectivity, and guiding restoration efforts. integrating reproductive phenology into ecological studies and conservation planning will improve detection of incipient species, preserve adaptive potential, and ensure management strategies reflect true indo-pacific coral diversity.",
