          "name": "Sean Bensadon",
          "search_text": "sean bensadon resolving reef-scale flows and local dispersal reveals connectivity pathways critical for coral resilience and actionable management applications coral reef resilience and recovery depends critically on connectivity, the process of larval transport and settlement between coral populations. in nearshore reef systems, larval dispersal is governed by fine-scale hydrodynamic flows interacting with reef morphological structures, yet most connectivity models lack the spatial resolution needed to resolve these reef-scale processes, limiting their utility for local-scale management. here, we develop a high-resolution (down to 25 m) biophysical model that couples wave and circulation dynamics to investigate local coral connectivity at management-relevant scales in coral bay, a wave-driven fringing reef system within ningaloo reef, northwestern australia. simulations spanning 30 years of mass coral spawning events from 1993 to 2022, revealed dominant patterns of local larval exchange and long-term interannual variability in connectivity. we find that local dispersal within the reef-lagoon was shaped by wave-driven circulation and reef morphology, producing persistent northward larval migrations and a robust network resilient to simulated coral mortality. broad connectivity patterns identified southern and central reef regions as important larval sources for seascape-wide connectivity, while network analysis at the reef-patch scale highlighted vulnerable reefs with limited external larval supply and the key reefs which contribute the most to network persistence. although coral bay includes two sanctuary zones, we find several important larval sources and vulnerable reef patches lie outside their boundaries, indicating areas for potential zone expansion or enhanced protection. by resolving reef-scale larval dispersal across variable oceanographic conditions, this study aligns connectivity estimates with practical local management to guide both broad- and targeted patch-scale conservation strategies.",
