          "name": "Stephen Swearer",
          "search_text": "stephen swearer a global synthesis of lunar spawning rhythms in fishes lunar cycles have long been linked to the timing of reproductive events in fishes, yet the strength, consistency and mechanisms of these patterns remain unclear across taxa and habitats. we conducted a systematic review of published studies examining lunar-related spawning behaviours in marine and freshwater fishes. across 249 species from 390 studies, three broad patterns emerged. first, lunar synchrony is the most widespread rhythm among fishes. while the timing of spawning peaks is quite variable, patterns are moderately conserved within broad taxonomic groups. second, the drivers of this timing differ among species, with evidence for higher prevalence of semi-lunar periodicity in species and locations with greater tidal amplitudes, and greater prevalence of lunar periodicity is species that aggregate to spawn. third, the strength of lunar periodicity within species is associated with a range of other egg and larval traits, indicative of different reproductive strategies among fishes. overall, the review highlights that lunar influences on spawning are context-dependent rather than universal, and that future work must integrate behavioural, environmental and physiological data to further resolve underlying mechanisms. these insights refine our understanding of reproductive timing in fishes and inform management strategies for species whose spawning aggregations coincide with predictable lunar windows.",
