          "name": "Mary Rose Makoski",
          "search_text": "mary rose makoski the real cost of marine eco-tourism in the caribbean the tourism industry is central to the caribbean economy, with reef-based activities like scuba diving playing a significant role. climate change poses serious threats to marine ecosystems, prompting a shift toward marine eco-tourism, which is a business model that seeks to integrate sustainability into tourism operations. while the general concept of eco-tourism has been studied since the 1960s, marine eco-tourism, particularly in the caribbean, remains underexplored despite its economic importance. this study presents a comprehensive analysis of the environmental, economic, and social impacts of marine eco-tourism in the caribbean, with a special focus on the scuba diving industry through the green fins membership. the study employed a mixed-methods approach, combining a literature review and conducting interviews with dive shops that were green fins members in mexico, honduras, bonaire, and the dominican republic. results revealed both positive and negative impacts of marine eco-tourism and the green fins membership on the sustainability dimensions: environmental, economic, and social. positives of marine eco-tourism included the development of marine protected areas, education and awareness of marine ecology, citizen science, implementation of a sustainable business model, employment opportunities, preservation of local heritage, and the overall improvement of local livelihood. negatives of marine eco-tourism included the degradation of marine ecosystems, tourism carbon footprint, economic vulnerability of the tourism industry, economic leakage, cultural commodification, and the exploitation of workers caused by overtourism. green fins is a great business tool that helps dive shops stay accountable to their sustainable goals. however, green fins has a gap in its sustainability model, focusing primarily on environmental impacts and not incorporating the economic and social dimensions of sustainability. overall, marine eco-tourism requires a holistic approach that balances environmental protection, economic resilience, and environmental justice and that fosters greater collaboration among dive shops, tourism operators, local communities, and government agencies.",
