          "name": "Sophia Pinter",
          "search_text": "sophia pinter reconstructing the timing and rates of relative sea level change during termination ii from the hawaiian drowned reefs integrated ocean discovery program (iodp) expedition 389 recovered cores from submerged reef terraces fringing hawai\u2019i to study the history of sea level, climate, and reef response.  hawai\u2019i is rapidly subsiding due to volcanic loading that induces flexure of the lithosphere.  unlike stable or uplifting coastlines, rapid subsidence creates expanded vertical sequences of reef accretion that offer an opportunity for high resolution reconstructions of sea level, climate, and reef response.  rapid sea-level rise during glacial terminations is thought to have drowned these reefs, creating a succession of down-stepping, submerged terraces spanning multiple glacial cycles.  the timing of reef drowning events in hawai\u2019i can therefore be used to pinpoint intervals of rapid sea-level rise when rates of relative sea-level rise outpaced the rate of reef accretion.  \r\n\r\nhere we focus on site m0104, drilled at 347 m below sea level on the h2 terrace off the coast of hilo.  this core extends to a depth of ~46 meters below sea floor and contains a variety of carbonate facies including coralgal boundstone, biodetrital grainstone, algal boundstone, coralgal microbial boundstone, microbial-algal boundstone, and rudstone.  seventy-five u-th dates generated for corals with < 2 % calcite from this core indicate that it spans the marine isotope stage (mis) 6 to 5 transition, also known as termination ii, an interval where few absolutely dated sea-level records exist.  this chronology reveals notable transitions in carbonate facies and in coral assemblages and/or morphology that coincide with changes in the rate of reef accretion. additionally, we have identified two distinct, rapid episodes of reef accretion that likely represent two meltwater pulses (mwp-2a and -2b) that occurred during the glacial termination.",
