          "name": "Anne-Sophie Palermo",
          "search_text": "anne-sophie palermo optimising coral-based ocean temperature records: a case study from the great barrier reef submitter has requested this abstract be considered for a poster presentation only. \r\n\r\nthe unesco world heritage listed great barrier reef (gbr) is threatened by mass coral bleaching in response to rising sea surface temperatures (sst). historical baseline estimates of gbr ssts provide critical context for predicting coral resilience. however, prior to satellite estimates, the instrumental sst record is limited in spatial and temporal coverage. coral skeletal geochemistry is a powerful alternative tool for reconstructing sst patterns revealing long-term climate sensitivity and the responses of long-lived corals to environmental stressors. this study evaluates whether recent advances in coral geochemical methods can reliably capture sst anomalies, while also accounting for environmental, biological, and analytical uncertainties. we analyse multi-decadal records from four coral cores collected from myrmidon reef, a remote offshore reef location. ssts will be reconstructed at sub-seasonal resolution and using multiple sst proxies, to assess precision, accuracy and reproducibility. the coral sst records will be benchmarked against in-water co-located instrumental temperature logger data and satellite sst records (ersst, noaa coral reef watch and himawari). our study will include an evaluation and adaptation of the universal multi-trace element calibration framework (umtecs; d\u2019olivo et al., 2018) in combination with unbiased calibration strategies (e.g., mudelsee, 2024). by refining calibration strategies and incorporating robust statistical techniques, this work aims to improve the accuracy and confidence level in coral-based paleoclimate reconstructions and provide a foundation for future gbr coral proxy studies. ultimately, advancing these methods and expanding our coral records will enhance our understanding of historical climate variability, improve the evaluation of coral resilience and inform conservation strategies critical for the long-term health of the gbr.",
