      "search_text": "university of wollongong anne-sophie palermo ariella arzey helen mcgregor hugo fahey anne-sophie palermo optimising coral-based ocean temperature records: a case study from the great barrier reef submitter has requested this abstract be considered for a poster presentation only. \r\n\r\nthe unesco world heritage listed great barrier reef (gbr) is threatened by mass coral bleaching in response to rising sea surface temperatures (sst). historical baseline estimates of gbr ssts provide critical context for predicting coral resilience. however, prior to satellite estimates, the instrumental sst record is limited in spatial and temporal coverage. coral skeletal geochemistry is a powerful alternative tool for reconstructing sst patterns revealing long-term climate sensitivity and the responses of long-lived corals to environmental stressors. this study evaluates whether recent advances in coral geochemical methods can reliably capture sst anomalies, while also accounting for environmental, biological, and analytical uncertainties. we analyse multi-decadal records from four coral cores collected from myrmidon reef, a remote offshore reef location. ssts will be reconstructed at sub-seasonal resolution and using multiple sst proxies, to assess precision, accuracy and reproducibility. the coral sst records will be benchmarked against in-water co-located instrumental temperature logger data and satellite sst records (ersst, noaa coral reef watch and himawari). our study will include an evaluation and adaptation of the universal multi-trace element calibration framework (umtecs; d\u2019olivo et al., 2018) in combination with unbiased calibration strategies (e.g., mudelsee, 2024). by refining calibration strategies and incorporating robust statistical techniques, this work aims to improve the accuracy and confidence level in coral-based paleoclimate reconstructions and provide a foundation for future gbr coral proxy studies. ultimately, advancing these methods and expanding our coral records will enhance our understanding of historical climate variability, improve the evaluation of coral resilience and inform conservation strategies critical for the long-term health of the gbr. ariella arzey regional differences in sea surface temperature across the 20th century on the great barrier reef submitter has requested that this abstract be considered for a poster presentation only. \r\n\r\nwarming sea surface temperatures (sst) have resulted in variable coral bleaching severity across the great barrier reef (gbr). to better understand the reef response, it is essential to examine sst trends at regional or finer spatial scales. this study compiles 21 high-resolution coral sr/ca records, an established proxy for sst, to assess spatial differences in sst between the northern, central, and southern gbr. \r\n\r\nall regions exhibit the 20th century global warming trend, but the northern gbr experienced a greater rate of warming compared to the central and southern gbr regions. this is partially due to minor cooling in the early 20th century in the central and northern gbr, and is consistent with a previous composite record from the central gbr. the post 1950s difference between regions is likely driven by regional oceanographic changes, including shifts in ocean currents and the expansion of the indo-pacific warm pool. notably, post-2000 sr/ca trends diverge from instrumental sst trends, which is potentially connected with heat stress causing physiological changes in coral calcification processes. \r\n\r\nanalysing sst trends at regional scales reveals local nuances within the broader global warming pattern and highlights changing oceanographic processes operating at scales relevant to gbr reef ecosystems. the results have important implications for coral reef resilience into the future and underscore the importance of region-based reef management. helen mcgregor screening framework for secondary aragonite in ancient corals from hawai'ian drowned reefs coral skeletons are a source of high-resolution proxy data for reconstructing past temperature, salinity, and ocean conditions. however, the aragonite skeleton can undergo post-depositional alteration (diagenesis) that disrupts the original geochemical proxy climate signal. secondary aragonite, a precipitate common in early marine diagenesis of coral skeletons, can introduce significant errors in ocean temperature reconstructions even in small amounts. in addition, secondary aragonite is difficult to detect by the most common screening methods such as x-ray diffraction, as it has the same crystal structure as the primary coral skeleton. here we present a screening framework for low levels of secondary aragonite, and we apply the framework to ancient coral samples that date to up to 500,000 years old. these coral samples were retrieved from a globally unique sequence of drowned fossil reefs offshore hawai\u2019i, collected as part of the international ocean discovery program expedition 389. the framework is based on continuous scanning electron microscopy (sem) imaging of the corals along their major growth axis, the same location where geochemical samples will be milled. the results show that the sem framework can identify sub-millimetre-scale areas of secondary aragonite, which can be avoided with careful sample collection, and provides a threshold below which coral geochemistry should be reliable for climate reconstructions. the results will be compared to other secondary aragonite screening methods. this new framework for screening secondary aragonite will complement published screening guides that focus on calcite and dissolution and will lead to more reliable paleoclimate and paleoenvironment reconstructions from corals. hugo fahey coral temperature proxies across environmental gradients on the great barrier reef the great barrier reef (gbr) is increasingly threatened by rising sea surface temperatures (sst) and the growing frequency and severity of mass coral bleaching events. accurately reconstructing past sst from coral geochemical proxy records is vital to understanding climate trends prior to the instrumental period, contextualising these changes. however, proxy behaviour can vary with local environmental and ecological conditions, complicating their interpretation. to strengthen the usage of coral-based sst proxies, we compare multiple sst proxies across environmental gradients along the gbr to evaluate their relative robustness. additionally, we experimentally test whether short-term thermal stress produces a consistent and detectable geochemical signature of coral bleaching.\r\n\r\nthe evaluation of proxy-temperature relationships uses records in the great barrier reef coral skeletal records database. records were compared to satellite and logger sst data and showed substantial variance in proxy fit with latitude, distance across the continental shelf, and analytical technique. specifically, linear regression correlations were consistently and significantly lower at near-shore (median of the medians for each proxy r2 = 0.29) and southern sites (0.19), in contrast to at offshore (0.71), central and northern reef sites (0.65) across all proxies examined. methodological differences were also significant, with solution chemistry showing higher proxy-temperature correlations (0.68) than laser ablation (0.22). cross-variances between this set of comparisons are being investigated. across the dataset, combining multiple proxies better replicates the instrumental sst signal than reliance on any single proxy, consistent with previous studies on multi-proxy sst reconstructions.\r\n\r\nin identifying how various sst proxies perform more reliably and in demonstrating the importance of combining multiple proxies, more robust reconstructions of past reef conditions can be developed. analysis of tank experiment samples will investigate possible bleaching geochemical signals in coral skeletons, improving the capacity to interpret long-term records in the context of increasing thermal stress across the gbr.",
