          "name": "Christine Baran",
          "search_text": "christine baran lasting demographic impacts of heatwave-induced mass bleaching on soft corals soft corals are one of the most competitive benthic taxa on shallow tropical reefs, yet they remain vulnerable to local and climate-driven disturbances. the 2024 global coral bleaching event (gcbe4) caused widespread coral mortality worldwide, including the bolinao-anda reef complex (barc), northwestern philippines \u2013 a region repeatedly exposed to thermal stress since 1983. in this study, we assessed the influence of bleaching on soft coral vital rates by tracking individual colonies (n = 300) from november 2024 to september 2025 every 3 months. demographic parameters measured included survival, recovery of pigmentation post-bleaching and growth rates. our results showed clear demographic separation among the three genera studied. colony survival was higher in sclerophytum and sarcophyton (> 90%) than in lobophytum (81%). however, only lobophytum exhibited recovery, with 41% of initially bleached colonies gaining normal coloration by the final monitoring period. in contrast, both sclerophytum (0%) and sarcophyton (-28%) showed no signs of recovery. across all genera, bleached colonies experienced substantial size reduction (-69% to -89%), as compared to non-bleached individuals (-18%). a secondary bleaching spike in may 2025 with heat stress remaining below 4 \u00b0c-weeks, may have caused heightened sensitivity or cumulative stress effects that delayed recovery. these findings highlight that the impacts of bleaching on soft corals are variable and genus-specific and can persist for at least a year. understanding these demographic responses provides novel insights into the dynamics of coral bleaching and soft coral recovery in tropical reef systems.",
