          "name": "Ma. Isabella Rayos del Sol",
          "search_text": "ma. isabella rayos del sol influence of submarine groundwater discharge (sgd) on the photosynthetic symbionts of translocated pocillopora verrucosa corals submarine groundwater discharge (sgd) areas, particularly in volcanic regions, release warm, acidic, nutrient- and co\u2082-rich waters, creating distinct biogeochemical conditions that mimic future climate change scenarios for coral reefs. sgd areas offer an opportunity to explore the effects of rising seawater temperature and acidification on corals and their symbionts in the natural setting. coral survival under these stresses is partly attributed to its photosynthetic symbionts, dinoflagellates belonging to the family symbiodiniaceae. to examine the impact of sgd conditions on these symbionts, a coral translocation experiment was performed in tingloy, batangas, a known volcanic region influenced by sgd. pocillopora verrucosa corals were translocated into established sites in tingloy: (1) \u201cvent\u201d site, an sgd-visible coral reef with active shallow hydrothermal vents, and (2) \u201cnon-vent\u201d site, a coral reef without any visible sgd emanations. translocated fragments were sampled after 1- and 7- months for analysis. results show that fragments translocated in the \u201cvent\u201d site significantly differed in terms of symbiont density from those translocated into the \u201cnon-vent\u201d site. this is especially prominent during the short-term deployment. differences are also evident in symbiont community composition in both short- and long-term deployments. identification of these short- and long-term influences of sgd conditions on coral symbionts provide insights into potential adaptation mechanisms of coral hosts in response to the unique environments of sgd areas, advancing our understanding of coral community resilience to climate change and its impacts on philippine coral reef biodiversity.",
