          "name": "Prince Jewel Federe",
          "search_text": "prince jewel federe peeling the layers of barc: modeling how chronic local stressors and climate change drive reef degradation in the bolinao\u2013anda reef complex, philippines the bolinao\u2013anda reef complex (barc) in the northwestern philippines has undergone profound ecological shifts driven by the interplay of local and global stressors. once dominated by branching and plating corals, the system is now characterized by stress-tolerant taxa such as heliopora coerulea and turfing macroalgae. chronic eutrophication from intensive mariculture and sedimentation from fish-farm effluents have reshaped benthic communities and constrained coral recruitment, while recurring thermal anomalies continue to erode recovery potential. yet the mechanistic interactions among these stressors\u2013how nutrient enrichment and sediment loading compound bleaching impacts and reshape coral\u2013algal competition\u2013remain poorly understood. here, we develop a spatially explicit model, reefmod-barc, to examine how these drivers generate demographic and ecological cascades. by integrating satellite-derived nutrient and turbidity data, hydrodynamic connectivity, and heatwave-induced bleaching mortality, we simulate the combined influence of eutrophication, sedimentation, and thermal stress across representative barc reefs. we test whether these stressors act synergistically to amplify coral decline, particularly near fish farms, and evaluate the resulting shifts in community structure. our findings demonstrate possible future trajectories for barc reefs and identify resilience thresholds that can guide mitigation strategies in this rapidly changing reefscape.",
