          "name": "Sophia Diane Gaton",
          "search_text": "sophia diane gaton survival and growth of acropora spp. and massive coral recruits one year after larval reseeding in artificial reefs in lobo, batangas, philippines the intensifying climate crisis has led to the rapid decline of reef-building corals on\r\nmany reefs, prompting the implementation of coral restoration initiatives using different\r\nmethods to promote coral population recovery. one coral restoration method that has\r\nbeen proven to increase recruitment and coral populations is larval enhancement. it can\r\nbe implemented in two ways: through direct larval release on degraded reefs or through\r\nthe use of small artificial substrate or pre-settlement devices. however, the integration\r\nof larval enhancement with artificial reefs (ars), which is a popular reef restoration\r\nintervention, has not been investigated. this study aimed to evaluate the potential use\r\nof artificial reef structures as settlement substrates by assessing the survival and growth\r\nof acropora spp. and massive coral recruits after larval enhancement in lobo,\r\nbatangas. three 2x2x2 m pyramid-shaped ars were each supplied with ~450,000 coral\r\nlarvae contained within mesh enclosures in april 2023, and three ars were non-\r\nreseeded controls. survival and growth of the coral recruits were monitored in april\r\n2024 and october 2024 to assess early growth performance. after one year, over 90\r\nlive recruits were observed on the reseeded ars with a mean diameter ranging from 8-\r\n50 mm for acropora spp. and 8-40 mm for massive coral recruits. some coral recruits\r\nwere recorded on the control ars which may be a result of natural larval supply from\r\nnearby reefs, or larvae escaping beyond the mesh enclosures. these findings indicate\r\nthat ars can support coral recruitment when larval supply is provided and proper\r\nbiological conditioning of the substrate is achieved, promoting larval settlement. overall,\r\nthis study highlights the effectiveness and versatility of larval enhancement as a\r\nrestoration method that can be integrated with other restoration approaches, including\r\nthe use of ars as settlement substrates to help replenish coral populations.",
