          "name": "Ryan Carl De Juan",
          "search_text": "ryan carl de juan shifts in planulation timing of pocilloporid corals (2004\u20132025): implications on coral recovery and resilience pocilloporid corals are known to brood larvae on a monthly cycle with strong seasonal peaks. as fast-growing \u201cweedy\u201d species, they rapidly recolonize disturbed areas, recover quickly from bleaching, and contribute significantly to maintaining reef biodiversity. although their reproductive cycles in the bolinao\u2013anda reef complex (barc) were described in the early 2000s, rising thermal stress, bleaching events, and environmental changes may have altered their planulation timing. this study re-evaluated the reproductive patterns of three common pocilloporids pocillopora acuta, stylophora pistillata, and seriatopora caliendrum by monitoring ten colonies per species maintained in an outdoor hatchery. larval release was recorded daily throughout 2025 for at least twelve consecutive months. all species displayed strong seasonality, with the greatest larval output during the april to may. their lunar patterns, however, differed. p. acuta followed a distinct timing, releasing most larvae from the new moon to the first quarter, with peak release shortly after the new moon. s. pistillata released larvae across all lunar phases but also peaked between the new moon and first quarter. s. caliendrum showed the shortest, restricted to around the new moon, with a clear peak on the new moon. of the three species, p. acuta was the most fecund, followed by s. caliendrum and s. pistillata. comparison with records from 2004 revealed substantial changes in terms of the lunar periodicity and larval output. both p. acuta and s. pistillata released larvae earlier in 2025 and produced fewer larvae overall. these differences likely reflect the cumulative effects of bleaching on coral abundance, physiology, and reproductive output within barc. increasing unpredictability in larval supply may compromise natural recruitment and restoration efforts, underscoring the importance of systematic monitoring and larval cryopreservation to maintain reliable sources of larvae.",
