          "name": "Adele Battini",
          "search_text": "adele battini coral recovery in acidified conditions after heat stress-induced bleaching marine heatwaves are causing increasing mass coral bleaching events, threatening coral reefs worldwide. under these circumstances, the recovery potential of corals after bleaching is a key determinant of reef resilience. however, ocean acidification (oa) may influence the recovery process, and its effects may be exacerbated as climate change progresses. while numerous studies have assessed the effects of oa on coral physiology, how oa affects the recovery of corals following heat stress remains unknown. in this study, we focused on the fast-growing coral acropora aff. tenuis, to evaluate the effects of oa on both their bleaching and subsequent recovery. we conducted a three-month tank experiment in which coral fragments were kept under four treatments: control (25.5\u00b0c; ph8.1), heat (31\u00b0c; ph8.1), oa (25.5\u00b0c; ph7.8) and oa+heat (31\u00b0c; ph7.8). nine colonies of a. aff. tenuis were sampled from the reef at sesoko island, okinawa, japan and coral fragments were distributed into 12 indoor tanks. the temperature in the heated treatments was ramped up to 31\u00b0c and maintained until at least 50% of fragments showed signs of bleaching.  temperature was then lowered to 25.5\u00b0c for 2 months during the recovery period. throughout the experiment, coral health was monitored by pam fluorometry, and routine mortality checks were conducted using optical microscopy paired with uv light. additionally, photosynthesis, respiration and calcification rates, symbiont density, chlorophyll a+c2 concentrations, sod and cat activities were measured at three timepoints (t0: after bleaching; t1: 2 weeks of recovery; t2: 2 months of recovery). fv/fm in both the heat and heat+oa treatments showed similar recovery trajectories during the recovery period. however, significantly higher mortality occurred in the oa+heat treatment, suggesting synergetic effects of oa on heat-stress recovery. we will further discuss potential mechanisms underlying these oa-mediated impacts on coral bleaching and recovery.",
