          "name": "Michael Izumiyama",
          "search_text": "michael izumiyama canary of the reef: can giant clams (tridacna crocea) warn us about reef health? coral reefs are facing a multitude of environmental stressors, including prolonged heatwaves, which are occurring with increasing frequency and intensity. these events can trigger mass bleaching and mortality in corals and giant clams, driven by the breakdown of their photosymbiotic relationships with symbiodiniaceae. early detection of potential bleaching events may help inform stakeholders about the potential risks to the ecosystems. often, molecular changes in organisms occur before any visible physical signs of stress are apparent. our study investigated the potential to detect stress response markers in environmental rna (erna) and its potential as a molecular indicator of heat stress using the giant clam (tridacna crocea). tridacna crocea were housed individually in tanks with a continuous flow of seawater. they were exposed to either control conditions at 29\u00b0c or heated conditions at 32\u00b0c for two weeks. samples were collected at 24 hours, 7 days, 14 days, during heat stress, and 4 weeks after exposure to assess recovery. all t. crocea exposed to heat stress exhibited a reduction in the maximum quantum yield of photosystem ii (fv/fm), which recovered after four weeks. gene expression patterns within the ctenidia were compared to those in water collected from the siphon to determine if erna expression patterns overlap with those within the organism. our study aims to highlight the potential for erna collected from giant clams to serve as an early indicator of heat stress on coral reefs, potentially warning stakeholders of the imminent threat to reefs.",
