          "name": "Misato Sakoh",
          "search_text": "misato sakoh taxonomy of octocoral using ai automatic image classification of sclerites the class octocorallia (subphylum anthozoa, phylum cnidaria) are colonial organisms inhabiting seafloors worldwide including on numerous coral reef areas. octocorals provide shelter and food for small animals such as crustaceans, molluscs, polychaetes and others. furthermore, within coral reef ecosystems, some octocorals are thought to be comparably more resistant to bleaching than many hard scleractinian corals. after scleractinian corals die off, the ecosystem may shift to other organisms, including soft corals.\r\nalthough octocorals are an important part of coral reef ecosystems, because of difficulty in identification and a comparatively small number of octocoral experts, ecological aspects of octocorals are often understudied. as well, there are only a few morphological traits useful for classification that accurately reflect molecular phylogeny, leading to long-standing taxonomic confusion. the small crystal caco3 skeletal elementals that occur in the tissues of colonies are called sclerites, and have served as key taxonomic characters for over a century. yet, subjective descriptions of sclerites based on expert experience are common, posing challenges in ensuring objectivity. while some papers have performed quantitative analyses using certain gorgonian genera exist, no such attempts exist for soft corals that lack an axis. therefore, we aimed to construct an auto-classification model for sclerite images using deep learning, focusing on efficient extraction of quantitative data such as length, width, and circularity of the objects. by using this approach to collect quantitative data on sclerites and visualize the regions used by ai as judgment criteria during automatic classification, it was possible to explore new classification traits that ensure objectivity. here, as a first step, we report on the results of investigating whether ai can extract quantitative trait states of sclerites, enabling automatic image classification.",
