          "name": "Saki Harii",
          "search_text": "saki harii bleaching response of seriatopora from mesophotic refugium in a shallow reef: field transplantation and proteomics approaches over a decade ago, a deep healthy population of seriatopora was discovered at 40 m depth off sesoko island in okinawa, japan, where it had gone extinct from shallow reefs following a massive bleaching event. this discovery prompted us to investigate whether this population could serve as a larval source for the recovery of shallow reefs. through in-situ transplant experiments, we examined how deep seriatopora can acclimatise to shallow reef conditions. although transplanted fragments had bleached at shallow depths, the survival, growth rates, and photo-physiological acclimatisation at intermediate depths suggest a potential for a stepping-stone recolonisation of shallow reefs. to understand the mechanisms underlying the bleaching, we subsequently conducted proteomic analyses of transplanted corals. the results indicate that the combination of high light and temperature is a major impediment to the acclimatisation of deep seriatopora to shallow conditions.",
