          "name": "Alicia Emberson-Semisi",
          "search_text": "alicia emberson-semisi assessing the interactions between benthic cyanobacterial mats and herbivorous reef fishes. cyanobacteria are being increasingly recognized as an emerging threat to coral reef functioning, yet their ecological impacts remain understudied compared to more widely discussed stressors such as overfishing, nutrient pollution, and macroalgal dominance. while benthic cyanobacterial mats (bcms) contribute to nitrogen fixation and primary production, their growing abundance\u2013driven in part by ocean warming and local disturbances\u2013has been implicated in the disruption of key ecological processes that underpin coral reef resilience. of particular concern is their influence on herbivorous reef fishes, whose grazing and scraping activities maintain reef surfaces in a favorable condition for coral recruitment.\r\n\r\nthis study quantified interactions between bcms and two important herbivorous functional groups: browsing fishes and territorial stegastes damselfishes. feeding assays were conducted to compare bite rates of browsing herbivores on brown macroalgae with and without overgrowing bcms across three different fijian reef systems (namada village, leleuvia island, and barefoot manta resort) selected to represent a gradient of environmental conditions and human pressures. video assays were conducted at vatuolalai village to examine whether stegastes spp. actively remove cyanobacteria placed on corals within their territories. browsers exhibited an 84% reduction in bite rates when macroalgal substrates were overgrown by bcms, demonstrating a strong deterrent effect with potential implications for the efficiency of herbivory on bcm-dominated reefs. in contrast, stegastes damselfish removed bcms almost immediately, suggesting a possible localized mechanism for mat suppression on corals within defended territories.\r\n\r\nthese results underscore the growing need to consider bcms in the management of coral reefs given these contrasting top-down controls on algal growth. as bcms continue to expand across tropical reefs, understanding their deterrent properties, ecological toxicity,\r\n\r\nand interactions with key herbivores will be essential for developing climate-smart, adaptive reef management approaches. future research should prioritize the chemical ecology of bcms and their influence on multiple herbivore guilds.",
