          "name": "Amanda Ford",
          "search_text": "amanda ford unpacking the problem of benthic cyanobacterial mats on coral reefs - ongoing research in the fiji islands the world\u2019s oceans are being fundamentally changed by multiple human impacts, and tropical coral reefs are among the most impacted ecosystems. widespread reports have documented coral reef shifts characterised by losses of scleractinian corals and emerging dominance of alternate groups such as macroalgae and sponges. benthic cyanobacterial mats (bcms) have until now received very little research attention, but are becoming increasingly common around tropical coastlines. in fiji, blooms can occur annually on nearshore coral reefs during warm months, but in recent years some bcms have begun to prevail through the cold season as well. little is known about how these bcms influence underlying reef processes and functions, and many fundamental knowledge gaps remain. our research aimed to better understand the drivers and dynamics of bcms, and their impact on coral reef communities in fiji, a pacific island country that has a high dependence on reef-associated ecosystem services. research objectives included (i) to compile data from reefs across fiji to generate baseline data on bcm spatial distribution and to determine how they relate to environmental features, (ii) to conduct fine-scale surveys into bcms and environmental conditions at a set of inshore reefs to generate data on temporal dynamics of bcms, and (iii) understand the impact of bcms on reef-associated organisms. our findings reveal that bcms are ubiquitous across many of fiji\u2019s reefs, even at surprisingly remote reefs distant from land. their abundance seems to be connected to increased temperatures and rainfall, with highest levels found in surveys between january and march. additionally, our findings have highlighted the negative impact bcms have on fish herbivory and their ability to rapidly cause bleaching and mortality as they overgrow hard coral tissue. a key challenge in reef monitoring is our ability to identify bcms, often leading to significant underestimations of their abundance.",
