          "name": "Courtney Powell",
          "search_text": "courtney powell from larvae to livelihoods: connectivity insights for fiji reef management coral reefs in the south pacific are globally significant, supporting extraordinary biodiversity and sustaining coastal communities through fisheries, coastal protection, and cultural values. in melanesia, climate change, population growth, and resource pressure are intensifying threats to reef ecosystems, highlighting the need for evidence-based conservation strategies. locally managed marine areas (lmmas) have emerged as community-driven tools for stewardship, yet their design often emphasises social or logistical factors rather than key ecological processes such as larval dispersal, which drive population replenishment, resilience, and fisheries productivity. this project investigates ecological connectivity and source\u2013sink dynamics of reef fish across fiji\u2019s lmma network to inform management strategies that integrate ecological and socioecological objectives. we focus on two culturally and ecologically important species, chlorurus microrhinos and lutjanus gibbus, sampled across six sites. population genomic analyses using dartseq-derived snp markers will quantify gene flow, genetic structure, and metapopulation connectivity. these empirical estimates are integrated with biophysical larval dispersal models to identify ecologically coherent reef networks and evaluate how connectivity influences lmma performance. by combining genomics, biophysical modelling, and seascape-scale perspectives, this research clarifies how connectivity underpins reef resilience and fisheries sustainability. the results will guide lmma design and governance to maximise ecological function and community benefits, demonstrating how foundational science, local stewardship, and applied management can converge. this approach contributes to sustaining biodiversity, enhancing ecosystem services, and strengthening the resilience of fiji coral reef systems under increasing environmental pressures.",
