          "search_text": "jasha dehm resilience and ecological patterns of an urban reef system under land-based stressors: insights from suva, fiji urbanization-driven water quality changes, including nutrient enrichment, turbidity, and sedimentation, directly influence coral reef communities and their resilience. we investigated benthic composition and coral community assemblages across reef zones in suva, fiji, one of the pacific\u2019s most urbanized reef systems, to assess how land-based stressors influence reef ecosystems. our benthic surveys revealed that suva\u2019s reefs persist in a degraded-but-stable state, with mean coral cover around 30%, lower than fiji\u2019s typical for undisturbed reef range but consistent with previous surveys 20 years ago on this system. reef zone was the dominant driver of community structure, exceeding proximity of the reef zone to the urban area in shaping benthic assemblages. predictive modeling identified temperature as the primary driver of benthic composition, while turbidity and nutrient enrichment contributed to shifts toward alternative substrate types, highlighting interactive effects of local and global stressors. coral community analyses recorded 27 genera, with acropora and porites dominating cover. juvenile coral distributions showed genus-specific responses to local environmental conditions: acropora thrived in stable, high water quality sites, porites exhibited clustered patterns tolerating moderate stress, and goniopora and pocillopora showed restricted or sporadic recruitment. our findings emphasize the capacity for urban reefs to persist under chronic anthropogenic pressures while remaining vulnerable to further degradation. integrating water quality management, and habitat-specific protection can enhance reef resilience. collectively, these results underscore both the resilience and vulnerabilities of urban reef systems, highlight them as priorities for future research, and demonstrate the need for adaptive, habitat-based management strategies in fiji and comparable pacific settings.",
