          "name": "Zahidah Afrin",
          "search_text": "zahidah afrin systems change in island nations coral reef economy via the scuba diving industry. in 2015, the united nations agenda 2030 for sustainable development goals (sdgs) took a significant step by integrating the sustainable use of oceans (sdg 14) with economic development for the first time. sdg14 provides the global coral reefs and commercial marine resource users and industries to adopt systems thinking approach to management and governance. at the sdgs midpoint review, sdg 14  is identified as one of the most under-implemented goals. small island developing states (sids) are among those most at risk from this lack of this policy implementation, as their marine economies, food security, and cultural identity are highly dependent on healthy oceans and tropical coral reef economy.\r\nin island nations like fiji, barbados, the bahamas, and grenada, a young diver often begins their journey guiding tourists on reef dives or collecting seafood for local restaurants and export markets. with guidance from diving instructors and support from marine scientists, many of these divers take on new responsibilities\u2013helping track coral health for climate change studies, monitoring marine pollution, and even teaching children the skills needed to protect underwater life.  the diving professionals are serving eco education marine tourism. this session presents the systems map of the diving industry in islands coral reefs economic use. it shares the diving industry actors and their networks  contributing not only to tourism sector but also to science, conservation, education, fisheries and maritime . yet, while governments across island nations have committed to sdg 14 they continue to lack marine science driven workforce at national scale.  the session introduces the blue economy diving alliance that advocates for systems change in coral reef economic actors  via the review of scuba diving  systems, actors, networks, and calls for innovation.  the session provides a framework that bridges divides between dive industry , governments and marine sciences as futures outlook.",
