          "name": "Ally Swan",
          "search_text": "ally swan the caribbean coral orbicella franksi shows decreasing thermal tolerance with depth increasing ocean temperatures and the intensification of marine heatwaves continue to drive widespread bleaching and mortality across caribbean coral reefs. mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces), found at depths of 30-150m, have been proposed as potential thermal refugia due to their cooler, more stable conditions, which has led to the deep reef refugia hypothesis (drrh). however, support for the drrh remains inconsistent across regions and species. in some cases, mces appear buffered from thermal stress, while others, including research on orbicella spp., suggest that corals may be acclimated to local depth-specific conditions, making them just as susceptible to temperature anomalies. this study investigated resilience and recovery in orbicella franksi from a shallow fringing reef (flat cay; 15m) and a mesophotic reef (hind bank; 40m) in st. thomas, usvi, under experimentally simulated thermal stress. we hypothesized that mesophotic o. franksi would exhibit greater bleaching susceptibility than their shallow reef counterparts due to their acclimatization to cooler, lower light environments. ramets from five genets per depth were exposed to three temperature treatments representing ambient conditions, moderate warming, and severe heat stress. throughout the experiment, corals were assessed via daily visual health checks, quantitative image-based bleaching analysis, and photosynthetic efficiency. symbiont density and chlorophyll content were measured across bleaching stages, while symbiont community composition was analyzed only pre-bleaching and post-recovery to detect shifts from cladocopium to heat-tolerant durusdinium. physiological samples are being processed, but preliminary results show that mesophotic colonies bleached faster than flat cay colonies, supporting our hypothesis that deeper corals are adapted to their local thermal environment and are therefore more sensitive to elevated temperatures. findings from this study will improve our understanding of depth-specific responses to thermal stress in o. franksi, helping assess the capacity of mces to function as climate refugia in the u.s. virgin islands.",
