          "name": "Lindsay Dade",
          "search_text": "lindsay dade population status of endangered coral species in the u.s. virgin islands caribbean coral reefs are experiencing rapid changes in benthic composition due to a myriad of environmental stressors. recent coral mortality events, including bleaching and stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld), have left left populations of highly susceptible stony corals severely depleted. current monitoring programs in the u.s. virgin islands lack the resolution to capture rare species distributions, prompting the development of the endangered coral mapping program (ecmp). ecmp aims to assess the population size and distributions of esa-listed and sctld-susceptible corals in shallow waters (<10m) across the territory. using a stratified random sampling design, coral demographic surveys were conducted at 600 sites across the u.s. virgin islands. preliminary results suggest that dendrogyra cylindrus (1.6% occurrence) is functionally extinct in the region, and acropora palmata (21.8% occurrence) is at risk of genetic isolation. species distribution models will be used to determine areas of probable occurrence and potential habitats suitable for restoration. these findings aim to guide management actions to support the conservation of these vulnerable species amidst rapidly changing reef conditions.",
