          "name": "Sonora Meiling",
          "search_text": "sonora meiling an ecological comparison of disease outbreaks distinguishing between different diseases can help direct limited resources in more efficient ways. stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) has caused mass coral mortality on caribbean reefs since its emergence in 2014. sctld affects a broad range of reef-building coral species and was recently demonstrated to be transmissible to pacific coral species. although sctld is far more lethal than most previously described diseases, its initial appearance can resemble other acute tissue-loss conditions, such as white plague (wp). we applied a range of comparative ecological metrics (lesion morphology, community dynamics, histopathology, and fate tracking results) to determine an emergent disease on an isolated reef system was more closely aligned with our previous observations of wp than sctld. this study highlights the importance of fate tracking and quantitative histopathology for distinguishing coral diseases. molecular results from these corals will be used to validate these phenotypic patterns, and preliminary findings further emphasize the importance of interdisciplinary approaches in disease etiology. mesophotic outplanting: an effective in situ gene banking strategy in spite of mass bleaching record-breaking seasonal temperature increases foreshadowed caribbean-wide mass bleaching in 2024. prior to peak bleaching, members of the reef response coral restoration program at the university of the virgin islands outplanted fragments of wild acropora cervicornis colonies to deeper reefs in st. thomas, u.s. virgin islands. outplant sites were chosen based on observed wild populations of a. cervicornis. along a single continuous reef slope, replicate cohorts of a. cervicornis were outplanted at 20ft, 85ft, 100ft, and 120ft. maximum temperatures were comparable among sites throughout the bleaching event; however, the shallow site sustained elevated temperatures longer than the deep sites. there was variable bleaching and survival among depths and among genotypes. in general, wild colonies on their native reef and corals outplanted to the shallow site bleached more and had more mortality than those outplanted to deeper sites. however, among the deeper sites there were notable genotypic differences in survival, with the majority of the genotypes performing the best at the 85ft site. these patterns suggest that the shortened period of thermal stress on the deeper sites may create a bleaching refuge for a. cervicornis restoration populations, but that genotype can play a significant role in bleaching and survival. these results also demonstrate that deep outplanting can be an effective gene banking strategy to complement or replace ex situ populations, which can be extremely important for facilities that do not have the capacity for extensive land-based nurseries. the survivors of this study represent the majority of the in situ a. cervicornis genotypic diversity in the northern u.s. virgin islands after wild populations were decimated in the 2024 bleaching event.",
