          "name": "Spencer Parr",
          "search_text": "spencer parr depth, host identity, and disturbance shape clionaidae sponge prevalence on usvi coral reefs over time bioeroding sponges of the family clionaidae weaken coral skeletons and affect carbonate budgets on coral reefs. previous studies indicate that clionaidae are more commonly found on large framework-forming corals and shallow reefs in the caribbean. we hypothesized that (1) clionaidae prevalence would be maximized on a limited subset of coral species dominant in the caribbean, (2) clionaidae prevalence would increase with colony size and decline with depth, and (3) recent declines in prevalence would suggest a decrease in available living coral substrate, especially after the 2023 bleaching event. to evaluate these hypotheses, we examined colony-level coral health records from 33 continuously monitored sites in the u.s. virgin islands (2012\u20132024). initially, we quantified the prevalence across all coral species. subsequently, we concentrated on the six most abundant framework-forming coral hosts (e.g., orbicella annularis, siderastrea siderea, stephanocoenia intercepta). lastly, we limited our analyses to shallower sites (\u226420 m) to evaluate temporal trends where clionaidae are most prevalent. clionaidae were disproportionately found on large corals, with more than 80% of all detections coming from six host species. prevalence increased significantly with colony size across all focal species. clionaidae prevalence was highest on shallow reefs and near zero at depths greater than 20 meters. across the 12-year recording period, clionaidae prevalence was stable until a sharp drop in 2023, when mass bleaching reduced the absolute number of clionaidae-positive corals. these results support that major thermal disturbances, rather than gradual trends, drive the most meaningful short-term shifts in clionaidae prevalence.",
