          "name": "Tyler Smith",
          "search_text": "tyler smith thermal stress is driving recent extreme degradation of coral reefs in the us virgin islands the long-projected global increases in tropical water temperatures and resulting coral thermal stress are now a reality.  in the 27 years since the first global coral bleaching event in 1997-98 bleaching has affected nearly all shallow water reefs.  in the caribbean coral abundance, typically measured as areal coverage, has declined precipitously in this same period.  in addition to thermal stress various other stressors have been implicated in this decline, including disease epizootics, land-based sources of pollution, overfishing, and other local stressors.  we used repeated annual coral reef assessments at 20 reef sites in the us virgin islands across a gradient of depths (shallow to mesophotic), distance to shore, and protected status to understand the degree of change between 2005 and 2024.  we were able to show disturbances in the time series from abrupt declines in coral cover versus periods with no disturbances and gradual change, attribute the proportion of reef change caused by chronic stress and specific disturbances, and determine the stressor most responsible for driving reef decline.  overall site-level coral cover declined from 22.8% \u00b1 3.0 sem to 4.8% \u00b1 0.78 between 2005 and 2025, with the largest absolute declines occurring during the 2005 bleaching event (-9.4% \u00b1 1.6), the epidemic phase of stony coral tissue loss disease from 2019 \u2013 2022 (-3.9% \u00b1 1.2), the 2023 bleaching event (-1.8% \u00b1 0.4), and the 2024 bleaching event (-0.9% \u00b1 0.3).  thermal stress and bleaching caused more than three quarters of the coral cover decline in this 20 year period, an overwhelming impact relative to other modern threats to coral reefs.  increasing ocean temperatures and persistent marine heat waves will continue to suppress meaningful recovery and are driving western atlantic coral reefs into new configurations where living stony corals are no longer major contributors to benthic processes.",
