          "search_text": "ruleo camacho distribution, size-structure, and condition of acropora palmata following the 2023/2024 mass coral bleaching events across three eastern caribbean islands. elkhorn coral, acropora palmata, is a key building species of caribbean coral reefs which has suffered extreme population losses across the caribbean during the mass coral bleaching events of 2023 and 2024. although knowledge of the population status of acroporids across the eastern caribbean has historically been limited, some pockets of 2023/2024 post-bleaching survivors have been reported across several islands. we here aim to (1) map the distribution of known survivor a. palmata colonies, (2) describe their size structure and post-bleaching condition, and (3) identify key environmental correlates of their abundance, size and condition, across the islands of antigua, barbuda and barbados.  \r\nin antigua, 70+ a. palmata colonies were recorded between 0.7m and 9.1m along the southern, eastern and northern coastlines, with the highest density observed between 3.1-6.1m depth. colony sizes ranged from 137.5 cm\u00b2 to 16,376.0 cm\u00b2, with approximately 50% of individuals falling within the \u201clarge\u201d size class (1,964\u20137,854 cm\u00b2). reproductive viability size threshold of living tissue (\u22653,000 cm\u00b2) was met by 45% of colonies assessed. colony size was positively correlated with depth, suggesting depth-driven differences in colony mortality and/or growth. partial tissue mortality was observed in 48% of colonies but was not associated with colony size nor depth. data from barbuda and barbados are currently being collected but preliminary findings suggest a higher abundance of post-bleaching survivor colonies than expected.  \r\nthis work will create a baseline to guide future monitoring of post-bleaching recovery of a.palmata in antigua, barbuda and barbados and support current efforts in genotyping at these islands using single nucleotide polymorphisms (snp) to adequately assess their risk of functional extinction for a. palmata while meaningfully informing coral restoration and reef management activities throughout the caribbean region.",
