          "name": "Clinton Oakley",
          "search_text": "clinton oakley impacts of the sunscreen oxybenzone on the oxidative proteome of the coral model exaiptasia sunscreens, including oxybenzone, have been identified as potential contributors to localized coral reef decline by exacerbating coral bleaching. oxybenzone has been proposed to have phototoxic effects on corals during ultraviolet (uv) light exposure through its metabolism to the photosensitizer oxybenzone-glucoside, and accumulation of oxybenzone-glucoside has been demonstrated in the anemone model exaiptasia diaphana. here, we investigated the effects of oxybenzone on the oxidative state of the host proteome in the exaiptasia\u2013dinoflagellate symbiosis. reversible cysteine oxidation is a widespread means of protein activation, regulation, and signalling. consequently, analyzing the cnidarian oxidative proteome in the presence of oxybenzone can reveal the cellular components and protein networks underlying the physiological response.\r\n\r\nwe assessed the reversible oxidative state of cysteine-containing peptides via liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry. exaiptasia diaphana either hosting breviolum minutum or lacking symbionts (aposymbiotic) were exposed to 8.8 \u00b5m oxybenzone in seawater at 26 \u00b0c and 100 \u00b5m quanta m\u22122 s\u22121 irradiance, with or without the presence of uv light. anemones were monitored daily by pulse-amplitude modulated fluorometry and sampled after seven days (symbiotic) or three days (aposymbiotic) oxybenzone/uv exposure. protein samples were processed by solid-phase extraction followed by resin-assisted capture of cysteine-containing peptides. \r\n\r\nwe quantified the reversible cysteine oxidative state in thousands of exaiptasia proteins exposed to oxybenzone with and without uv light. we found shifts in the redox state of host mitochondrial and cytoplasmic proteins that are consistent with host oxidative stress and stress responses. aposymbiotic exaiptasia showed larger shifts in the oxidative proteome under oxybenzone+uv exposure than symbiotic anemones. oxybenzone in the absence of uv light induced relatively limited changes to the host proteome. by better understanding the cellular effects and specific metabolic and signalling pathways affected by uv-absorbing compounds, we can better predict their biological effects in the reef environment and select for effective but less-toxic sunscreens.",
