          "name": "Denisa Berbece",
          "search_text": "denisa berbece inorganic carbon physiology and skeletal mineralogy of coralline algae living within a naturally variable environment. marine biogenic calcifiers are under severe threat from future global change. coralline algae are particularly susceptible to ocean acidification, affecting their physiology and mineralogy, which impacts their role as settlement substrate, reef accretors, and seed banks. laboratory environments offer an excellent opportunity to study the impacts of key drivers under controlled conditions. however, natural laboratories, where environmental conditions are already close to predicted levels under future global change, provide a unique opportunity to study the effects of these key drivers in-situ. bourak\u00e8, in new caledonia is a semi-enclosed bay where tidal variation drives extreme diel changes in temperature, ph, and oxygen. coralline algae can survive within this extreme environment, indicating that it is likely that some forms of adaptation to local environmental stressors could have occurred. previous laboratory studies have indicated that the ability of coralline algae to shift dissolved inorganic carbon (dic) use (from energetically expensive bicarbonate (hco3-) to readily available carbon dioxide (co2)), and to maintain control of their calcification physiology (i.e. high internal caco3 saturation state (\u03c9) and low % magnesium (%mg)) under low ph, is related to tolerance. in this study, we conducted a reciprocal transplant of coralline algae living within (1) a low ph and (2) a nearby control site in bourak\u00e8, to assess short-term acclimatisation processes and physiological responses to novel environmental conditions. our hypotheses are: (1) coralline algae from bourak\u00e8 will show physiological differences compared to those from the control site; (2) they will display greater control of their calcification physiology (high internal caco3 \u03c9 and low %mg) and preferentially take up co2 for photosynthesis; (3) individuals transplanted to novel environmental conditions will not be able to acclimatise over the short-term; and (4) those transplanted into the low ph (bourak\u00e8) environment, will be most negatively affected.",
