      "search_text": "western australian museum lisa kirkendale meggan miller lisa kirkendale giant leap forward for giant clam biogeography and symbiont profiling in western australia giant clams (tridacninae) are prevalent on eastern indian ocean reefs off the coast of western australia where they are commercially harvested for the aquarium trade and under threat due to ongoing heatwaves. like corals, giant clams host symbiodiniaceae that supplement filter-feeding and facilitate rapid calcification. recently, symbiont profiles were developed for giant clam samples across the western australian coast, revealing substantial differences in photosymbiont communities when comparing giant clams to other coral reef hosts, and when comparing offshore reef and nearshore reef habitats. simultaneously, genetic research revealed differences in the distribution of giant clams, specifically tridacna, across the western australian coast. tridacna noae has been found to be the dominant species in inshore areas from ningaloo up to the kimberley, while t. maxima has only been genotyped from offshore reefs, including rowley shoals and cocos-keeling islands in the indian ocean territories. across this work, three key coral reefs were not sampled for giant clams and their photosymbionts. these include the high latitude houtman abrolhos islands located in a subtropical marine transition zone, the tropical oceanic offshore scott reef system, and christmas island, which is an isolated tropical oceanic reef in the indian ocean territories. trends in symbiodiniaceae communities from these previously unsampled sites are discussed in the context of both giant clams and other symbiodiniaceae hosts of the region. together this work begins to form a synthetic baseline of host and symbiont biodiversity and biogeography from these iconic coral reef molluscs in the indian ocean. meggan miller uncovering the diversity of photosymbionts in giant clams of the western australian coast giant clams (family: cardiidae) are integral to reef structures, providing food and habitat to other organisms, but are threatened by climate change and overharvesting. they rely on supplemental nutrition through photosymbiosis, and evidence shows that harbouring multiple photosymbionts may help them withstand fluctuating conditions. globally, giant clams have been found to associate with photosymbionts of the genera symbiodinium, cladocopium, durusdinium, and gerakladium, however, this has not previously been investigated in western australian. using a dna metabarcoding approach, the nuclear ribosomal internal transcribed spacer region 2 (its2) was targeted to amplify symbiodiniaceae in four giant clam host species \u2013 hippopus hippopus, tridacna maxima, tridacna noae and tridacna squamosa. across 78 samples, it was found that photosymbiont assemblages differ across spatial scales, with higher diversity observed in offshore reefs. harbouring a combination of symbionts, including durusdinium, known as the \u2018extremophile\u2019 genus of symbiodiniaceae, may enhance the resilience of giant clams, improve their ability to withstand environmental change and secure their future in reef ecosystems. this study provides a foundation of genetic diversity of photosymbiont communities in giant clams of western australia and contributes to the building taxonomy of symbiodiniaceae.",
