          "name": "Sergei Nozdrenkov",
          "search_text": "sergei nozdrenkov planetary reef intelligence: from data to action coral reefs have been evolving for hundreds of millions of years. they are dying fast. we need to deploy more solutions faster. a lot of amazing people are fighting for their lives. we need to know what works, what doesn't work to coordinate precise action. the data exists, scattered everywhere. the methods exist. but stitching it together into something actionable? that's where we're stuck. we lack shared infrastructure to understand at scale - the way earth engine opened up satellite data for anyone to query.\r\n\r\nthat infrastructure is starting to take shape. a growing coalition - citizen divers, research institutions, organisations with years of archives - is pooling reef data into something queryable. photogrammetry at sub-centimeter resolution. photorealistic 3d visualization. open-source code. open data. it's early. the tools are rough. but the foundation for planetary-scale reef intelligence-fusing structure, species, environment - is being laid in the open.\r\n\r\nwhat becomes possible: asking \"where is bleaching spreading?\" or \"how has this site changed?\" - and getting answers that wire directly into restoration decisions. we'll demo early prototypes, discuss what works, what doesn't, and where help is needed. the biodiversity crisis isn't an unsolvable problem - it's an engineering and coordination challenge. there are no laws of physics saying we can't fix this. we can be the first generation that leaves nature better than we found it! come see where this is heading.",
