          "name": "Neelam Nandita Bhan",
          "search_text": "neelam nandita bhan from community visioning to conservation action: an integrated approach to coral reef conservation in fiji fiji hosts ~7,500 km\u00b2 of globally significant coral reef ecosystems, recognised for their diversity and resilience. reef ecosystems such as the great sea reef and vatu-i-ra seascape have shown the capacity to withstand and recover from climate-driven bleaching events and cyclones. coral reefs have supported the livelihoods of indigenous and non-indigenous fijian communities for generations and are the pillar of fiji\u2019s tourism sector. despite their resilience, fiji\u2019s reefs face escalating local threats from unsustainable fishing, pollution, and coastal development. in response, a national initiative is being undertaken to address these challenges through the development and implementation of a national action plan for coral reef conservation (napcrc). this process began with participatory community visioning workshops, which combined traditional ecological knowledge with contemporary scientific approaches to identify threats to reef ecosystems and consolidate the goals and desires of indigenous customary rights holders. through these workshops, customary rights holders articulated their vision of fiji's coral reef ecosystems as healthy and thriving, with emphasis on sustainable fishing practices and community inclusivity. to put this community vision into practice, a participatory design process brought together government agencies, coral reef experts, and community representatives to translate community aspirations into an actionable conservation strategy. the wildlife conservation society (wcs) conducted a spatial prioritisation analysis as part of this process, which involved a three-step approach: gathering data on important areas for biodiversity and communities, mapping human pressures and threats to coral reefs and associated ecosystems, and combining this information in a spatial prioritisation assessment to identify key conservation sites. this analysis identifies high-integrity, climate-resilient reef areas that require conservation, informed by scientific datasets and community knowledge. this work demonstrates how spatial planning tools can bridge indigenous knowledge systems and scientific analysis, translating community visions into geographically specific, evidence-based conservation action.",
