          "name": "Peter Musembi",
          "search_text": "peter musembi tracing wastewater footprints on coral reefs: chemical and biological evidence from an mpa system in kenya wastewater pollution from untreated sewage and agricultural runoff poses a growing but understudied threat to coral reef ecosystems, introducing excess nutrients, sediments and pathogens that alter reef structure and function. despite the malindi\u2013watamu marine protected area (mpa) system in kenya being important for tourism and artisanal fisheries, its reef ecosystems are increasingly exposed to wastewater inputs due to inadequate sanitation infrastructure and expanding coastal developments. while other coral reef stressors in kenya, such as climate change, unregulated tourism, and overfishing, have been extensively investigated, the impacts of wastewater pollution remain poorly characterized. this study assessed the presence, sources, and ecological impacts of wastewater contamination on coral reefs in the malindi\u2013watumu mpa. pollution hotspots were identified through field reconnaissance and stream network analysis to locate wastewater discharge points. water was sampled to points adjacent to the identified discharge points and analyzed for enterococci and caffeine as indicators of human wastewater input, while reef benthic community composition was evaluated using cyanobacteria, turf algae, and fleshy macroalgae as bioindicators of reef health. all 22 sampled points tested positive for enterococci, confirming widespread fecal contamination. caffeine was detected at four of the 22, indicating direct human wastewater influence. benthic assessments using line intercept transects (lit) conducted at 8 sites in january 2025 revealed high turf algae (37.3%) and fleshy macroalgae (14.2%) cover, with cyanobacteria contributing approximately 1% against a hard coral cover of 12%. these patterns suggest that nutrient enrichment associated with wastewater discharge may be promoting algal proliferation, potentially undermining coral recovery and reef resilience. these findings provide the first comprehensive evidence of domestic wastewater contamination in the malindi\u2013watumu mpa system. we also discuss how these findings are being used to support the rationale of investing in wastewater management infrastructure to protect coastal ecosystems and monitoring of domestic wastewater patterns. coral reef ecological integrity as a holistic ecological alternative for future reef status predictions evaluating the status of coral reefs is challenged by the different focuses on taxa, environmental changes, and ecological responses. we demonstrate how commonly collected coral and fish community field metrics can be combined into producing a useful single metric of coral reef ecological integrity (crei). we used 5 ecological variables (coral cover, coral taxa, coral susceptibility to thermal stress, fish biomass, and fish taxa) and evaluated their associations with 31 environmental variables using an expansive study of 536 sites in 423 x 6.25 km2 mapped reef cells in 6 countries in the western indian ocean (wio). we found that ~ 26 environmental variables provided good predictions of these coral reef metrics and produced high testing and training predictions (~85%) for crei. we then compared the crei metric with common cominations of heat stress, bleaching, and coral cover metrics used to project the future reefs states, referred to as coral reef climate change index (ccci). both categories of metrics indicated temporal stability in 26 sites over the 2008 and 2021 study period but higher integrity in high compliance protected areas for the crei index. the cci index suggests that corals are likely acclimating to heat stress and therefore not reflecting rapids declines in coral cover over this time series. mapping crei for the 7700 reefs cells in the wio indentified high integrity locations notably in tanzania and eastward to northern madagascar. evaluating crei can help to set priorities for local conservation and fishery interventions and potentially applicable on a global scale to inform monitoring and evaluating the impacts of climate change and management interventions.",
