          "name": "Tarau Janice Taga",
          "search_text": "tarau janice taga integrating science and traditional ecological knowledge (tek) for an effective community-based coral reef management in fiji for pacific island nations, the ocean is home, heritage, and a lifeline. coral reef ecosystems lie at the heart of this relationship, providing food security, livelihoods, cultural identity, and coastal protection. yet, these ecosystems and the communities that depend on them face escalating threats from climate change, extreme weather events, and unsustainable land- and sea-based activities. addressing these challenges requires approaches that meaningfully integrate scientific methods with traditional ecological knowledge (tek). when brought together, these knowledge systems strengthen community-based management and ensure practices are sustained across generations.\r\nthis study presents case studies from the i-qoliqoli (customary fishing grounds) of nakorotubu district (ra province) and dama district (bua province), fiji, where science and tek have been blended to support coral reef stewardship. historical datasets and long-standing local observations provide valuable long-term trends for understanding ecological change. between 2024\u20132025, we surveyed 21 sites in nakorotubu and 16 sites in dama using fisheries monitoring and benthic habitat assessments. tek identified spawning seasons and traditional closure sites, which were integrated with scientific monitoring. preliminary results show coral cover (30\u201345%) and fish biomass (2000\u20134000 kg/ha) were significantly higher in tabu areas and reef passages compared to fished areas. effectiveness of tabu areas was influenced by reef proximity to land, exposure to currents, and connectivity.\r\nthese findings demonstrate that co-produced approaches, supported by temporal datasets, can measurably enhance ecological outcomes, sustain cultural practices, and strengthen local capacity to adapt to climate change. by weaving science and tek, communities in fiji are building resilient reef management systems that safeguard both biodiversity and cultural heritage. a novel approach to identifying climate-resilient reefs in fiji as climate change intensifies, identifying locations of climate-resilient coral reefs and better managing them is critical to sustaining coastal ecosystems and livelihoods.  this study presents a data-driven approach to identifying climate refugia reefs using <3,680 benthic transects collected between 2009 and 2025 from a range of ngo and government agencies in fiji, stored within the open-source mermaid platform. notably, this is the first study in fiji to apply climate refugia classifications based on coral life history categories and identify sites for a climate refugia strategy according to avoidance, resistance and recovery (arr) benthic typologies. we use field records to validate and extend climate refugia locations identified in a global prioritisation, focusing on coral cover, diversity and life history categories to assess ecological resilience. we present spatial patterns of arr reef systems, including sites showing signatures of high resistance or rapid recovery potential. our findings demonstrate how existing ecological data can be combined with next generation modelling outputs to provide information pathways for national policy development and enable evidence-based adaptation in the pacific island context. these analyses provide the first national-scale evidence to guide climate-smart reef protection in fiji, supporting policy decisions, marine spatial planning, and targeted management of resilient reef areas.",
