          "name": "Vivien Samantha Ferrer",
          "search_text": "vivien samantha ferrer smalls islands, big pressures: linking watershed pressures, sanitation gaps, and coastal conditions in siquijor, philippines siquijor\u2019s coral reefs support local fisheries, coastal protection, and a rapidly expanding tourism economy, yet development pressure is increasing across the province. with tourism growth concentrated in municipalities such as san juan and maria, concerns have emerged regarding the cumulative impacts of land-use change, altered watersheds, and inland sanitation conditions on nearshore water quality. complementary assessments were conducted, including a pollution situation assessment, a sphere handbook\u2013aligned wash technical assessment, sediment delivery ratio (sdr) modelling, and biophysical surveys of wildlife conservation society\u2013assisted mpas to inform management decisions in this data-limited island province. more than 20 freshwater sites across the 6 municipalities of siquijior were documented to have recurring disturbance, including unprotected springs, bathing and washing activities, direct greywater discharge, and livestock trampling. sanitation systems remain largely informal: in san juan, 98% of households (3,464) rely on single-chamber or unlined septic tanks, with similar patterns observed in larena, lazi, and maria. without a septage treatment facility, desludging rarely occurs, increasing the likelihood of nutrient transport through siquijor\u2019s highly porous karst geology. sdr modelling identified central and southern watersheds\u2013those draining toward san juan, maria, and parts of siquijor municipality\u2013as producing some of the highest sediment-export values under both current and projected development scenarios. these priority watersheds overlap with coastal sites where biophysical surveys recorded patches of benthic filamentous algae and, in one mpa (caticugan), the presence of crown-of-thorns starfish (cots). while these observations do not establish direct causation, their spatial co-occurrence with upstream sanitation gaps and sediment pathways highlights areas where pollution risks may be emerging. for decision-makers facing accelerating tourism growth and limited monitoring infrastructure, these integrated ridge-to-reef assessments provide an applied framework for interpreting land\u2013sea linkages and identifying priority watersheds for reducing chronic stress on coral reefs.",
