          "search_text": "muhidin muhidin* an integrated approach to water quality management for strengthening coral reef resilience in karimunjawa national park the coral reef ecosystem in karimunjawa national park (knp) is experiencing increasing pressure from climate change and land-based human activities, including shrimp pond expansion, rapid tourism growth, and inadequate waste management. these pressures have degraded water quality and coral health, evidenced by bleaching, coral diseases, structural damage, and algal blooms across several sites. this study aimed to assess spatial variation in water quality across knp to understand how human activity intensity influences coral reef condition. \r\nwater quality monitoring was conducted at 21 sites over 18 months (2024 to 2025), representing gradients of anthropogenic impact. key parameters, including nitrate, phosphate, e. coli, dissolved oxygen, ph, salinity, water temperature, and total dissolved solids, were measured and compared with indonesia\u2019s national marine water quality standards (is). \r\nthe results indicate that at the sites where exceedances occurred, several parameters surpassed is values, including phosphate (is = 0.15 mg/l; knp = 0.16\u20130.048 mg/l; n = 13 sites), e. coli (is = 200 units/ml; knp = 240\u2013557.5 units/ml; n = 6), salinity (is = 33\u201334 ppt; knp = 19.3 ppt; n = 1), and dissolved oxygen (is > 5 mg/l; knp = 3.1\u20133.3 mg/l; n = 2). water quality declined progressively from nearshore to offshore, indicating a strong land-based influence. correspondingly, 10.5% of outlet sites, 5.1% of shallow sites, and 1.3% of reef sites showed at least one exceedance. by dominant activity, exceedances were highest at shrimp ponds (9%), followed by seaports (6.4%) and settlements (5.1%).\r\nthese findings highlight how terrestrial and coastal development degrade water quality and jeopardize coral reef health in knp. the evidence informed the closure of four shrimp farms and is now driving wcs and knp\u2019s co-development of an integrated sanitation and wastewater management approach to enhance island-wide water quality and secure long-term ecosystem and livelihood sustainability.",
