      "search_text": "world conservation monitoring centre rowana walton rowana walton rebuilding reefs, refining regulations: reviewing national regulatory frameworks as coral reef ecosystems approach critical ecological tipping points, coral reef restoration is increasingly promoted to accelerate recovery where natural regeneration is insufficient. however, the effectiveness and scalability of restoration efforts are often constrained by fragmented, outdated, or overly restrictive regulatory frameworks that have not kept pace with scientific and technological innovation. we examine the role of regulation as a foundational enabler of responsible, effective, and equitable coral reef restoration and present a structured, three-step approach\u2013collate, review, and adapt to reviewing regulatory frameworks. this supports governments in assessing and strengthening existing regulatory frameworks across multiple levels of governance, from international agreements to local permitting systems. we also situate national regulatory reform within international commitments, including the kunming\u2013montreal global biodiversity framework, particularly target 2 on ecosystem restoration. drawing on global best practice, we identify six core principles when reviewing regulatory frameworks: integration with broader management strategies, ecosystem health and function, stakeholder engagement, climate change and resilience, long-term adaptive project structures, and financial and organizational sustainability. through a policy analysis and illustrative national examples, we demonstrate how coherent and adaptive regulatory systems can reduce barriers to restoration, enhance accountability, enable innovation, and align ecological objectives with social, cultural, and economic priorities. strengthening regulatory frameworks is therefore critical not only to scaling coral reef restoration, but also to safeguarding reefs from harmful practices and supporting long-term resilience in a rapidly changing climate.",
