          "name": "Fatima Ramadhanty Rahmat",
          "search_text": "fatima ramadhanty rahmat strengthening coastal plastic and waste management to improve the effectiveness of conservation management in the derawan islands and surrounding waters the derawan conservation area in berau regency, east kalimantan province, covers 285,548.95 hectares and is divided into three management zones: the core zone, the limited utilization zone, and other zones. although the region holds substantial potential for marine tourism and fisheries development, it faces increasing environmental pressures, particularly the accumulation of coastal and marine debris originating from both domestic and tourism-related activities. data from 2023 show that 83 non-household establishments, including accommodations and restaurants, generated up to 630.8 kg of waste per day during the holiday idul fitri peak season. waste found in surrounding waters does not solely originate from local sources, raising concerns about the long-term sustainability of ecosystems within the derawan marine protected areas (mpa).\r\n\r\nresearch demonstrates that plastic debris has severe impacts on coral reefs. lamb et.al. (2018), indicate that plastic can facilitate microbial colonization by pathogens associated with marine disease outbreaks, increasing the probability of coral disease from 4% to 89% upon contact. structurally complex coral species are disproportionately affected, posing risks to reef-associated habitats and fisheries. wwf indonesia (2024) recorded daily waste generation of approximately 572.95 kg on derawan island, dominated by organic waste and multilayer plastics. monitoring by the plastic free ocean network (pfon) from june 2024 to july 2025 reported an average of 5.88 kg of marine debris, primarily soft plastics, indicating ongoing irresponsible disposal practices.\r\n\r\nmitigation measures include establishing a material recovery facility (mrf), conducting awareness campaigns, strengthening policy advocacy, and organizing regular coastal clean-ups. between february and september 2025, around 410 residents participated in environmental activities, supported by 12 public infographics and 77 media publications reaching nearly 99,703 accounts. the mrf facility can process approximately 3,159.2 kg of inorganic waste per month. continued assessments and enhanced multi-stakeholder collaboration are essential to sustain plastic waste management and protect coastal and marine ecosystems.",
