          "search_text": "steven schill scaling reef monitoring with ai: identifying elkhorn coral using uas imagery and deep learning coral reefs provide critical ecosystem services but face increasing threats from climate change, disease, and human impacts. monitoring these ecosystems at scale is essential for conservation and restoration, yet traditional diver-based surveys are costly, time-consuming, and prone to observer bias. deep learning offers a transformative solution by enabling automated, accurate detection of coral colonies from large volumes of remotely sensed imagery. in this study, we developed and evaluated a deep learning model specifically designed to identify elkhorn coral (acropora palmata)\u2013a keystone reef-building species in the caribbean\u2013and distinguish it from other coral species and non-coral elements such as dead coral, rocks, sargassum, and soft corals. to build a robust dataset, we collected high-resolution uncrewed aerial system (uas) imagery (~2 cm spatial resolution) under varying environmental conditions across a ~3 km reef section in the u.s. virgin islands, using a wingtraone vtol platform equipped with a 61 mp sony rgb61 camera. additional ultra-high-resolution imagery was acquired for visual verification, and snorkelers captured several hundred georeferenced images for field-referencing. these combined datasets were used to train and validate multiple deep learning models for colony-level detection. our models demonstrated strong capability in accurately classifying a. palmata while minimizing false positives from other reef features. this approach highlights the potential of integrating advanced computer vision techniques with aerial and in-water imagery to improve coral monitoring and restoration planning at scale. beyond species identification, such technology enables early detection of bleaching, disease, and structural damage, supporting rapid conservation responses. accurate mapping of coral colonies also informs restoration strategies, including prioritizing outplanting sites and modeling larval connectivity. by incorporating deep learning into reef monitoring workflows, researchers can process data efficiently, enhance precision, and deliver actionable insights for adaptive management and climate resilience. quantifying coral reef benefits and larval connectivity in the coralcarib project the coralcarib project, led by the nature conservancy (tnc), is a six-year initiative (2023\u20132028) funded by germany\u2019s international climate initiative (iki) to restore and protect coral reefs across cuba, the dominican republic, haiti, and jamaica. the project\u2019s overarching goals are to enhance coastal resilience, support sustainable livelihoods, and strengthen policy frameworks for long-term reef conservation. we present two critical components of coralcarib: (1) ecosystem service valuation of coral reefs and (2) coral larval connectivity modeling to inform restoration strategies. our ecosystem service valuation estimates that coralcarib reefs provide approximately $500 million annually in benefits, distributed across coastal protection (53%), tourism (41%), and fisheries (6%). coastal protection services alone prevent an estimated $264 million in flood damage each year, and when combined with mangroves and seagrass, this figure rises to $346 million during a major hurricane event. tourism benefits include both on-reef activities (e.g., snorkeling and diving) and reef-adjacent recreation (e.g., beach visits, boating), reflecting the reefs\u2019 role in sustaining local economies. fisheries contribute roughly $29 million per year, calculated using site-level data such as average daily catch, number of active boats, fishing effort, and market prices for key species. to guide restoration, we developed a coral larval dispersal and connectivity model using the delft3d flexible mesh (delft3d fm) hydrodynamic framework at 250 m and 1 km resolution. this model simulates density-driven currents, salinity and temperature gradients, tidal forces, and wave propagation, enabling high-resolution tracking of larval dispersal from current and potential outplant sites. connectivity outputs identify reefs most likely to benefit from restoration, providing actionable insights for optimizing coral outplanting strategies and enhancing recruitment across the region. together, these analyses demonstrate the substantial economic and ecological value of coralcarib reefs and provide science-based tools for maximizing restoration impact and resilience under climate change.",
