          "name": "Tiara Stark",
          "search_text": "tiara stark exploring drivers of coral outplant success on a priority reef in maui, hawai\u2018i coral reefs in hawai\u2018i are ecologically, culturally, and economically essential, yet they are increasingly threatened by ocean warming and local stressors. as coral restoration becomes a key tool for supporting reef resilience, identifying the biological and environmental factors that drive outplant success is critical. this pilot study investigates the drivers of coral outplant survival and performance at olowalu reef, a priority restoration site on maui, hawai\u2018i. through the super reefs alliance\u2013a collaboration among the nature conservancy, stanford university, and the woods hole oceanographic institution\u2013we experimentally evaluated thermal tolerance in coral colonies from eight reef habitats across west maui characterized by different historical temperature regimes. we outplanted 259 unheated control fragments representing a range of heat-tolerance scores into paired warm and cool plots at olowalu. fragments are monitored biannually for survival, growth, and bleaching, with analyses examining how biological traits and environmental conditions influence performance. preliminary observations indicate variation among species and across site conditions, with full-year results expected to clarify these patterns. to complement outplant monitoring, parent colonies are being tracked at three sites to determine whether experimentally measured thermal tolerance predicts bleaching responses in situ. by integrating genet identity, species, measured heat tolerance, origin site, and environmental context, this study aims to identify patterns that can guide adaptive coral restoration under climate change. ultimately, linking laboratory-based thermal tolerance with field-based outplant outcomes can support more effective, climate-informed restoration strategies in hawai\u2018i and beyond.",
