          "name": "Gina-marie Maddix",
          "search_text": "gina-marie maddix substrate augmentation strategy to enhance recruitment and survivorship of orbicella faveolata in east portland, jamaica coral reefs in east portland, jamaica, are in a degraded state, reflecting a persistent coral-macroalgae phase shift. this shift is primarily driven by anthropogenic stressors including high nutrient loads from in-land agricultural run-off and the loss of key herbivores due to overfishing and disease. the condition is quantified by a poor coral reef health index value of 2.4 (2023), as coral cover, commercial and herbivorous fish abundance were critically low, while macroalgal benthic coverage exceeded 50%. this dominance has resulted in a significant loss of biodiversity and structural complexity within the area which has impacted the reef\u2019s capacity to provide ecosystem services and its ability to recover from disturbances.   \r\nto test a scalable restoration intervention, manual macroalgal removal was combined with coral outplanting over a two-year period (2024-2026). on three fringing reefs: salt creek, turtle crawle, and winnifred, fleshy macroalgae were periodically removed by scraping and pulling from 25 m2 experimental plots. 162 'heat-tolerant' orbicella faveolata macrofragments were outplanted within selected plots and benthic cover changes were monitored using 3d photogrammetry. \r\nan average of 51.18kg of wet macroalgae was removed per cleaning event and the mean initial area of fragments outplanted in march 2025 was 13.43cm2. from march to september 2025, fragments had an average daily growth of 1.02mm2 and 1.06mm2 in algal removal plots and macroalgae plots respectively.  survivorship of orbicella faveolata fragments was 99.2% over the first 7 months, with one fragment mortality from an algal removal plot.  \r\npotentially, manual macroalgal removal paired with coral outplanting could be a cost-effective, low-tech intervention which reduces algal competition and facilitates coral recovery. this strategy is a viable, replicable and sustainable (less extractive) method for reef restoration in jamaica\u2019s marine protected areas and can be implemented by engaging local communities.  \r\n\r\nkeywords: coral restoration; macroalgal removal; orbicella faveolata, community-based management; jamaica",
